ack 1/4 inch from its original location, using the distinct line in the paint, stain, or varnish to align it. Nail the side and top piece of the door stop back on. This should allow the door to shut and lock normally. Additionally, you may need to enlarge the latch hole in the jamb, tighten the hinges, enlarge the strike plate hole, or move the strike plate if the latch doesn't align with the hole in the strike plate.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To fix a door that won't lock, first shut the door and try to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it loose. Remove the door stop and set it back 1/4 inch from its original location, using the distinct line in the paint, stain, or varnish to align it. Nail the side and top piece of the door stop back on. This should allow the door to shut and lock normally. Additionally, you may need to enlarge the latch hole in the jamb, tighten the hinges, enlarge the strike plate hole, or move the strike plate if the latch doesn't align with the hole in the strike plate.\", \"Answer: To fix a door that won't lock, follow these steps from the passages:  \\n1. Shut the door and attempt to lock it. If it fails to lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb. Pry up until it loosens and remove it.  \\n2. Use the chisel to remove the door stop on top, then reposition it 1/4 inch from its original location on the jamb (using the visible line in the paint, stain, or varnish as a guide).  \\n3. Nail the door stop back on, which should allow the door to shut and lock normally.  \\nThis method addresses misalignment issues in the door stop that prevent proper locking.\", \"Answer: To fix a door that won't lock, first shut the door and try to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it loose. Remove the door stop, then place it back 1/4 inch from its original location on the jamb, using the distinct line in the paint, stain, or varnish as a guide for alignment. Nail the side and top piece of door stop back on, which should allow the door to shut and lock normally.\", \"Answer: To fix a door that won't lock, first shut the door and try to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it loose. Remove the door stop on top as well, then place it back on the jamb set back 1/4 inch from its original location. Use the distinct line in the paint, stain, or varnish where it came off to align the door stop. Nail the side and top piece of door stop back on. The door should now shut and lock normally.\", \"Answer: To fix a door that won't lock, first shut the door and attempt to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it loose, then remove it. Use the chisel to remove the door stop on top as well, and place the door stop back on the jamb set back 1/4 inch from its original location, using the distinct line in the paint, stain, or varnish to align it. Nail the side and top piece of the door stop back on. The door should now shut and lock normally. Additionally, if the door latch won’t catch, tighten the hinges first, then enlarge the strike plate hole and move the strike plate to align with the latch.\"]", "time_sec": 54.899, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 65.61, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "32", "question": "how to fix a door that won't lock", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's a possible solution to fix a door that won't lock:\n\n1. Check if the door stops are properly aligned. If the door stops are not aligned, use a chisel to remove the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it up until it comes loose. Remove the door stop and use the chisel to remove the door stop on top as well. Place the door stop back on the jamb and set it back 1/4 inch from its original location. Nail the side and top piece of door stop back on.\n2. Check if the latch hole in the jamb is properly aligned with the strike plate. If the latch doesn't align with the hole in the strike plate, tighten the hinges first. Then, enlarge the strike plate hole. Move the strike plate if necessary.\n\nIf these steps do not solve the problem, it may be necessary to seek further assistance from a professional locksmith or carpenter.", "pred": "To fix a door that won't lock, follow these steps based on the passages:", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To fix a door that won't lock, follow these steps based on the passages:  \n1. Shut the door and attempt to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it loose.  \n2. Remove the door stop on top using the chisel.  \n3. Place the door stop back on the jamb, setting it 1/4 inch from its original location (a distinct line in the paint, stain, or varnish will show where it was removed).  \n4. Nail the side and top piece of the door stop back on.  \nThis adjustment should allow the door to shut and lock normally.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To fix a door that won't lock, follow these steps based on the passages:  \\n1. Shut the door and attempt to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it loose.  \\n2. Remove the door stop on top using the chisel.  \\n3. Place the door stop back on the jamb, setting it 1/4 inch from its original location (a distinct line in the paint, stain, or varnish will show where it was removed).  \\n4. Nail the side and top piece of the door stop back on.  \\nThis adjustment should allow the door to shut and lock normally.\", \"Answer: To fix a door that won't lock, first shut the door and try to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it loose. Remove the door stop, then set it back 1/4 inch from its original location on the jamb, using the distinct line in the paint, stain, or varnish as a guide for alignment. Nail the side and top piece of the door stop back on. The door should now shut and lock normally.\", \"Answer: To fix a door that won't lock, first shut the door and try to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it up until it comes loose. Remove the door stop and use the chisel to remove the door stop on top as well. Place the door stop back on the jamb, setting it back 1/4 inch from its original location, using the distinct line in the paint, stain, or varnish to align it. Nail the side and top piece of door stop back on. The door should now shut and lock normally. Additionally, you may need to tighten the hinges, enlarge the latch hole in the jamb, enlarge the strike plate hole, or move the strike plate if the latch doesn't align with the hole in the strike plate.\", \"Answer: Shut the door and try to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it loose. Remove the door stop, set it back 1/4 inch from its original location using the distinct line as a guide, and nail it back on. The door should now shut and lock normally.\", \"Answer: To fix a door that won't lock, follow these steps based on the given passages: 1) Shut the door and try to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it loose. 2) Remove the door stop and set it back 1/4 inch from its original location, using the distinct line in the paint, stain, or varnish as a guide for alignment. 3) Nail the side and top piece of the door stop back on. 4) If the door still won't lock, tighten the hinges and enlarge the latch hole in the jamb. 5) Enlarge the strike plate hole and move the strike plate to ensure proper alignment with the latch. 6) Perform a \\\"lipstick test\\\" to check for misalignment between the latch and the strike plate hole. If the latch contacts the strike plate too high or too low, ensure all door hinge screws are tight.\"]", "time_sec": 56.671, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 12.5, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "33", "question": "how to cook pollock?", "gt": "To cook pollock, first preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C). Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray. Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper and place them on the baking sheet. Layer the fillets with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices. Bake in the preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, which should take about 15 minutes. If desired, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet and return fish to the oven to allow cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "pred": "To cook pollock, preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C), season the fillets with paprika and black pepper, place them on a baking sheet that has been sprayed with butter flavored cooking spray and layered with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, and bake for about 15 minutes until the fish has cooked and flakes easily. Optionally, after the initial baking, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To cook pollock, preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C), season the fillets with paprika and black pepper, place them on a baking sheet that has been sprayed with butter flavored cooking spray and layered with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, and bake for about 15 minutes until the fish has cooked and flakes easily. Optionally, after the initial baking, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To cook pollock, preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C), season the fillets with paprika and black pepper, place them on a baking sheet that has been sprayed with butter flavored cooking spray and layered with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, and bake for about 15 minutes until the fish has cooked and flakes easily. Optionally, after the initial baking, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\", \"Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, then bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Optionally, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\", \"Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray. Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper. Place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices. Bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\", \"Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, then bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Optionally, after baking, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\", \"Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, then bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Optionally, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\"]", "time_sec": 42.034, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 85.94, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "34", "question": "how to cook pollock?", "gt": "To cook pollock, preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C). Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper. Place the fillets on a baking sheet sprayed with butter flavored cooking spray. Optionally, layer the fillets with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices. Bake in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes, or until the fish is cooked and flakes easily. If desired, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, and return the fish to the oven for approximately 3 minutes to allow the cheese to melt.", "pred": "To cook pollock, preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C), season the fillets with paprika and black pepper, place them on a baking sheet sprayed with butter flavored cooking spray, layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, and bake for about 15 minutes until the fish has cooked and flakes easily. Optionally, after baking, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To cook pollock, preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C), season the fillets with paprika and black pepper, place them on a baking sheet sprayed with butter flavored cooking spray, layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, and bake for about 15 minutes until the fish has cooked and flakes easily. Optionally, after baking, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To cook pollock, preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C), season the fillets with paprika and black pepper, place them on a baking sheet sprayed with butter flavored cooking spray, layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, and bake for about 15 minutes until the fish has cooked and flakes easily. Optionally, after baking, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\", \"Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, place seasoned pollock fillets (with paprika and black pepper) on it, layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, and bake in a preheated oven for about 15 minutes until the fish flakes easily.\", \"Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, bake in a preheated oven until fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Turn oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return fish to the oven and allow cheese to melt, about 3 minutes.\", \"Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, place seasoned pollock fillets (with paprika and black pepper) layered with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices on the sheet, and bake in a preheated oven until the fish flakes easily, about 15 minutes.\", \"Answer: To cook pollock, preheat your oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C). Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper. Place the seasoned fillets on a baking sheet that has been sprayed with butter flavored cooking spray, and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices. Bake in the preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, which should take about 15 minutes. For an additional step to melt cheese on the fillets, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\"]", "time_sec": 38.198, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "ima