us": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "36", "question": "how to cook pollock?", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how to cook pollock:\n\nPassage 1:\n\n* Spray a baking sheet with butter-flavored cooking spray.\n* Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper.\n* Place the fillets on the baking sheet and layer them with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices.\n* Bake in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes or until the fish has cooked and flakes easily.\n\nPassage 2:\n\n* Preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C).\n* Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper.\n* Bake the fillets in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes or until the fish has cooked and flakes easily.\n\nPassage 3:\n\n* Bake the pollock fillets in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes or until the fish has cooked and flakes easily.\n* Turn off the oven and place two slices of cheese on top of each fillet.\n* Return the fish to the oven and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\n\nTherefore, to cook pollock, you can follow these steps:\n\n1. Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper.\n2. Place the fillets on a baking sheet or in the oven.\n3. Cook the pollock until it flakes easily, either by baking it in the oven or on the stovetop.\n4. Add cheese to the pollock if desired and return it to the oven to melt the cheese.", "pred": "Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, bake in preheated oven until fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes, then turn oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return fish to the oven and allow cheese to melt, about 3 minutes.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, bake in preheated oven until fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes, then turn oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return fish to the oven and allow cheese to melt, about 3 minutes.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, bake in preheated oven until fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes, then turn oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return fish to the oven and allow cheese to melt, about 3 minutes.\", \"Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, place seasoned pollock fillets (with paprika and black pepper) layered with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices on the sheet, bake in a preheated oven for about 15 minutes until the fish flakes easily.\", \"Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, place seasoned pollock fillets (with paprika and black pepper) on the sheet layered with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, and bake in a preheated oven until the fish flakes easily, about 15 minutes.\", \"Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes, then turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\", \"Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, then bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Alternatively, you can preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C), season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, and bake until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. After baking, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\"]", "time_sec": 37.627, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 49.74, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "37", "question": "how to cook pollock?", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how you can cook pollock:\n\n1. Preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C) (Passage 2).\n2. Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper (Passage 1 and 2).\n3. Place the pollock fillets on a baking sheet lined with butter-flavored cooking spray (Passage 1).\n4. Add onion, green pepper, and tomato slices to the baking sheet (Passage 1).\n5. Bake the pollock fillets in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes, or until the fish has cooked and flakes easily (Passage 1 and 2).\n6. To add cheesy flavor, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, and return the fish to the oven for an additional 3 minutes (Passage 3).\n\nNote that Passage 3 does not provide any specific instructions for cooking the pollock, but rather suggests adding cheese to the existing dish.", "pred": "To cook pollock, preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C). Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper. Place the seasoned fillets on a baking sheet that has been sprayed with butter flavored cooking spray. Layer the fillets with slices of onion, green pepper, and tomato. Bake in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes, or until the fish has cooked and flakes easily. After 15 minutes, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To cook pollock, preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C). Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper. Place the seasoned fillets on a baking sheet that has been sprayed with butter flavored cooking spray. Layer the fillets with slices of onion, green pepper, and tomato. Bake in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes, or until the fish has cooked and flakes easily. After 15 minutes, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To cook pollock, preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C). Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper. Place the seasoned fillets on a baking sheet that has been sprayed with butter flavored cooking spray. Layer the fillets with slices of onion, green pepper, and tomato. Bake in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes, or until the fish has cooked and flakes easily. After 15 minutes, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\", \"Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes, then turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\", \"Answer: To cook pollock, first preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C). Then, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper. Place the seasoned fillets on a baking sheet that has been sprayed with butter flavored cooking spray, and layer them with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices. Bake in the preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, which should take about 15 minutes. After 15 minutes, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, and return the fish to the oven to allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\", \"Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, then bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Optionally, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\", \"Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, then bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Alternatively, you can preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C), season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, and bake until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. After baking, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\"]", "time_sec": 49.207, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 62.07, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "38", "question": "how to cook pollock?", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how to cook pollock:\n\nPreheat the oven to 350°F (175°C). Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper. Place the fillets on a baking sheet lined with parchment paper or aluminum foil. You can also add sliced onions, green peppers, and tomatoes on top of the fillets. Bake for 15 minutes or until the fish flakes easily with a fork. If desired, top with cheese and return to the oven for an additional 3 minutes or until the cheese is melted.\n\nTherefore, the answer is not \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\"", "pred": "Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray. Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper. Place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices. Bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, and return the fish to the oven to allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray. Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper. Place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices. Bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, and return the fish to the oven to allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray. Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper. Place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices. Bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, and return the fish to the oven to allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\", \"Answer: To cook pollock, preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C). Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper. Place the fillets on a baking sheet that has been sprayed with butter flavored cooking spray, and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices. Bake in the preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, which takes about 15 minutes. After 15 minutes, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\", \"Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, then bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Alternatively, you can preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C), season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, and bake until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. After baking, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\", \"Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes, then turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\", \"Answer: To cook pollock, first preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C). Then, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper. Place the seasoned fillets on a baking sheet that has been sprayed with butter flavored cooking spray. Layer the fillets with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices. Bake in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes, or until the fish has cooked and flakes easily. After baking, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, and return the fish to the oven for about 3 minutes to allow the cheese to melt.\"]", "time_sec": 48.471, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 42.11, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "39", "question": "how to get plastic out of clothes dryer", "gt": "To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, you can run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper. Be careful not to damage the dryer's interior. You can then apply 1 to 2 tsp. of nail polish remover to a clean cloth, and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Afterward, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. You can mix 1 tsp. of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup water, dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again. Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. Some plastic may be extremely difficult to remove, in which case you can try heating the plastic with a hair dryer or heat gun and then peel it off. If not all plastic comes off, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer.", "pred": "To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off carefully with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off carefully with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off carefully with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\", \"Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, follow these steps: run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Rinse the area with a damp cloth, then mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is difficult to remove, try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun, then peel it off. If not all of the plastic can be removed, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, and it is recommended to call a service technician for drum replacement.\", \"Answer: Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 tsp. nail polish remover to a clean cloth, and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 tsp. liquid laundry soap with 1 cup water. Dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again. Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\", \"Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is extremely difficult to remove, you can try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun, then peeling it off. If you cannot get all of the plastic off, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, in which case you should call a service technician to complete the repair.\", \"Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is extremely difficult to remove, you can try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun and then peeling it off. If you cannot remove all of the plastic, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, in which case you should call a service technician to complete the repair.\"]", "time_sec": 67.11, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 72.63, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "40", "question": "how to get plastic out of clothes dryer", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is a brief answer to the question:\n\nTo get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first, run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Next, apply nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Rinse the area with a damp cloth and then mix liquid laundry soap with water. Dip a clean cloth in this solution and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.", "pred": "To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off carefully with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off carefully with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off carefully with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\", \"Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Rinse the area with a damp cloth to remove the nail polish remover. Next, mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is extremely difficult to remove, you can try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun, then peeling it off. However, if not all of the plastic can be removed, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, in which case it is recommended to call a service technician for the repair.\", \"Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, follow these steps based on the passages:  \\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.  \\n2. Scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, avoiding damage to the dryer’s interior.  \\n3. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it.  \\n4. Rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover.  \\n5. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again.  \\n6. Rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.  \\nThe passages also note that plastic may not melt in a dryer unless using the high heat setting, and modern dryers have a synthetic enamel coating that resists heat and wear.\", \"Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, follow these steps based on the provided passage: 1) Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. 2) Scrape the softened plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. 3) Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. 4) Rinse the area with a damp cloth to remove the nail polish remover. 5) Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. 6) Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. The passage also notes that plastic may not melt in a dryer unless high heat is used, and modern dryers have a synthetic enamel coating that resists heat and wear.\", \"Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\"]", "time_sec": 68.482, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 79.43, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "42", "question": "how to get plastic out of clothes dryer", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are some ways to remove plastic from a clothes dryer:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper. (Passage 1)\n2. Heat the plastic with a hair dryer or heat gun to make it easier to peel off. Once heated, use a cloth to peel off the plastic. (Passage 2)\n3. Soak a cloth in an acetone-based nail polish remover and use it to scrub the remaining plastic off of the element after leaving it on the element for 5 minutes. Then, wipe the coils with a clean, damp cloth and remove any remaining residue with a cloth dipped in a solution of warm water and dish soap. (Passage 3)\n\nUnable to answer based on the given passages. The passages do not provide specific instructions on how to remove plastic from the drum of a clothes dryer.", "pred": "To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off carefully with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to avoid damaging the interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it, followed by rinsing with a damp cloth. Next, mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is extremely difficult to remove or if it has melted and cannot be fully peeled off, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, and it is recommended to call a service technician for drum replacement.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off carefully with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to avoid damaging the interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it, followed by rinsing with a damp cloth. Next, mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is extremely difficult to remove or if it has melted and cannot be fully peeled off, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, and it is recommended to call a service technician for drum replacement.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off carefully with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to avoid damaging the interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it, followed by rinsing with a damp cloth. Next, mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is extremely difficult to remove or if it has melted and cannot be fully peeled off, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, and it is recommended to call a service technician for drum replacement.\", \"Answer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Rinse the area with a damp cloth to remove the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is extremely difficult to remove, try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun, then attempt to peel it off. If not all of the plastic can be removed, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, and in this case, it is recommended to call a service technician for the repair.\", \"Answer: Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off with a wooden or plastic spatula, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it, followed by rubbing the area with a damp cloth to rinse the remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\", \"Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, follow these steps: Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is difficult to remove, heat it with a hair dryer or heat gun, then peel it off. If not all plastic can be removed, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, and should call a service technician for drum replacement.\", \"Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper while taking care not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it, then rinse the area with a damp cloth. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\"]", "time_sec": 63.181, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 41.88, "GAVIE-Rel": 5.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 3.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "43", "question": "how to get plastic out of clothes dryer", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how to get plastic out of a clothes dryer:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n2. Scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior.\n3. Apply 1 to 2 tsp. nail polish remover to a clean cloth, and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover.\n4. Mix 1 tsp. liquid laundry soap with 1 cup water. Dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again. Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\n\nIf the plastic is still difficult to remove, you can try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun before attempting to scrape it off. However, if the plastic cannot be removed completely, it may be necessary to replace the drum or the entire dryer.", "pred": "To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, follow these steps based on the passages:", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, follow these steps based on the passages:  \n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.  \n2. Scrape the softened plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, avoiding damage to the dryer’s interior.  \n3. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it.  \n4. Rinse the area with a damp cloth to remove the nail polish remover.  \n5. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again.  \n6. Rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.  \n\nThe passages note that plastic may not melt in a dryer unless using the high heat setting, and modern dryers have a synthetic enamel coating that resists heat and wear. If the plastic is stubborn and cannot be fully removed, the passages suggest replacing the drum (by calling a service technician) or the entire dryer.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, follow these steps based on the passages:  \\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.  \\n2. Scrape the softened plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, avoiding damage to the dryer’s interior.  \\n3. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it.  \\n4. Rinse the area with a damp cloth to remove the nail polish remover.  \\n5. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again.  \\n6. Rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.  \\n\\nThe passages note that plastic may not melt in a dryer unless using the high heat setting, and modern dryers have a synthetic enamel coating that resists heat and wear. If the plastic is stubborn and cannot be fully removed, the passages suggest replacing the drum (by calling a service technician) or the entire dryer.\", \"Answer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off carefully with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to avoid damaging the dryer’s interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it, followed by wiping the area with a damp cloth to rinse off the remover. Next, mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again before rinsing thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water. As noted in passage 2, if heating the plastic with a hair dryer or heat gun allows it to be peeled off, that method may work, but if not all plastic can be removed, replacing the drum or the entire dryer may be necessary, ideally with help from a service technician.\", \"Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off carefully with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to avoid damaging the interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it, followed by rinsing the area with a damp cloth. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again before rinsing thoroughly with clean water. If the plastic is stubborn, heat it with a hair dryer or heat gun until pliable, then peel it off; if not all plastic can be removed, the drum or entire dryer may need replacement, and a service technician should be called for drum replacement.\", \"Answer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\", \"Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off carefully with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to avoid damaging the dryer's interior. Next, apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it, followed by rinsing the area with a damp cloth. After that, mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again before rinsing well with a cloth dipped in clean water. As noted in passage 2, if the plastic is extremely difficult to remove or if heating with a hair dryer or heat gun does not allow for complete removal, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, and in such cases, it is recommended to call a service technician for the repair.\"]", "time_sec": 71.138, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 12.07, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "44", "question": "how to get plastic out of clothes dryer", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's a step-by-step guide on how to remove plastic from a clothes dryer:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n2. Scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior.\n3. Apply 1 to 2 tsp. nail polish remover to a clean cloth, and rub the plastic to dissolve it.\n4. Rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover.\n5. Mix 1 tsp. liquid laundry soap with 1 cup water. Dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again.\n6. Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\n\nIf the plastic does not come off with this method, another option is to use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic until it becomes soft and pliable. Then, attempt to peel it off carefully. However, be aware that this method may not work, and in that case, replacing the drum or the entire dryer may be necessary. It is recommended to call a service technician for this type of repair.\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question of how to get plastic out of clothes dryer is:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n2. Scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper.\n3. Apply nail polish remover to dissolve the plastic.\n4. Rinse with a damp cloth and soap solution.\n5. Use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic if necessary.\n\nIf the plastic still does not come off, consider replacing the drum or the entire dryer.", "pred": "To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, follow the instructions in Passage 1: Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Rinse the area with a damp cloth, then mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water. Dip a clean cloth in this solution and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, follow the instructions in Passage 1: Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Rinse the area with a damp cloth, then mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water. Dip a clean cloth in this solution and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, follow the instructions in Passage 1: Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Rinse the area with a damp cloth, then mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water. Dip a clean cloth in this solution and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\", \"Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again. Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic does not come off easily, you can try heating the plastic with a hair dryer or heat gun and then peeling it off. If you are unable to remove all of the plastic, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, in which case you should call a service technician to complete the repair.\", \"Answer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is extremely difficult to remove, you can try heating the plastic with a hair dryer or heat gun, and then attempt to peel it off. If all else fails, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, in which case it is recommended to call a service technician for the repair.\", \"Answer: Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 tsp. nail polish remover to a clean cloth, and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 tsp. liquid laundry soap with 1 cup water. Dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again. Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\", \"Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper while being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it, followed by rubbing the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again, then rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\"]", "time_sec": 62.484, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 50.0, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "46", "question": "how to fold a quilt", "gt": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "pred": "Fold the lower right corner up toward the center of the quilt making sure the fold is on the bias. Fold the lower left corner up toward the center of the quilt making sure the fold is on the bias. Fold the top left corner down toward the center of the quilt making sure the fold is on the bias. Fold the top right corner down toward the center of the quilt making sure the fold is on the bias.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Fold the lower right corner up toward the center of the quilt making sure the fold is on the bias. Fold the lower left corner up toward the center of the quilt making sure the fold is on the bias. Fold the top left corner down toward the center of the quilt making sure the fold is on the bias. Fold the top right corner down toward the center of the quilt making sure the fold is on the bias.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Fold the lower right corner up toward the center of the quilt making sure the fold is on the bias. Fold the lower left corner up toward the center of the quilt making sure the fold is on the bias. Fold the top left corner down toward the center of the quilt making sure the fold is on the bias. Fold the top right corner down toward the center of the quilt making sure the fold is on the bias.\", \"Answer: To fold a quilt as described in the passage, follow these steps:  \\n1. Lay the quilt on a flat surface.  \\n2. Fold the lower right corner up toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.  \\n3. Fold the lower left corner up toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.  \\n4. Fold the top left corner down toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.  \\n5. Fold the top right corner down toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.  \\n\\nUnable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Fold the lower right corner up toward the center of the quilt making sure the fold is on the bias. Fold the lower left corner up toward the center of the quilt making sure the fold is on the bias. Fold the top left corner down toward the center of the quilt making sure the fold is on the bias. Fold the top right corner down toward the center of the quilt making sure the fold is on the bias.\", \"Answer: Fold the lower right corner up toward the center of the quilt making sure the fold is on the bias; Fold the lower left corner up toward the center of the quilt making sure the fold is on the bias; Fold the top left corner down toward the center of the quilt making sure the fold is on the bias; Fold the top right corner down toward the center of the quilt making sure the fold is on the bias.\", \"Answer: To fold a quilt as described in the passage, follow these steps: 1) Lay your quilt on a flat surface. 2) Fold the lower right corner up toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias. 3) Fold the lower left corner up toward the center of the quilt, making sure the fold is on the bias. 4) Fold the top left corner down toward the center of the quilt, with the fold on the bias. 5) Finally, fold the top right corner down toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias. This method can be used for any size quilt, such as the Timepiece Quilt which measures 56 inches by 70 inches.\"]", "time_sec": 45.784, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "47", "question": "how to fold a quilt", "gt": "To fold a quilt, follow these steps:\n\n1. Lay the quilt on a flat surface such as a bed or table.\n2. Fold the lower right corner up towards the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.\n3. Fold the lower left corner up towards the center of the quilt, again on the bias.\n4. Fold the top left corner down towards the center of the quilt, again on the bias.\n5. Finally, fold the top right corner down towards the center of the quilt, making sure the fold is also on the bias.", "pred": "To fold a quilt as described in the passages, follow these steps:", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To fold a quilt as described in the passages, follow these steps:  \n1. Lay the quilt on a flat surface (e.g., bed or table).  \n2. Fold the lower right corner up toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.  \n3. Fold the lower left corner up toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.  \n4. Fold the top left corner down toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.  \n5. Fold the top right corner down toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.  \n\nThis method is demonstrated for the Timepiece Quilt (56 inches by 70 inches) but can be applied to any quilt size.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To fold a quilt as described in the passages, follow these steps:  \\n1. Lay the quilt on a flat surface (e.g., bed or table).  \\n2. Fold the lower right corner up toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.  \\n3. Fold the lower left corner up toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.  \\n4. Fold the top left corner down toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.  \\n5. Fold the top right corner down toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.  \\n\\nThis method is demon