{"id": "63", "question": "set default browser to windows", "gt": "Sure, I can help you with that! Based on the given passages, here are the steps to set the default browser to Windows:\n\nMethod 1:\n\n1. Open the Settings app by clicking the Start menu and then clicking the Settings icon, or by using the Windows + I keyboard shortcut.\n2. In the Settings app, click the first option titled System (display, notifications, apps, power).\n3. In the left-pane, click Default apps.\n4. On the right-side, scroll down the page to see Web browser section.\n5. Click on the Edge browser entry (or any browser other than Chrome) to reveal Choose an app, and then click Google Chrome to set it as default browser.\n\nAlternatively, you can also set Mozilla Firefox as your default Windows browser by following these steps:\n\n1. Open your Firefox browser.\n2. Click on the Firefox menu button, represented by three horizontal lines and located in the upper right-hand corner of the browser window.\n3. When the pop-out menu appears, select Options.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you have any further questions.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer:Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\"]", "time_sec": 7.073, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 4.58, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "64", "question": "what is the proper way to dispose of a worn usa flag", "gt": "The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is either by separating the blue field from the flag and burning it in a peaceful manner before burying the ashes, or by conducting a proper ceremony where the flag is folded in the customary triangle fold and then burned.", "pred": "The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, folding it in the customary triangle fold beforehand. The blue field must be removed first, then burned peacefully with ashes buried.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, folding it in the customary triangle fold beforehand. The blue field must be removed first, then burned peacefully with ashes buried.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, folding it in the customary triangle fold beforehand. The blue field must be removed first, then burned peacefully with ashes buried.\", \"Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony after folding it in the customary triangle fold. Alternatively, you can put the flag out for collection after removing the blue field, which should then be burned peacefully and the ashes buried.\", \"Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony after folding it in the customary triangle fold, and if necessary, remove the blue field first, then burn the blue field in a peaceful manner and bury the ashes.\", \"Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, folding the flag in the customary triangle fold beforehand. This involves personally discharging the worn flag from service and conducting the ceremony as the proper method, which includes burning the flag and then burying the ashes of the blue field if it has been removed.\", \"Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it in a proper ceremony after folding it in the customary triangle fold.\"]", "time_sec": 22.249, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 56.6, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "65", "question": "what is the proper way to dispose of a worn usa flag", "gt": "The proper way to dispose of a worn US flag is to burn it in a proper ceremony. The flag must be folded in the customary triangle fold before being burned.", "pred": "The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, folding it in the customary triangle fold beforehand.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, folding the flag in the customary triangle fold beforehand.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, folding the flag in the customary triangle fold beforehand.\", \"Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, folding it in the customary triangle fold beforehand.\", \"Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, where the flag must be folded in the customary triangle fold.\", \"Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, folding it in the customary triangle fold beforehand.\", \"Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, folding it in the customary triangle fold beforehand.\"]", "time_sec": 15.097, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 64.71, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "67", "question": "what is the proper way to dispose of a worn usa flag", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the proper way to dispose of a worn US flag is to either:\n\n1. Burn it in a peaceful manner, removing the blue field first and burying the ashes, or\n2. Perform a proper ceremony by folding the flag in a triangle fold before burning it.\n\nTherefore, the answer is: \"Option 1 or Option 2.\"", "pred": "The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, folding it in the customary triangle fold beforehand. This is specified in passage 3 as \"the other method\" and requires personal discharge of the worn flag from service. Passage 2 emphasizes honoring the flag during the ceremony by stating, \"We honor them all as we salute one Flag,\" and passage 1 describes an alternative method involving removing the blue field for burning and burying ashes, but passage 3 explicitly identifies burning the entire folded flag as the proper ceremonial method.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, folding it in the customary triangle fold beforehand. This is specified in passage 3 as \"the other method\" and requires personal discharge of the worn flag from service. Passage 2 emphasizes honoring the flag during the ceremony by stating, \"We honor them all as we salute one Flag,\" and passage 1 describes an alternative method involving removing the blue field for burning and burying ashes, but passage 3 explicitly identifies burning the entire folded flag as the proper ceremonial method.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, folding it in the customary triangle fold beforehand. This is specified in passage 3 as \\\"the other method\\\" and requires personal discharge of the worn flag from service. Passage 2 emphasizes honoring the flag during the ceremony by stating, \\\"We honor them all as we salute one Flag,\\\" and passage 1 describes an alternative method involving removing the blue field for burning and burying ashes, but passage 3 explicitly identifies burning the entire folded flag as the proper ceremonial method.\", \"Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it in a proper ceremony, folding it in the customary triangle fold before burning. The blue field should be removed and burned separately in a peaceful manner, with the ashes buried.\", \"Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, folding the flag in the customary triangle fold beforehand.\", \"Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, where the flag must be folded in the customary triangle fold before being burned.\", \"Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, folding it in the customary triangle fold beforehand. The blue field must be removed and burned separately, with its ashes buried.\"]", "time_sec": 23.802, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 39.6, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "68", "question": "what is the proper way to dispose of a worn usa flag", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the proper way to dispose of a worn US flag is to either:\n\n1. Remove the blue field and burn it in a peaceful manner, then bury the ashes.\n2. Fold the flag in the customary triangle fold and burn it in a proper ceremony.\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n1. Remove the blue field and burn it, or\n2. Fold the flag in the customary triangle fold and burn it in a proper ceremony.", "pred": "The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, folding the flag in the customary triangle fold before doing so.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, folding the flag in the customary triangle fold before doing so.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, folding the flag in the customary triangle fold before doing so.\", \"Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, where the flag must be folded in the customary triangle fold before burning.\", \"Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it in a proper ceremony, after folding it in the customary triangle fold. The blue field should be removed and burned separately, with the ashes buried.\", \"Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it yourself in a proper ceremony, folding the flag in the customary triangle fold beforehand.\", \"Answer: The proper way to dispose of a worn USA flag is to burn it in a proper ceremony, with the flag folded in the customary triangle fold.\"]", "time_sec": 16.446, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 39.34, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "73", "question": "how to tell if your male coworker is flirting with you", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are some ways to tell if your male coworker is flirting with you:\n\n1. Physical contact: Avoid physical contact that could be misinterpreted as fondling or sexual activity (Passage 1).\n2. Eye contact: If your coworker is consistently staring at you, it may be a sign that they are interested in you (Passage 2).\n3. Smiling: If your coworker smiles at you excessively, even when there is no apparent reason to do so, it may be a sign of flirtation (Passage 2).\n4. Seeking contact: If your coworker consistently finds reasons to be near you or seek you out discreetly, it may indicate that they have feelings for you (Passage 3).\n\nHowever, it is essential to remember that these signs alone do not necessarily mean that your coworker is interested in you romantically. It's important to pay attention to the context of their behavior and communicate openly to avoid misunderstandings.\n\nIf you are unsure about your coworker's intentions, it may be best to avoid physical contact and observe their behavior over time before making any conclusions. Additionally, it's crucial to respect your coworker's boundaries and maintain a professional work environment.", "pred": "Based on the provided passages, here are ways to tell if your male coworker is flirting with you:", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Based on the provided passages, here are ways to tell if your male coworker is flirting with you:\n\n1. **Physical Contact**: If he finds any reason to touch you, this is a sign he may be flirting with you. However, avoid physical contact that eludes to sexual activity or can be misconstrued as fondling to prevent misunderstandings or losing your job, especially if he seems uncomfortable or suggests you not continue flirting.\n\n2. **Staring**: If it always seems your male coworker is staring at you when you see her, that's a sign he might be interested in you.\n\n3. **Smiling**: If you notice your male coworker always smiles at you at work when there is no good reason for it, she may like you.\n\n4. **Seeking Discreet Contact**: If he is constantly at your desk and seeking you out discreetly, it's a guarantee that the person likes you.\n\n5. **Listening to Your Words**: Finding any reason to have contact with you or hinging on your every word are obvious signs of affection or admiration.\n\nRemember, if the guy you're flirting with seems uncomfortable or suggests that you not continue flirting with him, it's important to respect his feelings and avoid risking your job by flirting with someone who is not interested.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Based on the provided passages, here are ways to tell if your male coworker is flirting with you:\\n\\n1. **Physical Contact**: If he finds any reason to touch you, this is a sign he may be flirting with you. However, avoid physical contact that eludes to sexual activity or can be misconstrued as fondling to prevent misunderstandings or losing your job, especially if he seems uncomfortable or suggests you not continue flirting.\\n\\n2. **Staring**: If it always seems your male coworker is staring at you when you see her, that's a sign he might be interested in you.\\n\\n3. **Smiling**: If you notice your male coworker always smiles at you at work when there is no good reason for it, she may like you.\\n\\n4. **Seeking Discreet Contact**: If he is constantly at your desk and seeking you out discreetly, it's a guarantee that the person likes you.\\n\\n5. **Listening to Your Words**: Finding any reason to have contact with you or hinging on your every word are obvious signs of affection or admiration.\\n\\nRemember, if the guy you're flirting with seems uncomfortable or suggests that you not continue flirting with him, it's important to respect his feelings and avoid risking your job by flirting with someone who is not interested.\", \"Based on the provided passages, here is how to tell if your male coworker is flirting with you, using specific examples and details from the text:\\n\\n1.  **He avoids physical contact that eludes to sexual activity or can be misconstrued as fondling.** (Passage 1, points 1 and 3: \\\"Avoid physical contact that eludes to sexual activity or can be misconstrued as fondling.\\\" and \\\"You don't want to risk losing your job flirting with him, especially when he's not interested.\\\")\\n2.  **He seems uncomfortable or suggests that you not continue flirting with him.** (Passage 1, points 2 and 3: \\\"If the guy you're flirting with seems uncomfortable or suggests that you not continue flirting with him, do so.\\\")\\n3.  **He finds reasons to touch you, as this is a sign he likes you.** (Passage 2, point 4: \\\"Your co-worker is flirting with you if her finds any reason to touch you. If she didn’t like you, she probably wouldn’t do this since it is a little obnoxious.\\\")\\n4.  **He smiles at you without a good reason.** (Passage 2, point 3: \\\"Smiling is one of the signs a co-worker is flirting with you. If you notice a co-worker always smiles at you at work when there is no good reason for it, she may like you.\\\")\\n\\nThese points directly describe behaviors that indicate a male coworker is flirting with you, as stated in the passages.\", \"Answer: According to the passages, you can tell if your male coworker is flirting with you if he constantly finds reasons to touch you, as this is described as \\\"a little obnoxious\\\" if he doesn't like you, and is \\\"an obvious sign of affection or admiration.\\\" Additionally, if he constantly seeks you out discreetly and is always at your desk, this is a guarantee that he likes you. However, the passages do not provide specific examples or numbers to further illustrate these signs.\", \"Answer: According to the passages, signs that a male coworker may be flirting with you include:  \\n1. He **avoids physical contact** that could be misconstrued as sexual or fondling, as this is advised to prevent misunderstandings or losing your job, especially if he seems uncomfortable or uninterested (Passage 1).  \\n2. He **finds reasons to touch you**, which the passages describe as \\\"obnoxious\\\" if he doesn’t like you, implying it’s a deliberate sign of interest (Passage 2).  \\n3. He **smiles at you frequently without a clear reason**, which is explicitly listed as a sign of flirting (Passage 2).  \\n4. He **constantly seeks you out discreetly** (e.g., showing up at your desk) or **finds reasons to be near you**, which the passages state is a \\\"guarantee\\\" or \\\"obvious sign\\\" of affection (Passage 3).  \\n5. He **pays close attention to your words**, as the passages note that \\\"finding any reason to have contact with you\\\" and \\\"hinged on your every word\\\" are indicators of interest (Passage 3).  \\n\\nThese behaviors align with general workplace flirting cues, emphasizing subtle actions like touch, attention, and proximity, while avoiding overt physical advances to mitigate professional risks.\", \"Answer: To tell if your male coworker is flirting with you, look for these signs based on the passages:  \\n1. He finds any reason to touch you, as this is described as a sign of flirting (passage 2, point 4: \\\"Your co-worker is flirting with you if her finds any reason to touch you\\\").  \\n2. He constantly seeks you out discreetly or is always at your desk, which is a \\\"guarantee that the person likes you\\\" (passage 3, point 2: \\\"If she, or he, is constantly at your desk and seeking you out discreetly, it’s a guarantee that the person likes you\\\").  \\n3. He smiles at you without a good reason (passage 2, point 3: \\\"Smiling is one of the signs a co-worker is flirting with you\\\" and point 2: \\\"If you notice a co-worker always smiles at you at work when there is no good reason for it, she may like you\\\").  \\n4. He avoids physical contact that could be misconstrued as sexual or fondling, especially if you seem uncomfortable (passage 1, points 1 and 3: \\\"Avoid physical contact that eludes to sexual activity or can be misconstrued as fondling\\\" and \\\"You don't want to risk losing your job flirting with him, especially when he's not interested\\\").  \\n5. He suggests continuing flirting if you seem uncomfortable (passage 1, point 2: \\\"If the guy you're flirting with seems uncomfortable or suggests that you not continue flirting with him, do so\\\").  \\nNote: The passages emphasize avoiding physical contact that could be misinterpreted and respecting boundaries to prevent job-related risks, but the specific signs of flirting are the touch, constant proximity, smiling, and verbal engagement.\"]", "time_sec": 104.871, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 13.25, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "74", "question": "how to tell if your male coworker is flirting with you", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are some signs that a male coworker may be flirting with you:\n\n* He finds reasons to be near you and seek you out discreetly (Passage 3)\n* He is always staring at you and catches your eye (Passage 2)\n* He touches you unnecessarily or finds excuses to have physical contact with you (Passage 2)\n* He smiles at you excessively or without apparent reason (Passage 2)\n* He seems uncomfortable or suggests that you not continue flirting with him (Passage 1)\n\nIf you observe these behaviors from a male coworker, it may be a sign that he is interested in you and flirting with you. However, it's essential to remember that these signs alone do not necessarily mean the person likes you romantically, and it's possible they just have a friendly interest in you. To confirm their intentions, consider observing their behavior over time, and if you feel comfortable, try initiating a conversation or asking them out.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Based solely on the provided passages, the information about how to tell if a male coworker is flirting with you is limited and not explicitly stated. The passages primarily describe signs related to female coworkers (e.g., \"staring,\" \"touching,\" \"smiling\") and general behaviors like \"finding reasons to be near you\" or \"finding any reason to have contact with you\" (passage 3). However, passage 3 states these signs apply to \"she, or he,\" indicating they could include male coworkers, but the passages do not provide specific, distinct signs for male coworkers. No details are given about how a male coworker’s behavior (e.g., specific actions, words, or context) would differ from the described signs for female coworkers. The passages also do not address how to confirm if a male coworker is flirting beyond these general behavioral cues, and they omit critical context like workplace policies (e.g., avoiding physical contact to prevent misinterpretation or job loss), which are mentioned in passage 1 but not tied to identifying flirting behavior.\n\nSince the passages do not provide specific, actionable guidance for identifying flirting from a male coworker (beyond the generic \"she, or he\" reference in passage 3), the question cannot be answered using only the given information.\n\nAnswer: Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "[\"Based solely on the provided passages, the information about how to tell if a male coworker is flirting with you is limited and not explicitly stated. The passages primarily describe signs related to female coworkers (e.g., \\\"staring,\\\" \\\"touching,\\\" \\\"smiling\\\") and general behaviors like \\\"finding reasons to be near you\\\" or \\\"finding any reason to have contact with you\\\" (passage 3). However, passage 3 states these signs apply to \\\"she, or he,\\\" indicating they could include male coworkers, but the passages do not provide specific, distinct signs for male coworkers. No details are given about how a male coworker’s behavior (e.g., specific actions, words, or context) would differ from the described signs for female coworkers. The passages also do not address how to confirm if a male coworker is flirting beyond these general behavioral cues, and they omit critical context like workplace policies (e.g., avoiding physical contact to prevent misinterpretation or job loss), which are mentioned in passage 1 but not tied to identifying flirting behavior.\\n\\nSince the passages do not provide specific, actionable guidance for identifying flirting from a male coworker (beyond the generic \\\"she, or he\\\" reference in passage 3), the question cannot be answered using only the given information.\\n\\nAnswer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: According to the passages, signs that a male coworker may be flirting with you include: 1) He avoids physical contact that could be misconstrued as sexual or fondling, and if he seems uncomfortable or asks you to stop flirting, he likely isn't interested, so you shouldn't risk your job; 2) If his co-worker is constantly staring at you, this can indicate interest; 3) If she finds any reason to touch you, even if it seems obnoxious, this suggests she likes you; 4) Smiling at you without a good reason may also be a sign of interest. Additionally, finding reasons to be near you, constantly seeking you out discreetly, or making contact with you are clear indicators of affection or admiration.\", \"Based solely on the provided passages, here is how to tell if your male coworker is flirting with you:\\n\\n1.  He might find any reason to touch you, as this is described as \\\"a little obnoxious\\\" if he doesn't like you (passage 2, point 4).\\n2.  He might constantly seek you out discreetly and be at your desk (passage 3, point 2).\\n3.  He might find reasons to have contact with you, which is described as an \\\"obvious sign of affection or admiration\\\" (passage 3, point 3).\", \"Answer: To determine if your male coworker is flirting with you, look for signs such as him finding reasons to touch you, as this is a sign of interest since it might be considered obnoxious if he didn't like you. Additionally, if he constantly seeks you out discreetly and is always at your desk, these actions are a guarantee that he likes you. Smiling frequently at you without a good reason is another sign that he may be flirting. Also, be cautious of physical contact that could be misconstrued as sexual or fondling, and ensure that any flirting does not risk your job, especially if he seems uncomfortable or suggests you stop.\", \"Answer: According to the passages, you can tell if your male coworker is flirting with you if he avoids physical contact that could be misconstrued as fondling, especially if he seems uncomfortable or suggests you stop flirting to avoid risking your job, particularly when he’s not interested. Additionally, if he constantly finds reasons to be near you, seeks you out discreetly, and makes any reason to have contact with you, these are signs he likes you.\"]", "time_sec": 61.595, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 4.88, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "75", "question": "how to cook brats", "gt": "Based on the passages provided, the steps to cook brats are as follows:\n\n1. Prick the bratwurst with a fork to prevent them from exploding as they cook. \n2. Combine beer and onions in a large pot and bring it to a boil. Submerge the bratwurst in the beer, adding red pepper flakes, garlic powder, salt, and pepper.\n3. Place the pot over medium heat and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes.\n4. In the meantime, preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat and lightly oil the grate.\n5. After simmering, remove the bratwurst from the beer mixture and reduce the heat to low, continuing to cook the onions.\n6. Cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill, turning occasionally, for about 10 to 14 minutes until they are browned evenly.\n7. Serve the bratwurst with the beer and onion mixture as a topping or side.", "pred": "To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Combine beer, onions, butter, and spices in a pot, then submerge the bratwurst. Simmer over medium heat for 15 to 20 minutes. Preheat a grill to medium-high, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally. Alternatively, cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes after removing from the beer mixture. Store cooked bratwurst in the fridge for up to 5 days or freeze for up to 3 months.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Combine beer, onions, butter, and spices in a pot, then submerge the bratwurst. Simmer over medium heat for 15 to 20 minutes. Preheat a grill to medium-high, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally. Alternatively, cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes after removing from the beer mixture. Store cooked bratwurst in the fridge for up to 5 days or freeze for up to 3 months.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Combine beer, onions, butter, and spices in a pot, then submerge the bratwurst. Simmer over medium heat for 15 to 20 minutes. Preheat a grill to medium-high, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally. Alternatively, cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes after removing from the beer mixture. Store cooked bratwurst in the fridge for up to 5 days or freeze for up to 3 months.\", \"Answer: To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Place them in a large stock pot with onions, butter, and beer. Simmer over medium heat for 15 to 20 minutes. Then, preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, you can cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can last for about 5 days in the fridge or up to 3 months in the freezer.\", \"Answer: To cook bratwurst, you can follow these steps based on the given passages: 1) Prick the bratwurst with a fork to prevent them from exploding as they cook. 2) Place the bratwurst in a large stock pot with onions, butter, and beer. 3) Bring the mixture to a boil, then reduce the heat to medium and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes. 4) Preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat and lightly oil the grate. 5) Cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, you can reduce heat to medium and cook for another 10 to 12 minutes after removing the bratwurst from the beer mixture, then continue cooking the onions on low heat. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side.\", \"Answer: To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Place them in a large stock pot with onions, butter, and beer, then simmer over medium heat for 15 to 20 minutes. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, you can cook bratwurst on a preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can last for about 5 days in the fridge or up to 3 months in the freezer.\", \"Answer: To cook brats, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Combine bratwurst, onions, butter, and beer in a large stock pot, then simmer over medium heat for 15 to 20 minutes. After simmering, preheat an outdoor grill to medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, some recipes suggest cooking the bratwurst directly on the preheated grill (turning once) for 5 to 10 minutes, or cooking onions in butter for 1-2 minutes before serving with the bratwurst. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for about 5 days or frozen for up to 3 months.\"]", "time_sec": 60.941, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 57.89, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.4, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "77", "question": "how to cook brats", "gt": "To cook brats, follow these steps:\n\n1. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat.\n2. Lightly oil the grate.\n3. Prick the bratwurst with a fork to prevent them from exploding as they cook.\n4. Place the bratwurst in a large pot with onions, butter, and beer.\n5. Place the pot over medium heat and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes.\n6. Remove the bratwurst from the beer mixture and continue cooking on the preheated grill for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally.\n7. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side.", "pred": "To cook brats, first prick them with a fork to prevent them from exploding. Place the bratwurst in a large stock pot with onions, butter, and beer. Bring the mixture to a boil, then reduce heat to medium and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes. After simmering, preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat and lightly oil the grate. Cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, you can cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can last for about 5 days in the fridge or up to 3 months in the freezer.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To cook brats, first prick them with a fork to prevent them from exploding. Place the bratwurst in a large stock pot with onions, butter, and beer. Bring the mixture to a boil, then reduce heat to medium and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes. After simmering, preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat and lightly oil the grate. Cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, you can cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can last for about 5 days in the fridge or up to 3 months in the freezer.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To cook brats, first prick them with a fork to prevent them from exploding. Place the bratwurst in a large stock pot with onions, butter, and beer. Bring the mixture to a boil, then reduce heat to medium and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes. After simmering, preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat and lightly oil the grate. Cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, you can cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can last for about 5 days in the fridge or up to 3 months in the freezer.\", \"Answer: To cook brats, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Place them in a large stock pot with onions, butter, and beer, then simmer over medium heat for 15 to 20 minutes. After simmering, preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst on the grill for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, you can cook the bratwurst in the beer mixture on the stove for 10 to 12 minutes, then finish on the preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for up to 5 days or in the freezer for up to 3 months.\", \"Answer: To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding, then simmer in a stock pot with onions, butter, and beer for 15 to 20 minutes. After simmering, preheat a grill for medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, cook bratwurst on the preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side.\", \"Answer: To cook brats, you can follow these steps based on the passages: First, prick the bratwurst with a fork to prevent them from exploding as they cook. Then, place the bratwurst in a large stock pot with onions, butter, and beer. Bring the mixture to a boil over medium heat, then reduce the heat to low and continue cooking the onions. After that, cook the bratwurst on a preheated outdoor grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once, or for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, you can cook the bratwurst in the beer mixture for 15 to 20 minutes. Serve the bratwurst with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Remember to brown the onions in butter for 1-2 minutes, stirring frequently to ensure even browning. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for about 5 days or in the freezer for up to 3 months.\", \"Answer: To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding, then simmer in a pot with onions, butter, and beer for 15-20 minutes. After simmering, preheat a grill to medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10-14 minutes, turning occasionally. Alternatively, you can cook bratwurst on a preheated grill for 5-10 minutes after reducing heat to low while cooking onions. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side.\"]", "time_sec": 61.331, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 67.14, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "78", "question": "how to cook brats", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how to cook brats:\n\nStep 1: Preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat.\n\nStep 2: Lightly oil the grate.\n\nStep 3: Combine beer and onions in a large pot, bring to a boil, and submerge the bratwurst. Add red pepper flakes, garlic powder, salt, and pepper.\n\nStep 4: Simmer the bratwurst in the beer mixture for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly.\n\nStep 5: Remove the bratwurst from the beer mixture and grill it on the preheated grill for an additional 5 to 10 minutes, turning occasionally, until browned and cooked through.\n\nStep 6: Serve the bratwurst with the beer mixture as a topping or side dish.\n\nNote: It's important to prick the bratwurst with a fork before cooking to prevent them from exploding while cooking. Also, cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for up to 5 days or frozen for up to 3 months.", "pred": "To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent them from exploding. Then, combine the bratwurst with onions, butter, and beer in a large stock pot. Place the pot over medium heat and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes. After simmering, preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat and lightly oil the grate. Cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, you can cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for about 5 days or in the freezer for up to 3 months.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent them from exploding. Then, combine the bratwurst with onions, butter, and beer in a large stock pot. Place the pot over medium heat and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes. After simmering, preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat and lightly oil the grate. Cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, you can cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for about 5 days or in the freezer for up to 3 months.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent them from exploding. Then, combine the bratwurst with onions, butter, and beer in a large stock pot. Place the pot over medium heat and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes. After simmering, preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat and lightly oil the grate. Cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, you can cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for about 5 days or in the freezer for up to 3 months.\", \"Answer: To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding, then place them in a stock pot with onions, butter, and beer. Simmer over medium heat for 15 to 20 minutes. After simmering, preheat the grill to medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, you can cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes after removing them from the beer mixture, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can last for about 5 days in the fridge or up to 3 months in the freezer, so write the storage date on the container.\", \"Answer: To cook brats, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Place them in a stock pot with onions, butter, and beer. Simmer over medium heat for 15 to 20 minutes. Then, preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, you can cook them in a beer mixture by reducing heat to medium and cooking for 10 to 12 minutes, then removing the bratwurst and continuing to cook the onions on low heat. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can last for about 5 days in the fridge or up to 3 months in the freezer.\", \"Answer: To cook brats, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Place them in a large stock pot with onions, butter, and beer. Bring the mixture to a boil, then reduce heat to medium and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, after simmering, cook the bratwurst on a preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for about 5 days or in the freezer for up to 3 months.\", \"Answer: To cook bratwurst, you can follow these steps based on the provided passages: 1) Prick the bratwurst with a fork to prevent them from exploding as they cook. 2) Place the bratwurst in a large stock pot with onions, butter, and beer. 3) Preheat a grill for medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, you can simmer the bratwurst in the beer mixture with onions, red pepper flakes, garlic powder, salt, and pepper for 15 to 20 minutes before grilling them for 5 to 10 minutes on a preheated grill. After cooking, serve the bratwurst with the beer mixture as a topping or side.\"]", "time_sec": 65.581, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 60.11, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "79", "question": "how to cook brats", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's a brief guide on how to cook brats:\n\n1. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat.\n2. Lightly oil the grate.\n3. Combine beer, onions, red pepper flakes, garlic powder, salt, and pepper in a large pot. Bring to a boil.\n4. Submerge the bratwurst in the beer mixture.\n5. Reduce heat to medium and cook for 10 to 12 minutes.\n6. Remove the bratwurst from the beer mixture and place them on the preheated grill.\n7. Cook the bratwurst for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once.\n8. Serve the bratwurst with the beer mixture as a topping or side.\n\nSo, to cook brats, you first prepare the grill, then cook the bratwurst in a beer mixture before grilling them until they're done.", "pred": "To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Combine the bratwurst with onions, butter, and beer in a large stock pot, then simmer over medium heat for 15 to 20 minutes. After simmering, preheat the grill for medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, you can cook bratwurst on a preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes after removing them from the beer mixture. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for about 5 days or in the freezer for up to 3 months.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Combine the bratwurst with onions, butter, and beer in a large stock pot, then simmer over medium heat for 15 to 20 minutes. After simmering, preheat the grill for medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, you can cook bratwurst on a preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes after removing them from the beer mixture. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for about 5 days or in the freezer for up to 3 months.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Combine the bratwurst with onions, butter, and beer in a large stock pot, then simmer over medium heat for 15 to 20 minutes. After simmering, preheat the grill for medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, you can cook bratwurst on a preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes after removing them from the beer mixture. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for about 5 days or in the freezer for up to 3 months.\", \"Answer: To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding, then simmer them in a pot with onions, butter, and beer for 15 to 20 minutes. After simmering, preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, you can cook the bratwurst on a preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for about 5 days or in the freezer for up to 3 months; remember to write the storage date on the container.\", \"Answer: To cook brats, first prick them with a fork to prevent them from exploding. Combine the bratwurst with onions, butter, and beer in a large stock pot, then bring to a boil. Reduce heat to medium-low and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes, or until cooked through. After simmering, preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, you can cook the bratwurst directly on the preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for about 5 days or in the freezer for up to 3 months.\", \"Answer: To cook brats, first prick them with a fork to prevent them from exploding. Place the brats in a large stock pot with onions, butter, and beer. Bring the mixture to a boil, then reduce the heat to medium and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes. After simmering, preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat and lightly oil the grate. Cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, you can cook the bratwurst on a preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes after removing them from the beer mixture, turning once. Serve the brats with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for about 5 days or in the freezer for up to 3 months.\", \"Answer: To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Place them in a large stock pot with onions, butter, and beer. Bring the mixture to a boil, then submerge the bratwurst and add red pepper flakes, garlic powder, salt, and pepper. Reduce heat to medium and cook for 10 to 12 minutes, or simmer for 15 to 20 minutes. After cooking, preheat the grill to medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Alternatively, cook them on the preheated grill for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can last for about 5 days in the fridge or up to 3 months in the freezer; write the storage date on the container.\"]", "time_sec": 67.432, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 54.32, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "80", "question": "symptoms and causes of ibs", "gt": "The cause of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is unknown. However, it is believed to be a result of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, disruption in communication between the brain and the GI tract, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Risk factors may include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (either too fast or slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food. Symptoms of IBS can include diarrhea, constipation, or alternating between the two, belly pains or cramps that worsen after meals and feel better after a bowel movement, as well as a lot of gas or bloating.", "pred": "The cause of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is currently unknown, though it is thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in communication between the brain and the GI tract. IBS-D is a subtype characterized by diarrhea. Risk factors and contributing factors include alteration in GI tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Specific risk factors involve abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast, slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities possibly due to poor absorption of sugars or acids in food. Symptoms of IBS include diarrhea (often violent episodes), constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps typically in the lower abdomen that worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement, and significant gas or bloating.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The cause of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is currently unknown, though it is thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in communication between the brain and the GI tract. IBS-D is a subtype characterized by diarrhea. Risk factors and contributing factors include alteration in GI tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Specific risk factors involve abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast, slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities possibly due to poor absorption of sugars or acids in food. Symptoms of IBS include diarrhea (often violent episodes), constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps typically in the lower abdomen that worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement, and significant gas or bloating.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The cause of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is currently unknown, though it is thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in communication between the brain and the GI tract. IBS-D is a subtype characterized by diarrhea. Risk factors and contributing factors include alteration in GI tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Specific risk factors involve abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast, slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities possibly due to poor absorption of sugars or acids in food. Symptoms of IBS include diarrhea (often violent episodes), constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps typically in the lower abdomen that worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement, and significant gas or bloating.\", \"Answer: The cause of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is currently unknown, but it is thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. IBS-D is a type of IBS characterized by diarrhea.\\n\\nSymptoms of IBS can include:\\n- Diarrhea (often described as violent episodes of diarrhea)\\n- Constipation\\n- Constipation alternating with diarrhea\\n- Belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement\\n- A lot of gas or bloating\\n\\nRisk factors thought to cause IBS include:\\n- Abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast or slow, or too strong)\\n- Hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas\\n- Food sensitivities, possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food\", \"Answer: The cause of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is currently unknown, but it is thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. Risk factors include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast, slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food. Symptoms of IBS include diarrhea (often violent episodes), constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps in the lower half of the belly that worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement, and a lot of gas or bloating.\", \"Answer: The cause of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is currently unknown, but it is thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. IBS-D is a type of IBS characterized by diarrhea. Symptoms of IBS include diarrhea (often violent episodes), constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps in the lower half of the belly that worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement, and a lot of gas or bloating. Risk factors for IBS include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast, slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.\", \"Answer: IBS symptoms include diarrhea (often violent episodes), constipation, alternating constipation and diarrhea, lower belly pains or cramps that worsen after meals and improve after bowel movements, and significant gas or bloating. The exact cause of IBS is unknown, but it is thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, disrupted brain-GI tract communication, alteration in GI tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances (such as poor absorption of sugars or acids). Risk factors include abnormal colon and small intestine movements (too fast, slow, or strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities.\"]", "time_sec": 64.855, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 77.72, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "82", "question": "symptoms and causes of ibs", "gt": "Symptoms of irritable bowel syndrome include diarrhea (often described as violent episodes of diarrhea), constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement, and a lot of gas or bloating. Causes of irritable bowel syndrome are unknown but it is believed to be due to a number of factors including alteration in gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances.", "pred": "Symptoms of IBS include diarrhea (often violent episodes), constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps in the lower half of the belly that worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement, and a lot of gas or bloating. The causes of IBS are currently unknown but are thought to result from a combination of abnormal GI tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, disruption in brain-GI tract communication, alteration in GI tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, food intolerances, abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast, slow, or strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities possibly due to poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Symptoms of IBS include diarrhea (often violent episodes), constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps in the lower half of the belly that worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement, and a lot of gas or bloating. The causes of IBS are currently unknown but are thought to result from a combination of abnormal GI tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, disruption in brain-GI tract communication, alteration in GI tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, food intolerances, abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast, slow, or strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities possibly due to poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Symptoms of IBS include diarrhea (often violent episodes), constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps in the lower half of the belly that worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement, and a lot of gas or bloating. The causes of IBS are currently unknown but are thought to result from a combination of abnormal GI tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, disruption in brain-GI tract communication, alteration in GI tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, food intolerances, abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast, slow, or strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities possibly due to poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.\", \"Answer: IBS has unknown causes, thought to result from abnormal GI tract movements, increased bodily function awareness, and disrupted brain-GI communication. Risk factors include abnormal colon/small intestine movements (too fast/slow/strong), hypersensitivity to pain from full bowels/gas, and food sensitivities (e.g., poor absorption of sugars/acids). Symptoms include violent diarrhea, constipation, alternating constipation/diarrhea, lower belly cramps worsening after meals and improving after bowel movements, and significant gas/bloating.\", \"Answer: The cause of irritable bowel syndrome is currently unknown. It is thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. IBS-D is irritable bowel syndrome with diarrhea. The exact cause of irritable bowel syndrome is unknown. It is believed to be due to a number of factors, including alteration in the gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. People with IBS have symptoms that can include: 1. Diarrhea (often described as violent episodes of diarrhea) 2. Constipation. 3. Constipation alternating with diarrhea. 4. Belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement. 5. A lot of gas or bloating.\", \"Answer: IBS causes are unknown but involve abnormal GI tract movements, disrupted brain-GI communication, altered motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased pain sensitivity, and food intolerances. Symptoms include violent diarrhea, constipation, alternating constipation/diarrhea, lower belly pains or cramps worsening after meals and improving after bowel movements, and significant gas or bloating.\", \"Answer: IBS has no known exact cause and is thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and disruption in communication between the brain and GI tract. Risk factors include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast, slow, or strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food. Symptoms include violent episodes of diarrhea, constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps in the lower half of the belly that worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement, and a lot of gas or bloating.\"]", "time_sec": 51.05, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 57.34, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.4, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "84", "question": "symptoms and causes of ibs", "gt": "Sure, I can help you with that! Here are the symptoms and causes of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the given passages:\n\nSymptoms:\n\n* Diarrhea (often described as violent episodes of diarrhea)\n* Constipation\n* Constipation alternating with diarrhea\n* Belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement\n* A lot of gas or bloating\n\nCauses:\n\n* Abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements\n* Increased awareness of bodily functions\n* Disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract\n* Alterations in the GI tract motility\n* Abnormal nervous system signals\n* Increased sensitivity to pain\n* Food intolerances\n* Poor absorption of sugars or acids in food\n\nIt's worth noting that the exact cause of IBS is still unknown, and it's believed to be a complex interaction of various factors.", "pred": "The cause of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is currently unknown, but it is thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. IBS-D is a type of IBS characterized by diarrhea. Risk factors for IBS include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast, slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities, possibly due to poor absorption of sugars or acids in food. Symptoms of IBS can include violent episodes of diarrhea, constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps in the lower half of the belly that worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement, and a lot of gas or bloating.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The cause of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is currently unknown, but it is thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. IBS-D is a type of IBS characterized by diarrhea. Risk factors for IBS include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast, slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities, possibly due to poor absorption of sugars or acids in food. Symptoms of IBS can include violent episodes of diarrhea, constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps in the lower half of the belly that worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement, and a lot of gas or bloating.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The cause of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is currently unknown, but it is thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. IBS-D is a type of IBS characterized by diarrhea. Risk factors for IBS include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast, slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities, possibly due to poor absorption of sugars or acids in food. Symptoms of IBS can include violent episodes of diarrhea, constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps in the lower half of the belly that worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement, and a lot of gas or bloating.\", \"Answer: Symptoms of IBS include diarrhea (often violent episodes), constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps in the lower half of the belly that worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement, and excessive gas or bloating. Causes of IBS are unknown but involve abnormal GI tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, disrupted brain-GI tract communication, altered GI motility, abnormal nervous system signals, pain hypersensitivity, and food intolerances (e.g., poor absorption of sugars or acids). Risk factors include abnormal colon/small intestine movements (too fast/slow/strong), hypersensitivity to pain from full bowels or gas, and food sensitivities.\", \"Answer: The cause of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is unknown, but it is thought to result from a combination of abnormal GI tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in communication between the brain and the GI tract. IBS-D is IBS with diarrhea. Risk factors include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast or slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food. Symptoms include violent episodes of diarrhea, constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps in the lower half of the belly that worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement, and a lot of gas or bloating.\", \"Answer: The cause of IBS is currently unknown, with contributing factors including abnormal GI tract movements, increased bodily function awareness, disrupted brain-GI communication, alteration in GI motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased pain sensitivity, and food intolerances (e.g., poor sugar/acid absorption). IBS-D is a subtype characterized by diarrhea. Symptoms include violent diarrhea episodes, constipation, alternating constipation and diarrhea, lower abdominal cramps worsening after meals and improving post-bowel movement, and significant gas or bloating.\", \"Answer: Symptoms of IBS include diarrhea (often violent episodes), constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps in the lower half of the belly that worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement, and excessive gas or bloating. The causes of IBS are unknown but are thought to involve abnormal gastrointestinal tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, disruption in brain-GI tract communication, alteration in GI tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, food intolerances, abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast, slow, or strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities possibly due to poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.\"]", "time_sec": 54.957, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 60.77, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "85", "question": "how to prepare blood slide", "gt": "To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add a small drop of blood to one end. Then, take another clean slide and holding it at an angle of about 45 degrees, touch the blood with one end of the slide so that the blood runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry and label clearly. Once dried, place them in the provided slide transport containers. Always avoid coming into contact with another person's blood and if it is unavoidable, wear rubber gloves.", "pred": "To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Then, place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so it runs along the edge by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly before placing them in slide transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if unavoidable, as blood can carry diseases that can be transferred from person to person.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Then, place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so it runs along the edge by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly before placing them in slide transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if unavoidable, as blood can carry diseases that can be transferred from person to person.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Then, place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so it runs along the edge by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly before placing them in slide transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if unavoidable, as blood can carry diseases that can be transferred from person to person.\", \"Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end so it runs along the edge by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly before placing them in slide transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if unavoidable.\", \"Answer:To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly before placing them in slide transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if unavoidable, as blood can carry diseases transferable between people.\", \"Answer: To prepare a blood slide, follow these steps based on the provided passage:  \\n1. Sterilize a needle with a flame (from matches or a lighter).  \\n2. Prick your finger to obtain a blood drop (adult supervision is required).  \\n3. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end of the slide.  \\n4. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45° angle, and touch the blood drop with one end of this slide to allow the blood to run along the edge via capillary action.  \\n5. Push the slide carefully along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear of blood.  \\n6. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly.  \\n7. Place the dried slides in slide transport containers.  \\n\\nAnswer: To prepare a blood slide, sterilize a needle, prick your finger for a blood drop, place the drop on a slide, use another slide at a 45° angle to spread the blood via capillary action into a thin smear, dry the smears, label them, and store in transport containers.\", \"Answer: To prepare a blood slide, follow these steps: sterilize a needle with a flame, place a clean glass slide on a flat surface, add one small drop of blood to one end of the slide, take another clean slide and hold it at a 45-degree angle, touch the blood with one end of the second slide to allow the blood to run along the edge by capillary action, and then push carefully along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly before placing them in slide transport containers.\"]", "time_sec": 58.763, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 83.02, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.8, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "86", "question": "how to prepare blood slide", "gt": "To prepare a blood slide, you will need a clean glass slide and a drop of blood. Place the slide on a flat surface and add a small drop of blood to one end of the slide. Take another clean slide and touch the blood with one end at a 45-degree angle so that the blood runs along the edge of the slide. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly before placing them in slide transport containers.", "pred": "To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Collect a small drop of blood and place it at one end of a clean glass slide. Then, take another clean slide and hold it at a 45-degree angle, touching the blood with one end of the slide so that the blood runs along the edge by capillary action. Carefully push along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly before placing them in slide transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if contact is unavoidable.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Collect a small drop of blood and place it at one end of a clean glass slide. Then, take another clean slide and hold it at a 45-degree angle, touching the blood with one end of the slide so that the blood runs along the edge by capillary action. Carefully push along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly before placing them in slide transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if contact is unavoidable.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Collect a small drop of blood and place it at one end of a clean glass slide. Then, take another clean slide and hold it at a 45-degree angle, touching the blood with one end of the slide so that the blood runs along the edge by capillary action. Carefully push along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly before placing them in slide transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if contact is unavoidable.\", \"Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at about a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of this slide so the blood runs along the edge of the first slide by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly. Once dried, place the slides in slide transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if contact is unavoidable.\", \"Answer:To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly. Once dried, place the slides in provided slide transport containers.\", \"Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Collect a drop of blood and place it toward one end of a clean glass slide. Using another clean slide at a 45-degree angle, touch the blood to the first slide so it runs along the edge by capillary action, then push carefully along the length to create a thin smear. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly before placing them in slide transport containers.\", \"Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame, then collect a drop of blood and place it on one end of a clean glass slide. Take another clean slide at a 45-degree angle, touch it to the blood drop to allow capillary action to draw the blood along the edge, and push carefully along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly before placing them in slide transport containers.\"]", "time_sec": 45.733, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 78.52, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "88", "question": "how to prepare blood slide", "gt": "Sure, I'd be happy to help you with that! Here are the steps to prepare a blood slide based on the provided passages:\n\n1. First, sterilize a needle with a flame as described in Passage 1.\n2. Next, obtain a blood sample by pricking your finger or using a blood donation kit. This will require adult supervision, especially if you are doing it yourself.\n3. Place a drop of blood towards one end of a clean glass slide as instructed in Passage 2.\n4. Take another clean slide and, holding it at an angle of about 45 degrees, touch the blood with one end of the slide so that the blood runs along the edge of the slide through capillary action. Gently push the blood along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood. Repeat this step to make two smears.\n5. Allow the smears to air dry completely before labeling them clearly and placing them in the provided slide transport containers as suggested in Passage 3.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you have any further questions.", "pred": "To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Collect a small drop of blood and place it at one end of a clean glass slide. Using another clean slide held at a 45-degree angle, touch the blood with one end of the slide so it runs along the edge by capillary action. Carefully push along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly before placing them in slide transport containers.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Collect a small drop of blood and place it at one end of a clean glass slide. Using another clean slide held at a 45-degree angle, touch the blood with one end of the slide so it runs along the edge by capillary action. Carefully push along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly before placing them in slide transport containers.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Collect a small drop of blood and place it at one end of a clean glass slide. Using another clean slide held at a 45-degree angle, touch the blood with one end of the slide so it runs along the edge by capillary action. Carefully push along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly before placing them in slide transport containers.\", \"Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly before placing them in slide transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if unavoidable to prevent disease transfer.\", \"Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly before placing them in slide transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if contact is unavoidable.\", \"Answer:To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Then, place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly. Once dried, place the slides in provided slide transport containers. Avoid coming into contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if unavoidable, as blood can carry diseases that can be transferred from person to person.\", \"Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Collect a small drop of blood and place it at one end of a clean glass slide. Using another clean slide at a 45-degree angle, touch the blood with one end of the slide so it runs along the edge by capillary action. Carefully push along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly before placing them in slide transport containers. Avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if unavoidable contact occurs.\"]", "time_sec": 49.615, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 57.46, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "89", "question": "how to prepare blood slide", "gt": "Sure, I can help you with that! Based on the given passages, here are the steps to prepare a blood slide:\n\n1. Sterilize the needle with a flame before using it to prick your finger or obtain blood from another source.\n2. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface.\n3. Add one small drop of blood to one end of the slide.\n4. Hold another clean slide at an angle of about 45 degrees and gently touch the blood with one end of the slide, allowing the blood to run along the edge of the slide by capillary action.\n5. Carefully push the slide along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood.\n6. Repeat step 4 and 5 to make two smears.\n7. Allow the slides to air dry.\n8. Label the slides clearly once they are dry.\n9. Place the slides in provided slide transport containers.\n\nNote: It is important to avoid coming into contact with another person's blood, and to wear rubber gloves if it is unavoidable.", "pred": "To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Then, place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood. Make 2 smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly. Once dried, place the slide in a slide transport container.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer:To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Then, place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood. Make 2 smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly. Once dried, place the slide in a slide transport container.", "cot": "[\"Answer:To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Then, place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood. Make 2 smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly. Once dried, place the slide in a slide transport container.\", \"Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly. Once dried, place the slides in provided transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if unavoidable, as blood can carry diseases that can be transferred from person to person.\", \"Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Then, place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add a small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge by capillary action. Carefully push along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly. Once dried, place the slides in slide transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if contact is unavoidable.\", \"Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Then, place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide and, holding it at a 45-degree angle, touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly before placing them in slide transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if unavoidable, as blood can carry diseases that can be transferred from person to person.\", \"Answer: To prepare a blood slide, follow these steps: Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly. Once dried, place the slides in provided transport containers. Use a sterilized needle (flamed) for finger pricking, and avoid contact with others' blood by wearing rubber gloves if unavoidable.\"]", "time_sec": 53.19, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 63.74, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "90", "question": "how to prepare blood slide", "gt": "To prepare a blood slide, follow these steps:\n\n1. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface.\n2. Add one small drop of blood to one end of the slide.\n3. Take another clean slide and hold it at an angle of about 45 degrees. Touch the blood with one end of the slide so that it runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action.\n4. Push the second slide carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood.\n5. Repeat step 4 to make two smears.\n6. Allow the smears to air dry.\n7. Label the slides clearly.\n8. Once dried, place the slides in the provided slide transport containers.\n\nNote: It is important to sterilize the needle used to obtain the blood sample by heating it with a flame before using it. Additionally, it is essential to wear rubber gloves when handling blood to avoid coming into contact with potentially infectious materials.", "pred": "To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Then, place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add a small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so it runs along the edge by capillary action. Carefully push along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly before placing them in slide transport containers.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Then, place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add a small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so it runs along the edge by capillary action. Carefully push along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly before placing them in slide transport containers.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Then, place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add a small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so it runs along the edge by capillary action. Carefully push along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly before placing them in slide transport containers.\", \"Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Collect a small drop of blood and place it on one end of a clean glass slide. Using another clean slide held at a 45-degree angle, touch the blood with one end of the slide so it runs along the edge by capillary action, then push carefully along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly before placing them in slide transport containers.\", \"Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Then, place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly. Once dried, place the slides in provided slide transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if unavoidable.\", \"Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Then, place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly. Once dried, place the slides in provided transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if unavoidable, as blood can carry diseases transferable from person to person.\", \"Answer:To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to create a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly. Once dried, place the slides in slide transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if unavoidable, as blood can carry diseases that can be transferred from person to person.\"]", "time_sec": 49.946, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 65.06, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "91", "question": "what is the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids", "gt": "Essential amino acids are ones that the body cannot produce on its own, thus they must be obtained through diet. In adults, there are eight essential amino acids: valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan. For infants, histidine is also considered essential. On the other hand, nonessential amino acids are those that the body can produce, even if they are not present in the diet. These include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine. While not essential to obtain from diet, these amino acids are still necessary as they are used by the body to build proteins needed for cell repair, growth, and maintenance.", "pred": "Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.\", \"Answer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet. For example, adults need to obtain eight essential amino acids—valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan—from food, whereas nonessential amino acids such as alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine can be synthesized by the body.\", \"Answer: Essential amino acids are those that the body cannot produce on its own and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids are those that the body can synthesize from other amino acids or protein breakdown, making them unnecessary to obtain directly from food. For example, the body cannot make essential amino acids like valine, lysine, or methionine, but it can produce nonessential amino acids such as alanine, glycine, and serine.\", \"Answer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.\", \"Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.\"]", "time_sec": 31.172, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 36.84, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "92", "question": "what is the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids", "gt": "Essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet. Adults need to obtain eight essential amino acids: valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan. The ninth essential amino acid, histidine, is only essential for infants. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be made by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Some examples of nonessential amino acids are alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "pred": "Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.\", \"Answer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet. For example, the body can synthesize nonessential amino acids like alanine, asparagine, and serine, but must obtain essential amino acids such as valine, lysine, and tryptophan from food.\", \"Answer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.\", \"Essential amino acids are those that the body cannot make on its own, so they must be obtained from the diet. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be produced by the body from other compounds, so they do not need to be consumed in the diet. This is supported by the passages, which state that essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan (with histidine being essential only for infants), while nonessential amino acids such as alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine are created by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. \\n\\nAnswer: Essential amino acids are those the body cannot produce and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids are those the body can make from other compounds and thus do not need to be obtained from the diet.\", \"Answer: Essential amino acids are those that the body cannot produce on its own and must be obtained through diet, while nonessential amino acids are those that the body can synthesize from other compounds, even if they are not consumed directly in food. The passage states that the body creates nonessential amino acids from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, making them non-essential to obtain from the diet. In contrast, essential amino acids such as valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan (with histidine being essential only for infants) must be obtained through diet since the body cannot make them.\"]", "time_sec": 41.988, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 49.52, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "94", "question": "what is the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the main differences between essential and non-essential amino acids are:\n\nEssential Amino Acids:\n\n* Must be obtained from the diet\n* Cannot be produced by the body\n* Nine out of 20 amino acids are considered essential (valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan, and histidine for infants)\n\nNon-Essential Amino Acids:\n\n* Can be produced by the body\n* Not necessarily required to obtain from the diet\n* Include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine\n\nIt is important to note that even though non-essential amino acids can be produced by the body, it is still important to consume a balanced diet that includes all of the essential amino acids to ensure proper bodily functions.", "pred": "Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.\", \"Answer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.\", \"Answer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.\", \"Answer: Essential amino acids are those that the body cannot make on its own and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids are those that the body can synthesize from other compounds or essential amino acids. For example, the body can produce nonessential amino acids like alanine, asparagine, and glycine from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein, but it cannot produce essential amino acids such as valine, leucine, or lysine, which must be consumed through food.\", \"Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.\"]", "time_sec": 25.741, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 41.27, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "95", "question": "what is the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is the answer to the question:\n\nEssential amino acids are those that cannot be produced by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids are those that can be produced by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\n\nPassage 1 states that nine amino acids are essential for humans, but only eight of them are essential for adults (histidine is only essential for infants). Passage 2 explains that nonessential amino acids are not unnecessary, as the body can produce them from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Passage 3 reiterates that nonessential amino acids can be made by the body, while essential amino acids cannot be produced and must be obtained from the diet.\n\nTherefore, the main difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids cannot be produced by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.", "pred": "Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Essential amino acids are those that the body cannot produce on its own and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids are those that the body can synthesize from other compounds or essential amino acids. Passage 3 states that nonessential amino acids can be made by the body, whereas essential amino acids cannot be made and must be obtained from the diet. Passage 2 provides examples of nonessential amino acids such as alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine, which the body creates from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Passage 1 indicates that adults need to obtain eight essential amino acids (valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan), with histidine being essential only for infants.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Essential amino acids are those that the body cannot produce on its own and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids are those that the body can synthesize from other compounds or essential amino acids. Passage 3 states that nonessential amino acids can be made by the body, whereas essential amino acids cannot be made and must be obtained from the diet. Passage 2 provides examples of nonessential amino acids such as alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine, which the body creates from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Passage 1 indicates that adults need to obtain eight essential amino acids (valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan), with histidine being essential only for infants.\", \"Answer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.\", \"Answer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.\", \"Answer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet. For example, the body can synthesize nonessential amino acids such as alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine, whereas essential amino acids like valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan must be obtained from food, with histidine being essential only for infants.\", \"Answer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.\"]", "time_sec": 38.311, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 40.0, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.8, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "96", "question": "typical weather in indiana", "gt": "The typical weather in Indiana is characterized by long, warm, humid, and wet summers. The winters are chilly and windy with some snowfall. The temperature typically varies from 22°F to 85°F and is rarely below 3°F or above 92°F. The state also experiences some rainfall, with the wettest year recording 57.65 inches of rain and the driest 24.97 inches. The most snowfall recorded in a season was 74.5 inches.", "pred": "Indianapolis, Indiana experiences hot, humid summers and chilly, windy winters, with temperatures typically ranging from 22°F to 85°F annually, rarely dropping below 3°F or exceeding 92°F, and partly cloudy conditions year-round, with significant seasonal snowfall variations across regions.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Indianapolis, Indiana experiences hot, humid summers and chilly, windy winters, with temperatures typically ranging from 22°F to 85°F annually, rarely dropping below 3°F or exceeding 92°F, and partly cloudy conditions year-round, with significant seasonal snowfall variations across regions.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Indianapolis, Indiana experiences hot, humid summers and chilly, windy winters, with temperatures typically ranging from 22°F to 85°F annually, rarely dropping below 3°F or exceeding 92°F, and partly cloudy conditions year-round, with significant seasonal snowfall variations across regions.\", \"Answer: In Indiana, typical weather includes long, warm, humid, and wet summers, and chilly, windy winters. The temperature typically varies from 22°F to 85°F over the course of the year, with rare instances of temperatures falling below 3°F or exceeding 92°F.\", \"Answer: In Indiana, the typical weather includes long, warm, humid, and wet summers, and chilly, windy winters. The temperature generally ranges from 22°F to 85°F, with extreme lows rarely below 3°F and extreme highs rarely above 92°F. Indianapolis experiences partly cloudy skies throughout the year. The wettest year on record in Indianapolis was 1876 with 57.65 inches of rain, while the driest year was 1934 with 24.97 inches. Heavy snowstorms and snowfall averages are also documented for various cities, including Fort Wayne, Gary, Michigan City, and South Bend.\", \"Answer: The typical weather in Indiana features long, warm, humid, and wet summers, and chilly, windy winters. Temperatures generally range from 22°F to 85°F annually, with rare instances of temperatures dropping below 3°F or exceeding 92°F.\", \"Answer: The typical weather in Indiana includes long, warm, humid, and wet summers, and chilly, windy winters. The temperature generally ranges from 22°F to 85°F throughout the year, with extreme lows rarely below 3°F and extreme highs rarely above 92°F.\"]", "time_sec": 33.45, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 41.56, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "97", "question": "typical weather in indiana", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the typical weather in Indiana includes long, warm, humid, and wet summers, and chilly and windy winters. The temperature ranges from 22°F to 85°F throughout the year. The passages also mention snowfall and snow accumulation in specific cities, but do not provide a comprehensive overview of the typical weather in Indiana. Therefore, it is not possible to provide a complete answer based solely on the given passages.", "pred": "The typical weather in Indiana features long, warm, humid, and wet summers and chilly, windy winters, with temperatures ranging from 22°F to 85°F annually, and partly cloudy conditions year-round.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The typical weather in Indiana features long, warm, humid, and wet summers and chilly, windy winters, with temperatures ranging from 22°F to 85°F annually, and partly cloudy conditions year-round.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The typical weather in Indiana features long, warm, humid, and wet summers and chilly, windy winters, with temperatures ranging from 22°F to 85°F annually, and partly cloudy conditions year-round.\", \"Answer: The typical weather in Indiana features long, warm, humid, and wet summers and chilly, windy winters, with temperatures ranging from 22°F to 85°F annually, and partly cloudy conditions year round.\", \"Answer: The typical weather in Indiana features long, warm, humid, and wet summers and chilly, windy winters, with temperatures ranging from 22°F to 85°F annually, rarely dropping below 3°F or exceeding 92°F, and the state experiences partly cloudy conditions year-round.\", \"Answer: Typical weather in Indiana includes long, warm, humid, and wet summers and chilly, windy winters, with temperatures typically ranging from 22°F to 85°F year‑round, and partly cloudy conditions throughout the year.\", \"Answer: The typical weather in Indiana features long, warm, humid, and wet summers and chilly, windy winters, with temperatures ranging from 22°F to 85°F annually, rarely dropping below 3°F or exceeding 92°F, and partly cloudy conditions year round.\"]", "time_sec": 24.07, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 40.0, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "100", "question": "how to sync audiobook from iphone to itunes", "gt": "To sync an audiobook from iPhone to iTunes, follow these steps:\n\n1. Open iTunes on your computer.\n2. Turn on the Manually manage music setting in iTunes.\n3. Download the audiobook from iTunes onto your iPhone.\n4. Connect your iPhone to the USB cable and plug the other end of the USB cable into a USB port on the computer.\n5. Wait for iTunes to sync the audiobook to your iPhone.\n6. After the sync is complete, the audiobook will be on your iPhone.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "[\"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\"]", "time_sec": 7.017, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "101", "question": "how to sync audiobook from iphone to itunes", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are the steps to sync audiobooks from an iPhone to iTunes:\n\n1. Turn on the Manually manage music setting in iTunes.\n2. Download the audiobook you want from iTunes.\n3. Connect your iPhone to the computer via USB cable.\n4. Click on \"Audiobooks\" on the left task pane of the iTunes window.\n5. Select the audiobook you want to sync by clicking on its title.\n6. Click \"Sync Audiobooks\" and choose the audiobooks you want to transfer.\n7. Click \"Apply\" to start the syncing process.\n\nAlternatively, you can use Syncios Audiobook Transfer to transfer audiobooks from your iPhone to iTunes. The software allows two-way transfer of files, so you can not only backup files to your PC but also transfer audiobooks from your PC to your iPhone. Here are the steps to use Syncios Audiobook Transfer:\n\n1. Launch Syncios Audiobook Transfer on your computer.\n2. Connect your iPhone to the computer via USB cable.\n3. Click on \"Audiobooks\" on the top menu bar.\n4. Select the audiobooks you want to transfer and click \"Export\" to save them to your computer.\n5. Alternatively, you can also click \"Import\" to add new audiobooks from your computer to your iPhone.\n6. Click \"Apply\" to start the transfer process.\n\nNote that the above steps are based on the assumption that you have already turned on the Manually manage music setting in iTunes. If you have not done so, you will need to do so before attempting to sync your audiobooks.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "[\"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\"]", "time_sec": 6.868, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 3.16, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "102", "question": "how to sync audiobook from iphone to itunes", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how to sync audiobooks from iPhone to iTunes:\n\n1. Connect your iPhone to your computer using a USB cable.\n2. Open iTunes on your computer.\n3. Click on the \"Audiobooks\" tab on the left side of the iTunes window.\n4. Check the boxes next to the audiobooks you want to sync to your iPhone.\n5. Click the \"Sync Audiobooks\" button and choose the audiobooks you want to transfer or select \"All Audiobooks\" to transfer all of them to your iPhone.\n6. Click \"Apply\" at the bottom of the screen to finish the syncing process.\n\nAlternatively, you can use Syncios Audiobook Transfer to transfer audiobooks from your computer to your iOS device. To do this, follow these steps:\n\n1. Launch Syncios Audiobook Transfer on your computer.\n2. Connect your iPhone to your computer using a USB cable.\n3. Click on the \"Books\" tab on the top menu bar.\n4. Select the audiobooks you want to transfer from your computer to your iPhone.\n5. Click the \"Import\" button to transfer the selected audiobooks to your iPhone.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you have any further questions.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "[\"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\"]", "time_sec": 6.873, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 4.14, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "103", "question": "how the body systems work together", "gt": "The various systems in the human body work together to ensure its normal functioning. For instance, the urinary system works with all other systems to clean the blood by removing waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems. This includes organs like the kidneys and lower intestines. Similarly, the respiratory system also collaborates with all other systems to provide oxygen to all body parts and remove carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems. The respiratory system comprises the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs.", "pred": "The body systems work together to maintain normal functioning through coordinated interactions. For example, the urinary system cleans blood by removing waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, as stated in passage 2. Similarly, the respiratory system provides oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems, as described in passage 3. The passage 1 lists all the systems (skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, urinary, endocrine, lymphatic, immune, reproductive, and nervous) as working together to help keep the body functioning normally. These examples show that systems operate interdependently—such as the respiratory system supplying oxygen to support metabolic processes in other systems, and the urinary system filtering waste generated by various systems—to ensure overall bodily homeostasis.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The body systems work together to maintain normal functioning through coordinated interactions. For example, the urinary system cleans blood by removing waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, as stated in passage 2. Similarly, the respiratory system provides oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems, as described in passage 3. The passage 1 lists all the systems (skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, urinary, endocrine, lymphatic, immune, reproductive, and nervous) as working together to help keep the body functioning normally. These examples show that systems operate interdependently—such as the respiratory system supplying oxygen to support metabolic processes in other systems, and the urinary system filtering waste generated by various systems—to ensure overall bodily homeostasis.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The body systems work together to maintain normal functioning through coordinated interactions. For example, the urinary system cleans blood by removing waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, as stated in passage 2. Similarly, the respiratory system provides oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems, as described in passage 3. The passage 1 lists all the systems (skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, urinary, endocrine, lymphatic, immune, reproductive, and nervous) as working together to help keep the body functioning normally. These examples show that systems operate interdependently—such as the respiratory system supplying oxygen to support metabolic processes in other systems, and the urinary system filtering waste generated by various systems—to ensure overall bodily homeostasis.\", \"Answer: The body systems work together in several ways. The urinary system cleans blood by removing waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems. For example, the kidneys, which are part of the urinary system, filter waste from the blood. The respiratory system provides oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems. For instance, the lungs in the respiratory system take in oxygen and expel carbon dioxide. These are just a few examples of how the systems work together to maintain normal body function.\", \"Answer: The body systems work together in several ways. The urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory and nervous systems. For example, it cleans blood from the body. The respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary and nervous systems. For instance, it removes carbon dioxide from the muscular system. These examples demonstrate how the systems work together to maintain normal body functioning.\", \"Answer: The body systems work together in several ways. The urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, including the kidneys and lower intestines. The respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems, including the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs. The systems are interconnected, with each system contributing to the overall functioning of the body. For example, the skeletal system provides structure and support for the body, while the muscular system enables movement and helps maintain body temperature. The digestive system breaks down food and absorbs nutrients, which are then distributed to other systems through the circulatory system. The endocrine system produces hormones that regulate various bodily functions, including growth and metabolism. The lymphatic and immune systems work together to defend the body against pathogens and disease. The reproductive system enables the continuation of the species. Overall, the body systems work together in a complex and interconnected manner to maintain homeostasis and support normal bodily functioning.\", \"Answer: The body systems work together through coordinated functions: the urinary system cleans blood by removing waste from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems; the respiratory system provides oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems.\"]", "time_sec": 52.166, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 54.55, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "104", "question": "how the body systems work together", "gt": "The body systems work together in order to maintain the normal functioning of the human body. These systems include the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, urinary, endocrine, lymphatic, immune, reproductive, and nervous systems. Each system has its own specific functions, but they also work in conjunction with one another to ensure the overall well-being of the body. For example, the urinary system cleans waste products from various systems such as the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems. Similarly, the respiratory system provides oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems.", "pred": "The body systems work together to maintain normal function through coordinated interactions. The urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, including the kidneys and lower intestines. The respiratory system provides oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems, including the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs. For example, the respiratory system supplies oxygen to the muscular system for energy, while the circulatory system transports oxygen to various parts of the body. Similarly, the urinary system removes waste products generated by the muscular and digestive systems, demonstrating how systems collaborate to support overall bodily functions.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The body systems work together to maintain normal function through coordinated interactions. The urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, including the kidneys and lower intestines. The respiratory system provides oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems, including the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs. For example, the respiratory system supplies oxygen to the muscular system for energy, while the circulatory system transports oxygen to various parts of the body. Similarly, the urinary system removes waste products generated by the muscular and digestive systems, demonstrating how systems collaborate to support overall bodily functions.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The body systems work together to maintain normal function through coordinated interactions. The urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, including the kidneys and lower intestines. The respiratory system provides oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems, including the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs. For example, the respiratory system supplies oxygen to the muscular system for energy, while the circulatory system transports oxygen to various parts of the body. Similarly, the urinary system removes waste products generated by the muscular and digestive systems, demonstrating how systems collaborate to support overall bodily functions.\", \"Answer: The body systems work together through coordinated functions where each system supports others. The urinary system cleans blood by removing waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems. The respiratory system provides oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems. For example, the respiratory system works with the circulatory system to deliver oxygen to tissues and remove carbon dioxide, while the urinary system collaborates with multiple systems to filter waste from the blood.\", \"Answer: The body systems work together in several ways. The skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, urinary, endocrine, lymphatic, immune, reproductive, and nervous systems all function together to maintain normal body function. Specifically, the urinary system cleans blood by removing waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, and includes the kidneys and lower intestines. The respiratory system provides oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems, and includes the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs.\", \"Answer: The body systems work together to maintain normal functioning. The skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, urinary, endocrine, lymphatic, immune, reproductive, and nervous systems collaborate to keep the body functioning normally. For example, the urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, while the respiratory system provides oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems.\", \"Answer: The body systems work together in several ways. For example, the urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, including the kidneys and lower intestines. Similarly, the respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems, including the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs. These examples demonstrate how the systems work closely together to maintain normal body function.\"]", "time_sec": 45.628, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 64.56, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "105", "question": "how the body systems work together", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here's how the body systems work together:\n\n1. The skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, urinary, endocrine, lymphatic, immune, and nervous systems all work together to help keep the body functioning normally. (Passage 1)\n2. The urinary system works closely with all other systems of the body to clean blood from the body and remove waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems. (Passage 2)\n3. The respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems. (Passage 3)\n\nTherefore, the body systems work together in the following ways:\n\n* The skeletal system provides support and structure for the body, while the muscular system helps move the body and its parts.\n* The digestive system breaks down food into nutrients that can be absorbed and used by the body, while the respiratory system provides oxygen for energy production.\n* The circulatory system delivers oxygenated blood to the body's cells and removes carbon dioxide, while the urinary system removes waste products from the blood.\n* The endocrine system produces hormones that regulate various bodily functions, including growth and development, metabolism, and reproductive processes.\n* The lymphatic system helps defend the body against infection and disease, while the immune system fights off foreign invaders.\n* The nervous system controls and coordinates the body's functions, including movement, sensation, and thought processes.\n\nOverall, the body's systems work together in a complex interplay to maintain homeostasis and ensure the proper functioning of the body.", "pred": "The body systems work together in interconnected ways to maintain normal function. The urinary system cleans blood by removing waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, and includes the kidneys and lower intestines. The respiratory system provides oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems, and includes the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The body systems work together in interconnected ways to maintain normal function. The urinary system cleans blood by removing waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, and includes the kidneys and lower intestines. The respiratory system provides oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems, and includes the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The body systems work together in interconnected ways to maintain normal function. The urinary system cleans blood by removing waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, and includes the kidneys and lower intestines. The respiratory system provides oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems, and includes the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs.\", \"Answer: The body systems work together in interconnected ways to maintain normal function. The urinary system cleans blood by removing waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory and nervous systems, and includes the kidneys and lower intestines. The respiratory system provides oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary and nervous systems, and includes the nose, larynx, trachea and the lungs. For example, the respiratory system works closely with the circulatory system to oxygenate blood, while the urinary system works closely with multiple systems including the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory and nervous systems to clean waste from the body.\", \"Answer: The body systems work together in several ways. For instance, the urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems. Similarly, the respiratory system provides oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems. The skeletal system provides structure and support to the body, while the muscular system works with it to enable movement. The digestive system breaks down food and absorbs nutrients, which are then distributed to other systems. The circulatory system transports blood, oxygen, and nutrients to all parts of the body. The endocrine system produces hormones that regulate various bodily functions. The lymphatic system helps maintain fluid balance and supports the immune system. The immune system protects the body from harmful pathogens. The reproductive system allows for the continuation of the species. Overall, these systems work together to maintain homeostasis and ensure the body functions normally.\", \"Answer: The body systems work together to maintain normal functioning. For example, the urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, while the respiratory system provides oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems.\", \"Answer: The body systems work together in several ways. The skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, urinary, endocrine, lymphatic, immune, reproductive and nervous systems all work together to keep the body functioning normally. The urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory and nervous systems. The respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary and nervous systems. The systems work closely with each other to maintain the body's normal functioning.\"]", "time_sec": 46.817, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 31.36, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
