{"id": "127", "question": "building a lean-to shed", "gt": "Sure! Here's my attempt to answer your question based on the provided passages:\n\nHow do you prepare the site for building a lean-to shed?\n\nAccording to passage 1, step 1, you should prepare the site by laying down a 4\" layer of compacted gravel. This will provide a stable base for the shed's foundation. Additionally, passage 2 mentions cutting two pressure-treated 4x4s to a length of 6 feet for the skids, which will serve as the foundation for the shed. Finally, passage 3 provides instructions for building the shed's floor, including cutting rim joists to a length of 6 feet. Based on these passages, it appears that preparing the site involves laying down a layer of gravel and setting the skids in place before constructing the floor.", "pred": "Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with a 4″ layer of compacted gravel, cutting 4 x 4 skids to 70 2/4″, and assembling the floor frame using 2 x 6 rim joists (7 3/4″) and six joists (44 3/4″). The frame is set on skids, checked for square, and anchored with four joist clip angles. Alternatively, 4 by 4s are cut to 6 feet for skids, the frame is positioned on them, and floor joists are toenailed to the skids. The shed shown is 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, with walls constructed separately and then raised onto the frame.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with a 4″ layer of compacted gravel, cutting 4 x 4 skids to 70 2/4″, and assembling the floor frame using 2 x 6 rim joists (7 3/4″) and six joists (44 3/4″). The frame is set on skids, checked for square, and anchored with four joist clip angles. Alternatively, 4 by 4s are cut to 6 feet for skids, the frame is positioned on them, and floor joists are toenailed to the skids. The shed shown is 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, with walls constructed separately and then raised onto the frame.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with a 4″ layer of compacted gravel, cutting 4 x 4 skids to 70 2/4″, and assembling the floor frame using 2 x 6 rim joists (7 3/4″) and six joists (44 3/4″). The frame is set on skids, checked for square, and anchored with four joist clip angles. Alternatively, 4 by 4s are cut to 6 feet for skids, the frame is positioned on them, and floor joists are toenailed to the skids. The shed shown is 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, with walls constructed separately and then raised onto the frame.\", \"Answer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with a 4″ layer of compacted gravel, cutting 4 x 4 skids to 70 2/4″, and assembling the floor frame using two 2 x 6 rim joists at 7 3/4″ and six joists at 44 3/4″. The frame is set on the skids, checked for square, and anchored with four joist clip angles. Alternatively, using pressure-treated 4 by 4s cut to 6 feet for skids and 2 x 4s for rim joists, the floor frame is positioned, squared, and toenailed to the skids before attaching 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring. The walls are built separately and then raised onto the frame. The shed dimensions can be 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, with expansions possible in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\", \"Answer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing a site with a 4″ layer of compacted gravel, cutting 4 x 4 skids to 70 2/4″, setting and leveling them, and assembling a floor frame with two 2 x 6 rim joists at 7 3/4″ and six joists at 44 3/4″. The frame is anchored to the skids with four joist clip angles, and 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring is attached. Walls are built separately and then raised onto the frame. The example shed is 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, with dimensions expandable in 2 or 4-foot increments.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer:Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with a 4″ layer of compacted 