{"id": "130", "question": "difference between vietnam and india", "gt": "The passages suggest that trends in India are more typical than those in Vietnam, particularly in the area of education. Vietnam's education system is described as relatively equitable, where poorer children receive the same treatment as their better-off counterparts. In contrast, India's early focus was on getting children into school, which involved setting up schools, and training teachers. Politically, India supported Vietnam's independence from France and opposed American involvement in the Vietnam War. Both countries have a history of colonialism, with India being a former colony of the United Kingdom and Vietnam of France. Post-colonization, India experienced division and conflict among Hindus, Muslims and Sikhs. Cultural and economic links between India and Vietnam date back to the 2nd century.", "pred": "India and Vietnam differ significantly in their education systems and historical trajectories. While India focused on establishing schools and training teachers to get children into school, Vietnam's education system is relatively equitable, ensuring poorer children receive the same opportunities as better-off children. Historically, India supported Vietnam's independence from France and opposed American involvement in the Vietnam War, while also supporting the unification of Vietnam. Culturally, India and Vietnam share links dating back to the 2nd century, with the Indic Chăm Pa kingdom influencing Vietnamese music. In contrast, France and the UK, which held large colonies, experienced similar decolonization processes but left their colonies in different conditions—India became divided between Hindus, Muslims, and Sikhs, leading to conflicts, while Vietnam's decolonization from Indochina did not result in such visible internal divisions.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: India and Vietnam differ significantly in their education systems and historical trajectories. While India focused on establishing schools and training teachers to get children into school, Vietnam's education system is relatively equitable, ensuring poorer children receive the same opportunities as better-off children. Historically, India supported Vietnam's independence from France and opposed American involvement in the Vietnam War, while also supporting the unification of Vietnam. Culturally, India and Vietnam share links dating back to the 2nd century, with the Indic Chăm Pa kingdom influencing Vietnamese music. In contrast, France and the UK, which held large colonies, experienced similar decolonization processes but left their colonies in different conditions—India became divided between Hindus, Muslims, and Sikhs, leading to conflicts, while Vietnam's decolonization from Indochina did not result in such visible internal divisions.", "cot": "[\"Answer: India and Vietnam differ significantly in their education systems and historical trajectories. While India focused on establishing schools and training teachers to get children into school, Vietnam's education system is relatively equitable, ensuring poorer children receive the same opportunities as better-off children. Historically, India supported Vietnam's independence from France and opposed American involvement in the Vietnam War, while also supporting the unification of Vietnam. Culturally, India and Vietnam share links dating back to the 2nd century, with the Indic Chăm Pa kingdom influencing Vietnamese music. In contrast, France and the UK, which held large colonies, experienced similar decolonization processes but left their colonies in different conditions—India became divided between Hindus, Muslims, and Sikhs, leading to conflicts, while Vietnam's decolonization from Indochina did not result in such visible internal divisions.\", \"Answer: Vietnam's education system is relatively equitable, providing poorer children with the same opportunities as better-off children, whereas India's early education focus was on establishing schools, training teachers, and improving resources and facilities, with less emphasis on equity.\", \"Answer: The passage states that the trends in India are more typical compared to Vietnam, and the early focus in India was on getting children into school, which involved looking at resources and facilities, setting up schools, and training teachers. In contrast, Vietnam's education system is relatively equitable, meaning poorer children receive the same opportunities as better-off children, making them not less advantaged in the school system.\", \"Answer: India supported Vietnam's independence from France and opposed American involvement in the Vietnam War, while also supporting the unification of Vietnam. India and Vietnam have strong bilateral relations with cultural and economic links dating back to the 2nd century, including the influence of the Indic Chăm Pa kingdom on Vietnamese music. In contrast, the education system in Vietnam is relatively equitable, ensuring poorer children receive the same opportunities as better-off children, whereas the early focus in India was on establishing schools and training teachers to get children into school, with less emphasis on equity in the education system.\", \"Answer: India and Vietnam differ in their education systems and historical relationships. In education, India focused on creating schools and training teachers to get children into school, while Vietnam's system is relatively equitable, providing poorer children with the same opportunities as better-off children. Historically, India supported Vietnam's independence from France and opposed American involvement in the Vietnam War, while also supporting Vietnam's unification. The two countries have strong bilateral relations and cultural and economic links dating back to the 2nd century.\"]", "time_sec": 40.068, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 52.51, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "132", "question": "how to roast individual garlic cloves", "gt": "Preheat your oven to 400°F (205° C). Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, but keep the skins of the individual cloves intact. Using a sharp knife, cut 1/4 to a 1/2 inch from the top of cloves to expose them. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. Drizzle a couple teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, using your fingers to rub the oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves. Season the garlic cloves to taste with herbs and spices if you like. Roasting time is dependent on the size of the cloves and may take up to 45 minutes or an hour for larger cloves. Once roasted, the garlic cloves can be stored in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "[\"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\"]", "time_sec": 6.723, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "133", "question": "how to roast individual garlic cloves", "gt": "To roast individual garlic cloves, preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C). Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the garlic bulb, leaving the skins of the cloves intact. Cut 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of the cloves to expose them. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up, and drizzle a couple teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, rubbing it over the cloves. Roast the cloves in the preheated oven for about 45 minutes to an hour, or until they are softened. Store the roasted garlic cloves in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "[\"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\"]", "time_sec": 6.726, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "135", "question": "how to roast individual garlic cloves", "gt": "Here are brief answers to the question on how to roast individual garlic cloves based on the provided passages:\n\nPassage 1: The passage does not provide information on how to roast individual garlic cloves specifically. However, it does mention that larger garlic cloves may take up to an hour to soften when roasted, so it is possible to assume that smaller cloves will take less time.\n\nPassage 2: This passage provides some options for seasoning the garlic before roasting, but it does not provide instructions on how to roast individual garlic cloves.\n\nPassage 3: This passage provides a method for roasting entire garlic heads, but it does not address how to roast individual garlic cloves. The passage does mention that cutting the top of the garlic bulb and exposing the individual cloves can help them roast more evenly, but it does not provide specific instructions on how to do this.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "[\"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\"]", "time_sec": 6.731, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 8.7, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "136", "question": "how to roast individual garlic cloves", "gt": "Sure! Here's how to roast individual garlic cloves based on the provided passages:\n\nTo roast individual garlic cloves, follow these steps:\n\n1. Preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C).\n2. Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, leaving intact the skins of the individual cloves of garlic.\n3. Using a sharp knife, cut 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of each clove, exposing the individual cloves of garlic.\n4. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up.\n5. Drizzle a couple of teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, using your fingers to rub the olive oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves.\n6. Season the garlic cloves to taste with herbs and spices, if desired.\n7. Roast the garlic in the preheated oven for up to 45 minutes or until the cloves are softened to your liking.\n8. Store roasted garlic cloves in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days.\n\nNote that the roasting time may vary depending on the size of your garlic cloves, so check on them periodically to avoid overcooking.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "[\"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\"]", "time_sec": 6.72, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 2.94, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "137", "question": "how to roast individual garlic cloves", "gt": "To roast individual garlic cloves, follow these steps:\n\n1. Preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C).\n2. Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, leaving intact the skins of the individual cloves of garlic.\n3. Using a sharp knife, cut 1/4 to a 1/2 inch from the top of cloves, exposing the individual cloves of garlic.\n4. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. (A muffin pan works great for this, as it keeps the garlic bulbs from rolling around.)\n5. Drizzle a couple teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, using your fingers to rub the olive oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves.\n6. Roast in the preheated oven for 45 minutes to an hour, or until the cloves are softened.\n7. Store roasted garlic cloves in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days.\n\nYou can also season the garlic cloves to taste with herbs and spices before roasting.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "[\"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\"]", "time_sec": 6.734, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "138", "question": "what is the treatment when asset is discarded as per income tax act", "gt": "Based on the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded but not sold, the scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded as per section 43(6). This is done under the block concept, where all assets of the same class and subject to the same rate of depreciation are considered as a single asset. Any changes to the block's value must abide by the provisions of Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961. Additionally, the assessee can claim depreciation on the residual value of such discarded asset, even if it's not used for the purposes of business or profession in that year and subsequent years.", "pred": "As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by a business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by a business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.", "cot": "[\"Answer: As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by a business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.\", \"Answer: As per section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset is permitted to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.\", \"Answer: As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.\", \"Answer: According to section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.\", \"Answer: As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by a business and not sold, section 43(6) allows the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded. This reduction is permissible under the block concept, where assets in the same class and depreciation rate are grouped together and treated as a single asset.\"]", "time_sec": 24.529, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 47.42, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "139", "question": "what is the treatment when asset is discarded as per income tax act", "gt": "Based on the given passages, when an asset is discarded but not sold, the scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded. The block concept is followed, where assets of the same class and subject to the same rate of depreciation are grouped together and considered as a single asset. The assessee is entitled to claim depreciation on the residual value of the discarded asset, even if it is not used for business or profession in that year and subsequent years.", "pred": "As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: As per section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.", "cot": "[\"Answer: As per section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.\", \"Answer: According to section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.\", \"Answer: As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.\", \"Answer: As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.\", \"Answer: As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded. This reduction is made under the block concept, where assets in the same class and depreciation rate are treated as a single asset. The assessee can claim depreciation on the residual value (opening written down value minus scrap value) even if the asset is no longer used for business purposes.\"]", "time_sec": 26.532, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 44.74, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "140", "question": "what is the treatment when asset is discarded as per income tax act", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it is clear that when an asset is discarded by a business but not sold, the Income Tax Act allows the scrap value of the asset to be deducted from the opening work-in-progress (WDV) of such asset in the previous year in which it was discarded. This reduction is taken into account while computing the depreciation expense for the subsequent years. However, any changes made to the value of the block must be made strictly in accordance with the provisions of Chapter IV D of Income Tax Act 1961.", "pred": "As per section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset is permitted to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: As per section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset is permitted to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.", "cot": "[\"Answer: As per section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset is permitted to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.\", \"Answer: When an asset is discarded by a business but not sold, section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.\", \"Answer: According to section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded. This reduction is permissible under the block concept, where assets in the same class and subject to the same depreciation rate are considered a single asset.\", \"Answer: As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.\", \"Answer: According to the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by a business but not sold, section 43(6) allows the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset was discarded.\"]", "time_sec": 24.402, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 48.1, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "141", "question": "what is the treatment when asset is discarded as per income tax act", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the treatment when an asset is discarded as per the Income Tax Act is as follows:\n\nAccording to passage 1, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which such asset is discarded. This reduction is allowed under section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act.\n\nPassage 3 further states that the assessee is entitled to claim depreciation on the residual value of such discarded asset (i.e., opening WDV of such asset less scrap value) even though such discarded asset is not used for the purposes of business or profession in such year and subsequent years.\n\nTherefore, the treatment when an asset is discarded as per the Income Tax Act is to reduce the block value of the asset by the scrap value in the previous year in which it is discarded, and to claim depreciation on the residual value of the asset in the year of discard and subsequent years.", "pred": "When an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: When an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.", "cot": "[\"Answer: When an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.\", \"Answer: As per section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset is permitted to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.\", \"Answer: As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.\", \"Answer: As per section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.\", \"Answer: As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which such asset is discarded.\"]", "time_sec": 22.345, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 36.07, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "142", "question": "what is the treatment when asset is discarded as per income tax act", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the treatment for an asset that is discarded as per the Income Tax Act is as follows:\n\nThe scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded, as per Section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act. The block concept applies to all assets falling within the same class and subject to the same rate of depreciation, and any alterations to the value of the block must be made in accordance with the provisions of Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act. The assessee can claim depreciation on the residual value of the discarded asset (i.e., opening WDV of such asset less scrap value) even if the asset is not used for business or professional purposes in that year or subsequent years.\n\nTherefore, the treatment for an asset that is discarded as per the Income Tax Act is to reduce the scrap value from the block in the previous year and claim depreciation on the residual value.", "pred": "As per section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset is permitted to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: As per section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset is permitted to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.", "cot": "[\"Answer: As per section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset is permitted to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.\", \"Answer: As per section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.\", \"Answer: As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by a business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.\", \"Answer: According to the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.\", \"Answer: As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded. This reduction is made under the block concept, where all assets in the same class and depreciation rate are treated as a single asset, and any value alteration must strictly comply with Chapter IV D provisions.\"]", "time_sec": 25.323, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 33.6, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "144", "question": "how to sew a zipper gusset", "gt": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "pred": "To sew a zipper gusset, first trace and cut the pattern to the rest of the fabric. Make a few stitches near the top stop of the zipper. Pin the zipper gusset to the zipper with the right sides facing each other, placing it 1/4\" away from the center. Change the presser foot to a zipper foot and sew a line near the seam line to secure the position. Place the zipper gusset lining on top with the right side facing down and stitch on the seam line. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and top stitch near the seam line. Repeat the process for the other side of the zipper gusset. Ensure the width of the zipper between the gusset measures 1/2\". Change the presser foot back to a normal foot and stitch on the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabric together nicely. Lightly roll the piping seam allowance at the curve. Place the zipper gusset on the pencil case body with the ends aligned to the center markings and sew from marking to marking at both ends. Place the lining on top with the right side facing down.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To sew a zipper gusset, first trace and cut the pattern to the rest of the fabric. Make a few stitches near the top stop of the zipper. Pin the zipper gusset to the zipper with the right sides facing each other, placing it 1/4\" away from the center. Change the presser foot to a zipper foot and sew a line near the seam line to secure the position. Place the zipper gusset lining on top with the right side facing down and stitch on the seam line. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and top stitch near the seam line. Repeat the process for the other side of the zipper gusset. Ensure the width of the zipper between the gusset measures 1/2\". Change the presser foot back to a normal foot and stitch on the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabric together nicely. Lightly roll the piping seam allowance at the curve. Place the zipper gusset on the pencil case body with the ends aligned to the center markings and sew from marking to marking at both ends. Place the lining on top with the right side facing down.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To sew a zipper gusset, first trace and cut the pattern to the rest of the fabric. Make a few stitches near the top stop of the zipper. Pin the zipper gusset to the zipper with the right sides facing each other, placing it 1/4\\\" away from the center. Change the presser foot to a zipper foot and sew a line near the seam line to secure the position. Place the zipper gusset lining on top with the right side facing down and stitch on the seam line. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and top stitch near the seam line. Repeat the process for the other side of the zipper gusset. Ensure the width of the zipper between the gusset measures 1/2\\\". Change the presser foot back to a normal foot and stitch on the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabric together nicely. Lightly roll the piping seam allowance at the curve. Place the zipper gusset on the pencil case body with the ends aligned to the center markings and sew from marking to marking at both ends. Place the lining on top with the right side facing down.\", \"Answer: To sew a zipper gusset, first trace and cut the pattern to the rest of the fabric. Make a few stitches near the top stop of the zipper. Pin the zipper gusset to the zipper, ensuring the right sides are facing each other and the gusset is 1/4″ away from the center. Change the presser foot to a zipper foot and sew a line near the seam line to secure the position. Place the zipper gusset lining on top with the right side facing down and stitch on the seam line. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and topstitch near the seam line. Repeat the process for the other side of the zipper gusset. The width of the zipper between the gusset should measure 1/2″. Change the presser foot back to a normal foot and stitch on the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabric together nicely.\", \"Answer: To sew a zipper gusset, trace the pattern to the fabric and cut it, then make a few stitches near the top stop of the zipper. Pin the zipper gusset to the zipper 1/4″ away from the center with right sides facing each other, change the presser foot to a zipper foot, and sew a line near the seam line to secure the position. Place the zipper gusset lining on top with the right side facing down and stitch on the seam line. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and top stitch near the seam line. Repeat for the other side of the zipper gusset. The width of the zipper in between the gusset should measure 1/2″. Change the presser foot back to the normal foot and stitch on the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabric together nicely.\", \"Answer: To sew a zipper gusset, first trace and cut the pattern pieces. Trace all pattern to the rest of the fabric and cut. Make a few stitches near the top stop of the zipper. Pin the zipper gusset to the zipper (1/4″ away from the center) with right side facing each other. Change the presser foot to zipper foot, sew a line near to the seam line to secure the position. Place the zipper gusset lining on top of them with right side facing down. Stitch on the seam line. Flip the lining to the bottom of main zipper gusset, top stitch near the seam line. Repeat sewing another side of the zipper gusset. The width of the zipper in between the gusset should measure 1/2″. Change the presser foot back to normal foot, stitch on the seam allowance around the zipper gusset so that the main and lining fabric attach together nicely.\", \"Answer: To sew a zipper gusset, first trace and cut the pattern, then make a few stitches near the top stop of the zipper. Pin the zipper gusset to the zipper 1/4\\\" away from the center with right sides facing each other, and use a zipper foot to sew a line near the seam line to secure it. Place the zipper gusset lining on top with the right side facing down, stitch on the seam line, flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset, and top stitch near the seam line. Repeat for the other side of the zipper gusset. The width of the zipper between the gusset should measure 1/2\\\". Then, change back to the normal foot and stitch on the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabric together.\"]", "time_sec": 78.611, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "146", "question": "how to sew a zipper gusset", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are the steps to sew a zipper gusset:\n\n1. Make a casing in the side seams by stitching the outer edge of the gusset and the back side seam.\n2. Slide the boning right down into the casing.\n3. Push the seam allowance to the back and tack the casing to the lining, if necessary, so it doesn't move.\n4. Make a zipper sandwich by placing one piece of lining fabric face up, followed by the zipper, and then the outer fabric right side facing down.\n5. Sew across the top of the zipper sandwich with a zipper foot, making sure to line up the three edges.\n6. Repeat the process for the other side of the zipper.\n7. Trace all pattern pieces to the remaining fabric and cut them out.\n8. Pin the zipper gusset to the zipper, with the right sides facing each other, and sew a line near the seam line to secure the position.\n9. Place the zipper gusset lining on top of the zipper gusset, with the right sides facing down, and stitch along the seam line.\n10. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and top stitch near the seam line.\n11. Repeat the process for the other side of the zipper gusset.\n12. The width of the zipper between the gusset should measure 1/2\".\n13. Change the presser foot back to a normal foot and stitch along the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabrics together nicely.\n\nTherefore, to sew a zipper gusset, you need to make a casing in the side seams, insert the boning, make a zipper sandwich, sew the zipper gusset to the zipper, and attach the lining to the zipper gusset.", "pred": "To sew a zipper gusset, first trace and cut the pattern. Pin the zipper gusset to the zipper 1/4\" away from the center with right sides facing each other, then change to a zipper foot and sew a line near the seam line to secure it. Place the zipper gusset lining on top with the right side facing down and stitch on the seam line. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and topstitch near the seam line. Repeat for the other side of the zipper gusset. Ensure the width of the zipper between the gusset measures 1/2\". Change back to a normal foot and stitch on the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabric together. Finally, place the zipper gusset on the pencil case body with ends aligned to the center markings and sew from marking to marking at both ends, then place the lining on top with the right side facing down.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To sew a zipper gusset, first trace and cut the pattern. Pin the zipper gusset to the zipper 1/4\" away from the center with right sides facing each other, then change to a zipper foot and sew a line near the seam line to secure it. Place the zipper gusset lining on top with the right side facing down and stitch on the seam line. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and topstitch near the seam line. Repeat for the other side of the zipper gusset. Ensure the width of the zipper between the gusset measures 1/2\". Change back to a normal foot and stitch on the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabric together. Finally, place the zipper gusset on the pencil case body with ends aligned to the center markings and sew from marking to marking at both ends, then place the lining on top with the right side facing down.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To sew a zipper gusset, first trace and cut the pattern. Pin the zipper gusset to the zipper 1/4\\\" away from the center with right sides facing each other, then change to a zipper foot and sew a line near the seam line to secure it. Place the zipper gusset lining on top with the right side facing down and stitch on the seam line. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and topstitch near the seam line. Repeat for the other side of the zipper gusset. Ensure the width of the zipper between the gusset measures 1/2\\\". Change back to a normal foot and stitch on the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabric together. Finally, place the zipper gusset on the pencil case body with ends aligned to the center markings and sew from marking to marking at both ends, then place the lining on top with the right side facing down.\", \"Answer: To sew a zipper gusset, first trace and cut the pattern onto fabric. Make a few stitches near the top stop of the zipper, then pin the zipper gusset to the zipper 1/4\\\" away from the center with right sides facing each other. Use a zipper foot to sew a line near the seam line to secure the position. Place the zipper gusset lining on top with right side facing down and stitch on the seam line. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and topstitch near the seam line. Repeat for the other side of the zipper gusset. The width of the zipper between the gusset should measure 1/2\\\". Change back to a normal foot and stitch on the seam allowance around the zipper gusset so the main and lining fabric attach together nicely.\", \"Answer: To sew a zipper gusset, first trace and cut the pattern. Make a few stitches near the top stop of the zipper. Pin the zipper gusset to the zipper 1/4″ away from the center with right sides facing each other. Change the presser foot to a zipper foot and sew a line near the seam line to secure the position. Place the zipper gusset lining on top with the right side facing down, then stitch on the seam line. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and top stitch near the seam line. Repeat for the other side of the zipper gusset, ensuring the width between the gusset and zipper is 1/2″. Finally, change the presser foot back to normal and stitch on the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabric together.\", \"Answer: To sew a zipper gusset, first trace and cut the pattern pieces. Make stitches near the top stop of the zipper, then pin the zipper gusset to the zipper with right sides facing each other, 1/4″ away from the center. Use a zipper foot to sew a line near the seam line to secure the position. Place the zipper gusset lining on top with right sides facing down, and stitch on the seam line. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and topstitch near the seam line. Repeat for the other side of the zipper gusset. The width of the zipper between the gussets should be 1/2″. Then, change the presser foot to normal and stitch on the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabric together. Additionally, align the ends of the zipper gusset to the center markings on the pencil case body and sew from marking to marking at both ends. Finally, place the lining on top of the zipper gusset with right sides facing down.\", \"Answer: To sew a zipper gusset, first trace and cut the pattern, then make a few stitches near the top stop of the zipper. Pin the zipper gusset to the zipper 1/4\\\" away from the center with right sides facing each other, change the presser foot to zipper foot, and sew a line near the seam line to secure the position. Place the zipper gusset lining on top with right side facing down and stitch on the seam line. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and top stitch near the seam line. Repeat for the other side of the zipper gusset, ensuring the width between the gusset measures 1/2\\\". Change the presser foot back to normal, stitch on the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabric, and lightly lip on the piping seam allowance at the curve. Finally, place the zipper gusset on the pencil case body with ends aligned to center markings and sew from marking to marking at both ends, then place the lining on top with right side facing down.\"]", "time_sec": 79.16, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 53.61, "GAVIE-Rel": 5.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 4.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "150", "question": "how to bake hard boiled egg", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how to bake hard-boiled eggs:\n\n1. Place cold eggs in a single layer in a pan with enough cold water to cover them by about 1 inch.\n2. Place the pan on the stove over high heat.\n3. As soon as the water comes to a boil, turn the heat off, cover the pan, and remove it from the heat.\n4. Allow the eggs to sit in the water for the appropriate amount of time, depending on the size of the eggs (9 minutes for medium, 12 minutes for large, and 15 minutes for extra-large).\n\nNote: Do not peek during the cooking process, and do not worry if the eggs are not perfectly symmetrical or have a slightly greenish-gray yolk.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\"]", "time_sec": 7.159, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 7.14, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "158", "question": "how to factory reset a htc one vx", "gt": "To factory reset an HTC One VX, you can follow the steps mentioned in passage 3. Slide the Notifications panel open, tap on Storage, then Factory data reset, and finally tap on Reset phone and Erase everything.", "pred": "To factory reset an HTC One VX, first back up any data you want to keep. Then, slide open the Notifications panel and tap the appropriate option to access Storage Factory data reset. Tap Reset phone, followed by Erase everything.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To factory reset an HTC One VX, first back up any data you want to keep. Then, slide open the Notifications panel and tap the appropriate option to access Storage Factory data reset. Tap Reset phone, followed by Erase everything.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To factory reset an HTC One VX, first back up any data you want to keep. Then, slide open the Notifications panel and tap the appropriate option to access Storage Factory data reset. Tap Reset phone, followed by Erase everything.\", \"Answer: To factory reset an HTC One VX, first switch off the phone using the Power button. Then press and hold the Volume Down and Power buttons together for a couple of seconds until the Bootloader Mode appears on the screen. Release all keys, choose \\\"Factory Reset\\\" by pressing the Volume Down button to scroll and the Power key to select, then press and hold Volume Down + Power for a couple of seconds. When the Bootloader Mode menu appears, release the buttons, select \\\"RECOVERY MODE\\\" using the Volume buttons to scroll and the Power key to choose, and confirm. This will initiate the factory reset process, erasing all data. Additionally, it's advised to back up any important data and files before performing the reset, as this process will erase everything on the device.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: To factory reset an HTC One VX, first back up any data and files you want to keep. Then, slide open the Notifications panel and tap the appropriate option. Next, tap \\\"Storage Factory data reset,\\\" followed by \\\"Reset phone,\\\" and finally \\\"Erase everything.\\\" Alternatively, you can switch off the phone by using the Power rocker, then press and hold the Volume Down and Power buttons together for a couple of seconds. Release all keys when the Bootloader Mode appears on the screen, choose the Factory Reset option by pressing the Volume Down button to scroll and the Power key to select, and then press and hold the Volume Down + Power key for a couple of seconds. When you see the Bootloader Mode menu, release the held buttons and choose the RECOVERY MODE option by using the Volume buttons to scroll and the Power key to select.\", \"Answer: To factory reset an HTC One VX, first back up any data and files you want to keep. Then, switch off the phone by using the Power rocker. After that, press and hold the Volume Down and Power buttons together for a couple of seconds. Release all keys when the Bootloader Mode appears on the screen. Choose the Factory Reset option by pressing the Volume Down button to scroll and the Power key to select. Then, press and hold the Volume Down + Power key for a couple of seconds. When you see the Bootloader Mode menu, release the held buttons. From the menu, choose the RECOVERY MODE option by using the Volume buttons to scroll and the Power key to select options. Congratulations! You should now be in the expected mode.\"]", "time_sec": 43.553, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 68.97, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "159", "question": "how to factory reset a htc one vx", "gt": "To factory reset an HTC One VX, follow these steps:\n\n1. Switch off the device by holding down the Power button for a while.\n2. Press and hold the Volume Down + Power key for a couple of seconds until Bootloader Mode menu appears on the screen.\n3. From the menu, choose \"Factory Data Reset\" by using Volume buttons to scroll and Power key to select.\n4. Confirm the reset by pressing \"Erase Everything\".\n5. Release all keys when the process is complete.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\"]", "time_sec": 6.907, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "162", "question": "what is psa levels mean", "gt": "PSA, or Prostate-specific antigen, is a protein produced by cells of the prostate gland. The level of PSA in a man's blood is measured by the PSA test, which involves sending a blood sample to a laboratory for analysis. The results are usually reported as nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate conditions such as prostate cancer, a noncancerous condition like prostatitis, or an enlarged prostate. Total PSA levels between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml may indicate prostate cancer, benign prostate hyperplasia, or inflammation of the prostate.", "pred": "PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) in a man’s blood, measured in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH), prostatitis, or other non‑cancerous prostate conditions.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) in a man’s blood, measured in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH), prostatitis, or other non‑cancerous prostate conditions.", "cot": "[\"Answer: PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) in a man’s blood, measured in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH), prostatitis, or other non‑cancerous prostate conditions.\", \"Answer: PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) in a man's blood, measured in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, a noncancerous condition such as prostatitis, or an enlarged prostate, with levels between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml suggesting a 25% chance of prostate cancer according to the ACS, or benign prostate hyperplasia (BPH) or inflammation of the prostate.\", \"Answer: PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) in a man's blood, measured in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, a noncancerous condition such as prostatitis, or an enlarged prostate. The passage also states that total PSA levels between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml may indicate prostate cancer (with about a 25% chance according to the ACS), benign prostate hyperplasia (BPH), or inflammation of the prostate.\", \"Answer: PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) in a man's blood, measured in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, a noncancerous condition such as prostatitis, or an enlarged prostate (benign prostate hyperplasia, BPH). According to the American Cancer Society (ACS), total PSA levels between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml may indicate prostate cancer (about a 25% chance), BPH, or inflammation of the prostate. These conditions are more common in the elderly, as is a general increase in PSA levels. If the digital rectal exam (DRE) is normal but PSA is moderately elevated, a free PSA test may be used to look at the ratio of free to total PSA, which can help distinguish between prostate cancer and other non-cancer causes of elevated PSA.\", \"Answer: PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) in a man's blood, measured in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, a noncancerous condition such as prostatitis, or an enlarged prostate. PSA levels between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml may suggest prostate cancer (about a 25% chance), benign prostate hyperplasia (BPH), or inflammation of the prostate, especially in elderly men.\"]", "time_sec": 46.207, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 53.76, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "166", "question": "what is psa levels mean", "gt": "Based on the given passages, PSA levels refer to the amount of prostate-specific antigen present in a man's blood. The level of PSA is measured in nanograms per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, a noncancerous condition such as prostatitis, or an enlarged prostate. Normal PSA levels range between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml, and a moderate elevation in PSA levels may require further testing, such as a free PSA test, to determine the ratio of free to total PSA and help distinguish between prostate cancer and other non-cancer causes of elevated PSA. Therefore, the PSA test is used to monitor the progression of prostate cancer and to detect elevated PSA levels that may indicate prostate cancer or other prostate issues.", "pred": "PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) in a man's blood, typically reported in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, with levels between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml associated with about a 25% chance of prostate cancer according to the American Cancer Society (ACS), benign prostate hyperplasia (BPH), or prostate inflammation (prostatitis). PSA levels can also be elevated in noncancerous conditions such as an enlarged prostate or prostatitis, and the test is used to monitor the progression of prostate cancer or distinguish between cancer and other non-cancer causes of elevated PSA.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) in a man's blood, typically reported in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, with levels between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml associated with about a 25% chance of prostate cancer according to the American Cancer Society (ACS), benign prostate hyperplasia (BPH), or prostate inflammation (prostatitis). PSA levels can also be elevated in noncancerous conditions such as an enlarged prostate or prostatitis, and the test is used to monitor the progression of prostate cancer or distinguish between cancer and other non-cancer causes of elevated PSA.", "cot": "[\"Answer: PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) in a man's blood, typically reported in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, with levels between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml associated with about a 25% chance of prostate cancer according to the American Cancer Society (ACS), benign prostate hyperplasia (BPH), or prostate inflammation (prostatitis). PSA levels can also be elevated in noncancerous conditions such as an enlarged prostate or prostatitis, and the test is used to monitor the progression of prostate cancer or distinguish between cancer and other non-cancer causes of elevated PSA.\", \"Answer: PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) in a man’s blood, measured in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL). The passages indicate that PSA is a protein produced by prostate gland cells, and elevated levels may suggest prostate cancer, benign conditions such as prostatitis or benign prostate hyperplasia (BPH), or prostate inflammation. For example, passage 3 states that total PSA levels between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml may indicate prostate cancer (about a 25% chance), BPH, or inflammation, and passage 1 notes the test measures PSA in ng/mL.\", \"Answer: PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) in a man's blood, measured in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, a noncancerous condition such as prostatitis, or an enlarged prostate. Specifically, total PSA levels between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml may indicate prostate cancer (about a 25% chance), benign prostate hyperplasia (BPH), or inflammation of the prostate.\", \"Answer: PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) in a man’s blood, measured in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL). The passages indicate that PSA is a protein produced by the prostate gland, and elevated levels may indicate prostate cancer, benign conditions such as prostatitis or benign prostate hyperplasia (BPH), or prostate inflammation. For example, total PSA levels between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml may suggest prostate cancer (with about a 25% chance according to the ACS), BPH, or inflammation, particularly in elderly men.\", \"Answer: PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) in a man's blood, measured in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, a noncancerous condition such as prostatitis, or an enlarged prostate. The passage also mentions that total PSA levels between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml may indicate prostate cancer (about a 25% chance), benign prostate hyperplasia (BPH), or inflammation of the prostate.\"]", "time_sec": 48.703, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 65.82, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "167", "question": "show me how can i make gragh for my research", "gt": "To create a graph for your research, you first need to collect all of your data, remembering that the graph should show a comparison among categories. If you are creating a bar graph, you will use rectangular bars to represent different values. You need to title your graph and determine how high your graph needs to go. If your data points are substantially different in scale, you can use a pie chart. The y-axis of your graph will have a scale, and each bar’s height should be proportionate to the size of the category it represents. Make sure to consider any disproportionate responses that might impact the scale of your graph. Regardless of the type of graph you choose, it should provide a clear visual representation of your information.", "pred": "Collect your data first, then title the graph and determine the scale for the y-axis. Use rectangular bars whose heights are proportionate to the values they represent to compare categories, such as rainfall amounts across months.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer:Collect your data first, then title the graph and determine the scale for the y-axis. Use rectangular bars whose heights are proportionate to the values they represent to compare categories, such as rainfall amounts across months.", "cot": "[\"Answer:Collect your data first, then title the graph and determine the scale for the y-axis. Use rectangular bars whose heights are proportionate to the values they represent to compare categories, such as rainfall amounts across months.\", \"Answer: To make a bar graph for your research, first collect all your data, ensuring it compares categories (e.g., rainfall in inches for different months). Title the graph and determine the y-axis scale so each bar’s height is proportional to the category’s value. Use graph paper if desired, and ensure the graph visually represents the comparison among categories.\", \"Answer: To make a graph for your research, follow these steps based on the passages:  \\n1. **Collect your data** (Passage 1 states: \\\"The first thing you have to do is to collect all of your data\\\").  \\n2. **Title the graph and determine the scale** (Passage 2 states: \\\"First, you'll need to title it and find out how high your graph needs to go\\\").  \\n3. **Use graph paper** (Passage 2 states: \\\"you may use graph paper to help you make a great graph\\\").  \\n4. **Ensure bars are proportionate to the category size** (Passage 3 states: \\\"each bar’s height should be proportionate to the size of the category it represents\\\").  \\n\\nFor example, if your goal is to show rainfall data (as in Passage 1), collect monthly rainfall measurements (e.g., inches for February 2005 to February 2006), title the graph, set the y-axis scale to match the rainfall values, and use graph paper to create proportional bars for each month.  \\n\\nAnswer: Collect data, title the graph, determine the scale, use graph paper, and ensure bar heights are proportionate to category sizes.\", \"Answer: To make a bar graph for your research, first collect all the data you need to compare, such as the amount of rainfall for different months. Then, title the graph and determine the scale for the y-axis so each bar’s height is proportional to the category it represents. Use graph paper if desired, and ensure the graph clearly shows the comparison among categories.\", \"Answer: To make a graph for your research, first collect all the relevant data. Then, decide on the type of graph (e.g., bar graph, line graph, pie chart) based on the data and the comparison you want to show. For a bar graph, which is useful for comparing categories, follow these steps: 1) title the graph, 2) determine the scale for the y-axis, and 3) ensure each bar’s height is proportionate to the size of the category it represents. You can use graph paper to help create the graph, and make sure it visually represents your information clearly and adds color if desired. If the story of the data is unclear, consider using a pie chart as a backup, especially when you have only a few substantially different data points.\"]", "time_sec": 47.205, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 27.83, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "169", "question": "show me how can i make gragh for my research", "gt": "Sure, I can help you with that! Based on the given passages, here's a step-by-step guide on how to create a bar graph for your research:\n\n1. Collect your data: The first step is to gather all the data you want to represent in your graph. Make sure the data is organized and easy to compare.\n2. Decide on the x-axis and y-axis: The x-axis typically represents the category or group being compared, while the y-axis represents the value or amount of each category.\n3. Determine the height of the graph: The height of the graph will depend on the highest value in your data set. Make sure to choose a height that allows for clear representation of all the data points.\n4. Create the bars: Use rectangular bars to represent each category, making sure the height of each bar is proportional to the value it represents.\n5. Add labels and titles: Label each bar with its corresponding category and add a title to the graph that describes the data being represented.\n6. Consider using a pie chart: If you have only a few data points to display and each one is substantially different in scale, a pie chart might be a better option.\n7. Format and refine: Make sure the graph is visually appealing and easy to read. Adjust the scaling of the y-axis if needed, and consider adding colors or shading to make the graph more engaging.\n\nBy following these steps, you should be able to create a clear and effective bar graph for your research. Good luck!", "pred": "To make a bar graph for your research, follow these steps based on the passages:", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To make a bar graph for your research, follow these steps based on the passages:  \n1. **Collect data** relevant to your research question, such as measurements for different categories (e.g., rainfall in inches for each month between February 2005 and February 2006, as specified in passage 1).  \n2. **Title the graph** and determine the scale for the y-axis (e.g., inches of rainfall), ensuring the scale is proportional to the category sizes to avoid distortion from disproportionate responses (passage 3).  \n3. **Draw rectangular bars** where each bar’s height corresponds to the value of a category (e.g., height proportional to rainfall amount for each month), enabling clear visual comparisons among categories (passage 1 and 2).  \n4. **Use graph paper** to create a structured layout, as recommended in passage 2 for accuracy and visual clarity.  \n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To make a bar graph for your research, follow these steps based on the passages:  \\n1. **Collect data** relevant to your research question, such as measurements for different categories (e.g., rainfall in inches for each month between February 2005 and February 2006, as specified in passage 1).  \\n2. **Title the graph** and determine the scale for the y-axis (e.g., inches of rainfall), ensuring the scale is proportional to the category sizes to avoid distortion from disproportionate responses (passage 3).  \\n3. **Draw rectangular bars** where each bar’s height corresponds to the value of a category (e.g., height proportional to rainfall amount for each month), enabling clear visual comparisons among categories (passage 1 and 2).  \\n4. **Use graph paper** to create a structured layout, as recommended in passage 2 for accuracy and visual clarity.  \\n\\nUnable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: To make a bar graph for your research, first collect all relevant data (e.g., rainfall amounts for February 2005–February 2006 as specified in passage 1). Then, title the graph and determine the scale for the y-axis (e.g., inches of rainfall), ensuring each bar’s height is proportionate to the category’s value (passage 3). Passage 2 states that bar graphs are used to compare groups or types, so organize your data by categories (e.g., months) for visual comparison. Use graph paper to create the visual representation, as suggested in passage 2. \\n\\nAnswer: Collect data, title the graph, set the y-axis scale proportionally to the values, and draw bars for each category (e.g., months) to show comparisons. Use graph paper for accuracy.\", \"Answer: To make a bar graph for your research, follow these steps: First, collect all relevant data, such as measurements for different categories (e.g., rainfall in inches for each month between February 2005 and February 2006). Next, title the graph and determine the scale for the y-axis, ensuring the bar heights are proportionate to the category values. Then, draw rectangular bars where each bar's height corresponds to the value of its category, using graph paper if needed for clarity. Bar graphs are ideal for comparing groups or types, as they provide a visual representation of information and add color to your display. If the data story remains unclear, consider using a pie chart as a backup, particularly when you have only a few substantially different data points.\", \"Answer:To make a graph for your research, follow these steps based on the passages:  \\n1. **Collect all data** relevant to your comparison (e.g., rainfall amounts by month, as in Passage 1).  \\n2. **Choose the graph type**: Use a bar graph to compare categories (e.g., rainfall in different months, as in Passage 1 and Passage 2).  \\n3. **Title the graph** and determine the scale for the y-axis (e.g., inches of rainfall, as in Passage 1).  \\n4. **Ensure bars are proportional** to the values they represent (e.g., height of each bar matches rainfall amount).  \\n5. **Use graph paper** to create a clear visual representation (as in Passage 2).  \\n\\nNote: The passages do not cover line graphs or pie charts for your specific research context.  \\n\\nAnswer: To make a graph, collect your data, title it, determine the y-axis scale, and create proportional bars for categorical comparisons (e.g., rainfall by month). Use graph paper for clarity.\", \"Answer: To make a bar graph for your research, follow these steps based on the passages:  \\n1. Collect all relevant data (e.g., rainfall amounts for each month between February 2005 and February 2006, as stated in passage 1).  \\n2. Title the graph and determine the scale for the y-axis, ensuring each bar’s height is proportionate to the category’s value (passage 3).  \\n3. Use graph paper to create the graph, as recommended in passage 2, to visually represent comparisons among categories (e.g., monthly rainfall amounts).  \\n\\nAnswer: Collect data, title the graph, set the y-axis scale proportionate to values, and use graph paper to create the bar graph for visual comparison.\"]", "time_sec": 69.113, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 8.89, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "170", "question": "how to soften sugar", "gt": "To soften brown sugar, you can place it in a plastic container with a small piece of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar. Then put a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the plastic/foil and cover tightly. Remove the damp paper towel in a couple of days. To make hardened granulated sugar soft again, preheat an oven to its lowest temperature, 150-200 degrees. Remove the sugar from its package and put it in an ovenproof container. Place it in the warm oven for approximately 15 minutes. Tap the sugar with a spoon and if it starts to fall apart, turn the oven off and leave the sugar in the oven for an hour or two to completely dry out. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container.", "pred": "To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart when tapped with a spoon, turn off the oven and leave the sugar inside for an hour or two to fully dry out. For brown sugar, keep it in an airtight container with minimal air exposure, such as using a small container to reduce air space between the sugar and the lid, and replace a damp paper towel or napkin every couple of days.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart when tapped with a spoon, turn off the oven and leave the sugar inside for an hour or two to fully dry out. For brown sugar, keep it in an airtight container with minimal air exposure, such as using a small container to reduce air space between the sugar and the lid, and replace a damp paper towel or napkin every couple of days.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart when tapped with a spoon, turn off the oven and leave the sugar inside for an hour or two to fully dry out. For brown sugar, keep it in an airtight container with minimal air exposure, such as using a small container to reduce air space between the sugar and the lid, and replace a damp paper towel or napkin every couple of days.\", \"Answer:To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If it begins to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave the sugar inside for an hour or two to dry completely. For brown sugar, place it in a plastic container with plastic wrap or foil over the sugar, add a damp paper towel or napkin on top, cover tightly, and after a couple of days, remove the damp paper towel.\", \"Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it in the oven for about 15 minutes. Tap the sugar with a spoon; if it begins to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave the sugar inside for an hour or two to dry completely. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, the most efficient way to keep it soft is to seal it in an airtight container, minimizing air exposure by using the smallest possible container.\", \"Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat your oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart when tapped with a spoon, turn off the oven and leave the sugar inside for an hour or two to dry completely. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container, limit air exposure by using a small container, and place a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the sugar (covered with plastic wrap or foil) for a couple of days to restore softness.\", \"Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave it inside for an hour or two to dry completely. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container, limit air exposure by using a small container, and place a damp paper towel or napkin on top of plastic wrap or foil covering the sugar, then cover tightly; remove the damp paper towel after a couple of days.\"]", "time_sec": 50.034, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 62.41, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "172", "question": "how to soften sugar", "gt": "To soften sugar, place brown sugar in a plastic container with a small piece of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar. Put a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the plastic/foil. Cover tightly. Remove damp paper towel in a couple of days. Alternatively, preheat oven to lowest temperature (150-200 degrees) and remove sugar from package. Put in an ovenproof container and place in the warm oven for 15 minutes. Tap sugar with a spoon and if it starts to fall apart, turn oven off and leave sugar in the oven for an hour or two to completely dry out. Store granulated sugar in an airtight container and confectioners sugar in a cool, dry location.", "pred": "To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart when tapped with a spoon, turn off the oven and leave the sugar in the oven for an hour or two to fully dry out. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, the most efficient way to keep it soft is to seal it in an airtight container and limit air exposure by using the smallest container possible.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer:To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart when tapped with a spoon, turn off the oven and leave the sugar in the oven for an hour or two to fully dry out. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, the most efficient way to keep it soft is to seal it in an airtight container and limit air exposure by using the smallest container possible.", "cot": "[\"Answer:To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart when tapped with a spoon, turn off the oven and leave the sugar in the oven for an hour or two to fully dry out. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, the most efficient way to keep it soft is to seal it in an airtight container and limit air exposure by using the smallest container possible.\", \"Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart when tapped with a spoon, turn off the oven and leave the sugar in the oven for an hour or two to dry out completely. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container to limit air exposure, and pack it into the smallest container possible to minimize air space between the sugar and the lid.\", \"Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it in the oven for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar starts to fall apart when tapped with a spoon, turn off the oven and leave the sugar in the oven for an hour or two to dry out completely. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container, limit air exposure by using a small container, and place a damp paper towel or napkin on top of plastic wrap or foil covering the sugar, then cover tightly; remove the damp paper towel in a couple of days.\", \"Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat your oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for about 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave it inside for an hour or two to dry completely. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container with minimal air exposure, and consider placing a damp paper towel or napkin on top of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar, then covering it tightly; remove the damp paper towel after a couple of days.\", \"Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. Tap the sugar with a spoon; if it starts to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave the sugar in for an hour or two to dry completely. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container with minimal air exposure, and place a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the sugar (covered with plastic wrap or foil) for a couple of days to restore softness.\"]", "time_sec": 52.598, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 54.41, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "173", "question": "how to soften sugar", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are some ways to soften sugar:\n\n1. To soften brown sugar, place it in a plastic container with a small piece of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar, and cover tightly. Remove the damp paper towel after a couple of days. (Passage 1)\n2. To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to a low temperature (150-200°F), remove the sugar from its package, and place it in an ovenproof container. Warm the sugar in the oven for about 15 minutes, then tap it with a spoon. If it starts to fall apart, leave it in the oven for a longer period of time to completely dry out. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container. (Passage 2)\n3. To keep brown sugar soft, pack it into the smallest container possible to minimize air exposure. (Passage 3)\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages. The passages do not provide information on how to soften other types of sugar, such as confectioner's sugar.", "pred": "To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it in the oven for about 15 minutes. If the sugar starts to fall apart when tapped with a spoon, turn off the oven and leave the sugar in the oven for an hour or two to dry out completely. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container with minimal air exposure, using a small container to limit air space between the sugar and the lid.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it in the oven for about 15 minutes. If the sugar starts to fall apart when tapped with a spoon, turn off the oven and leave the sugar in the oven for an hour or two to dry out completely. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container with minimal air exposure, using a small container to limit air space between the sugar and the lid.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it in the oven for about 15 minutes. If the sugar starts to fall apart when tapped with a spoon, turn off the oven and leave the sugar in the oven for an hour or two to dry out completely. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container with minimal air exposure, using a small container to limit air space between the sugar and the lid.\", \"Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave the sugar inside for an hour or two to dry completely. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening.\", \"Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart when tapped with a spoon, turn off the oven and leave the sugar in the oven for an hour or two to dry completely. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container with minimal air exposure, using the smallest container possible to limit air space between the sugar and the lid, and place a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the sugar, covered with plastic wrap or foil, for a couple of days.\", \"Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave the sugar inside for an hour or two to fully dry out. For brown sugar, the most efficient way to keep it soft is to store it in an airtight container, limiting air exposure by using the smallest container possible.\", \"Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave the sugar inside for an hour or two to dry completely. For brown sugar, store it in a plastic container with a small piece of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar, a damp paper towel or napkin on top, and cover tightly; remove the damp paper towel after a couple of days. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container and keep it in a cool, dry location.\"]", "time_sec": 48.019, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 47.95, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "175", "question": "how to soften sugar", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how to soften sugar:\n\n1. Place brown sugar in an airtight container with a damp paper towel or napkin on top. Cover tightly and let it sit for a couple of days. Remove the damp paper towel, and the sugar should be soft. (Passage 1)\n2. Preheat the oven to a low temperature (150-200 degrees), place hardened granulated sugar in an ovenproof container, and tap it with a spoon after 15 minutes. If it starts to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave it in for an hour or two to dry out completely. Store granulated sugar in an airtight container. (Passage 2)\n3. Keep brown sugar soft by storing it in an airtight container with minimal air space between the sugar and the lid. Pack the sugar into the smallest container possible. (Passage 3)", "pred": "To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave it inside for an hour or two to dry completely. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container with minimal air exposure by using the smallest container possible and covering it with plastic wrap or foil and a damp paper towel for a couple of days.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave it inside for an hour or two to dry completely. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container with minimal air exposure by using the smallest container possible and covering it with plastic wrap or foil and a damp paper towel for a couple of days.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave it inside for an hour or two to dry completely. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container with minimal air exposure by using the smallest container possible and covering it with plastic wrap or foil and a damp paper towel for a couple of days.\", \"Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If it begins to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave the sugar inside for an hour or two to dry completely. For brown sugar, the most efficient method is to store it in an airtight container with minimal air exposure, such as packing it into the smallest container possible to limit air space between the sugar and the lid.\", \"Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart when tapped with a spoon, turn off the oven and leave the sugar inside for an hour or two to fully dry out. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening.\", \"Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart when tapped with a spoon, turn off the oven and leave the sugar in the oven for an hour or two to dry completely. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, place it in a plastic container with plastic wrap or foil over the sugar, add a damp paper towel or napkin on top, cover tightly, and remove the damp towel after a couple of days. The most efficient way to keep brown sugar soft is to store it in an airtight container with minimal air exposure.\", \"Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If it begins to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave the sugar inside for an hour or two to dry completely. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container with minimal air exposure, such as using a small container to limit air space between the sugar and the lid.\"]", "time_sec": 46.839, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 56.16, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "179", "question": "how to change the sleep time on my computer", "gt": "Sure, here's a brief answer to the question \"How to change the sleep time on my computer\" based on the given passages:\n\nTo change the sleep time on your computer, follow these steps:\n\n1. Click the Start menu and select Control Panel.\n2. In the Control Panel, click on Power Options.\n3. In the Power Options window, click on \"Change when the computer sleeps\" on the left side of the window.\n4. In the \"Put the computer to sleep\" section, change the duration of time to the desired amount of time you want the computer to sit idle before it goes to sleep.\n5. Alternatively, you can also toggle off the option to enter Sleep mode automatically.\n\nIf you are using Windows 10, you can also search for \"Power Options\" in the taskbar search box and select it to open the Power Options window.\n\nIf you are using Windows 8.1 / Windows RT 8.1, you can move your mouse to the lower left-hand corner of the screen, right-click the Start button, and select Shut down or sign out, and then select Shut down.", "pred": "To change the sleep time on your computer, follow these steps based on the provided passages:", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To change the sleep time on your computer, follow these steps based on the provided passages:\n\n1. **For Windows 7:**\n   - Click the Start Menu to load the Help Center.\n   - Enter \"Power Management Settings\" in the search field.\n   - Click \"Change Power Management settings.\"\n   - In the Power Options window, on the left side, click \"Change when the computer sleeps.\"\n   - Adjust the \"Put the computer to sleep\" entry to your desired amount of idle time before the computer sleeps, or choose to turn off the option to enter Sleep mode.\n\n2. **For Windows 10:**\n   - Click the Start Menu and type \"power options\" in the search box on the taskbar.\n   - Select \"Power Options\" from the search results.\n\n3. **For Windows 8.1 / Windows RT 8.1:**\n   - Select the Start button and then select Power > Shut down.\n   - Alternatively, move your mouse to the lower left-hand corner of the screen, right-click the Start button, or press the Windows logo key + X on your keyboard.\n   - Tap or click \"Shut down or sign out\" and choose \"Shut down.\"\n\nThese steps will guide you through changing the sleep time on your computer according to the instructions provided in the passages.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To change the sleep time on your computer, follow these steps based on the provided passages:\\n\\n1. **For Windows 7:**\\n   - Click the Start Menu to load the Help Center.\\n   - Enter \\\"Power Management Settings\\\" in the search field.\\n   - Click \\\"Change Power Management settings.\\\"\\n   - In the Power Options window, on the left side, click \\\"Change when the computer sleeps.\\\"\\n   - Adjust the \\\"Put the computer to sleep\\\" entry to your desired amount of idle time before the computer sleeps, or choose to turn off the option to enter Sleep mode.\\n\\n2. **For Windows 10:**\\n   - Click the Start Menu and type \\\"power options\\\" in the search box on the taskbar.\\n   - Select \\\"Power Options\\\" from the search results.\\n\\n3. **For Windows 8.1 / Windows RT 8.1:**\\n   - Select the Start button and then select Power > Shut down.\\n   - Alternatively, move your mouse to the lower left-hand corner of the screen, right-click the Start button, or press the Windows logo key + X on your keyboard.\\n   - Tap or click \\\"Shut down or sign out\\\" and choose \\\"Shut down.\\\"\\n\\nThese steps will guide you through changing the sleep time on your computer according to the instructions provided in the passages.\", \"Answer: To change the sleep time on your computer, follow these steps based on the provided passages:  \\n1. For Windows 7: Click Start Menu → Enter \\\"Power Management Settings\\\" in the search field → Click \\\"Change Power Management settings\\\" → Select \\\"Change when the computer sleeps\\\" on the left → Modify the \\\"Put the computer to sleep\\\" entry to your desired idle time.  \\n2. For Windows 10: Click Start Menu → Select Control Panel → Type \\\"power options\\\" in the taskbar search box → Select \\\"Power Options\\\" → Adjust the sleep time under \\\"Change when the computer sleeps\\\" (note: the passage does not specify the exact navigation path for Windows 10 beyond the search step, but references Windows 7 steps for sleep settings).  \\n\\nUnable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: To change the sleep time on your computer, follow these steps based on the provided passages:\\n\\n- **For Windows 7**: \\n  1. Click the Start Menu to load the Help Center.\\n  2. Enter \\\"Power Management Settings\\\" in the search field.\\n  3. Click \\\"Change Power Management settings.\\\"\\n  4. On the left side of the Power Options window, click \\\"Change when the computer sleeps.\\\"\\n  5. Adjust the \\\"Put the computer to sleep\\\" entry to your desired idle time before the computer sleeps, or choose to turn off its option to enter Sleep mode.\\n\\n- **For Windows 10**:\\n  1. Click the Start Menu.\\n  2. In the search box on the taskbar, type \\\"power options\\\" and then select \\\"Power Options\\\" to open the power settings.\\n\\n- **For Windows 8.1 / Windows RT 8.1**:\\n  1. Select the Start button and then select \\\"Power\\\" > \\\"Shut down.\\\" \\n  2. Alternatively, move your mouse to the lower left-hand corner of the screen and right-click the Start button or press the Windows logo key + X on your keyboard. Tap or click \\\"Shut down or sign out\\\" and choose \\\"Shut down.\\\"\\n\\nNote: The provided passages do not specify how to change the sleep time on Windows 7 beyond the steps listed, and there is no information on how to change the sleep time on Windows 10 or Windows 8.1. The information on Windows 7 is the most detailed regarding changing the sleep time.\", \"Answer: To change the sleep time on your computer, follow these steps based on the provided passages:\\n\\nFor Windows 7:\\n1. Click the Start Menu to load the Help Center.\\n2. Enter \\\"Power Management Settings\\\" in the search field.\\n3. Click \\\"Change Power Management settings\\\" to open the Power Options window.\\n4. On the left side of the window, click \\\"Change when the computer sleeps.\\\"\\n5. Adjust the \\\"Put the computer to sleep\\\" entry to your desired amount of idle time before the computer sleeps, or choose to turn off the option to enter Sleep mode automatically.\\n\\nFor Windows 10:\\n1. Click the Start Menu and type \\\"power options\\\" in the search box on the taskbar.\\n2. Select \\\"Power Options\\\" from the search results.\\n3. Follow the on-screen instructions to adjust the sleep settings according to your preference.\\n\\nFor Windows 8.1 / Windows RT 8.1:\\n1. Select the Start button and then select Power > Shut down.\\n2. Alternatively, move your mouse to the lower left-hand corner of the screen and right-click the Start button or press Windows logo key + X on your keyboard.\\n3. Tap or click \\\"Shut down or sign out\\\" and choose \\\"Shut down\\\" to access power options.\\n\\nThese instructions provide a general guide to changing the sleep time on your computer, with specific steps for different Windows versions.\", \"Answer: To change the sleep time on your computer, follow these steps based on the provided passages:\\n\\n1. Click the Start Menu to load the Help Center (Passage 1).\\n2. Enter \\\"Power Management Settings\\\" in the search field (Passage 1).\\n3. Click \\\"Change Power Management settings\\\" to open the Power Options window (Passage 1).\\n4. On the left side of the window, click \\\"Change when the computer sleeps\\\" (Passage 1).\\n5. Adjust the \\\"Put the computer to sleep\\\" entry with the desired amount of time you will allow the computer to sit idle before it sleeps, or select the option to automatically turn off its setting to enter into Sleep mode (Passage 1).\\n\\nThese steps are applicable to Windows 7, as mentioned in Passage 1. For Windows 10, you can open power options by typing \\\"power options\\\" in the search box on the taskbar and then selecting Power Options (Passage 2). Note that the instructions in Passage 3 are for shutting down Windows 8.1 / Windows RT 8.1 and do not pertain to changing the sleep time.\"]", "time_sec": 96.024, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 13.64, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "180", "question": "psychological effects of hugging", "gt": "Hugging has significant psychological effects. It creates a warm feeling that affects the entire body, akin to the effect of many drugs operating simultaneously. This could be due to the feeling of a loving embrace, leading to good relationships and emotional intimacy. It also enhances empathy and listening skills, which can lead to a psychological hug. Furthermore, research has found that hugging can significantly reduce worries about mortality and boost self-esteem. This soothing effect can be achieved even when hugging an inanimate object like a teddy bear.", "pred": "Psychological hugs are described as a result of empathy and listening skills (empathic listening), which can foster good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages. They may also make a conversation partner feel as though they are receiving a hug, indicating a psychological effect that provides comfort or emotional support.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Psychological hugs are described as a result of empathy and listening skills (empathic listening), which can foster good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages. They may also make a conversation partner feel as though they are receiving a hug, indicating a psychological effect that provides comfort or emotional support.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Psychological hugs are described as a result of empathy and listening skills (empathic listening), which can foster good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages. They may also make a conversation partner feel as though they are receiving a hug, indicating a psychological effect that provides comfort or emotional support.\", \"Answer: Psychological effects of hugging include fostering empathy and listening skills, leading to good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages. Hugging can make individuals feel like they are receiving a \\\"psychological hug,\\\" which helps soothe existential fears and reduce worry of mortality, even when hugging inanimate objects like teddy bears.\", \"Answer: Psychological hugs are described as the feeling of receiving empathy and listening skills, which can lead to good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages. They make conversation partners feel like they are receiving a hug, providing emotional comfort.\", \"Answer: Psychological hugs, facilitated by empathy and listening skills (empathic listening), can foster good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages. They may also make conversation partners feel as though they are receiving a hug, providing emotional comfort and support.\", \"Answer: Psychological hugs, as described in the passages, refer to the emotional and mental benefits derived from hugging, even when the hug is from an inanimate object like a teddy bear. Hugging helps reduce existential fears and worry about mortality, as shown in a study published in the journal Psychological Science. Additionally, empathy and listening skills, which can lead to a psychological hug, contribute to good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages. Hugs affect the entire body, providing physiological benefits that are comparable to the effects of many different drugs operating simultaneously.\"]", "time_sec": 27.893, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 26.8, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "181", "question": "psychological effects of hugging", "gt": "Hugging has physiological benefits and can have a similar effect on the body as certain drugs. It can also lead to emotional intimacy and happy relationships. Hugging and touch, even with inanimate objects, can help reduce existential fears and worry about mortality.", "pred": "Psychological hugs, facilitated by empathy and listening skills (empathic listening), can foster good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages. They may also make a conversation partner feel as though they are receiving a comforting embrace.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Psychological hugs, facilitated by empathy and listening skills (empathic listening), can foster good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages. They may also make a conversation partner feel as though they are receiving a comforting embrace.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Psychological hugs, facilitated by empathy and listening skills (empathic listening), can foster good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages. They may also make a conversation partner feel as though they are receiving a comforting embrace.\", \"Answer: Psychological hugs are achieved through the use of empathy and listening skills, also known as empathic listening. This can lead to good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages, and can make the conversation partner feel like they are receiving a hug.\", \"Answer: Psychological hugs, as described in the passages, are achieved through the use of empathy and listening skills (empathic listening), which can lead to emotional intimacy, happy marriages, and make the conversation partner feel emotionally comforted, akin to receiving a physical hug.\", \"Answer:Hugging has psychological effects, including creating a \\\"psychological hug\\\" through empathy and listening skills, which can lead to emotional intimacy and happy marriages. Additionally, hugging—even with inanimate objects like teddy bears—significantly reduces existential fears related to mortality, as shown in a study published in Psychological Science.\", \"Answer: Psychological hugs, as described in the passage, involve the use of empathy and listening skills (empathic listening) to create emotional intimacy and happy marriages. This type of hug makes the conversation partner feel as though they are receiving a hug, providing emotional comfort and support.\"]", "time_sec": 23.525, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 20.69, "GAVIE-Rel": 7.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "182", "question": "psychological effects of hugging", "gt": "Based on the given passages, we can see that the physiological effects of hugging are numerous and significant. Hugs affect the entire body and can potentially be compared to the effects of multiple drugs operating at once. Furthermore, the act of hugging can also have psychological effects. Empathic listening can lead to emotional intimacy and happy relationships, as well as making conversation partners feel like they are receiving a hug, which can provide comfort and ease their existential fears. Overall, the psychological effects of hugging are positive and suggest that this simple gesture can have a significant impact on our emotional and physical wellbeing.", "pred": "Psychological hugs, as described in passage 2, are a result of empathy and listening skills (empathic listening), which can foster good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages. Additionally, a study mentioned in passage 3 found that hugs, even from inanimate objects like teddy bears, significantly reduce worry of mortality and soothe existential fears, indicating a positive psychological impact.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Psychological hugs, as described in passage 2, are a result of empathy and listening skills (empathic listening), which can foster good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages. Additionally, a study mentioned in passage 3 found that hugs, even from inanimate objects like teddy bears, significantly reduce worry of mortality and soothe existential fears, indicating a positive psychological impact.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Psychological hugs, as described in passage 2, are a result of empathy and listening skills (empathic listening), which can foster good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages. Additionally, a study mentioned in passage 3 found that hugs, even from inanimate objects like teddy bears, significantly reduce worry of mortality and soothe existential fears, indicating a positive psychological impact.\", \"Answer: Psychological hugs are achieved through the use of empathy and listening skills, also known as empathic listening. This can lead to good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages. Additionally, a conversation partner may feel like they are receiving a hug, providing a psychological benefit.\", \"Answer: Psychological hugs, which stem from empathy and listening skills (empathic listening), can lead to good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages. They also make conversation partners feel as though they are receiving a hug, providing emotional comfort and connection.\", \"Answer: Psychological hugs are a result of empathy and listening skills, specifically empathic listening, which can lead to good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages. Additionally, hugging, even with inanimate objects like teddy bears, significantly reduces worry of mortality and helps soothe individuals' existential fears, as shown in a study published in the journal Psychological Science.\", \"Answer: Psychological hugs, facilitated by empathy and listening skills (empathic listening), can lead to good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages. They make conversation partners feel like they are receiving a hug, providing emotional comfort and connection.\"]", "time_sec": 25.945, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 25.86, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "183", "question": "how to cook a small prime rib roast in oven", "gt": "Before cooking the prime rib roast, let it stand at room temperature for at least 1 hour. Preheat the oven to 375 degrees F. Season the roast with a small amount of salt and lots of fresh ground black pepper. Place the roast on a rack in a pan with the rib side down and the fatty side up. Roast it in the oven for 1 hour. After cooking, you can prepare au jus by placing the roasting pan on the stove over high heat. Add wine and cook for 8-10 minutes, stirring and scraping any brown bits. Add beef stock and cook to reduce the juice by half, which might take about 20 minutes. Add salt and pepper to taste.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\"]", "time_sec": 7.053, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "185", "question": "how long does it take to make chicken tender in crock pot", "gt": "To make chicken tender in a crock pot, you can cook it on low for 6 - 8 hours or until done. Alternatively, you can cook it on high for 1 hour, then on low for about 5 hours or until done. Another option is cooking it for 4 hours on high.", "pred": "It takes 6-8 hours on low or 4 hours on high to make chicken tender in a crock pot.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: It takes 6-8 hours on low or 4 hours on high to make chicken tender in a crock pot.", "cot": "[\"Answer: It takes 6-8 hours on low or 4 hours on high to make chicken tender in a crock pot.\", \"Answer: It takes 6 - 8 hours on low or 1 hour on high then 5 hours on low to make chicken tender in a crock pot.\", \"Answer: It takes 6-8 hours on low or 4 hours on high to make chicken tender in a crock pot.\", \"Answer: It takes 6 - 8 hours to make chicken tender in a crock pot on low, or 4 hours on high.\", \"Answer: It takes 6 - 8 hours to make chicken tender in a crock pot on low, or 1 hour on high followed by about 5 hours on low.\"]", "time_sec": 14.822, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 45.45, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "189", "question": "how long does it take to make chicken tender in crock pot", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the answer to the question \"How long does it take to make chicken tender in a crock pot?\" is:\n\n6-8 hours on low heat, or 4 hours on high heat if you're short on time.\n\nThe first passage suggests cooking the chicken on low for 6-8 hours, or 1 hour on high and then 5 hours on low. The second passage repeats the same information. The third passage provides additional information that chicken breast will always come out tender as long as there is enough moisture, and suggests cooking it for 4 hours on high if you're short on time.\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question is 6-8 hours on low heat, or 4 hours on high heat if you're short on time.", "pred": "It takes 6 - 8 hours on low or 1 hour on high then 5 hours on low to make chicken tender in a crock pot.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: It takes 6-8 hours on low or 4 hours on high to make chicken tender in a crock pot.", "cot": "[\"Answer: It takes 6-8 hours on low or 4 hours on high to make chicken tender in a crock pot.\", \"Answer: It takes 6 - 8 hours on low or 1 hour on high then 5 hours on low to make chicken tender in a crock pot.\", \"Answer: It takes 6 - 8 hours on low or 1 hour on high then 5 hours on low to make chicken tender in a crock pot.\", \"Answer: It takes 6-8 hours on low or 1 hour on high then 5 hours on low to make chicken tender in a crock pot.\", \"Answer: It takes 6-8 hours on low in a crock pot to make chicken tender. You may also cook it for 1 hour on high, then 5 hours on low, or 4 hours on high if short on time.\"]", "time_sec": 16.684, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 26.8, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "191", "question": "how to attach multiple picture frames together", "gt": "To attach multiple picture frames together, you can use small hinges to connect the frames. Lay the frames beside each other to determine the desired arrangement. For two frames, use identical ones, and for three frames, either choose three identical frames or use a larger one as the middle frame. Mark the hinge placement on the connecting sides of the frames, ensuring it is the same distance from the bottom edges. Attach the hinges to securely join the frames.", "pred": "To attach multiple picture frames together, use identical frames for joining two frames, and either three identical frames or one larger frame in the middle for joining three frames. Lay them beside each other to check the effect, then place two frames back to back and mark the hinge placement at the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame to ensure stability. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, use identical frames for joining two frames, and either three identical frames or one larger frame in the middle for joining three frames. Lay them beside each other to check the effect, then place two frames back to back and mark the hinge placement at the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame to ensure stability. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, use identical frames for joining two frames, and either three identical frames or one larger frame in the middle for joining three frames. Lay them beside each other to check the effect, then place two frames back to back and mark the hinge placement at the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame to ensure stability. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.\", \"Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, use identical frames for joining two together or select three frames of the same size or one larger frame for the middle when joining three. Lay the frames beside each other to check the effect, then place two frames back to back, mark the hinge placement ensuring it is the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame for secure standing, and attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, use identical frames for joining two frames and either three identical frames or one larger frame as the middle when joining three frames. Lay the frames beside each other to check the effect. Place two frames back to back, mark the hinge placement ensuring the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame for secure standing on a table top, and attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.\", \"Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, first select two or three identical wood molding frames. For joining two frames, use identical frames; for three, choose three of the same size or one larger frame as the middle. Lay them beside each other to check the effect. Place two frames back to back and mark the hinge placement, ensuring the distance from the bottom edges of each frame is the same to ensure secure standing on a table top. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames. This method is supported by passage 1 and passage 3, which both mention marking hinge placement based on equal distance from the bottom edges and attaching hinges to connect the frames. Passage 2 provides additional context on preparing multiple pictures but does not detail the attachment method. The instructions are specific to using hinges for joining frames back to back, with emphasis on consistent placement for stability.\"]", "time_sec": 35.957, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 72.9, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "193", "question": "how to attach multiple picture frames together", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are some steps for attaching multiple picture frames together:\n\nPassage 1: Table-Stand Grouping\nTo join two or three frames together, you can use identical frames or one larger frame as the middle piece. Place the frames beside each other to see if you like the effect, and then mark the hinge placement on the connecting sides. Attach two small hinges to the marked areas to join the frames together.\n\nPassage 2: Hanging Multiple Pictures Together\nBefore attaching the frames together, test different arrangements of the group of multiple pictures on the floor. Once you have decided on the layout, screw the frames to the backer board, repeating the process for each frame. Attach a hanging wire to the back of the frames and hang them on the wall to enjoy the finished result.\n\nPassage 3: Attaching Frames Together\nTo attach two frames together, place them back to back and mark the hinge placement on the connecting sides. The placement should be the same distance from the bottom edge of each frame to ensure stability when the grouped pictures are placed on a table. Attach two small hinges to the marked areas to join the frames together.\n\nIn conclusion, the recommended method for attaching multiple picture frames together involves using identical or matching frames, marking the hinge placement on the connecting sides, and attaching small hinges to join the frames together. It's also important to test different arrangements of the group of pictures on the floor before attaching the frames together, and to ensure that the frames are securely attached to the backer board before hanging them on the wall.", "pred": "To attach multiple picture frames together, use identical frames for joining two, or select frames of the same or different sizes for joining three, ensuring the frames are placed back to back with hinges attached to the connecting sides. The hinge placement must be the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame to ensure stability on the table top.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, use identical frames for joining two, or select frames of the same or different sizes for joining three, ensuring the frames are placed back to back with hinges attached to the connecting sides. The hinge placement must be the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame to ensure stability on the table top.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, use identical frames for joining two, or select frames of the same or different sizes for joining three, ensuring the frames are placed back to back with hinges attached to the connecting sides. The hinge placement must be the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame to ensure stability on the table top.\", \"Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, select two or three frames of the same wood molding. For joining two together, use identical frames. For joining three together, choose either three of the same or one which is larger to be the middle frame. Lay them beside each other to see if you like the effect. Put two frames together back to back, and mark the hinge placement. The placement must be the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame to ensure that the grouped pictures stand securely on the table top. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.\", \"Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, use identical frames for two frames or choose frames of the same size or one larger frame for the middle when joining three. Lay the frames beside each other to check the effect, then place two frames back to back and mark the hinge placement ensuring it is the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame for secure standing on a table top. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.\", \"Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, use identical frames for joining two, and either three identical frames or one larger frame in the middle for joining three. Lay the frames beside each other to check the effect, then place two frames back to back and mark hinge placement ensuring it is the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame for secure standing. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.\", \"Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, use identical frames for joining two together, and either three identical frames or one larger frame in the middle for joining three. Lay the frames beside each other to check the effect. Place two frames back to back, mark the hinge placement ensuring it is the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame for secure standing. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.\"]", "time_sec": 35.742, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 28.32, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "194", "question": "how to attach multiple picture frames together", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are the steps to attach multiple picture frames together:\n\n1. Select two or three frames of the same wood molding (Passage 1).\n2. Lay the frames beside each other to see if you like the effect (Passage 1).\n3. Mark the hinge placement on the connecting sides of the frames, making sure the placement is the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame (Passage 1).\n4. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames (Passage 1).\n5. Test ideas by laying the group of multiple pictures out on the floor first (Passage 2).\n6. Screw the frame to the backer board, repeat with each frame, and attach a hanging wire to the back (Passage 3).\n\nIt's important to note that the passages do not provide specific information on how to attach multiple frames together other than the steps mentioned above. Therefore, if more detailed instructions are needed, it may be best to consult additional resources or seek advice from a professional framer.", "pred": "To attach multiple picture frames together, select two or three frames of the same wood molding and join two frames using identical frames or three frames by choosing three of the same or one larger frame as the middle. Lay them beside each other to check the effect. Place two frames back to back and mark the hinge placement, ensuring the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame for secure standing. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, select two or three frames of the same wood molding and join two frames using identical frames or three frames by choosing three of the same or one larger frame as the middle. Lay them beside each other to check the effect. Place two frames back to back and mark the hinge placement, ensuring the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame for secure standing. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, select two or three frames of the same wood molding and join two frames using identical frames or three frames by choosing three of the same or one larger frame as the middle. Lay them beside each other to check the effect. Place two frames back to back and mark the hinge placement, ensuring the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame for secure standing. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.\", \"Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, use identical frames for joining two frames and either identical or one larger frame in the middle for joining three frames. Lay the frames beside each other to check the effect. Place two frames back to back and mark the hinge placement, ensuring the placement is the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame for secure standing on a table top. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.\", \"Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, use two or three identical wood molding frames. For joining two frames, place them back to back and mark the hinge placement at the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame to ensure stability. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames. This method is described in passage 1 and passage 3.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, use identical frames for joining two frames and choose either three identical frames or one larger frame as the middle when joining three frames. Lay the frames beside each other to check the effect, then place two frames back to back and mark the hinge placement, ensuring the placement is the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame for secure standing on a table top. Finally, attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.\"]", "time_sec": 29.656, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 43.33, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "195", "question": "how to attach multiple picture frames together", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's a brief answer to the question:\n\nTo attach multiple picture frames together, you can follow these steps:\n\n1. Select two or three frames of the same wood molding.\n2. Lay them beside each other to see if you like the effect.\n3. Put two frames together back to back, and mark the hinge placement. The placement must be the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame to ensure that the grouped pictures stand securely on the table top.\n4. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.\n5. Repeat the process for additional frames, ensuring that the hinge placement is consistent across all frames.\n6. Attach a hanging wire to the back of the grouped frames and hang them up.\n\nTherefore, the answer is: Select frames with the same wood molding, lay them together, mark hinge placement, attach hinges, and attach a hanging wire to the back.", "pred": "To attach multiple picture frames together, use identical frames for two frames or choose frames of the same size or one larger frame for the middle when joining three. Place the frames beside each other to check the effect, then put two frames together back to back and mark the hinge placement, ensuring the placement is the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame for secure standing on a table top. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, use identical frames for two frames or choose frames of the same size or one larger frame for the middle when joining three. Place the frames beside each other to check the effect, then put two frames together back to back and mark the hinge placement, ensuring the placement is the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame for secure standing on a table top. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, use identical frames for two frames or choose frames of the same size or one larger frame for the middle when joining three. Place the frames beside each other to check the effect, then put two frames together back to back and mark the hinge placement, ensuring the placement is the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame for secure standing on a table top. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, use identical frames for two frames and either identical frames or a larger middle frame for three frames. Place the frames beside each other to check the effect, then position two frames back to back and mark the hinge placement at the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame to ensure secure standing on a table top. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.\", \"Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, use identical frames for joining two together or select frames of the same size or one larger frame for the middle when joining three. Lay the frames beside each other to check the effect, then place two frames back to back and mark the hinge placement, ensuring the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame for secure standing. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.\", \"Answer: To attach multiple picture frames together, use two or three identical wood molding frames. For joining two frames, use identical frames and attach two small hinges to the connecting sides, ensuring the hinge placement is the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame for secure standing. For three frames, choose either three of the same size or one larger frame as the middle. Lay the frames beside each other to check the effect, then place two frames back to back, mark the hinge placement, and attach the hinges. Additionally, you can lay the group of pictures on the floor first to test the arrangement, then screw each frame to a backer board, attach a hanging wire, and hang the assembled frames.\"]", "time_sec": 33.624, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 51.5, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "197", "question": "leasing meaning", "gt": "Leasing refers to a process in which a firm obtains the use of fixed assets by making contractual, periodic, and tax-deductible payments to the owner of those assets. The lessee is the party receiving the services or assets under the lease contract, while the lessor is the owner of the assets.", "pred": "Leasing is a process by which a firm can obtain the use of a certain fixed asset for which it must pay a series of contractual, periodic, tax-deductible payments. The lessee is the receiver of the services or assets under the lease contract, and the lessor is the owner of the assets. A lease is a contractual arrangement where the lessee pays the lessor for the use of an asset, such as property, buildings, vehicles, or industrial/business equipment, in return for regular rental payments and agrees to abide by various conditions.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Leasing is a process by which a firm can obtain the use of a certain fixed asset for which it must pay a series of contractual, periodic, tax-deductible payments. The lessee is the receiver of the services or assets under the lease contract, and the lessor is the owner of the assets. A lease is a contractual arrangement where the lessee pays the lessor for the use of an asset, such as property, buildings, vehicles, or industrial/business equipment, in return for regular rental payments and agrees to abide by various conditions.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Leasing is a process by which a firm can obtain the use of a certain fixed asset for which it must pay a series of contractual, periodic, tax-deductible payments. The lessee is the receiver of the services or assets under the lease contract, and the lessor is the owner of the assets. A lease is a contractual arrangement where the lessee pays the lessor for the use of an asset, such as property, buildings, vehicles, or industrial/business equipment, in return for regular rental payments and agrees to abide by various conditions.\", \"Answer: Leasing is a process by which a firm can obtain the use of a certain fixed asset by paying a series of contractual, periodic, tax-deductible payments to the owner (lessor), with the recipient of the service or asset being the lessee, and the relationship between the tenant and landlord in property leasing being referred to as tenancy, which can be for a fixed or indefinite period, with the consideration for the lease being called rent.\", \"Answer: Leasing is a process by which a firm can obtain the use of a certain fixed asset for which it must pay a series of contractual, periodic, tax-deductible payments. The lessee is the receiver of the services or the assets under the lease contract, and the lessor is the owner of the assets. A lease is a contractual arrangement where the lessee pays the lessor for the use of an asset, such as property, buildings, vehicles, or industrial/business equipment, in return for regular rental payments, with the lessee agreeing to abide by various conditions.\", \"Leasing is a process by which a firm can obtain the use of certain fixed assets by paying a series of contractual, periodic, tax‑deductible payments. The firm that receives the asset is the lessee, while the asset’s owner is the lessor. The payment for the lease is called rent.\", \"Answer: Leasing is a process by which a firm obtains the use of fixed assets by paying a series of contractual, periodic, tax-deductible payments. The lessee is the recipient of the assets under the lease contract, and the lessor is the owner of the assets. The consideration for the lease is called rent, and the relationship between the tenant and landlord is termed a tenancy, which may be for a fixed or indefinite period. Leasing involves a contractual arrangement where the lessee agrees to pay the lessor for the use of assets such as property, buildings, vehicles, or industrial and business equipment.\"]", "time_sec": 40.834, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 48.65, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "199", "question": "what causes necrotizing fasciitis", "gt": "Necrotizing fasciitis is caused primarily by bacteria, specifically group A beta-hemolytic streptococci, also known as Streptococcus pyogenes. This is the same bacteria that causes strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria types, such as staphylococcus, are also associated with the disease. The bacteria infect the superficial fascia, a layer of connective tissue below the skin. Prior trauma, such as a cut or scrape, insect bite, burn, or needle puncture wound can serve as a source of infection as well as surgical incision sites and various surgical procedures. In rare cases, fungi can lead to this condition.", "pred": "Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, most commonly group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), which also cause strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. It can also be caused by other bacteria such as staphylococcus and fungi, though these are less common. The infection typically enters the body through breaks in the skin, such as cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or surgical incisions.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, most commonly group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), which also cause strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. It can also be caused by other bacteria such as staphylococcus and fungi, though these are less common. The infection typically enters the body through breaks in the skin, such as cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or surgical incisions.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, most commonly group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), which also cause strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. It can also be caused by other bacteria such as staphylococcus and fungi, though these are less common. The infection typically enters the body through breaks in the skin, such as cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or surgical incisions.\", \"Answer: Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, most commonly group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), which also cause strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria, such as staphylococcus, can also rarely cause the condition. Infections typically occur through prior trauma (e.g., cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, needle punctures, or surgical incisions), where bacteria enter the superficial fascia—a layer of connective tissue below the skin—and rapidly destroy skin, fat, and muscle tissue, sometimes leading to Fournier gangrene when affecting the genitals.\", \"Answer: Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, most commonly group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), which also cause strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria, such as staphylococcus, can also rarely cause the condition. Infections typically occur through breaks in the skin, including minor trauma like cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or needle punctures, as well as surgical incision sites or procedures.\", \"Answer: Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, most commonly group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), which also causes strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria, such as staphylococcus, can also be associated with the disease. Infections typically occur when bacteria enter through breaks in the skin, such as cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or surgical wounds.\", \"Answer: Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, most commonly group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), which is the same bacteria responsible for strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria, such as staphylococcus, can also cause the disease. Infections typically occur through breaks in the skin, such as cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or surgical wounds, where bacteria enter the superficial fascia, a layer of connective tissue beneath the skin.\"]", "time_sec": 40.996, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 47.17, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "203", "question": "what causes necrotizing fasciitis", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the main cause of necrotizing fasciitis is bacterial infection, specifically group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes) and Staphylococcus. Additionally, other types of bacteria have also been associated with the disease. Trauma, such as cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or needle puncture wounds, can also play a role in the development of necrotizing fasciitis. Surgical incision sites and various surgical procedures can also serve as a source of infection. Therefore, the answer is: Bacterial infection, including group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes) and Staphylococcus, and trauma.", "pred": "Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, most commonly group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), which also cause strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria, such as staphylococcus, can also be associated with the disease. Infections typically enter through minor skin breaks like cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or surgical sites.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, most commonly group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), which also cause strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria, such as staphylococcus, can also be associated with the disease. Infections typically enter through minor skin breaks like cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or surgical sites.", "cot": "[\"Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, most commonly group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), which also cause strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria, such as staphylococcus, can also be associated with the disease. Infections typically enter through minor skin breaks like cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or surgical sites.\", \"Answer: Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, most commonly group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), which also cause strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria, including staphylococcus, can also be responsible. Infections typically occur through breaks in the skin such as cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or surgical incisions.\", \"Answer: Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, most commonly group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), which also cause strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. It can also be caused by other bacteria such as staphylococcus, and rarely by fungi. Infections typically enter through breaks in the skin, including minor trauma like cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or surgical wounds.\", \"Answer: Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, most commonly group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), which also cause strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria, such as staphylococcus, can also be associated with the disease. Infections typically occur when bacteria enter through breaks in the skin, such as cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, needle punctures, or surgical incisions.\", \"Answer: Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, most commonly group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), which also cause strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria, including staphylococcus and various types, have also been associated with the condition. Infections typically occur when bacteria enter the body through breaks in the skin, such as cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or surgical wounds.\"]", "time_sec": 36.036, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 44.23, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "204", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "gt": "Chard, also known as Swiss chard, is a vegetable with large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It has a flavor that is stronger and more assertive than spinach, and some people find it bitter. Chard can be eaten raw in salads when it's young, but mature leaves and stalks are usually cooked or sautéed. Chard is actually a type of beet that is grown for its leaves rather than its root. It can produce white, yellow, or red leaf stalks. Chard is highly perishable and should be kept in the refrigerator for only 2 to 3 days.", "pred": "Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, and is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavour. Chard can be used raw in salads or cooked, and its bitterness fades with cooking, leaving a refined flavor more delicate than that of cooked spinach. It is cultivated descendants of the sea beet, Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima, and can be harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when larger have slightly tougher stems. Chard is extremely perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, and is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavour. Chard can be used raw in salads or cooked, and its bitterness fades with cooking, leaving a refined flavor more delicate than that of cooked spinach. It is cultivated descendants of the sea beet, Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima, and can be harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when larger have slightly tougher stems. Chard is extremely perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, and is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavour. Chard can be used raw in salads or cooked, and its bitterness fades with cooking, leaving a refined flavor more delicate than that of cooked spinach. It is cultivated descendants of the sea beet, Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima, and can be harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when larger have slightly tougher stems. Chard is extremely perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.\", \"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been selected for leaf production rather than root storage, featuring large, fleshy deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks that can be white, yellow, or red. It is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor, and while young chard can be eaten raw, mature chard is typically cooked to reduce its bitterness.\", \"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It is cultivated for its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be used raw in salads when young or cooked when mature. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor, and it is extremely perishable, requiring storage in a crisper for 2 to 3 days. It can have white, yellow, or red leaf stalks and is an attractive ornamental plant in gardens.\", \"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavour. It can be used raw in salads or cooked, and its bitterness fades with cooking.\", \"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It is cultivated for its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be used raw in salads when young or cooked when mature. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor, and it comes in varieties with white, yellow, or red leaf stalks.\"]", "time_sec": 42.345, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 51.66, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "205", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "gt": "Chard is a vegetable that has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked or sautéed. Chard is in the same subspecies as beetroot and is cultivated for leaf production. It can be harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when the stems are slightly tougher. Chard is perishable and should be stored in the refrigerator for a minimum amount of time.", "pred": "Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It is a leafy vegetable with large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be harvested when young and tender or after maturity. Chard is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive flavor, and its bitterness fades with cooking. It can be used raw in salads or cooked in dishes like pizzoccheri or sautéed.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It is a leafy vegetable with large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be harvested when young and tender or after maturity. Chard is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive flavor, and its bitterness fades with cooking. It can be used raw in salads or cooked in dishes like pizzoccheri or sautéed.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It is a leafy vegetable with large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be harvested when young and tender or after maturity. Chard is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive flavor, and its bitterness fades with cooking. It can be used raw in salads or cooked in dishes like pizzoccheri or sautéed.\", \"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, and is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavour. Chard can be used raw in salads or cooked, and its bitterness fades with cooking, leaving a refined flavor more delicate than that of cooked spinach. It is cultivated descendants of the sea beet, Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima, and can be harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when larger have slightly tougher stems. Chard is also an attractive ornamental that adds color to the vegetable garden.\", \"Answer: Chard is a beet cultivar selected for leaf production rather than root storage, featuring large, fleshy deep green leaves and thick crisp stalks that can be white, yellow, or red. It is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (sometimes bitter) flavor, and its bitterness fades when cooked.\", \"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation, with fresh white, yellow, or red leaf stalks, and is cultivated as a vegetable for its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be used raw in salads or cooked like spinach, with a stronger, more assertive flavor than spinach.\", \"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation, with fresh white, yellow or red leaf stalks, and is cultivated for its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks.\"]", "time_sec": 37.1, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 63.72, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "206", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "gt": "Based on the given passages, Swiss chard (also known as just plain chard) is a type of beet plant that has been selected for its leaf production rather than storage root formation. It produces large, tender deep green leaves with thick, crisp stalks. The leaves have a more assertive and sometimes bitter taste compared to spinach. Swiss chard is a perishable vegetable and should be stored in the refrigerator. It can be used raw in salads or cooked like in pizzoccheri.", "pred": "Chard is a leafy vegetable that is a type of beet, cultivated for its large, fleshy, tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor, and can be used raw in salads or cooked, with its bitterness fading upon cooking. Chard is also an ornamental vegetable that comes in various colors, including white, yellow, or red leaf stalks.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Chard is a leafy vegetable that is a type of beet, cultivated for its large, fleshy, tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor, and can be used raw in salads or cooked, with its bitterness fading upon cooking. Chard is also an ornamental vegetable that comes in various colors, including white, yellow, or red leaf stalks.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Chard is a leafy vegetable that is a type of beet, cultivated for its large, fleshy, tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor, and can be used raw in salads or cooked, with its bitterness fading upon cooking. Chard is also an ornamental vegetable that comes in various colors, including white, yellow, or red leaf stalks.\", \"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been selected for leaf production instead of storage root formation. It is cultivated as a vegetable with large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, and is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive (sometimes bitter) flavor.\", \"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be white, yellow, or red. Chard is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. It can be used raw in salads when young or cooked when mature, and it is cultivated as a descendant of the sea beet, Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima.\", \"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation, with fresh white, yellow, or red leaf stalks, and is cultivated as a vegetable for its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks.\", \"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been selected for leaf production, resulting in large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor. Chard can be used raw in salads when young or cooked, such as in pizzoccheri or sautéed, to reduce its bitterness. It comes in varieties with white, yellow, or red leaf stalks and is cultivated from the sea beet, Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima, as a descendant of beetroot and other beet cultivars.\"]", "time_sec": 35.846, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 50.49, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "207", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is what can be inferred about the vegetable \"chard\":\n\n* Chard is a type of beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima) that has been chosen for leaf production over storage root formation.\n* Chard has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks.\n* The flavor of chard is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive taste (some consider it bitter).\n* Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked to reduce bitterness and bring out a refined flavor.\n* Chard is an attractive ornamental plant that adds color to the vegetable garden.\n* Chard is perishable and should be stored in the refrigerator to minimize spoilage.\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question \"What is chard vegetable?\" is: A beet variety (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima) grown for leaf production, characterized by large, fleshy leaves and stalks with a strong, bitter flavor that becomes more refined with cooking.", "pred": "Chard is a leafy vegetable, also known as Swiss chard or just plain chard, characterized by large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive flavor that some find bitter. Chard is cultivated for its leaves, which can be harvested when young and tender or after maturity when the stems are tougher. It is related to beetroot and all other beet cultivars, being a descendant of the sea beet, Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima. Chard is extremely perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days to maintain freshness.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Chard is a leafy vegetable, also known as Swiss chard or just plain chard, characterized by large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive flavor that some find bitter. Chard is cultivated for its leaves, which can be harvested when young and tender or after maturity when the stems are tougher. It is related to beetroot and all other beet cultivars, being a descendant of the sea beet, Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima. Chard is extremely perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days to maintain freshness.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Chard is a leafy vegetable, also known as Swiss chard or just plain chard, characterized by large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive flavor that some find bitter. Chard is cultivated for its leaves, which can be harvested when young and tender or after maturity when the stems are tougher. It is related to beetroot and all other beet cultivars, being a descendant of the sea beet, Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima. Chard is extremely perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days to maintain freshness.\", \"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, and can have white, yellow, or red leaf stalks. It is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive flavor, and can be used raw in salads or cooked.\", \"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It is cultivated for its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be used raw in salads when young or cooked (like in pizzoccheri) or sautéed when mature. Chard is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive flavor, and it comes in varieties with white, yellow, or red leaf stalks.\", \"Answer: Chard is a beet cultivar that has been selected for leaf production rather than storage root formation. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks that can be white, yellow, or red. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (sometimes bitter) flavor. It can be used raw in salads when young or cooked (e.g., sautéed or in dishes like pizzoccheri) when mature, and its bitterness fades with cooking. Chard is extremely perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the refrigerator for 2 to 3 days.\", \"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation, and it can be harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when larger have slightly tougher stems.\"]", "time_sec": 40.539, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 41.99, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "209", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "gt": "Based on the given passages, chard is a type of vegetable that belongs to the same subspecies as beetroot and is characterized by its large, fleshy leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It has a stronger, more assertive flavor than spinach and can be used raw in salads or cooked like in pizzoccheri. Chard is a beet that has been selected for leaf production rather than storage root formation and can be harvested when young and tender or after maturity. It is an attractive ornamental that adds color to the vegetable garden, but it is extremely perishable and should be stored in the refrigerator for a maximum of 2-3 days.", "pred": "Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It is related to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavour. Chard has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be used raw in salads or cooked. It is an attractive ornamental that adds color to the vegetable garden and is cultivated descendants of the sea beet, Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It is related to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavour. Chard has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be used raw in salads or cooked. It is an attractive ornamental that adds color to the vegetable garden and is cultivated descendants of the sea beet, Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It is related to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavour. Chard has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be used raw in salads or cooked. It is an attractive ornamental that adds color to the vegetable garden and is cultivated descendants of the sea beet, Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima.\", \"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It is cultivated for its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be used raw in salads when young or cooked (such as in pizzoccheri or sautéed) when mature. It is related to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor, and is also known as Swiss chard or plain chard.\", \"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation, and it is cultivated for its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be used raw in salads or cooked like spinach, with a stronger, more assertive flavor than spinach.\", \"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation, with fresh white, yellow or red leaf stalks, and is cultivated for its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be used raw in salads or cooked like spinach, with a stronger, more assertive flavor than spinach.\", \"Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation, with fresh white, yellow or red leaf stalks, large fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick crisp stalks, and is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavour.\"]", "time_sec": 34.114, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 57.63, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "210", "question": "differences between elements compounds and mixtures", "gt": "Elements are the purest form of a chemical substance and are composed of only one type of atom, meaning all the atoms in an element have the same number of protons. Compounds, on the other hand, are substances composed of two or more different elements and are also pure substances. Mixtures are physical combinations of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and so retain their own identity. The individual components of a mixture can be physically separated from each other. Mixtures have variable compositions and show the properties of their constituent substances. Elements and compounds just mix together to form a mixture and no new compound is formed.", "pred": "Elements are pure substances composed of only one type of atom. Compounds are pure substances made of two or more different elements chemically bonded together. Mixtures are physical combinations of elements or compounds where the parts retain their own identity and can be separated by physical means, with variable composition and properties of constituent substances.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Elements are pure substances composed of only one type of atom. Compounds are pure substances made of two or more different elements chemically bonded together. Mixtures are physical combinations of elements or compounds where the parts retain their own identity and can be separated by physical means, with variable composition and properties of constituent substances.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Elements are pure substances composed of only one type of atom. Compounds are pure substances made of two or more different elements chemically bonded together. Mixtures are physical combinations of elements or compounds where the parts retain their own identity and can be separated by physical means, with variable composition and properties of constituent substances.\", \"Answer: Elements are pure substances composed of one type of atom. Compounds are pure substances made of two or more different elements chemically bonded together. Mixtures are physical combinations of elements or compounds where the parts retain their identity and can be separated by physical means, with variable composition.\", \"Answer: Elements are pure substances composed of only one type of atom, meaning all atoms have the same number of protons. Compounds are pure substances made of two or more different elements chemically bonded together. Mixtures are physical combinations of elements or compounds where the parts are not chemically bonded, retain their own identity, and can be separated by physical methods; mixtures have variable composition and show the properties of their constituent substances.\", \"Answer: Elements are pure substances made of only one type of atom. Compounds are pure substances made of two or more different elements chemically bonded together. Mixtures are physical combinations of elements or compounds where the components are not chemically bonded and can be separated by physical means.\", \"Answer: Elements are pure substances composed of only one type of atom. Compounds are pure substances made of two or more different elements chemically bonded together. Mixtures are physical combinations of elements or compounds where the components are not chemically bonded and can be separated by physical means, with variable composition and properties of the constituent substances.\"]", "time_sec": 26.427, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 61.4, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
