iently with the same amount of work, increasing energy levels by making cell walls more permeable, helping absorb more nutrients (vitamins and minerals) and enhancing the utilization of co-enzyme Q10, relieving muscular aches and pains, and reducing inflammation due to injury or inflammatory diseases such as arthritis.\", \"Answer: MSM powder benefits the body by helping get oxygen into the blood more efficiently, increasing energy, making cell walls more permeable, improving nutrient absorption (especially vitamins and minerals when combined with co-enzyme Q10), relieving muscular aches and pains, and reducing inflammation from injury or conditions like arthritis.\", \"Answer: MSM powder benefits the body by helping get oxygen into the blood more efficiently, increasing energy, making cell walls more permeable, helping absorb more nutrients (vitamins and minerals), requiring MSM (sulfur) for Co-enzyme Q10 utilization, relieving muscular aches and pains, and reducing inflammation due to injury or inflammatory diseases such as arthritis.\", \"Answer: MSM powder benefits the body by helping get oxygen into the blood more efficiently with the same amount of work, increasing energy levels by making cell walls more permeable, helping absorb more nutrients (vitamins and minerals) especially when combined with co-enzyme Q10, relieving muscular aches and pains, and reducing inflammation due to injury or inflammatory diseases such as arthritis.\"]", "time_sec": 30.046, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 30.61, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "265", "question": "the benefits of msm powder", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the benefits of MSM powder include:\n\n* Helping to increase oxygen efficiency in the blood\n* Increasing energy levels by making cell walls more permeable\n* Enhancing nutrient absorption, particularly when taken with Co-enzyme Q10\n* Relieving muscular aches and pains\n* Reducing inflammation due to injury or inflammatory diseases such as arthritis\n\nTherefore, MSM powder has several benefits for overall health and wellness, including improved oxygenation, energy, and reduction of pain and inflammation.", "pred": "MSM powder benefits the body by helping get oxygen into the blood more efficiently, increasing energy, making cell walls more permeable, aiding nutrient absorption (vitamins and minerals), enhancing co-enzyme Q10 utilization, relieving muscular aches and pains, and reducing inflammation from injury or conditions like arthritis.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: MSM powder benefits the body by helping get oxygen into the blood more efficiently, increasing energy, making cell walls more permeable, aiding nutrient absorption (vitamins and minerals), enhancing co-enzyme Q10 utilization, relieving muscular aches and pains, and reducing inflammation from injury or conditions like arthritis.", "cot": "[\"Answer: MSM powder benefits the body by helping get oxygen into the blood more efficiently, increasing energy, making cell walls more permeable, aiding nutrient absorption (vitamins and minerals), enhancing co-enzyme Q10 utilization, relieving muscular aches and pains, and reducing inflammation from injury or conditions like arthritis.\", \"Answer: MSM powder benefits the body by helping get oxygen into the blood more efficiently with the same amount of work, increasing energy levels, making cell walls more permeable, helping absorb more nutrients (vitamins and minerals), requiring MSM (sulfur) for co-enzyme Q10 to be fully utilized, relieving muscular aches and pains, and reducing inflammation due to injury or inflammatory diseases such as arthritis.\", \"Answer: The benefits of MSM powder include: helping oxygen enter the blood more efficiently with the same amount of work, increasing energy levels by making cell walls more permeable, helping absorb more nutrients (vitamins and minerals) and enabling co-enzyme Q10 to be fully utilized by the body, relieving muscular aches and pains, and reducing inflammation due to injury or inflammatory diseases such as arthritis.\", \"Answer: MSM powder benefits the body by helping get oxygen into the blood more efficiently with the same amount of work, increasing energy levels, making cell walls more permeable, helping absorb more nutrients (vitamins and minerals), requiring MSM (sulfur) for the body to fully utilize co-enzyme Q10, relieving muscular aches and pains, and reducing inflammation due to injury or inflammatory diseases such as arthritis.\", \"Answer: MSM powder benefits the body by helping get oxygen into the blood more efficiently, increasing energy levels, improving nutrient absorption (especially vitamins and minerals), enhancing the utilization of co-enzyme Q10, relieving muscular aches and pains, and reducing inflammation from injury or conditions like arthritis.\"]", "time_sec": 30.899, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 55.91, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "266", "question": "introduction to visual merchandising and relation to the consumer", "gt": "Visual merchandising is a strategy used by retailers to attract customers to their store and influence their buying behavior. It involves the presentation of the store and its merchandise in a way that aligns with the retailer's goals and marketing strategy. The visual merchandiser's role is crucial as they not only help bring customers to the store but also influence their purchasing decisions, akin to \"making the horse drink\" as per G. Surrendar's analogy. Particularly, visual merchandising related to shop windows has been found to significantly impact consumer buying behavior, with variations observed based on gender and the store attributes most valued by consumers in shopping centers.", "pred": "Visual merchandising connects a retailer's strategy and goals to the store environment, directly influencing consumer behavior. Earlier retailers relied solely on advertising to attract customers, while modern visual merchandising actively guides purchasing decisions—evidenced by the study's focus on how shop window displays affect buying behavior based on consumer sex and store attributes. The passage states visual merchandising \"makes the horse drink\" (i.e., drives consumer action), contrasting with marketing's role in merely bringing customers to the store.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Visual merchandising connects a retailer's strategy and goals to the store environment, directly influencing consumer behavior. Earlier retailers relied solely on advertising to attract customers, while modern visual merchandising actively guides purchasing decisions—evidenced by the study's focus on how shop window displays affect buying behavior based on consumer sex and store attributes. The passage states visual merchandising \"makes the horse drink\" (i.e., drives consumer action), contrasting with marketing's role in merely bringing customers to the store.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Visual merchandising connects a retailer's strategy and goals to the store environment, directly influencing consumer behavior. Earlier retailers relied solely on advertising to attract customers, while modern visual merchandising actively guides purchasing decisions—evidenced by the study's focus on how shop window displays affect buying behavior based on consumer sex and store attributes. The passage states visual merchandising \\\"makes the horse drink\\\" (i.e., drives consumer action), contrasting with marketing's role in merely bringing customers to the store.\", \"Answer: Visual merchandising is a strategic practice where retailers use store layout, displays, and presentation to communicate the retailer’s strategy and goals to consumers, thereby influencing their buying behavior. As stated in the introduction, earlier retailers relied solely on advertising to attract customers, but visual merchandising now serves as a critical tool to \\\"make the horse drink\\\" — meaning it actively engages consumers at the point of purchase, aligning store attributes with consumer preferences to enhance the likelihood of purchase.\", \"Answer: Visual merchandising connects a retailer's strategy and goals to the store environment, directly influencing consumer buying behaviour. Earlier retailers relied solely on advertising to attract customers, while modern visual merchandising—particularly shop window displays—shapes consumer decisions by aligning with their sex and valued store attributes, as stated in the paper's objective to study its influence on consumer behaviour.\", \"Answer: Visual merchandising connects a retailer’s strategy and goals with consumer behavior, using store presentation to influence buying decisions. It goes beyond traditional advertising by making the store itself attractive, encouraging customers to “drink” (purchase) as described by G. Surrendar. The paper specifically examines how shop‑window visual merchandising affects consumer buying behavior, considering differences by sex and valued store attributes.\", \"Answer: Visual merchandising connects the retailer’s strategy and goals to the store environment, enabling it to influence consumer buying behaviour. While earlier retailers relied solely on advertising to attract customers, visual merchandising actively guides consumer decisions by presenting products in ways that align with shopper preferences and store attributes.\"]", "time_sec": 32.522, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 39.69, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "271", "question": "earliest signs and symptoms of pregnancy", "gt": "The earliest signs of pregnancy often include mood swings and unusual emotional behavior due to a flood of hormones in the body. Dizziness can also occur as pregnancy causes blood vessels to dilate and blood pressure to drop. Other early symptoms can include a missed period, bloating, spotting, and cramping. Approximately half of all pregnant women experience nausea and vomiting, and around 3 in 10 women experience nausea without vomiting, with these symptoms typically starting around six weeks after their last period.", "pred": "The earliest signs and symptoms of pregnancy include mood swings, dizziness, bloating, spotting, and cramping. Around half of all pregnant women experience nausea and vomiting, and around 3 in 10 women experience nausea without vomiting, with symptoms typically starting around six weeks after the last period.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The earliest signs and symptoms of pregnancy include mood swings, dizziness, bloating, spotting, and cramping. Around half of all pregnant women experience nausea and vomiting, and around 3 in 10 women experience nausea without vomiting, with symptoms typically starting around six weeks after the last period.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The earliest signs and symptoms of pregnancy include mood swings, dizziness, bloating, spotting, and cramping. Around half of all pregnant women experience nausea and vomiting, and around 3 in 10 women experience nausea without vomiting, with symptoms typically starting around six weeks after the last period.\", \"Answer: The earliest signs and symptoms of pregnancy mentioned in the passages include a missed period, bloating, spotting, cramping, mood swings, and dizziness. Passage 2 states that \\\"Many women know that a missed period may indicate pregnancy, but so can bloating, spotting, and cramping.\\\" Passage 1 describes \\\"Mood swings\\\" and \\\"Dizziness\\\" as common early pregnancy symptoms. Passage 3 mentions nausea and vomiting, noting that \\\"Around half of all pregnant women experience nausea and vomiting, and around 3 in 10 women experience nausea without vomiting,\\\" with symptoms typically starting \\\"around six weeks after their last period.\\\" These details collectively identify the earliest signs and symptoms referenced in the provided text.\\n\\nAnswer: The earliest signs and symptoms of pregnancy include a missed period, bloating, spotting, cramping, mood swings, dizziness, and nausea (with or without vomiting).\", \"Answer: The earliest signs and symptoms of pregnancy mentioned in the passages include a missed period, bloating, spotting, cramping, mood swings, and dizziness. Passage 2 states that many women know a missed period may indicate pregnancy, but also notes that bloating, spotting, and cramping can be early signs. Passage 1 describes mood swings as common due to hormonal changes in early pregnancy and dizziness as a result of lowered blood pressure from dilated blood vessels. Passage 3 adds that nausea and vomiting (morning sickness) affect around half of pregnant women, typically starting around six weeks after the last period, though this is presented as a common symptom rather than one of the earliest signs.\", \"Answer: The earliest signs of pregnancy mentioned in the passages include a missed period, bloating, spotting, cramping, mood swings, and dizziness. Passage 2 states that many women know a missed period may indicate pregnancy, but also notes that bloating, spotting, and cramping can be early signs. Passage 1 describes mood swings as common due to hormonal changes in early pregnancy, and dizziness resulting from lowered blood pressure. Passage 3 mentions nausea and vomiting (including morning sickness) as common early symptoms, typically starting around six weeks after the last period.\", \"Answer: The earliest signs and symptoms of pregnancy include a missed period, bloating, spotting, cramping, mood swings, and dizziness.\"]", "time_sec": 42.497, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 71.43, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "272", "question": "earliest signs and symptoms of pregnancy", "gt": "The earliest signs and symptoms of pregnancy include mood swings, dizziness, bloating, spotting, cramping, nausea, and vomiting.", "pred": "The earliest signs and symptoms of pregnancy include mood swings, dizziness, bloating, spotting, and cramping, as mentioned in the provided passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The earliest signs and symptoms of pregnancy include mood swings, dizziness, bloating, spotting, and cramping, as mentioned in the provided passages.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The earliest signs and symptoms of pregnancy include mood swings, dizziness, bloating, spotting, and cramping, as mentioned in the provided passages.\", \"Answer: The earliest signs and symptoms of pregnancy include mood swings, dizziness, bloating, spotting, cramping, and nausea (with or without vomiting), which typically begin around six weeks after the last period.\", \"Answer: The earliest signs of pregnancy include a missed period, bloating, spotting, cramping, mood swings, and dizziness. Mood swings are caused by hormonal changes in early pregnancy, and dizziness results from lowered blood pressure due to blood vessel dilation. Nausea and vomiting (morning sickness) also occur early, affecting around half of pregnant women, with symptoms typically starting around six weeks after the last period.\", \"Answer: The earliest signs of pregnancy include a missed period, bloating, spotting, cramping, mood swings, and dizziness. Around half of all pregnant women experience nausea and vomiting, and around 3 in 10 women experience nausea without vomiting, with symptoms typically starting around six weeks after the last period.\", \"Answer: The earliest signs and symptoms of pregnancy include mood swings, dizziness, bloating, spotting, cramping, and nausea (with or without vomiting). Mood swings are linked to hormonal changes in early pregnancy, while dizziness results from blood vessel dilation and lowered blood pressure. Bloating, spotting, and cramping are also recognized as early indicators, and nausea affects approximately half of pregnant women, typically starting around six weeks after the last period.\"]", "time_sec": 26.922, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 81.48, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "276", "question": "what is severe osteoporosis", "gt": "Severe osteoporosis is a condition characterized by significant bone density loss, resulting in weakened bones and increased risk of fractures. Symptoms may include sudden and severe back pain, loss of height over time, fractures of the vertebrae, wrists, hips, or other bones. Without treatment, fractures, particularly in the spine or hips, are likely to occur.", "pred": "Severe osteoporosis is a condition where bone density loss has progressed to the point that it causes symptoms such as back pain, loss of height with a stooped posture, and fractures of the vertebrae, wrists, hips, or other bones, often without significant trauma like a fall or injury. It is characterized by the bones becoming so weak that they compress, leading to spinal compression fractures, which are a sign of weak bones and can indicate the presence of severe osteoporosis.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Severe osteoporosis is a condition where bone density loss has progressed to the point that it causes symptoms such as back pain, loss of height with a stooped posture, and fractures of the vertebrae, wrists, hips, or other bones, often without significant trauma like a fall or injury. It is characterized by the bones becoming so weak that they compress, leading to spinal compression fractures, which are a sign of weak bones and can indicate the presence of severe osteoporosis.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Severe osteoporosis is a condition where bone density loss has progressed to the point that it causes symptoms such as back pain, loss of height with a stooped posture, and fractures of the vertebrae, wrists, hips, or other bones, often without significant trauma like a fall or injury. It is characterized by the bones becoming so weak that they compress, leading to spinal compression fractures, which are a sign of weak bones and can indicate the presence of severe osteoporosis.\", \"Answer: Severe osteoporosis is a condition characterized by significant bone density loss, leading to fragile bones that are prone to fractures, particularly in the spine, hips, and wrists, often without any prior injury or fall.\", \"Answer: Severe osteoporosis is a condition where bone density loss has progressed to the point that bones become extremely weak, leading to symptoms such as back pain, loss of height over time with a stooped posture, and increased risk of fractures—including spinal compression fractures that can occur without trauma, as well as fractures in the wrists, hips, or other bones.\", \"Answer: Severe osteoporosis is a condition characterized by significant bone density loss, leading to weakened bones that are prone to fractures, particularly in the spine, hips, and wrists, often without any obvious injury or fall.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\"]", "time_sec": 22.145, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 52.27, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "278", "question": "what is severe osteoporosis", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, severe osteoporosis refers to a condition where the bones have been significantly weakened to the point of causing symptoms such as back pain, loss of height, and fractures. According to passage 3, osteoporosis typically has no symptoms until significant bone damage has occurred, making it a \"silent disease\" until it reaches this advanced stage. Passage 2 also highlights that compression fractures of the spine are a common cause of osteoporosis pain, which can be severe and debilitating. Therefore, severe osteoporosis is characterized by a high risk of fractures, particularly in the spine and hips, as well as persistent back pain and loss of height.", "pred": "Severe osteoporosis is a condition where bone density loss has progressed to the point that it causes noticeable symptoms such as back pain, loss of height over time with a stooped posture, and fractures of the vertebrae, wrists, hips, or other bones, often without significant trauma or injury.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Severe osteoporosis is a condition where bone density loss has progressed to the point that it causes noticeable symptoms such as back pain, loss of height over time with a stooped posture, and fractures of the vertebrae, wrists, hips, or other bones, often without significant trauma or injury.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Severe osteoporosis is a condition where bone density loss has progressed to the point that it causes noticeable symptoms such as back pain, loss of height over time with a stooped posture, and fractures of the vertebrae, wrists, hips, or other bones, often without significant trauma or injury.\", \"Answer: Severe osteoporosis is a condition where bone density loss has progressed to the point that it causes significant symptoms, including back pain, loss of height due to stooped posture, and fractures of the vertebrae, hips, wrists, or other bones, often occurring without trauma or injury.\", \"Answer: Severe osteoporosis is a condition where bone density loss has progressed to the point that bones become extremely weak, leading to symptoms such as sudden severe back pain (especially in older women), loss of height over time with stooped posture, and increased risk of fractures (including compression fractures of the spine, wrist fractures from bracing against falls, and hip fractures) even without injury or falls. The passages indicate that osteoporosis is \\\"a condition that has virtually no symptoms until severe bone damage has already occurred,\\\" and once severe bone damage occurs, symptoms include back pain, height loss, and fractures of vertebrae or other bones, with spinal fractures occurring \\\"even without a fall or an injury\\\" due to weakened bones.\", \"Answer: Severe osteoporosis is a condition where bone density loss has progressed to the point that it causes noticeable symptoms such as back pain, loss of height over time with a stooped posture, and fractures of the vertebrae, wrists, hips, or other bones, often occurring without trauma.\", \"Answer: Severe osteoporosis is a condition where bone density loss has progressed to the point that bones become extremely weak and fragile, leading to symptoms such as sudden, severe back pain (often from compression fractures of the spine without trauma), loss of height over time with a stooped posture, and increased risk of fractures in the spine, hips, wrists, or other bones, even from minor injuries or without any apparent fall.\"]", "time_sec": 31.653, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 32.38, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "279", "question": "what is severe osteoporosis", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, severe osteoporosis refers to a condition where there is significant bone density loss, leading to symptoms such as sudden and severe back pain, loss of height, and an increased risk of fractures, particularly in the spine, hips, and wrists.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Severe osteoporosis is a condition where bone density loss has progressed to the point that it causes noticeable symptoms such as back pain, loss of height with stooped posture, and fractures of the vertebrae, wrists, hips, or other bones, often without significant trauma like a fall or injury. It is characterized by weakened bones that can lead to sudden, severe back pain (e.g., from compression fractures) and increased risk of fractures, particularly in the spine, hips, and wrists.\", \"Answer: Severe osteoporosis is a condition characterized by significant bone density loss, leading to weakened bones that are prone to fractures, particularly in the spine, hips, and wrists. It may present with symptoms such as sudden, severe back pain (often indicating a spinal compression fracture), loss of height over time with a stooped posture, and increased risk of fractures even without a fall or injury.\", \"Answer: Severe osteoporosis is a condition characterized by significant bone density loss, leading to fragile bones that can result in symptoms such as back pain, loss of height over time with a stooped posture, and an increased risk of fractures, particularly in the spine, hips, and wrists. These fractures may occur without a fall or injury, indicating the severity of bone weakening.\", \"Answer: Severe osteoporosis is a condition characterized by significant bone density loss, leading to fragile bones that are prone to fractures, particularly in the spine, hips, and wrists, often without any apparent injury. It can cause symptoms such as sudden severe back pain, loss of height over time with a stooped posture, and an increased risk of fractures even from minor falls or everyday activities. The condition is often silent until severe bone damage has occurred, emphasizing the importance of early detection and treatment to prevent complications.\"]", "time_sec": 29.088, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 12.5, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "280", "question": "what is severe osteoporosis", "gt": "Based on the given passages, severe osteoporosis refers to a advanced stage of the disease where the bones have weakened significantly, leading to severe bone loss and increased risk of fractures. At this stage, symptoms such as back pain, loss of height, and fractures are more pronounced and frequent. The risk of fractures is particularly high in the spine, hips, and wrists, and can occur even without a fall or injury.\n\nIn summary, severe osteoporosis is characterized by significant bone weakening, heightened risk of fractures, and more pronounced symptoms.", "pred": "Severe osteoporosis is a condition characterized by significant bone density loss that leads to symptoms such as back pain, loss of height over time with a stooped posture, and an increased risk of fractures, particularly in the spine, hips, and wrists. These fractures can occur without a fall or injury due to the extreme weakness of the bones. The condition is often silent until severe bone damage has occurred, as indicated by the passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Severe osteoporosis is a condition characterized by significant bone density loss that leads to symptoms such as back pain, loss of height over time with a stooped posture, and an increased risk of fractures, particularly in the spine, hips, and wrists. These fractures can occur without a fall or injury due to the extreme weakness of the bones. The condition is often silent until severe bone damage has occurred, as indicated by the passages.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Severe osteoporosis is a condition characterized by significant bone density loss that leads to symptoms such as back pain, loss of height over time with a stooped posture, and an increased risk of fractures, particularly in the spine, hips, and wrists. These fractures can occur without a fall or injury due to the extreme weakness of the bones. The condition is often silent until severe bone damage has occurred, as indicated by the passages.\", \"Answer: Severe osteoporosis is a condition where bone density loss is significant, leading to symptoms such as back pain, loss of height over time with a stooped posture, and fractures of the vertebrae, wrists, hips, or other bones. It is characterized by the bones of the spine becoming so weak that they start to compress, potentially causing compression fractures without the need for a fall or injury.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Severe osteoporosis is a condition where bone density loss has progressed to the point that bones become extremely weak, leading to symptoms such as sudden severe back pain (especially in older women), height loss, and increased risk of fractures—particularly spinal compression fractures that may occur without falls or injuries, as well as fractures in the hips, wrists, or vertebrae due to minimal stress.\", \"Answer: Severe osteoporosis is a condition where bones become so weak that they can compress and fracture, often without a fall or injury. This is evident from the passage stating, \\\"Fractures of the spine can happen even without a fall or an injury. The bones of the spine become so weak that they start to compress.\\\" Additionally, the passage mentions that osteoporosis is \\\"a condition that has virtually no symptoms until severe bone damage has already occurred,\\\" indicating that by the time symptoms like back pain, loss of height, or fractures appear, the osteoporosis is severe.\"]", "time_sec": 29.409, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 54.37, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "283", "question": "difference between an adverb clause and an adjective clause", "gt": "Based on the given passages, an adverb clause modifies a verb, adjective or another adverb, while an adjective clause modifies a noun or pronoun.", "pred": "An adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, typically answering questions of time, reason, condition, or contrast, and is introduced by subordinating conjunctions such as \"because,\" \"although,\" or \"when.\" For example, in the sentence \"She left because she was tired,\" the adverb clause \"because she was tired\" modifies the verb \"left\" by explaining the reason for the action. In contrast, an adjective clause is a dependent clause that modifies a noun or pronoun, providing more information about the noun or pronoun it modifies, and is introduced by relative pronouns like \"who,\" \"which,\" or \"that,\" or by the subordinating conjunctions \"when\" or \"where.\" For instance, in the sentence \"The book that I read was fascinating,\" the adjective clause \"that I read\" modifies the noun \"book\" by specifying which book is being discussed. The key difference is that adverb clauses modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs, while adjective clauses modify nouns or pronouns.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: An adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, typically answering questions of time, reason, condition, or contrast, and is introduced by subordinating conjunctions such as \"because,\" \"although,\" or \"when.\" For example, in the sentence \"She left because she was tired,\" the adverb clause \"because she was tired\" modifies the verb \"left\" by explaining the reason for the action. In contrast, an adjective clause is a dependent clause that modifies a noun or pronoun, providing more information about the noun or pronoun it modifies, and is introduced by relative pronouns like \"who,\" \"which,\" or \"that,\" or by the subordinating conjunctions \"when\" or \"where.\" For instance, in the sentence \"The book that I read was fascinating,\" the adjective clause \"that I read\" modifies the noun \"book\" by specifying which book is being discussed. The key difference is that adverb clauses modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs, while adjective clauses modify nouns or pronouns.", "cot": "[\"Answer: An adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, typically answering questions of time, reason, condition, or contrast, and is introduced by subordinating conjunctions such as \\\"because,\\\" \\\"although,\\\" or \\\"when.\\\" For example, in the sentence \\\"She left because she was tired,\\\" the adverb clause \\\"because she was tired\\\" modifies the verb \\\"left\\\" by explaining the reason for the action. In contrast, an adjective clause is a dependent clause that modifies a noun or pronoun, providing more information about the noun or pronoun it modifies, and is introduced by relative pronouns like \\\"who,\\\" \\\"which,\\\" or \\\"that,\\\" or by the subordinating conjunctions \\\"when\\\" or \\\"where.\\\" For instance, in the sentence \\\"The book that I read was fascinating,\\\" the adjective clause \\\"that I read\\\" modifies the noun \\\"book\\\" by specifying which book is being discussed. The key difference is that adverb clauses modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs, while adjective clauses modify nouns or pronouns.\", \"Answer: An adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, typically answering questions like \\\"how,\\\" \\\"when,\\\" \\\"where,\\\" \\\"why,\\\" or \\\"under what conditions.\\\" It is introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as \\\"because,\\\" \\\"although,\\\" \\\"if,\\\" \\\"when,\\\" \\\"while,\\\" etc. For example, in the sentence \\\"She left because she was tired,\\\" the adverb clause \\\"because she was tired\\\" modifies the verb \\\"left\\\" by explaining the reason for the action.\\n\\nIn contrast, an adjective clause is a dependent clause that modifies a noun or pronoun, providing more information about the noun or pronoun it follows. It answers questions like \\\"which one,\\\" \\\"what kind,\\\" or \\\"whose.\\\" Adjective clauses are introduced by relative pronouns such as \\\"who,\\\" \\\"whom,\\\" \\\"whose,\\\" \\\"which,\\\" \\\"that,\\\" or subordinate conjunctions like \\\"when\\\" and \\\"where.\\\" For instance, in the sentence \\\"The book that I read last night was fascinating,\\\" the adjective clause \\\"that I read last night\\\" modifies the noun \\\"book\\\" by specifying which book is being discussed.\\n\\nThe key difference is that adverb clauses modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs, while adjective clauses modify nouns or pronouns.\", \"Answer: An adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, typically answering questions like \\\"how,\\\" \\\"when,\\\" \\\"where,\\\" \\\"why,\\\" or \\\"under what condition.\\\" It is introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as \\\"because,\\\" \\\"although,\\\" \\\"if,\\\" \\\"when,\\\" and \\\"while.\\\" In contrast, an adjective clause is a dependent clause that modifies a noun or pronoun, providing more information about the noun or pronoun it modifies. It begins with a relative pronoun like \\\"who,\\\" \\\"which,\\\" or \\\"that,\\\" or a subordinate conjunction like \\\"when\\\" or \\\"where.\\\" For example, in the sentence \\\"The book that I read was interesting,\\\" the clause \\\"that I read\\\" is an adjective clause modifying \\\"the book.\\\" In the sentence \\\"She left because she was tired,\\\" the clause \\\"because she was tired\\\" is an adverb clause modifying the verb \\\"left.\\\"\", \"Answer: An adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, typically answering questions of time, reason, condition, or manner (e.g., \\\"She left **because it was raining**\\\"). It is introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as *because*, *if*, *when*, or *although*. In contrast, an adjective clause is a dependent clause that modifies a noun or pronoun, providing specific details about the noun (e.g., \\\"The book **that I read** was fascinating\\\"). It is introduced by relative pronouns (*who*, *which*, *that*) or subordinate conjunctions (*when*, *where*). The key difference is that adverb clauses modify verbs (actions/states), while adjective clauses modify nouns (people/things).\", \"Answer: An adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, typically answering questions like \\\"how,\\\" \\\"when,\\\" \\\"where,\\\" or \\\"why.\\\" It is introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as \\\"because,\\\" \\\"although,\\\" \\\"if,\\\" and \\\"when.\\\" For example, in the sentence \\\"She left because she was tired,\\\" the adverb clause \\\"because she was tired\\\" modifies the verb \\\"left\\\" by explaining the reason. In contrast, an adjective clause is a dependent clause that modifies a noun or pronoun, providing more information about the noun or pronoun it modifies. It begins with a relative pronoun (who, whose, whom, which, that) or a subordinate conjunction (when, where). For instance, in the sentence \\\"The book that I read was interesting,\\\" the adjective clause \\\"that I read\\\" modifies the noun \\\"book\\\" by specifying which book. The key difference is that adverb clauses modify verbs, adjectives, or adverbs, while adjective clauses modify nouns or pronouns.\"]", "time_sec": 76.616, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 22.64, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.8, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "286", "question": "how to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven", "gt": "To cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, start by preheating the oven to 325-350 degrees Fahrenheit. If you don't have time to defrost the roast, put it in the oven at 250 F for about an hour. Afterwards, take it out and season it with salt, pepper, and any seasonings you prefer, such as thyme, oregano or garlic. Rub the surface with 2 tsp. of olive oil. Place the roast in a roasting pan with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards, and put it back in the oven for about 1.5 to 3 hours depending on its size, or about 20-30 minutes per pound. Make sure the roast is cooked thoroughly by using a meat thermometer; the internal temperature should reach 150 degrees Fahrenheit. Once it is cooked, cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.", "pred": "Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat facing upwards. Cook for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat; when the internal temperature reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove it from the oven. Cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer:Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat facing upwards. Cook for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat; when the internal temperature reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove it from the oven. Cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.", "cot": "[\"Answer:Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat facing upwards. Cook for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat; when the internal temperature reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove it from the oven. Cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Answer:Preheat oven to 350°F. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on a roasting rack with the fat side up. Cook for 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer; when the internal temperature reaches 150°F, remove from the oven. Cover with aluminum foil and let rest for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Answer: To cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat facing upwards. Cook for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat; when the internal temperature reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove it from the oven. Cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Answer: Preheat oven to 350°F. Place frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on a roasting rack with fat facing up. Cook for 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer; when internal temperature reaches 150°F, remove from oven. Cover with aluminum foil and let sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Answer: Preheat the oven to 350°F. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on a roasting rack with the fat side up. Cook for 20–30 minutes per pound, inserting a meat thermometer to check the internal temperature. When the temperature reaches 150°F, remove the roast, cover it with aluminum foil, and let it rest for 30 minutes before serving.\"]", "time_sec": 37.196, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 50.36, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "287", "question": "how to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven", "gt": "To cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, preheat the oven to 325 degrees F. Place the frozen roast in a roasting pan and rub the surface with olive oil and season with salt, pepper, and desired seasonings. Place the roast, uncovered, in the preheated oven. Cook for about 20-30 minutes per pound or until the internal temperature reaches 150 degrees F. Once cooked, cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.", "pred": "Preheat oven to 350°F. Place frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on a roasting rack with fat facing up. Cook for 20-30 minutes per pound. Test internal temperature with a meat thermometer; when it reaches 150°F, remove from oven. Cover with aluminum foil and let sit for 30 minutes before serving.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Preheat oven to 350°F. Place frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on a roasting rack with fat facing up. Cook for 20-30 minutes per pound. Test internal temperature with a meat thermometer; when it reaches 150°F, remove from oven. Cover with aluminum foil and let sit for 30 minutes before serving.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Preheat oven to 350°F. Place frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on a roasting rack with fat facing up. Cook for 20-30 minutes per pound. Test internal temperature with a meat thermometer; when it reaches 150°F, remove from oven. Cover with aluminum foil and let sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Answer: Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards. Cook the pork loin for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer, and test the meat after cooking. When the internal temperature of the meat is 150 degrees Fahrenheit, the meat is ready to be taken out of the oven. Cover the roast with aluminum foil, and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Answer: Preheat oven to 350°F. Place frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on a roasting rack with fat side up. Cook for 20-30 minutes per pound. Test with meat thermometer; remove when internal temperature reaches 150°F. Cover with aluminum foil and let sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Answer: Preheat oven to 350°F. Place frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on a roasting rack with fat side up. Cook for 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert meat thermometer and test until internal temperature reaches 150°F. Cover with aluminum foil and let sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Answer: Preheat the oven to 350°F. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on a roasting rack with the fat side up. Cook for about 20–30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer and cook until the internal temperature reaches 150°F. Cover with aluminum foil and let sit for 30 minutes before serving.\"]", "time_sec": 34.925, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 55.32, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "289", "question": "how to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven:\n\nPassage 1 states to defrost the roast first and then place it in the oven at 250°F for about an hour. After taking it out, season the roast and turn the oven temperature to 350°F. Cook for 1.5 to 3 hours, depending on the size of the roast, making sure it's cooked all the way through.\n\nPassage 2 provides specific instructions for preparing the roast before cooking. Rub the surface of the frozen pork loin with olive oil and season with salt, pepper, and any desired herbs. Place the roast in a preheated oven at 325°F and determine the cooking time based on the weight of the roast.\n\nPassage 3 suggests starting by preheating the oven to 350°F and then placing the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on a roasting rack. Cook for 20-30 minutes per pound, inserting a meat thermometer to check the internal temperature once cooked. Once the temperature reaches 150°F, remove the roast from the oven and let it rest for 30 minutes before slicing and serving.\n\nBased on these passages, the general steps to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven are:\n\n1. Defrost the roast if not already thawed.\n2. Season the roast with salt, pepper, and herbs.\n3. Place the roast in a preheated oven at the appropriate temperature (250°F or 325°F) for the specified time.\n4. Check the internal temperature of the roast using a meat thermometer to ensure it reaches the desired doneness.\n5. Remove the roast from the oven and let it rest for 30 minutes before slicing and serving.", "pred": "Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat facing upwards. Cook for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Test the internal temperature with a meat thermometer; when it reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove the roast. Cover it with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer:Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat facing upwards. Cook for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Test the internal temperature with a meat thermometer; when it reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove the roast. Cover it with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.", "cot": "[\"Answer:Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat facing upwards. Cook for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Test the internal temperature with a meat thermometer; when it reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove the roast. Cover it with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Answer: Preheat oven to 350°F. Place frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on a roasting rack with fat facing up. Cook for 20-30 minutes per pound. Test internal temperature with a meat thermometer; when it reaches 150°F, remove from oven. Cover with aluminum foil and let sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Answer: Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards. Cook the pork loin for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer, and test the meat after cooking. When the internal temperature of the meat is 150 degrees Fahrenheit, it is ready to be taken out of the oven. Cover the roast with aluminum foil, and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Answer: To cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards. Cook the pork loin for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer, and test the meat after cooking. When the internal temperature of the meat is 150 degrees Fahrenheit, the meat is ready to be taken out of the oven. Cover the roast with aluminum foil, and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Answer: Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat facing upwards. Cook for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat; when the internal temperature reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove it from the oven. Cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\"]", "time_sec": 41.572, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 24.43, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "290", "question": "how to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how you can cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven:\n\n1. Defrost the pork loin if possible. If you don't have time to defrost, you can place it in the oven at 250°F for about an hour.\n2. Preheat the oven to 325°F (passage 2).\n3. Rub the surface of the frozen pork loin with 2 tsp of olive oil and season the outside with salt, pepper, and desired herbs (thyme, oregano, or garlic work well) (passage 2).\n4. Place the pork loin in a roasting pan and put it in the preheated oven (passage 2).\n5. Cook the pork loin for about 20-30 minutes per pound, or until the internal temperature reaches 150°F (passage 3).\n6. Use a meat thermometer to check the internal temperature of the meat (passage 3).\n7. Once the meat is cooked, remove it from the oven and cover it with aluminum foil for 30 minutes before serving (passage 3).\n\nNote that the exact cooking time may vary depending on the size of the roast, so adjust the cooking time accordingly. Also, make sure to check the internal temperature of the meat to ensure that it is cooked all the way through.", "pred": "Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards. Cook the pork loin for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer, and test the meat after cooking. When the internal temperature of the meat is 150 degrees Fahrenheit, the meat is ready to be taken out of the oven. Cover the roast with aluminum foil, and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer:Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards. Cook the pork loin for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer, and test the meat after cooking. When the internal temperature of the meat is 150 degrees Fahrenheit, the meat is ready to be taken out of the oven. Cover the roast with aluminum foil, and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.", "cot": "[\"Answer:Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards. Cook the pork loin for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer, and test the meat after cooking. When the internal temperature of the meat is 150 degrees Fahrenheit, the meat is ready to be taken out of the oven. Cover the roast with aluminum foil, and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Answer: Preheat the oven to 350°F. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on a roasting rack with the fat side up. Cook for 20–30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer and cook until the internal temperature reaches 150°F. Cover with aluminum foil and let sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Answer:Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards. Cook for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat after cooking. When the internal temperature reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, the meat is ready to be taken out of the oven. Cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Answer:Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat facing upwards. Cook for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat; when the internal temperature reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove it from the oven. Cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Answer: Preheat the oven to 350°F. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat facing upwards. Cook for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat; when the internal temperature reaches 150°F, remove it from the oven. Cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\"]", "time_sec": 40.223, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 35.6, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "291", "question": "how to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven:\n\n1. Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit.\n2. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards.\n3. Cook the pork loin for about 20-30 minutes per pound.\n4. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat after cooking.\n5. When the internal temperature of the meat reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\n\nSo, the steps to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven are: preheat the oven, place the roast in a shallow baking pan, cook for 20-30 minutes per pound, check the internal temperature, cover and let it sit before serving.", "pred": "Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat facing upwards. Cook for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat; when the internal temperature reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove it from the oven. Cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer:Preheat the oven to 350°F. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on a roasting rack with the fat side up. Cook for about 20–30 minutes per pound. Test the internal temperature with a meat thermometer; when it reaches 150°F, remove the roast. Cover it with aluminum foil and let it rest for 30 minutes before serving.", "cot": "[\"Answer:Preheat the oven to 350°F. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on a roasting rack with the fat side up. Cook for about 20–30 minutes per pound. Test the internal temperature with a meat thermometer; when it reaches 150°F, remove the roast. Cover it with aluminum foil and let it rest for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Answer: Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat facing upwards. Cook for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat; when the internal temperature reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove it from the oven. Cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Answer: Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat facing upwards. Cook for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat after cooking. When the internal temperature reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove the roast from the oven. Cover it with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Answer: Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards. Cook the pork loin for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat after cooking. When the internal temperature of the meat is 150 degrees Fahrenheit, the meat is ready to be taken out of the oven. Cover the roast with aluminum foil, and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Answer: Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat facing upwards. Cook for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat; when the internal temperature reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove it from the oven. Cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\"]", "time_sec": 40.433, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 64.34, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "294", "question": "what is the proper way to fertilize your grass", "gt": "To properly fertilize your grass, you should fertilize at the beginning of the growing season for your specific grass type. For warm season grasses, this would be when the grass turns green in the early spring. You should also know the type of fertilizer to use based on the type of grass you have and do a pH soil test to ensure the fertilizer has the appropriate pH for your soil. Once you've determined the correct fertilizer and timing, apply it in a perpendicular pattern and check the lawn after application by stepping on the turf. If the leaf blade bounces back up, the lawn is in good shape, but if it lays flat, you've over-fertilized.", "pred": "Fertilize warm season grasses at the beginning of spring when the grass begins to turn green, ensuring you apply the fertilizer at the start of the growing season for optimal effectiveness.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Fertilize warm season grasses at the beginning of spring when the grass begins to turn green, ensuring you apply the fertilizer at the start of the growing season for optimal effectiveness.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Fertilize warm season grasses at the beginning of spring when the grass begins to turn green, ensuring you apply the fertilizer at the start of the growing season for optimal effectiveness.\", \"Answer: Fertilize at the beginning of the growing season, specifically when warm season grasses begin to turn green in spring, after determining the appropriate fertilizer type based on your grass type and soil pH, and applying the rest of the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern while ensuring the lawn is not over-fertilized (leaf blade should bounce back).\", \"Answer: Fertilize at the right time of year, specifically at the beginning of the growing season. For warm season grasses, fertilize when the grass begins to turn green at the beginning of spring. Also, know your grass type to determine the appropriate fertilizer and frequency, do a pH soil test to determine the ideal pH for your fertilizer, and measure your lawn. Apply the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern, and ensure proper fertilization by checking if the leaf blade bounces back up when stepped on.\", \"Answer: Fertilize warm season grasses when the grass begins to turn green at the beginning of spring, and apply the rest of the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern.\", \"Answer: Fertilize warm season grasses at the beginning of spring when the grass begins to turn green, and apply the rest of the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern to ensure proper coverage.\"]", "time_sec": 23.684, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 29.47, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "295", "question": "what is the proper way to fertilize your grass", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the proper way to fertilize your grass is to:\n\n1. Fertilize at the right time of year, which is at the beginning of the growing season, and varies for different types of grasses.\n2. Choose a fertilizer that is suitable for your type of grass and soil pH, as determined by a soil test.\n3. Apply the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern, avoiding over-fertilization, which can be determined by checking if the leaf blades bounce back up after being stepped on.", "pred": "Fertilize warm season grasses at the beginning of spring when the grass starts turning green, and apply the rest of the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern. First, determine the type of grass you have to know what type of fertilizer to use and how often to fertilize, then do a pH soil test to determine the appropriate pH for your fertilizer, and measure your lawn before applying.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Fertilize warm season grasses at the beginning of spring when the grass starts turning green, and apply the rest of the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern. First, determine the type of grass you have to know what type of fertilizer to use and how often to fertilize, then do a pH soil test to determine the appropriate pH for your fertilizer, and measure your lawn before applying.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Fertilize warm season grasses at the beginning of spring when the grass starts turning green, and apply the rest of the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern. First, determine the type of grass you have to know what type of fertilizer to use and how often to fertilize, then do a pH soil test to determine the appropriate pH for your fertilizer, and measure your lawn before applying.\", \"Answer: Fertilize at the beginning of the growing season, specifically when warm season grasses begin to turn green in spring; choose fertilizer based on your grass type and soil pH, apply the rest of the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern, and ensure the lawn is not over-fertilized by checking if the leaf blades bounce back after stepping on the turf.\", \"Answer: Fertilize warm season grasses at the beginning of spring when the grass begins to turn green, and apply the rest of the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern. Also, know your grass type, do a pH soil test, and measure your lawn to determine the right type and frequency of fertilizer to use.\", \"Answer: Fertilize at the beginning of the growing season, specifically when warm season grasses begin to turn green in spring; choose fertilizer based on grass type and soil pH, apply the rest in a perpendicular pattern, and ensure proper fertilization by checking if the leaf blade bounces back after stepping on the turf.\", \"Answer: Fertilize at the beginning of the growing season, timing it according to the grass type—warm season grasses should be fertilized when they begin to turn green in spring—and follow the product instructions for application, including applying the rest of the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern.\"]", "time_sec": 27.69, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 38.3, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "296", "question": "what are examples of potential and kinetic energy?", "gt": "Examples of kinetic energy include an airplane in flight, due to its large mass and fast velocity, and a baseball thrown by a pitcher, which can have a large amount of kinetic energy due to its fast velocity. Examples of potential energy include gravitational potential energy, which is the energy stored in an object as the result of its vertical position or height, and elastic potential energy. Other activities like a car moving, a ball rolling, a river flowing, an athlete running, rides in an amusement park, and a satellite in orbit also involve potential and kinetic energy.", "pred": "Examples of potential energy include a car at the top of a hill, a ball held at a certain height, a stretched rubber band, and a satellite in orbit. Examples of kinetic energy include an airplane in flight, a baseball thrown by a pitcher, a car moving, a ball rolling, a river flowing, and an athlete running.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Examples of potential energy include a car at the top of a hill, a ball held at a certain height, a stretched rubber band, and a satellite in orbit. Examples of kinetic energy include an airplane in flight, a baseball thrown by a pitcher, a car moving, a ball rolling, a river flowing, and an athlete running.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Examples of potential energy include a car at the top of a hill, a ball held at a certain height, a stretched rubber band, and a satellite in orbit. Examples of kinetic energy include an airplane in flight, a baseball thrown by a pitcher, a car moving, a ball rolling, a river flowing, and an athlete running.\", \"Answer: Examples of potential energy include a car at the top of a hill (gravitational potential energy due to height), a compressed spring (elastic potential energy), and a satellite in orbit (gravitational potential energy). Examples of kinetic energy include an airplane in flight (large mass and fast velocity), a baseball thrown by a pitcher (small mass but high velocity), a car moving, a ball rolling, a river flowing, and an athlete running.\", \"Answer: Examples of kinetic energy include an airplane in flight, a baseball thrown by a pitcher, a car moving, a ball rolling, a river flowing, an athlete running, and a satellite in orbit. Examples of potential energy include gravitational potential energy (e.g., an object at a height) and elastic potential energy.\", \"Answer: Examples of kinetic energy include an airplane in flight due to its large mass and fast velocity, and a baseball thrown by a pitcher which can have large kinetic energy due to its fast velocity despite its small mass. Examples of potential energy include gravitational potential energy, such as an object's energy due to its vertical position or height, and elastic potential energy.\", \"Answer: Examples of kinetic energy include an airplane in flight and a baseball thrown by a pitcher. Examples of potential energy include gravitational potential energy, such as an object at a certain height, and elastic potential energy.\"]", "time_sec": 27.778, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 49.48, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
