{"id": "303", "question": "how to make hot water heater circulate", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's a brief answer to the question \"how to make hot water heater circulate\":\n\nA hot water circulation pump can be used to circulate water through the hot water heater and ensure that hot water is always available. The pump sends cooled water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line and circulates it through the water heater as needed to keep it hot. Additionally, demand systems can be controlled by the push of a button, a timer, or motion sensor to activate the pump and circulate the water.", "pred": "A hot water circulation pump sends cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line, creating a continuous loop that keeps water hot and available. This can also be achieved with demand systems controlled by a button, timer, or motion sensor, which rapidly pull hot water from the heater and return cool water to be reheated.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: A hot water circulation pump sends cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line, creating a continuous loop that keeps water hot and available. This can also be achieved with demand systems controlled by a button, timer, or motion sensor, which rapidly pull hot water from the heater and return cool water to be reheated.", "cot": "[\"Answer: A hot water circulation pump sends cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line, creating a continuous loop that keeps water hot and available. This can also be achieved with demand systems controlled by a button, timer, or motion sensor, which rapidly pull hot water from the heater and return cool water to be reheated.\", \"Answer: A hot water circulation pump can make a hot water heater circulate by sending cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line, creating a continuous loop of water through the water heater to keep it hot and ensure hot water is always available.\", \"Answer: A hot water circulation pump sends cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line, which then circulates the water through the water heater to keep it hot and ensure hot water is always available. This can also be achieved with recirculating pumps that rapidly pull hot water from the water heater and send cool water back to be reheated, which can be controlled by a button, timer, or motion sensor.\", \"Answer: A hot water circulation pump can be used to make the hot water heater circulate water. This pump sends cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line, creating a continuous loop of water through the water heater to keep it hot and ensure hot water is always available (Passage 2). Additionally, recirculating pumps rapidly pull hot water from a water heater and send cool water from the hot water lines back to the water heater to be reheated and reused, which can be controlled by a button, timer, or motion sensor (Passage 3).\", \"Answer: To make a hot water heater circulate, a recirculating pump can be used, which rapidly pulls hot water from the water heater and sends cool water from the hot water lines back to the water heater to be reheated and reused, as described in passage 3. Additionally, demand systems for recirculating pumps can be controlled by a push of a button, a timer, or a motion sensor.\"]", "time_sec": 33.329, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 56.31, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "304", "question": "what causes watermelons to explode", "gt": "Watermelons can explode due to external pressure, such as when a bunch of rubber bands are placed around the middle of the watermelon, causing potential energy to build up and eventually cause the watermelon to explode. Another cause of watermelon explosions is the buildup of gas inside the watermelon due to rotting. This increases the internal pressure and when the skin of the watermelon can no longer hold the gas inside, it will explode.", "pred": "Watermelons explode due to the buildup of gas pressure inside them. This gas is produced from the rotting process within the watermelon, which slowly increases the internal pressure until the watermelon's skin can no longer contain it, causing it to burst. This is described in the passages as the gas trying to escape and the pressure continuing to increase until the skin gives way.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Watermelons explode due to the buildup of gas pressure inside them. This gas is produced from the rotting process within the watermelon, which slowly increases the internal pressure until the watermelon's skin can no longer contain it, causing it to burst. This is described in the passages as the gas trying to escape and the pressure continuing to increase until the skin gives way.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Watermelons explode due to the buildup of gas pressure inside them. This gas is produced from the rotting process within the watermelon, which slowly increases the internal pressure until the watermelon's skin can no longer contain it, causing it to burst. This is described in the passages as the gas trying to escape and the pressure continuing to increase until the skin gives way.\", \"Answer: Watermelons explode due to the increasing pressure from gas produced by rotting inside the watermelon, which eventually exceeds the strength of the watermelon's skin, causing it to burst.\", \"Answer: Watermelons explode due to the buildup of pressure from gas that is produced by rotting inside the watermelon. This gas tries to escape, but as the pressure continues to increase, the skin of the watermelon can no longer hold it in, causing it to burst.\", \"Answer: The explosion of watermelons is caused by the increase in internal pressure due to gas production from rotting, which the watermelon's skin can no longer contain, leading to a rupture and explosion.\", \"Answer: The pressure from gas that increases due to rotting inside the watermelon causes it to explode when the skin can no longer hold the gas.\"]", "time_sec": 21.657, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 41.38, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "310", "question": "avg cost to install a second floor in a house", "gt": "Hiring a remodeling contractor to build a second floor addition can cost between $100-$300 a square foot for a typical addition, but the cost can go as high as $400-$500 per square foot depending on the complexity, quality of materials and local labor rates. Building a second floor can cost at least 20 percent more than building the same square footage at ground level.", "pred": "The average cost to install a second floor in a house is $100-$300 per square foot for a typical addition (2-3 bedrooms and a bath plus a flight of stairs), but can go as high as $400-$500 per square foot depending on complexity, quality of materials, and local labor rates. Building a second floor costs at least 20 percent more than building the same square footage at ground level.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The average cost to install a second floor in a house is $100-$300 per square foot for a typical addition (2-3 bedrooms and a bath plus a flight of stairs), but can go as high as $400-$500 per square foot depending on complexity, quality of materials, and local labor rates. Building a second floor costs at least 20 percent more than building the same square footage at ground level.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The average cost to install a second floor in a house is $100-$300 per square foot for a typical addition (2-3 bedrooms and a bath plus a flight of stairs), but can go as high as $400-$500 per square foot depending on complexity, quality of materials, and local labor rates. Building a second floor costs at least 20 percent more than building the same square footage at ground level.\", \"Answer: The average cost to install a second floor in a house ranges from $100-$300 per square foot for a typical addition (2-3 bedrooms and a bath plus a flight of stairs), with the potential to go as high as $400-$500 per square foot depending on complexity, quality of materials, and local labor rates. Additionally, building a second floor can cost at least 20 percent more than building the same square footage at ground level.\", \"Answer: $100-$300 per square foot, with potential increases to $400-$500 per square foot based on complexity, materials, and labor rates, and at least 20% more than ground-level construction costs.\", \"Answer: The average cost to install a second floor in a house ranges from $100 to $500 per square foot, depending on complexity, quality of materials, and local labor rates. This is in addition to the base cost of building the same square footage at ground level, which is at least 20 percent more expensive than ground-level construction. For example, a second floor addition for 2-3 bedrooms and a bath with a flight of stairs typically costs $100-$300 per square foot, while more complex projects can reach $400-$500 per square foot.\", \"Answer: $100-$300 per square foot, with potential increases up to $400-$500 depending on complexity, quality of materials, and local labor rates, and at least 20 percent more than ground-level construction costs.\"]", "time_sec": 34.365, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 76.92, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
