{"id": "403", "question": "2006 chevy remove door panel", "gt": "To remove the rear door panel in a 2006 Chevy HHR, you will need to remove the two screws located inside of the door pull cup and the one in the middle of the inside door handle. You will also need to remove a pin on the outer edge of the door. Once these are removed, you can use a door panel removal tool to pry the door panel off of the HHR rear door.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\"]", "time_sec": 7.465, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "404", "question": "2006 chevy remove door panel", "gt": "The passage provides step-by-step instructions for removing the rear door panel on a 2006 Chevy HHR. To perform this task, the passenger must locate and remove the two screws inside the door pull cup and the middle of the inside door handle. A pin on the outside edge of the door must also be removed using a door panel removal tool. Once all these steps are completed, the door panel can be carefully pulled away from the frame, taking care not to damage any wiring connections.\n\nAccordingly, the answer to the question is:\n\nRemoving the rear door panel on a 2006 Chevy HHR involves locating and removing the screws inside the door pull cup and the middle of the inside door handle, as well as removing the pin on the outside edge of the door using a door panel removal tool.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\"]", "time_sec": 7.457, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 2.04, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "405", "question": "2006 chevy remove door panel", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is the answer to the question:\n\nTo remove the rear door panel in a 2006 Chevy HHR, you should follow these steps:\n\n1. Remove the two screws located inside the door pull cup.\n2. Remove the screw located in the middle of the inside door handle.\n3. Remove the pin on the outer edge of the door that holds the panel in place.\n4. Use a door panel removal tool to pry the door panel off of the HHR rear door.\n\nIt is important to note that an instructional video is also available that shows the entire process and location of the screws and pin.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\"]", "time_sec": 7.459, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 10.96, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "406", "question": "2006 chevy remove door panel", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is the answer to the question:\n\nTo remove the door panel on a 2006 Chevy, you will need to:\n\n1. Remove the two screws located inside the door pull cup.\n2. Remove the screw in the middle of the inside door handle.\n3. Remove the pin that holds the panel in place on the outer edge of the door.\n4. Use a door panel removal tool to pry the door panel off the rear door.\n5. Remove any wiring connections.\n6. Gently pull the panel away from the frame.\n\nAfter removing the screws and clips, you will be able to remove the door panel. It is recommended to watch an instructional video to see the process and locate the parts mentioned.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\"]", "time_sec": 7.461, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 9.09, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "408", "question": "how to make panko crumbs", "gt": "To make panko crumbs, preheat the oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn pieces or chunks of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to make coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake at 300 degrees F for 6 to 8 minutes, shaking the sheet twice during baking. Remove from the oven and cool completely.", "pred": "To make panko crumbs, push torn or chunk pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs, then spread them on a baking sheet. Bake in a preheated 300 °F oven for 6–8 minutes, shaking the sheet twice during baking, until the crumbs are dry but not toasted. (Alternative method: blend all ingredients in a blender for about 2 minutes, spread on two half‑sheet pans, and bake for about 5 minutes until just beginning to brown and crisp.)", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To make panko crumbs, push torn or chunk pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs, then spread them on a baking sheet. Bake in a preheated 300 °F oven for 6–8 minutes, shaking the sheet twice during baking, until the crumbs are dry but not toasted. (Alternative method: blend all ingredients in a blender for about 2 minutes, spread on two half‑sheet pans, and bake for about 5 minutes until just beginning to brown and crisp.)", "cot": "[\"Answer: To make panko crumbs, push torn or chunk pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs, then spread them on a baking sheet. Bake in a preheated 300 °F oven for 6–8 minutes, shaking the sheet twice during baking, until the crumbs are dry but not toasted. (Alternative method: blend all ingredients in a blender for about 2 minutes, spread on two half‑sheet pans, and bake for about 5 minutes until just beginning to brown and crisp.)\", \"Answer:To make panko crumbs, preheat an oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn or chunk pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs evenly on a baking sheet or two half sheet pans. Bake for 5 to 8 minutes, shaking the sheet twice during baking, until the crumbs are dry but not toasted (or just begin to brown and are crisp, as in Alton Brown's method). Be careful not to let the crumbs brown. Cool completely before storing in an airtight container at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\", \"Answer: To make panko crumbs, push torn or chunk pieces of white bread through a food processor's shredding disk to create coarse crumbs, spread them on a baking sheet, and bake at 300°F (150°C) for 6–8 minutes (or until dry but not toasted), shaking the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying; avoid browning, as noted in the passages, and cool completely before storing in an airtight container for up to 2 weeks, as Alton Brown’s method specifies.\", \"Answer: To make panko crumbs, first preheat your oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Then, push torn or chunk pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs evenly on a baking sheet and bake for 6 to 8 minutes, shaking the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying. Be careful not to let the crumbs brown. Alternatively, you can spread the crumbs on two half sheet pans and bake for about 5 minutes until they just begin to brown and become crisp. After baking, cool the crumbs on the pans for 15 to 20 minutes, then transfer them to an airtight container and store at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\", \"Answer: To make panko crumbs, first preheat your oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Tear or push chunks of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs evenly on a baking sheet (or two half sheet pans) and bake for 5 to 8 minutes, shaking the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying. Be careful not to let the crumbs brown. Once dry but not toasted, remove from the oven and cool completely. For storage, transfer the cooled crumbs to an airtight container and keep at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\"]", "time_sec": 52.012, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 52.53, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "409", "question": "how to make panko crumbs", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how to make panko crumbs:\n\nPassage 1 states to push torn pieces of bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to make coarse crumbs. Then, spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake in a preheated oven until they are dry but not toasted, about 6 to 8 minutes.\n\nPassage 2 provides similar instructions, stating to push chunks of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to make coarse crumbs. Then, spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake at 300°F until the crumbs are dry but not toasted, about 6 to 8 minutes.\n\nPassage 3 provides additional information, suggesting to preheat the oven to 300°F before making the crumbs. The passage also recommends using a blender carafe to pulse the bread until coarse crumbs form, and then spreading the crumbs evenly on two half-sheet pans and baking until they just begin to brown and are crisp, about 5 minutes. After cooling the crumbs on the pans for 15 to 20 minutes, transfer them to an airtight container and store them at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\n\nTherefore, to make panko crumbs based on the provided passages, you can use a food processor or blender to shred or pulse bread into coarse crumbs, and then bake them in a preheated oven until they are dry but not toasted.", "pred": "Preheat an oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn or chunk pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to make coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake until dry but not toasted, for 6 to 8 minutes. Shake the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying, and avoid letting the crumbs brown. Cool completely before storing in an airtight container at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Preheat an oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn or chunk pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to make coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake until dry but not toasted, for 6 to 8 minutes. Shake the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying, and avoid letting the crumbs brown. Cool completely before storing in an airtight container at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Preheat an oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn or chunk pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to make coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake until dry but not toasted, for 6 to 8 minutes. Shake the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying, and avoid letting the crumbs brown. Cool completely before storing in an airtight container at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\", \"Answer: To make panko crumbs, preheat an oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn or chunk pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs, as described in the passages. Spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake in the preheated oven for 6 to 8 minutes until dry but not toasted, shaking the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying, as stated in passages 1 and 2. Alternatively, as per passage 3, spread the crumbs evenly on two half sheet pans and bake for about 5 minutes until they just begin to brown and become crisp. After baking, cool the crumbs completely on the pans for 15 to 20 minutes before transferring them to an airtight container for storage at room temperature for up to 2 weeks. Be careful not to let the crumbs brown, as emphasized in passage 2.\", \"Answer: To make panko crumbs, preheat the oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn or chunk pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs in a single layer on a baking sheet. Bake for 6 to 8 minutes, shaking the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying, until the crumbs are dry but not toasted. Alternatively, some methods involve baking for about 5 minutes until the crumbs just begin to brown and become crisp. After baking, cool the crumbs completely on the baking sheet for 15 to 20 minutes before transferring them to an airtight container for storage at room temperature, where they can last up to 2 weeks.\", \"Answer: To make panko crumbs, preheat the oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn or chunked pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs evenly on a baking sheet and bake for 6 to 8 minutes, or until dry but not toasted, shaking the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying. Alternatively, spread the crumbs on two half-sheet pans and bake for about 5 minutes until they just begin to brown and become crisp. Cool the crumbs completely before storing them in an airtight container at room temperature for up to 2 weeks. Be careful not to let the crumbs brown during baking.\", \"Answer: To make panko crumbs, push torn or chunked white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to form coarse crumbs, then spread them on a baking sheet and bake in a preheated 300°F (150°C) oven for 6–8 minutes, shaking the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying, and remove to cool completely (as detailed in the first passage); alternatively, some methods involve pulsing ingredients in a blender for about 2 minutes, spreading on two half-sheet pans, and baking for about 5 minutes until just beginning to brown and crisp, followed by cooling for 15–20 minutes before storing in an airtight container for up to 2 weeks (as described in the third passage).\"]", "time_sec": 59.848, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 36.71, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "410", "question": "how to make panko crumbs", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how to make panko crumbs:\n\n1. Preheat the oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C).\n2. Torn pieces of bread are pushed through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs.\n3. The crumbs are spread on a baking sheet.\n4. The sheet is baked in the preheated oven until the crumbs are dry but not toasted, for 6 to 8 minutes.\n5. The sheet is shaken twice during baking to ensure even drying.\n6. After baking, the crumbs are removed from the oven and allowed to cool completely.\n\nAlternatively, according to passage 2, you can also make panko crumbs by:\n\n1. Pushing chunks of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs.\n2. Spreading the crumbs on a baking sheet and baking at 300 degrees F until the crumbs are dry but not toasted, for 6 to 8 minutes.\n3. Shaking the sheet twice during baking.\n4. Being careful not to let the crumbs brown.\n\nFinally, passage 3 provides a third method for making panko crumbs, which involves blending the ingredients in a blender carafe and baking them on half sheet pans until they are crisp and lightly browned.", "pred": "To make panko crumbs, preheat an oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn or chunk pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs evenly on a baking sheet or two half sheet pans. Bake in the preheated oven for 6 to 8 minutes (or about 5 minutes if using two pans), shaking the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying, and avoid letting the crumbs brown. Remove from the oven and cool completely before transferring to an airtight container for storage at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To make panko crumbs, preheat an oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn or chunk pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs evenly on a baking sheet or two half sheet pans. Bake in the preheated oven for 6 to 8 minutes (or about 5 minutes if using two pans), shaking the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying, and avoid letting the crumbs brown. Remove from the oven and cool completely before transferring to an airtight container for storage at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To make panko crumbs, preheat an oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn or chunk pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs evenly on a baking sheet or two half sheet pans. Bake in the preheated oven for 6 to 8 minutes (or about 5 minutes if using two pans), shaking the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying, and avoid letting the crumbs brown. Remove from the oven and cool completely before transferring to an airtight container for storage at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\", \"Answer: To make panko crumbs, preheat an oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn or chunk pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs evenly on a baking sheet (or two half sheet pans). Bake for 6 to 8 minutes (or about 5 minutes until just beginning to brown and crisp), shaking the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying. Remove from the oven and cool completely (or cool on the pans for 15 to 20 minutes before transferring to an airtight container). Store at room temperature in an airtight container for up to 2 weeks.\", \"Answer: To make panko crumbs, preheat the oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn or chunk pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs evenly on a baking sheet or two half sheet pans. Bake in the preheated oven for 6 to 8 minutes (or about 5 minutes if using two pans), shaking the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying. Be careful not to let the crumbs brown. Once dry but not toasted, remove from the oven and cool completely. The crumbs can then be transferred to an airtight container and stored at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\", \"Answer: To make panko crumbs, push torn or chunk pieces of white bread through a food processor's shredding disk to create coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake in a preheated oven at 300 degrees F (150 degrees C) for 6 to 8 minutes, shaking the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying, until the crumbs are dry but not toasted. Alternatively, spread crumbs on two half sheet pans and bake for about 5 minutes until they just begin to brown and are crisp, then cool on the pans for 15 to 20 minutes before transferring to an airtight container for storage at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\", \"Answer: To make panko crumbs, preheat an oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn or chunk pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs on a baking sheet, shake the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying, and bake until the crumbs are dry but not toasted, which takes 6 to 8 minutes. Be careful not to let the crumbs brown. For an alternative method, place ingredients in a blender and pulse for about 2 minutes, then spread on half sheet pans and bake for about 5 minutes until just beginning to brown and crisp. Cool the crumbs on the pans for 15 to 20 minutes before transferring to an airtight container for storage at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\"]", "time_sec": 58.364, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 47.62, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "411", "question": "how to make panko crumbs", "gt": "Sure, I can help you with that! Based on the given passages, here's a step-by-step guide on how to make panko crumbs:\n\n1. Preheat the oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C).\n2. Push torn pieces of bread or chunks of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to make coarse crumbs.\n3. Spread the crumbs on a baking sheet.\n4. Bake in preheated oven until crumbs are dry but not toasted, 6 to 8 minutes. Shake the sheet twice during baking to evenly dry the crumbs.\n5. Remove from the oven and cool completely.\n\nIf you want to store the panko crumbs for later use, you can transfer them to an airtight container and keep them at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you have any further questions.", "pred": "To make panko crumbs, preheat an oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn or chunked white bread through a food processor’s shredding disk to create coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake for 6 to 8 minutes, shaking the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying. Ensure the crumbs are dry but not toasted, and avoid browning. Alternatively, place crumbs on two half-sheet pans and bake for about 5 minutes until they just begin to brown and become crisp. Cool the crumbs on the pans for 15 to 20 minutes, then transfer to an airtight container and store at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To make panko crumbs, preheat an oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn or chunked white bread through a food processor’s shredding disk to create coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake for 6 to 8 minutes, shaking the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying. Ensure the crumbs are dry but not toasted, and avoid browning. Alternatively, place crumbs on two half-sheet pans and bake for about 5 minutes until they just begin to brown and become crisp. Cool the crumbs on the pans for 15 to 20 minutes, then transfer to an airtight container and store at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To make panko crumbs, preheat an oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn or chunked white bread through a food processor’s shredding disk to create coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake for 6 to 8 minutes, shaking the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying. Ensure the crumbs are dry but not toasted, and avoid browning. Alternatively, place crumbs on two half-sheet pans and bake for about 5 minutes until they just begin to brown and become crisp. Cool the crumbs on the pans for 15 to 20 minutes, then transfer to an airtight container and store at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\", \"Answer: To make panko crumbs, first preheat an oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn or chunk pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs evenly on a baking sheet (or two half sheet pans) and bake until dry but not toasted, for about 6 to 8 minutes (or until just beginning to brown and crisp, about 5 minutes). Shake the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying. Remove from the oven and cool completely on the pans for 15 to 20 minutes before transferring to an airtight container and storing at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\", \"Answer: To make panko crumbs, preheat an oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn or chunk pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake for 6 to 8 minutes, or until dry but not toasted, shaking the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying. Alternatively, bake on two half sheet pans for about 5 minutes until the crumbs just begin to brown and are crisp. Cool the crumbs completely before storing in an airtight container at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\", \"Answer: To make panko crumbs, preheat the oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn or chunked white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs evenly on a baking sheet (or two half sheet pans) and bake for 6 to 8 minutes (or about 5 minutes if using two pans) until the crumbs are dry but not toasted or browned. Shake the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying. Remove the crumbs from the oven and cool them completely before storing in an airtight container at room temperature, where they can last up to 2 weeks. Be careful not to let the crumbs brown during baking.\", \"Answer: To make panko crumbs, preheat an oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake for 6 to 8 minutes, shaking the sheet twice during baking to ensure even drying. Be careful not to let the crumbs brown. Alternatively, preheat the oven to 300 degrees F, pulse all ingredients in a blender for about 2 minutes until coarse crumbs form, spread them on two half sheet pans, and bake for about 5 minutes until they just begin to brown and become crisp. Cool the crumbs on the pans for 15 to 20 minutes, then transfer them to an airtight container and store at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\"]", "time_sec": 59.332, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 56.0, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "412", "question": "how do i breed a shugabeats in my singing monsters", "gt": "To breed a Shugabeats in My Singing Monsters, you must first obtain the Shugabush Monster from another island. This will turn your Shugabush Monsters into an egg. After hatching them, buy the breeding structure. Then, use the Shugabush and the Furcorn in the Shugabush Island breeding structure to get the Shugabeats monster. The order of the monsters does not matter.", "pred": "To breed a Shugabeats in My Singing Monsters, you need to obtain the Shugabush Monster from the other island. Then, you will need to hatch the Shugabush Monster and buy the breeding structure. Once you have the breeding structure, you can breed the Shugabeats by using the Shugabush and the Furcorn in the Shugabush Island breeding structure. Keep in mind that the breeding order (left or right) does not matter.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "To breed a Shugabeats in My Singing Monsters, you need to obtain the Shugabush Monster from the other island. Then, you will need to hatch the Shugabush Monster and buy the breeding structure. Once you have the breeding structure, you can breed the Shugabeats by using the Shugabush and the Furcorn in the Shugabush Island breeding structure. Keep in mind that the breeding order (left or right) does not matter.", "cot": "[\"To breed a Shugabeats in My Singing Monsters, you need to obtain the Shugabush Monster from the other island. Then, you will need to hatch the Shugabush Monster and buy the breeding structure. Once you have the breeding structure, you can breed the Shugabeats by using the Shugabush and the Furcorn in the Shugabush Island breeding structure. Keep in mind that the breeding order (left or right) does not matter.\", \"Answer: To breed a Shugabeats in My Singing Monsters, you need to obtain the Shugabush Monster from the other island. Then, you must hatch the Shugabush Monster and buy the breeding structure. Once you have the breeding structure, you can breed the Shugabeats by using the Shugabush and the Furcorn in the Shugabush Island breeding structure. The order of the monsters in the breeding structure does not matter.\", \"Answer: To breed a Shugabeats in My Singing Monsters, obtain the Shugabush Monster from the other island, hatch it, buy the breeding structure, and then breed it using the Shugabush and the Furcorn in the Shugabush Island breeding structure.\", \"Answer: To breed a Shugabeats in My Singing Monsters, you need to obtain the Shugabush Monster from the other island. Then, you will need to hatch the Shugabush Monster and buy the breeding structure. Once you have the breeding structure, you can breed the Shugabeats by using the Shugabush and the Furcorn in the Shugabush Island breeding structure. Keep in mind that the order of the monsters (left or right) does not matter.\", \"Answer: To breed a Shugabeats in My Singing Monsters, you need to obtain the Shugabush Monster from the other island. Then, you must turn your Shugabush Monsters into an egg, hatch them, buy the breeding structure, and breed them using the Shugabush and the Furcorn in the Shugabush Island breeding structure. Breeding the monsters left or right does not matter.\"]", "time_sec": 35.206, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 62.92, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
