the point of sale, per GS1 specifications.\", \"Answer: A Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology consisting of 12 numeric digits that are uniquely assigned to each trade item, widely used in multiple countries for tracking trade items in stores and scanning at point of sale per GS1 specifications.\", \"Answer: A Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology consisting of 12 numeric digits that are uniquely assigned to each trade item. It is widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, Europe, and other countries for tracking trade items in stores and is the barcode mainly used for scanning of trade items at the point of sale, per GS1 specifications.\"]", "time_sec": 28.603, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 80.7, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.8, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "425", "question": "what is a upc description", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, an UPDescription is:\n\n1. A barcode symbology used for tracking trade items in stores, consisting of 12 numeric digits that are uniquely assigned to each trade item. (Passage 1)\n2. A process involving receiving a specimen bottle from a member, ensuring it contains a minimum of 30 milliliters of urine, initialing the label in the member's presence, and verifying the information on the label and DD Form 2624 (a military form). (Passage 2)\n3. A barcode partner that provides GS1 Originated UPC and EAN codes, as well as a toll-free number and positive customer reviews. (Passage 3)\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "pred": "The Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology consisting of 12 numeric digits that are uniquely assigned to each trade item. It is widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, Europe, and other countries for tracking trade items in stores. The UPC, specifically UPC-A, is used alongside EAN barcodes for scanning trade items at the point of sale, as specified by GS1.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology consisting of 12 numeric digits that are uniquely assigned to each trade item. It is widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, Europe, and other countries for tracking trade items in stores. The UPC, specifically UPC-A, is used alongside EAN barcodes for scanning trade items at the point of sale, as specified by GS1.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology consisting of 12 numeric digits that are uniquely assigned to each trade item. It is widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, Europe, and other countries for tracking trade items in stores. The UPC, specifically UPC-A, is used alongside EAN barcodes for scanning trade items at the point of sale, as specified by GS1.\", \"Answer: A Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology consisting of 12 numeric digits that are uniquely assigned to each trade item, widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, Europe, and other countries for tracking trade items in stores and scanning at point-of-sale per GS1 specifications, as described in passage 1.\", \"Answer: The Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology that consists of 12 numeric digits, uniquely assigned to each trade item. It is widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, Europe, and other countries for tracking trade items in stores, particularly for scanning at the point of sale per GS1 specifications. UPC is also known as UPC-A and is often used alongside the related EAN barcode.\", \"Answer: The Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology consisting of 12 numeric digits that are uniquely assigned to each trade item, widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, Europe, and other countries for tracking trade items in stores and scanning at point of sale per GS1 specifications.\", \"Answer: The Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology used for tracking trade items in stores, consisting of 12 numeric digits that are uniquely assigned to each trade item. It is widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, Europe, and other countries, and is the barcode mainly used for scanning at the point of sale, per GS1 specifications, alongside the related EAN barcode.\"]", "time_sec": 34.125, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 31.93, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "427", "question": "what is a upc description", "gt": "Based on the given passages, I am unable to answer the question \"What is a UPC description?\" as none of the passages provide a clear definition or explanation of what a UPC description is.", "pred": "The Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology that consists of 12 numeric digits uniquely assigned to each trade item, widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, Europe, and other countries for tracking trade items in stores and scanning at point-of-sale per GS1 specifications.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology that consists of 12 numeric digits uniquely assigned to each trade item, widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, Europe, and other countries for tracking trade items in stores and scanning at point-of-sale per GS1 specifications.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology that consists of 12 numeric digits uniquely assigned to each trade item, widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, Europe, and other countries for tracking trade items in stores and scanning at point-of-sale per GS1 specifications.\", \"Answer: The Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology that is widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, in Europe and other countries for tracking trade items in stores. UPC (technically refers to UPC-A) consists of 12 numeric digits, that are uniquely assigned to each trade item. Along with the related EAN barcode, the UPC is the barcode mainly used for scanning of trade items at the point of sale, per GS1 specifications.\", \"Answer: The Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology consisting of 12 numeric digits that are uniquely assigned to each trade item, widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, Europe, and other countries for tracking trade items in stores and scanning at the point of sale per GS1 specifications.\", \"Answer: The Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology consisting of 12 numeric digits that are uniquely assigned to each trade item. It is widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, Europe, and other countries for tracking trade items in stores and is the primary barcode used for scanning at the point of sale, per GS1 specifications.\", \"Answer: The Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology that consists of 12 numeric digits uniquely assigned to each trade item. It is widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, Europe, and other countries for tracking trade items in stores, particularly for scanning at the point of sale as specified by GS1.\"]", "time_sec": 32.37, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 3.64, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "428", "question": "how do twins happen", "gt": "Twins are two offspring produced by the same pregnancy. They can be either monozygotic (identical), meaning they develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal), meaning they develop from two different eggs with each being fertilized by its own sperm cell. Monoamniotic twins occur when the split takes place after the ninth day after fertilization. Conjoined twins occur when the division of the developing zygote into 2 embryos occurs within 8 days of fertilization. Dizygotic twin pregnancies are more likely in women of West African descent, especially Yoruba, aged between 30 and 40 years, of above average height and weight, and who have had several previous pregnancies.", "pred": "Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. This can occur in two ways: monozygotic (identical) twins, which develop from one zygote that splits to form two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal) twins, which develop from two different eggs fertilized by separate sperm cells.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. This can occur in two ways:  \n- **Monozygotic (identical) twins**: Develop from one zygote that splits into two embryos.  \n- **Dizygotic (fraternal) twins**: Develop from two different eggs fertilized by separate sperm cells.  \n\nAdditionally, factors such as maternal age (30–40 years), being of West African descent (especially Yoruba), greater height and weight, and prior pregnancies increase the likelihood of dizygotic twin pregnancies.", "cot": "[\"Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. This can occur in two ways:  \\n- **Monozygotic (identical) twins**: Develop from one zygote that splits into two embryos.  \\n- **Dizygotic (fraternal) twins**: Develop from two different eggs fertilized by separate sperm cells.  \\n\\nAdditionally, factors such as maternal age (30–40 years), being of West African descent (especially Yoruba), greater height and weight, and prior pregnancies increase the likelihood of dizygotic twin pregnancies.\", \"Answer: Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. This can occur in two ways: monozygotic (identical) twins, which develop from one zygote that splits to form two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal) twins, which develop from two different eggs fertilized by separate sperm cells.\", \"Answer: Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. This can occur in two ways: monozygotic (identical) twins, which develop from one zygote that splits to form two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal) twins, which develop from two different eggs fertilized by separate sperm cells.\", \"Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. This can occur in two ways:  \\n\\n1. **Monozygotic (identical) twins**: Develop from one zygote that splits into two embryos (as stated in passage 1 and passage 3).  \\n2. **Dizygotic (fraternal) twins**: Develop from two separate eggs fertilized by different sperm cells (as stated in passage 3).  \\n\\nAdditional context from passage 2 explains factors that increase the likelihood of dizygotic twins (e.g., maternal age 30–40, West African descent, higher height/weight, prior pregnancies), but the core mechanism of twinning is covered in passage 3.  \\n\\nAnswer: Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring, either through the splitting of one zygote (monozygotic/identical) or the fertilization of two separate eggs (dizygotic/fraternal).\", \"Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. They can be monozygotic (identical), which means they develop from one zygote that splits into two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal), which means they develop from two different eggs fertilized by separate sperm cells.\"]", "time_sec": 39.786, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 42.86, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "429", "question": "how do twins happen", "gt": "Twins can happen in two ways: monozygotic (identical) twins occur when one zygote splits into two embryos, while dizygotic (fraternal) twins happen when two different eggs are fertilized by two separate sperm cells. The likelihood of dizygotic twins is influenced by factors such as West African descent, being between 30 and 40 years old, being taller and heavier than average, and having had several previous pregnancies. Conjoined twins occur when the division of the zygote happens within 8 days of fertilization and can result in complications due to shared organs.", "pred": "Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. They can be either monozygotic (identical), which means they develop from one zygote that splits to form two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal), which means they develop from two different eggs that are each fertilized by its own sperm cell.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. They can be either monozygotic (identical), which means they develop from one zygote that splits to form two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal), which means they develop from two different eggs that are each fertilized by its own sperm cell.", "cot": "[\"Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. They can be either monozygotic (identical), which means they develop from one zygote that splits to form two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal), which means they develop from two different eggs that are each fertilized by its own sperm cell.\", \"Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. This can occur in two ways: \\n\\n1. Monozygotic (identical) twins: They develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos. This split typically occurs within 8 days of fertilization.\\n2. Dizygotic (fraternal) twins: They develop from two different eggs, each fertilized by its own sperm cell. Factors that increase the likelihood of dizygotic twins include a woman being of West African descent (especially Yoruba), being between 30 and 40 years old, having a greater than average height and weight, and having had several previous pregnancies.\\n\\nAnswer: Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring, either through the splitting of one zygote (monozygotic) or the fertilization of two separate eggs (dizygotic).\", \"Answer: Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. This can occur in two ways: monozygotic (identical) twins, which develop from one zygote that splits into two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal) twins, which develop from two different eggs fertilized by separate sperm cells.\", \"Answer: Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. This can occur in two ways: 1) Monozygotic (identical) twins develop when one zygote splits into two embryos, and 2) Dizygotic (fraternal) twins occur when two different eggs are fertilized by two different sperm cells.\", \"Answer: Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. This can occur in two ways: monozygotic (identical) twins, which develop from one zygote that splits to form two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal) twins, which develop from two different eggs fertilized by separate sperm cells.\"]", "time_sec": 34.964, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 35.19, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "430", "question": "how do twins happen", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how twins happen:\n\n* Monoamniotic twins: These twins occur when the split takes place after the ninth day after fertilization.\n* Conjoined twins: When the division of the developing zygote into 2 embryos occurs, it happens within 8 days of fertilization, and mortality is highest for conjoined twins due to the many complications resulting from shared organs.\n* Dizygotic twin pregnancies: These twins are slightly more likely to occur in women who are of West African descent (especially Yoruba), between the ages of 30 and 40, are taller and heavier than average, and have had several previous pregnancies.\n* Identical twins (monozygotic): These twins develop from one zygote, which splits and forms two embryos.\n* Fraternal twins (dizygotic): These twins develop from two different eggs, and each twin is fertilized by its own sperm cell.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages: The passage does not provide information on the exact timing or mechanism of twinning beyond what is mentioned above.", "pred": "Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. This can occur in two ways: monozygotic (identical) twins, which develop from one zygote that splits into two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal) twins, which develop from two different eggs fertilized by separate sperm cells.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. This can occur in two ways: monozygotic (identical) twins, which develop from one zygote that splits into two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal) twins, which develop from two different eggs fertilized by separate sperm cells.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. This can occur in two ways: monozygotic (identical) twins, which develop from one zygote that splits into two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal) twins, which develop from two different eggs fertilized by separate sperm cells.\", \"Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. This can occur in two ways: monozygotic (identical) twins, which develop from one zygote that splits to form two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal) twins, which develop from two different eggs fertilized by separate sperm cells.\", \"Answer: Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. This can occur in two ways: monozygotic (identical) twins, which develop from one zygote that splits to form two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal) twins, which develop from two different eggs fertilized by separate sperm cells.\", \"Answer: Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. This can occur in two ways: monozygotic (identical) twins, which develop from one zygote that splits into two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal) twins, which develop from two different eggs fertilized by separate sperm cells.\", \"Answer: Twins happen when a single pregnancy results in two offspring. This can occur in two ways: monozygotic (identical) twins, which develop from one zygote that splits into two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal) twins, which develop from two different eggs fertilized by separate sperm cells.\"]", "time_sec": 26.908, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 32.68, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "431", "question": "how to clean tarnished jewelry at home", "gt": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, first determine the extent of the tarnish. If the jewelry is slightly dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip a soft cloth into the solution. Rub the jewelry with the cloth, rinse in cool water, and buff with a cloth until dry. If the tarnish is heavier, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the jewelry and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. If your silver jewelry is lightly tarnished, you can also try submerging it in a hot saltwater bath. Ensure that if your jewelry has gemstones, they won't be affected by the saltwater. For diamonds, clean with warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush, and pat dry with a soft cloth.", "pred": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, first, for items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water. Dip a soft cloth into the solution, rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, create a paste using three parts baking soda and one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth, avoiding paper towels to prevent scratching. Alternatively, you can use a salt bath: pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, ensuring there's enough to cover the jewelry. This method gently removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If the jewelry is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should effectively remove the tarnish. For multiple pieces, use more water; for a single piece, use less. If the jewelry contains gemstones, verify that they won't be affected by submersion in the saltwater solution.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, first, for items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water. Dip a soft cloth into the solution, rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, create a paste using three parts baking soda and one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth, avoiding paper towels to prevent scratching. Alternatively, you can use a salt bath: pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, ensuring there's enough to cover the jewelry. This method gently removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If the jewelry is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should effectively remove the tarnish. For multiple pieces, use more water; for a single piece, use less. If the jewelry contains gemstones, verify that they won't be affected by submersion in the saltwater solution.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, first, for items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water. Dip a soft cloth into the solution, rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, create a paste using three parts baking soda and one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth, avoiding paper towels to prevent scratching. Alternatively, you can use a salt bath: pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, ensuring there's enough to cover the jewelry. This method gently removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If the jewelry is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should effectively remove the tarnish. For multiple pieces, use more water; for a single piece, use less. If the jewelry contains gemstones, verify that they won't be affected by submersion in the saltwater solution.\", \"Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip a soft cloth into the solution. Rub the jewelry, rinse in cool water, and buff with a cloth until dry for items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor. For heavier tarnish, create a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels). Alternatively, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, cover the jewelry, and use a salt bath to gently remove tarnish without abrading the silver; ensure gemstones are not affected by the saltwater solution.\", \"Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, for items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth in the solution, rub the jewelry, rinse in cool water, and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. Alternatively, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, submerge the jewelry (ensuring gemstones won't be affected by saltwater), and let it sit until the tarnish lifts.\", \"Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, for items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth in the solution, rub the jewelry, rinse in cool water, and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, create a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels). Alternatively, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl to cover the jewelry, add salt (implied for a salt bath), and submerge the jewelry; ensure gemstones are not affected by saltwater. For diamonds, use warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush, then pat dry with a soft cloth.\", \"Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, for items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth in the solution, rub the jewelry, rinse in cool water, and buff until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. Alternatively, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, add enough salt to cover the jewelry, submerge the jewelry, and let it sit until the tarnish lifts; ensure gemstones are not damaged by saltwater. Always pat dry with a soft cloth.\"]", "time_sec": 62.446, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 64.36, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "432", "question": "how to clean tarnished jewelry at home", "gt": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few different methods based on the passages provided. For silver jewelry, you can mix mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth into the mixture, and rub the jewelry. Then rinse it in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. If the tarnish is heavier, you can make a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, apply it to the silver with a soft cloth, and rinse it off. For diamonds, you can clean them with warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush, then pat them dry with a soft cloth. If you have silver jewelry with gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "pred": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, for items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth in the solution, rub the jewelry, rinse in cool water, and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, create a paste using three parts baking soda and one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. Alternatively, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl to cover the jewelry, add enough salt to create a bath, and submerge the jewelry; this gentle method removes tarnish without abrasion, but ensure gemstones are not affected by saltwater.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, for items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth in the solution, rub the jewelry, rinse in cool water, and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, create a paste using three parts baking soda and one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. Alternatively, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl to cover the jewelry, add enough salt to create a bath, and submerge the jewelry; this gentle method removes tarnish without abrasion, but ensure gemstones are not affected by saltwater.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, for items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth in the solution, rub the jewelry, rinse in cool water, and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, create a paste using three parts baking soda and one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. Alternatively, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl to cover the jewelry, add enough salt to create a bath, and submerge the jewelry; this gentle method removes tarnish without abrasion, but ensure gemstones are not affected by saltwater.\", \"Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few methods. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth in the solution, rub the jewelry, rinse in cool water, and buff until dry. For heavier tarnish, create a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. Another method involves pouring two cups of hot water into a bowl, adding enough salt to cover the jewelry, and submerging the jewelry in the solution to remove tarnish without abrading the silver. If the jewelry has gemstones, ensure they won't be affected by the saltwater solution.\", \"Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, for items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip a soft cloth in the solution. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch). Alternatively, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, ensuring enough to cover the jewelry, and use a salt bath to remove tarnish gently without abrading the silver. If the jewelry has gemstones, ensure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\", \"Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use one of the following methods: For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch). Alternatively, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, ensuring enough to cover the jewelry. This salt bath method gently removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\", \"Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use several methods. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip a soft cloth in the solution. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, create a paste by mixing three parts baking soda with one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch). Additionally, you can use a salt bath: pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, ensuring there's enough to cover the jewelry. This gentle cleanser removes tarnish without abrading the silver. For lightly tarnished jewelry, the salt bath should effectively remove the tarnish. When cleaning a lot of jewelry at once, use more water, and for a single piece, use less water. If your jewelry has gemstones, ensure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\"]", "time_sec": 65.929, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 54.22, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "433", "question": "how to clean tarnished jewelry at home", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are some ways to clean tarnished jewelry at home:\n\n1. Mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip a soft cloth to shine silver jewelry that is merely dull or starting to discolor. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry.\n2. Use warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush to clean diamonds. Pat the jewelry dry with a soft cloth.\n3. Soak tarnished silver jewelry in a saltwater solution of two cups of hot water. This method is gentle and can remove tarnish without abrading the silver. Make sure the gemstones will not be affected by the solution before submerging them.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages. The passages do not provide information on how to clean other types of jewelry, such as gold or platinum, or what to do if the tarnish is heavy or if the jewelry has intricate designs that may be damaged during cleaning.", "pred": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few methods based on the type of tarnish and jewelry material. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth in the solution, rub the jewelry, rinse in cool water, and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, create a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth, avoiding paper towels to prevent scratches. Alternatively, you can use a salt bath by pouring two cups of hot water into a bowl, ensuring enough water to cover the jewelry, and submerging the jewelry to remove light tarnish. However, if the jewelry has gemstones, check if they will be affected by the saltwater solution before submerging.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few methods based on the type of tarnish and jewelry material. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth in the solution, rub the jewelry, rinse in cool water, and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, create a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth, avoiding paper towels to prevent scratches. Alternatively, you can use a salt bath by pouring two cups of hot water into a bowl, ensuring enough water to cover the jewelry, and submerging the jewelry to remove light tarnish. However, if the jewelry has gemstones, check if they will be affected by the saltwater solution before submerging.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few methods based on the type of tarnish and jewelry material. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth in the solution, rub the jewelry, rinse in cool water, and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, create a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth, avoiding paper towels to prevent scratches. Alternatively, you can use a salt bath by pouring two cups of hot water into a bowl, ensuring enough water to cover the jewelry, and submerging the jewelry to remove light tarnish. However, if the jewelry has gemstones, check if they will be affected by the saltwater solution before submerging.\", \"Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, for items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth in the solution, rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, create a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (avoiding paper towels to prevent scratches). Alternatively, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl to cover the jewelry, add salt to make a salt bath, and submerge the jewelry; this gentle method removes tarnish without abrasion, but ensure gemstones won't be affected by saltwater submersion. For diamond cleaning, use warm soapy water with a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush, then pat dry with a soft cloth.\", \"Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few methods. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip a soft cloth in the solution. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, create a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels). Alternatively, you can use a salt bath: pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, add enough salt to cover the jewelry, and ensure the jewelry is submerged. If the jewelry has gemstones, verify they won't be affected by saltwater. Another method involves using warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush, as recommended by Loretta Castoro, vice president and international jewelry buyer for Jewelry Television. Pat the jewelry dry with a soft cloth. Always ensure that the cleaning method is suitable for the jewelry's material to avoid damage.\", \"Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few methods. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth in the solution, rub the jewelry, rinse in cool water, and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, create a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. Another method involves pouring two cups of hot water into a bowl, adding enough salt to cover the jewelry, and submerging the jewelry in the solution. This salt bath gently removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your jewelry has gemstones, ensure they won't be affected by the saltwater solution before using this method.\", \"Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, for items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth in the solution, rub the jewelry, rinse in cool water, and buff until dry. For heavier tarnish, create a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. Alternatively, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl to cover the jewelry, add salt (as implied by \\\"salt bath\\\"), and submerge the jewelry; ensure gemstones are not affected by saltwater. For diamonds, use warm soapy water with a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush, then pat dry with a soft cloth.\"]", "time_sec": 71.627, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 52.83, "GAVIE-Rel": 5.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 4.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "434", "question": "how to clean tarnished jewelry at home", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's a brief answer to the question \"How to clean tarnished jewelry at home\":\n\nTo clean tarnished jewelry at home, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip a soft cloth in the solution. Rub the jewelry gently, then rinse it in cool water and buff it with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water and apply it with a soft, lint-free cloth. You can also use a saltwater solution by pouring two cups of hot water into a bowl and submerging the jewelry in it. Let it sit for a while, then remove and rinse it with cool water. Be sure to use a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush to clean diamonds.", "pred": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few different methods. For items that are merely dull or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth in the solution, and rub the jewelry. Rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, make a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. Alternatively, you can pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, add enough salt to cover the jewelry, and let it soak. This salt bath method gently removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your jewelry has gemstones, ensure they won't be affected by the saltwater solution before using this method.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few different methods. For items that are merely dull or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth in the solution, and rub the jewelry. Rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, make a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. Alternatively, you can pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, add enough salt to cover the jewelry, and let it soak. This salt bath method gently removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your jewelry has gemstones, ensure they won't be affected by the saltwater solution before using this method.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few different methods. For items that are merely dull or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth in the solution, and rub the jewelry. Rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, make a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. Alternatively, you can pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, add enough salt to cover the jewelry, and let it soak. This salt bath method gently removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your jewelry has gemstones, ensure they won't be affected by the saltwater solution before using this method.\", \"Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, for items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth in the solution, rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, create a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (avoiding paper towels to prevent scratching). Alternatively, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl to cover the jewelry, add salt (implied for a salt bath), and submerge the jewelry; this gentle method removes tarnish without abrasion, but ensure gemstones are not affected by saltwater submersion.\", \"Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, for items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip a soft cloth, then rub the jewelry, rinse in cool water, and buff until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels). Alternatively, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, submerge the jewelry (ensuring gemstones won't be affected by saltwater), and let it sit until the tarnish lifts.\", \"Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, for items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip a soft cloth in the solution. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch). Alternatively, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, ensuring enough to cover the jewelry, and use a salt bath to remove tarnish without abrading the silver. If the jewelry has gemstones, check that they won't be affected by submersion in a saltwater solution. For diamonds, use warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush, then pat dry with a soft cloth.\", \"Answer: To clean tarnished jewelry at home, for items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip a soft cloth in the solution. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels). Alternatively, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, add enough salt to cover the jewelry, and submerge the jewelry (ensuring gemstones won't be affected by saltwater) to gently remove tarnish without abrasion.\"]", "time_sec": 62.229, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 62.15, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "435", "question": "how to find someone in france", "gt": "To find someone in France, you can use a website for people search in France. Sending an email or letter to the Town Hall in the person's city of birth can also be helpful, especially if you have lost relatives or friends. Try to find out the person's middle name, it could make the search easier. You can also use the French white pages, available in English at www.pagesjaunes.fr. Enter the person's last name in the 'Nom' field and first name in the 'Prenom' field, enter address information if known, and then click 'Trouver' to search. For searching in Paris and its suburbs, instead of typing Paris in the City field, write IDF (or Ile de France) in the Dept district or region field. Posting a missed connection on Paris Craigslist might also be helpful.", "pred": "To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Jaunes). Visit www.pagesjaunes.fr, enter the person's last name in the \"Nom\" field and their first name in the \"Prenom\" field. If you know their address, enter it in the \"Ou\" section; otherwise, leave it blank to search the entire country. Click \"Trouver\" to search. For searching in Paris and its suburbs, enter \"IDF\" (Ile de France) in the \"Dept district\" or \"region\" field instead of \"Paris\" in the \"City\" field. If you have lost relatives in cities like Paris, Avignon, Bordeaux, Lyon, or Nice, you can also send an email or letter to the town hall in their city of birth, and try to find out the person's middle name to make the search easier.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Jaunes). Visit www.pagesjaunes.fr, enter the person's last name in the \"Nom\" field and their first name in the \"Prenom\" field. If you know their address, enter it in the \"Ou\" section; otherwise, leave it blank to search the entire country. Click \"Trouver\" to search. For searching in Paris and its suburbs, enter \"IDF\" (Ile de France) in the \"Dept district\" or \"region\" field instead of \"Paris\" in the \"City\" field. If you have lost relatives in cities like Paris, Avignon, Bordeaux, Lyon, or Nice, you can also send an email or letter to the town hall in their city of birth, and try to find out the person's middle name to make the search easier.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Jaunes). Visit www.pagesjaunes.fr, enter the person's last name in the \\\"Nom\\\" field and their first name in the \\\"Prenom\\\" field. If you know their address, enter it in the \\\"Ou\\\" section; otherwise, leave it blank to search the entire country. Click \\\"Trouver\\\" to search. For searching in Paris and its suburbs, enter \\\"IDF\\\" (Ile de France) in the \\\"Dept district\\\" or \\\"region\\\" field instead of \\\"Paris\\\" in the \\\"City\\\" field. If you have lost relatives in cities like Paris, Avignon, Bordeaux, Lyon, or Nice, you can also send an email or letter to the town hall in their city of birth, and try to find out the person's middle name to make the search easier.\", \"Answer: To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Jaunes). Visit www.pagesjaunes.fr, enter the person's last name in the \\\"Nom\\\" field and their first name in the \\\"Prenom\\\" field. If you know their address, enter it in the \\\"Ou\\\" section; otherwise, leave it blank to search the entire country. Additionally, you can search by using \\\"IDF\\\" (Ile de France) in the \\\"Dept\\\" district or region field instead of \\\"Paris\\\" to include Paris and its suburbs. If you have lost contact with a relative born in a specific French city or town, you may also send an email or letter to the town hall in their city of birth. Ensure you gather detailed information such as the person's middle name to improve the chances of finding the correct individual.\", \"Answer: To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Jaunes). Visit www.pagesjaunes.fr and enter the person's last name in the \\\"Nom\\\" field and their first name in the \\\"Prenom\\\" field if known. If you know their address, enter it in the \\\"Ou\\\" section; otherwise, leave it blank to search the entire country. Click \\\"Trouver\\\" to search. For searching in Paris and its suburbs, use \\\"IDF\\\" (Ile de France) in the \\\"Dept\\\" or \\\"region\\\" field instead of \\\"Paris\\\" in the \\\"City\\\" field. Additionally, you can send an email or letter to the town hall in the city of birth if you know it, especially if the person was born there and you have lost contact for many years.\", \"Answer: To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Blanches) at www.pagesjaunes.fr. Enter the person's last name in the \\\"Nom\\\" field and their first name in the \\\"Prenom\\\" field if known. If you have an address, enter it in the \\\"Ou\\\" section; otherwise, leave it blank to search the entire country. Click \\\"Trouver\\\" to search. Additionally, you can try sending an email or letter to the town hall in the city where the person was born, especially if you know their middle name, which can be very helpful. For searching in Paris and its suburbs, use \\\"IDF\\\" (Ile de France) in the \\\"Dept\\\" or region field instead of \\\"Paris\\\" on the French white pages site.\", \"Answer: To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Blanches) at www.pagesjaunes.fr. Enter the person's last name in the \\\"Nom\\\" field and their first name in the \\\"Prenom\\\" field if known. If you have an address, input it in the \\\"Ou\\\" section; otherwise, leave it blank to search the entire country. Click \\\"Trouver\\\" to view search results, which include names, addresses, and phone numbers. For Paris and its suburbs, use \\\"IDF\\\" or \\\"Ile de France\\\" in the department or region field instead of \\\"Paris.\\\" Additionally, you can send an email or letter to the town hall in the person's city of birth if you know it, though this method may not be effective if you haven't seen the person in over ten or twenty years.\"]", "time_sec": 65.373, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 72.22, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "436", "question": "how to find someone in france", "gt": "To find someone in France, you can use the website www.pagesjaunes.fr, the French version of the white pages. Enter the last name of the person in the \"Nom\" field and optionally enter their first name in the \"Prenom\" field. If you know the address, you can enter it in the \"Ou\" section. Leave it blank if you don't know the address. Click on \"Trouver\" to search for the name. The search results will display the name, address, and phone number of the matching listings.", "pred": "To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Jaunes) by visiting www.pagesjaunes.fr. Enter the person’s last name in the “Nom” field and, if known, their first name in the “Prenom” field. You may also add address details in the “Ou” section or leave it blank to search the entire country. For a broader search in Paris and its suburbs, substitute “IDF” (Île-de-France) for “Paris” in the department/region field. Additionally, you can contact the town hall in the person’s city of birth (e.g., Paris, Avignon, Bordeaux, Lyon, Nice) via email or letter, especially if you know their birthplace and middle name, which can aid identification.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Jaunes) by visiting www.pagesjaunes.fr. Enter the person’s last name in the “Nom” field and, if known, their first name in the “Prenom” field. You may also add address details in the “Ou” section or leave it blank to search the entire country. For a broader search in Paris and its suburbs, substitute “IDF” (Île-de-France) for “Paris” in the department/region field. Additionally, you can contact the town hall in the person’s city of birth (e.g., Paris, Avignon, Bordeaux, Lyon, Nice) via email or letter, especially if you know their birthplace and middle name, which can aid identification.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Jaunes) by visiting www.pagesjaunes.fr. Enter the person’s last name in the “Nom” field and, if known, their first name in the “Prenom” field. You may also add address details in the “Ou” section or leave it blank to search the entire country. For a broader search in Paris and its suburbs, substitute “IDF” (Île-de-France) for “Paris” in the department/region field. Additionally, you can contact the town hall in the person’s city of birth (e.g., Paris, Avignon, Bordeaux, Lyon, Nice) via email or letter, especially if you know their birthplace and middle name, which can aid identification.\", \"Answer: To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Jaunes) at www.pagesjaunes.fr. Enter the person's last name in the \\\"Nom\\\" field and their first name in the \\\"Prenom\\\" field. If you know their address, input it in the \\\"Ou\\\" section; otherwise, leave it blank to search the entire country. Additionally, for searching in Paris and its suburbs, use \\\"IDF\\\" or \\\"Ile de France\\\" in the \\\"Dept district or region\\\" field instead of \\\"Paris\\\" in the City field. If the person was born in a specific French city like Paris, Avignon, Bordeaux, Lyon, or Nice, you can also contact the town hall in their city of birth via email or letter, especially if you know their middle name for better identification.\", \"Answer: To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Jaunes) by visiting www.pagesjaunes.fr. Enter the person’s last name in the Nom field and their first name in the Prenom field if known. If you know their address, input it in the Ou section; otherwise, leave it blank to search the entire country. Click Trouver to initiate the search, which will display the name, address, and phone number of all matching listings. For more targeted searches, use IDF (Ile de France) in the Dept or region field instead of typing Paris, and consider sending an email or letter to the town hall in the city where the person was born if you have that information. Additionally, you may try posting a missed connection on Paris Craigslist (http://paris.craigslist.org) or contacting the town hall directly for assistance.\", \"Answer: To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Blanches) by visiting www.pagesjaunes.fr. Enter the person's last name in the Nom field and their first name in the Prenom field if known. You can also provide an address in the Ou section or leave it blank to search the entire country. Additionally, you can send an email or letter to the town hall in the city where the person was born, especially if you have lost contact for over ten or twenty years. For searching in Paris and its suburbs, use IDF (Ile de France) in the Dept district or region field instead of Paris.\", \"Answer: To find someone in France, visit www.pagesjaunes.fr (the French white pages), enter the person’s last name in the “Nom” field and their first name in the “Prenom” field, and optionally add address details in the “Ou” field; you can also search by using “IDF” (Ile de France) instead of “Paris” in the region/department field. Additionally, you may contact the town hall in the city where the person was born, especially if you have lost contact for many years, and consider sending an email, letter, or posting a missed connection on Paris Craigslist.\"]", "time_sec": 59.286, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 46.15, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "438", "question": "how to find someone in france", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are the steps to find someone in France:\n\n1. Check the French White Pages (in English): You can use the website pagesjaunes.fr to search for people in France. To search for someone in Paris or its suburbs, enter \"IDF\" (Ile de France) in the department or region field instead of typing Paris.\n2. Use the nom field to enter the last name of the person you are looking for, and you can also include their first name in the prenom field.\n3. Provide address information, if known, in the ou section to refine your search.\n4. Click \"trouver\" to search for the name, and the search results will display name, address, and phone number for all listings that match your search criteria.\n\nNote that the effectiveness of these methods may depend on how up-to-date the information in the white pages is and whether the person you are looking for has listed their contact information publicly.", "pred": "To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Jaunes) by visiting www.pagesjaunes.fr. Enter the person's last name in the \"Nom\" field and their first name in the \"Prenom\" field if known. If you have an address, input it in the \"Ou\" section; otherwise, leave it blank to search the entire country. Additionally, you can try sending an email or letter to the town hall in the city of birth if the person was born there. For Paris and its suburbs, enter \"IDF\" (Ile de France) in the \"Dept\" or \"region\" field instead of \"Paris\" in the City field. If you've lost contact for over ten or twenty years, finding the person's middle name can help narrow down the search.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Jaunes) by visiting www.pagesjaunes.fr. Enter the person's last name in the \"Nom\" field and their first name in the \"Prenom\" field if known. If you have an address, input it in the \"Ou\" section; otherwise, leave it blank to search the entire country. Additionally, you can try sending an email or letter to the town hall in the city of birth if the person was born there. For Paris and its suburbs, enter \"IDF\" (Ile de France) in the \"Dept\" or \"region\" field instead of \"Paris\" in the City field. If you've lost contact for over ten or twenty years, finding the person's middle name can help narrow down the search.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Jaunes) by visiting www.pagesjaunes.fr. Enter the person's last name in the \\\"Nom\\\" field and their first name in the \\\"Prenom\\\" field if known. If you have an address, input it in the \\\"Ou\\\" section; otherwise, leave it blank to search the entire country. Additionally, you can try sending an email or letter to the town hall in the city of birth if the person was born there. For Paris and its suburbs, enter \\\"IDF\\\" (Ile de France) in the \\\"Dept\\\" or \\\"region\\\" field instead of \\\"Paris\\\" in the City field. If you've lost contact for over ten or twenty years, finding the person's middle name can help narrow down the search.\", \"Answer: To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Jaunes). Enter the person's last name in the \\\"Nom\\\" field and their first name in the \\\"Prenom\\\" field. If you know their address, input it in the \\\"Ou\\\" section; otherwise, leave it blank to search the entire country. Click \\\"Trouver\\\" to search. Additionally, you can try searching on Paris Craigslist or use IDF (Ile de France) in the region field instead of Paris to search in Paris and its suburbs. If you have lost relatives in cities like Paris, Avignon, Bordeaux, Lyon, or Nice, you can send an email or letter to the town hall in their city of birth, but be aware that without specific details like a middle name, it may be difficult to confirm the right person.\", \"Answer: To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Jaunes) by visiting www.pagesjaunes.fr. Enter the person's last name in the \\\"Nom\\\" field and their first name in the \\\"Prenom\\\" field if known. If you have an address, input it in the \\\"Ou\\\" section; otherwise, leave it blank to search the entire country. For searching in Paris and its suburbs, enter \\\"IDF\\\" (Ile de France) in the \\\"Dept\\\" or region field instead of \\\"Paris.\\\" Additionally, you can contact the town hall in the city where the person was born by sending an email or letter, especially if you know their middle name to narrow down the search. If the person has been missing for over ten or twenty years, visual identification from search results may not be helpful. You can also try posting a missed connection on Paris Craigslist or sending an email/letter to the town hall in the city of birth for further assistance.\", \"Answer: To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Jaunes). Visit www.pagesjaunes.fr, enter the person's last name in the Nom field and their first name in the Prenom field if known. If you know their address, enter it in the Ou section; otherwise, leave it blank to search the entire country. For Paris and its suburbs, enter IDF (Ile de France) in the Dept district or region field instead of typing Paris. You can also send an email or letter to the town hall in the city where the person was born if you know that information, and try to find their middle name to make the search easier. If you've lost contact for over ten or twenty years, the search results may not be helpful without additional identifying details.\", \"Answer: To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Jaunes) by visiting www.pagesjaunes.fr. Enter the person's last name in the \\\"Nom\\\" field and their first name in the \\\"Prenom\\\" field if known. If you know their address, input it in the \\\"Ou\\\" section; otherwise, leave it blank to search the entire country. Click \\\"Trouver\\\" to search, which will display name, address, and phone number for matching listings. For Paris and its suburbs, use \\\"IDF\\\" (Ile de France) in the Dept district or region field instead of \\\"Paris\\\" in the City field. Additionally, you can send an email or letter to the town hall in the city of birth if you know it, though this may not be effective if you haven't seen the person for over ten or twenty years.\"]", "time_sec": 67.162, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 47.06, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "439", "question": "how to find someone in france", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how to find someone in France:\n\n1. Use a people search website specifically designed for France, such as People Search France.\n2. Check the French white pages (in English) at PagesJaunes.fr. Instead of typing Paris in the City field, write IDF (or Ile de France) in the Dept district or region field to search for the person in Paris and its suburbs.\n3. Post a missed connection (MC) on Paris Craigslist.\n4. Visit the Pages Blanches (the French version of the white pages) at PagesJaunes.fr. Enter the last name of the person you're searching for in the Nom field, and optionally enter a first name in the Prenom field. Enter address information, if known, in the Ou section. Click Trouver to search for the name. The search results will display name, address, and phone number for all listings that match your search criteria.", "pred": "To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Jaunes) at www.pagesjaunes.fr. Enter the person's last name in the \"Nom\" field and their first name in the \"Prenom\" field. If you know their address, enter it in the \"Ou\" section; otherwise, leave it blank to search the entire country. For searching in Paris and its suburbs, enter \"IDF\" or \"Ile de France\" in the \"Dept district or region\" field instead of \"Paris\" in the \"City\" field. Additionally, you can contact the town hall in the city of birth if you know it and send an email or letter, especially if the person was born there. However, if you haven't seen the person for over ten or twenty years, the search results may not be helpful without additional information like the person's middle name.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (Pages Jaunes) at www.pagesjaunes.fr. Enter the person's last name in 