{"id": "450", "question": "how is a caldera different from a crater", "gt": "A caldera is a large depression caused by volcanic activity when a chamber of magma and lava gets emptied. This cavity creates pressure and the overground rocks collapse to create a large depression. On the other hand, a volcanic crater is a bowl-like structure at the top of a volcano around the opening that is used for the eruption of magma and lava. This is a depression that is the result of sinking in of the rocks because of high pressure and it typically has the opening through which lava and ash flows upwards. Craters can also be caused by impacts from meteorites, and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across.", "pred": "A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while a crater is a bowl like structure at the top of a volcano around the opening that is used for eruption of magma and lava, which is the result of sinking in of the rocks because of high pressure. The main difference between craters and calderas is how they are formed; craters are caused by impacts from meteorites, and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across, whereas calderas are formed by the collapse of a magma chamber.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while a crater is a bowl like structure at the top of a volcano around the opening that is used for eruption of magma and lava, which is the result of sinking in of the rocks because of high pressure. The main difference between craters and calderas is how they are formed; craters are caused by impacts from meteorites, and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across, whereas calderas are formed by the collapse of a magma chamber.", "cot": "[\"Answer: A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while a crater is a bowl like structure at the top of a volcano around the opening that is used for eruption of magma and lava, which is the result of sinking in of the rocks because of high pressure. The main difference between craters and calderas is how they are formed; craters are caused by impacts from meteorites, and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across, whereas calderas are formed by the collapse of a magma chamber.\", \"Answer: A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while a crater is a bowl-like structure at the top of a volcano around the opening used for eruption of magma and lava, formed by the sinking of rocks due to high pressure. The main difference is that calderas are formed by the collapse of a magma chamber, whereas craters are typically formed by meteorite impacts or the sinking of rocks due to volcanic activity.\", \"Answer: A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while a crater is a bowl like structure at the top of a volcano around the opening used for eruption of magma and lava, formed by the sinking in of rocks due to high pressure. The main difference between the two is how they are formed: craters are caused by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across, while calderas are formed by the collapse of a magma chamber and are at least one km across.\", \"Answer: Calderas are large circular depressions formed when a volcanic magma chamber collapses, while craters are bowl-shaped structures at the top of a volcano formed by the sinking of rocks due to high pressure or meteorite impacts. The main difference is in their formation: calderas result from magma chamber collapse, whereas craters can form from volcanic activity or meteorite impacts.\", \"Answer: A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while a crater is a bowl-like structure at the top of a volcano around the opening used for eruption of magma and lava, formed by the sinking of rocks due to high pressure, and is typically caused by impacts from meteorites. Calderas are formed by the collapse of a magma chamber, whereas craters are formed by the sinking of rocks or meteorite impacts.\"]", "time_sec": 41.707, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 58.27, "GAVIE-Rel": 7.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 5.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "451", "question": "how is a caldera different from a crater", "gt": "A caldera is a large depression created by volcanic activity when a chamber of magma and lava empties, causing the overlying rocks to collapse. It is formed by the collapsing of the ground. On the other hand, a crater is a bowl-shaped structure at the top of a volcano around the opening used for the eruption of magma and lava. It is formed by the sinking in of rocks due to high pressure.", "pred": "A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while a crater is a bowl‑like structure at the top of a volcano around the opening used for eruption, formed by the sinking of rocks due to high pressure (and sometimes by meteorite impacts). Calderas result from the collapse of a magma chamber, whereas craters are formed by rock sinking or, in the case of impact craters, by meteorite collisions.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while a crater is a bowl‑like structure at the top of a volcano around the opening used for eruption, formed by the sinking of rocks due to high pressure (and sometimes by meteorite impacts). Calderas result from the collapse of a magma chamber, whereas craters are formed by rock sinking or, in the case of impact craters, by meteorite collisions.", "cot": "[\"Answer: A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while a crater is a bowl‑like structure at the top of a volcano around the opening used for eruption, formed by the sinking of rocks due to high pressure (and sometimes by meteorite impacts). Calderas result from the collapse of a magma chamber, whereas craters are formed by rock sinking or, in the case of impact craters, by meteorite collisions.\", \"Answer: A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while a crater is a bowl‑shaped depression at the top of a volcano formed by the sinking of rocks due to high pressure, and is typically created by eruption or impact processes. The passages state that calderas result from the collapse of a magma chamber, whereas craters result from high‑pressure rock sinking (and in the case of meteorite impacts, from external impact).\", \"Answer: Calderas are formed when a magma chamber collapses, creating a large (at least one km across) circular depression, while craters are typically formed by meteorite impacts and can vary in size from very small to several hundred km across.\", \"Answer: A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed when a magma chamber collapses, while a crater is a bowl-like structure at the top of a volcano formed by the sinking of rocks due to high pressure and is used for the eruption of magma and lava. The main difference between the two is how they are formed: craters are caused by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across, whereas calderas are formed by the collapse of a magma chamber and are typically larger than craters.\", \"Answer: A caldera is a large circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when a magma chamber collapses, while a volcanic crater is a bowl-like structure at the top of a volcano around the opening used for the eruption of magma and lava, formed by the sinking of rocks due to high pressure. Calderas are specifically associated with volcanic activity and are at least one km across, whereas craters can be caused by meteorite impacts and vary in size.\"]", "time_sec": 37.246, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.