{"id": "491", "question": "how can you tell who liked your tweet", "gt": "You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites such as Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, or by going to Twitter.com and logging in with your Twitter username and password. Once logged in, click on your Twitter name, located on the upper right hand corner of your account's Home page, then click on Settings and finally Blue Notifications link at the top of the screen. By setting this feature, you will receive an email notification whenever someone marks one of your tweets as one of their favorites.", "pred": "You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track who is favoriting your tweets or the same tweets as you, or by setting up email notifications in your Twitter account settings under the Notifications link.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track who is favoriting your tweets or the same tweets as you, or by setting up email notifications in your Twitter account settings under the Notifications link.", "cot": "[\"Answer: You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track who is favoriting your tweets or the same tweets as you, or by setting up email notifications in your Twitter account settings under the Notifications link.\", \"Answer: You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track favorited tweets and show who has favorited your tweets or the same tweets as you. Additionally, you can enable email notifications through Twitter’s settings to be notified when someone marks your tweet as a favorite.\", \"Answer: You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites such as Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track who is favoriting your tweets or the same tweets as you.\", \"Answer: You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites such as Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track who is favoriting your tweets or the same tweets as you, as mentioned in the first passage.\", \"Answer: You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track who is favoriting your tweets or the same tweets as you.\"]", "time_sec": 21.27, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 47.73, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "492", "question": "how can you tell who liked your tweet", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, there are several ways to track who likes your tweets:\n\n1. Favotter.net and Favstar.fm - These websites allow you to track who has favorited your tweets and which tweets have been favorited by multiple users. (Passage 1)\n2. Using a web-based website - There are websites that allow you to check Twitter users who favorite tweets. (Passage 2)\n3. On Twitter - You can set up your account settings to receive notifications via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as a favorite. (Passage 3)\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question is: You can use Favotter.net, Favstar.fm, or a web-based website to track who likes your tweets, or you can set up your Twitter account settings to receive email notifications.", "pred": "You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track favorited tweets and show who has favorited your tweets or the same tweets as you.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track favorited tweets and show who has favorited your tweets or the same tweets as you.", "cot": "[\"Answer: You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track favorited tweets and show who has favorited your tweets or the same tweets as you.\", \"Answer: You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track favorited tweets and show who has favorited your tweets or the same tweets as you.\", \"Answer: You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track who is favoriting your tweets or the same tweets as you. Additionally, you can set up email notifications through Twitter's Settings under the Notifications link to be informed when someone marks your tweet as a favorite.\", \"Answer: You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track favorited tweets and show who has favorited your tweets or the same tweets as you.\", \"Answer: You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track favorited tweets and show you who has favorited your tweets or the same tweets as you.\"]", "time_sec": 20.957, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 30.77, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "493", "question": "how can you tell who liked your tweet", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how you can tell who liked your tweet:\n\nPassage 1 suggests using websites such as Favotter.net and Favstar.fm to track who has favorited your tweets. These websites show you a list of users who have favorited your tweets, allowing you to see who has shown interest in your content.\n\nPassage 2 provides a step-by-step guide on how to track who favorited your tweets. According to the passage, you can use a simple web-based website to check who favorited your tweets. Once you have access to this website, you can enter your tweet's URL to view a list of users who have favorited it.\n\nPassage 3 does not provide any information on tracking who favorited your tweets. However, it does mention that you can set up your Twitter account to notify you via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as one of their favorites.\n\nIn summary, to tell who liked your tweet, you can use websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, or set up your Twitter account to notify you via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as one of their favorites.", "pred": "You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track favorited tweets and show you who has favorited your tweets or the same tweets as you.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track favorited tweets and show you who has favorited your tweets or the same tweets as you.", "cot": "[\"Answer: You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track favorited tweets and show you who has favorited your tweets or the same tweets as you.\", \"Answer: You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track favorited tweets, or by checking Twitter's notifications as described in the instructions where you can see who favorited your tweets.\", \"Answer: You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track favorited tweets and show you who has favorited your tweets or the same tweets as you.\", \"Answer: You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track favorited tweets and show who has favorited your tweets or the same tweets as you.\", \"Answer: You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites such as Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track favorited tweets and show you who has favorited your tweets or the same tweets as you.\"]", "time_sec": 20.011, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 27.27, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "495", "question": "salary exchange pension contributions", "gt": "Salary exchange pension contributions, also known as salary sacrifice, is a method where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This can allow both employers and employees to save money. The salary that is waived under the salary exchange is equal to the value of the regular pension contribution made. For example, it could be 7.5% or 9% of the pay, depending on the pension scheme. The employer then pays a corresponding amount into the relevant pension scheme.", "pred": "Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This arrangement allows both parties to save money, as employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary, and employees receive pension contributions without directly paying for them from their salary. The salary waived is equivalent to the value of the employee’s regular pension contribution to schemes such as USS, USPAS, or SGSS, excluding additional contributions (AVCs). For instance, USS members have a regular contribution rate of 7.5% of their pay (effective from 1 October 2011), while USPAS members contribute 9% (effective from 1 August 2011). The employer then matches this waived amount by paying it into the respective pension scheme.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This arrangement allows both parties to save money, as employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary, and employees receive pension contributions without directly paying for them from their salary. The salary waived is equivalent to the value of the employee’s regular pension contribution to schemes such as USS, USPAS, or SGSS, excluding additional contributions (AVCs). For instance, USS members have a regular contribution rate of 7.5% of their pay (effective from 1 October 2011), while USPAS members contribute 9% (effective from 1 August 2011). The employer then matches this waived amount by paying it into the respective pension scheme.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This arrangement allows both parties to save money, as employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary, and employees receive pension contributions without directly paying for them from their salary. The salary waived is equivalent to the value of the employee’s regular pension contribution to schemes such as USS, USPAS, or SGSS, excluding additional contributions (AVCs). For instance, USS members have a regular contribution rate of 7.5% of their pay (effective from 1 October 2011), while USPAS members contribute 9% (effective from 1 August 2011). The employer then matches this waived amount by paying it into the respective pension scheme.\", \"Answer: Salary exchange allows employees to give up part of their salary in exchange for increased pension contributions from their employer, resulting in potential savings for both parties.\", \"Answer: Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This arrangement allows both parties to save money, as employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary, and employees receive pension contributions without directly paying for them from their salary. For example, in the University Scheme (USS), the regular contribution is 7.5% of pay from October 1, 2011, and for the University Scheme for Public Officers (USPAS), it is 9% from August 1, 2011. The amount the employee waives under the salary exchange is equal to the value of their regular pension contribution to these schemes, excluding additional contributions (AVCs), and the employer matches this amount by paying it into the relevant pension scheme.\", \"Answer: Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer, allowing both parties to save money. Employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary, while employees receive pension contributions as a non-cash benefit. The amount waived by the employee equals their regular pension contribution (excluding additional voluntary contributions) to schemes like USS (7.5% of pay from 1 October 2011) or USPAS (9% of pay from 1 August 2011), with the employer matching this amount into the relevant pension scheme.\", \"Answer: Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer, which can result in savings for both parties. Employers may pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary, and the salary waived is equivalent to the regular pension contribution to schemes such as USS, USPAS, or SGSS, excluding additional contributions like AVCs. For example, members of USS contribute 7.5% of their pay, while members of USPAS contribute 9%.\"]", "time_sec": 49.942, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 50.0, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "496", "question": "salary exchange pension contributions", "gt": "Salary exchange pension contributions allow employees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This arrangement benefits both employers and employees as employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary, and employees can save money by making pension contributions through this method. The amount of salary waived under the salary exchange is equivalent to the regular pension contribution, and the employer pays a corresponding amount into the pension scheme. The specific details of the contributions may vary depending on the pension scheme being used.", "pred": "Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money, as employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary, and employees receive pension contributions without directly paying for them from their salary. For instance, in the context of the University Scheme (USS), employees' regular pension contributions are 7.5% of their pay, and for the University Scheme for Public Sector Employees (USPAS), the regular contribution is 9% of their pay. The employer matches these contributions into the respective pension scheme, thereby facilitating a mutual saving and a secure retirement benefit for the employee.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money, as employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary, and employees receive pension contributions without directly paying for them from their salary. For instance, in the context of the University Scheme (USS), employees' regular pension contributions are 7.5% of their pay, and for the University Scheme for Public Sector Employees (USPAS), the regular contribution is 9% of their pay. The employer matches these contributions into the respective pension scheme, thereby facilitating a mutual saving and a secure retirement benefit for the employee.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money, as employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary, and employees receive pension contributions without directly paying for them from their salary. For instance, in the context of the University Scheme (USS), employees' regular pension contributions are 7.5% of their pay, and for the University Scheme for Public Sector Employees (USPAS), the regular contribution is 9% of their pay. The employer matches these contributions into the respective pension scheme, thereby facilitating a mutual saving and a secure retirement benefit for the employee.\", \"Answer: Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money, as employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary, while employees receive pension contributions without directly contributing from their salary. The salary waived is equivalent to the regular pension contribution to schemes such as USS, USPAS, or SGSS, excluding additional contributions (AVCs). For instance, USS members contribute 7.5% of their pay from October 1, 2011, and USPAS members contribute 9% from August 1, 2011. The employer then matches this waived amount into the respective pension scheme. This system is also known as salary sacrifice and is facilitated by tools like the Salary Exchange Calculator to assist in decision-making.\", \"Answer: Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer, which can lead to savings for both parties. Employers may pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary, and the salary waived by the employee is equal to the value of their regular pension contribution to schemes like USS, USPAS, or SGSS, excluding additional contributions (AVCs). For instance, members of USS contribute 7.5% of their pay (from 1 October 2011), while members of USPAS contribute 9% (from 1 August 2011), with the university matching these contributions into the respective pension schemes.\", \"Answer: Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money, as employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary, while employees receive pension contributions without direct salary reduction. For example, under this scheme, employees may forgo a portion of their salary, which the employer then uses to make corresponding pension contributions to schemes such as USS or USPAS. The amount waived by the employee equals the value of their regular pension contribution (e.g., 7.5% for USS members from October 1, 2011, or 9% for USPAS members from August 1, 2011), excluding additional voluntary contributions (AVCs). This mutual benefit structure supports retirement savings while providing financial advantages to both parties.\", \"Answer: Salary exchange is a method where employees give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension contribution made by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money, as employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary, and employees receive pension contributions without additional tax implications on the sacrificed salary. For example, in the context of the University Scheme (USS), the regular pension contribution is 7.5% of pay from October 1, 2011, and for the University Scheme for the Public Sector (USPAS), it is 9% from August 1, 2011. The salary waived by the employee is equal to the value of their regular pension contribution, excluding additional contributions (AVCs), and the employer matches this amount by paying it into the relevant pension scheme.\"]", "time_sec": 65.255, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 59.6, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "497", "question": "salary exchange pension contributions", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it appears that salary exchange is a way for employees to give up part of their salary in exchange for a pension contribution made by their employer. The salary that is waived is equal to the value of the employee's regular pension contribution, minus any additional contributions. The employer then pays the corresponding amount into the relevant pension scheme. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to potentially save money through reduced national insurance contributions.", "pred": "Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer, resulting in potential savings for both parties. Employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to reduced employee salaries, while the employee's waived salary amount is matched by the employer's contribution to the pension scheme (e.g., 7.5% of pay for USS members or 9% for USPAS members), excluding additional contributions (AVCs). This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money through reduced taxable income and lower NICs.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer, resulting in potential savings for both parties. Employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to reduced employee salaries, while the employee's waived salary amount is matched by the employer's contribution to the pension scheme (e.g., 7.5% of pay for USS members or 9% for USPAS members), excluding additional contributions (AVCs). This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money through reduced taxable income and lower NICs.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer, resulting in potential savings for both parties. Employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to reduced employee salaries, while the employee's waived salary amount is matched by the employer's contribution to the pension scheme (e.g., 7.5% of pay for USS members or 9% for USPAS members), excluding additional contributions (AVCs). This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money through reduced taxable income and lower NICs.\", \"Answer: Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money, as employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary, while employees receive pension contributions without directly paying for them from their salary. For example, in the context of University Scheme for Superannuation (USS) members, the regular contribution is 7.5% of pay from October 1, 2011, and for University Scheme for Public Service and Local Government (USPAS) members, it is 9% from August 1, 2011. The amount the employee waives in salary is equal to the value of their regular pension contribution to USS, USPAS, or SGSS (excluding Additional Voluntary Contributions), and the University then pays a corresponding amount into the relevant pension scheme.\", \"Answer: Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money, as employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary, and employees receive pension contributions without additional tax implications on the sacrificed salary. For example, in the context of the University Scheme (USS), employees may forgo a salary amount equivalent to 7.5% of their pay (effective from 1 October 2011) in exchange for the University contributing the same amount into their pension scheme. Similarly, for members of USPAS, the regular contribution is 9% of their pay (effective from 1 August 2011), which is exchanged for the University's corresponding pension contribution. This method of pension contribution is also referred to as salary sacrifice.\", \"Answer: Salary exchange is a method where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension contribution paid by their employer, allowing both parties to save money. Employers benefit from lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to reduced salary, while employees receive enhanced pension contributions. For instance, under this arrangement, employees may forgo a portion of their salary, and the employer matches this by contributing an equivalent amount to the employee’s pension scheme (e.g., USS or USPAS), often at rates such as 7.5% or 9% of the employee’s pay, depending on the scheme and effective dates. This setup is designed to facilitate savings for retirement while providing tax and financial advantages to both employer and employee.\", \"Answer: Salary exchange allows employees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension contribution made by their employer. This arrangement enables both employers and employees to save money, as employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to reduced employee salaries. For example, under this scheme, an employee’s waived salary amount equals their regular pension contribution to schemes like USS (7.5% of pay from October 1, 2011), USPAS (9% of pay from August 1, 2011), or SGSS, excluding additional voluntary contributions (AVCs). The employer then matches this waived amount as a pension payment into the relevant scheme.\"]", "time_sec": 61.709, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 55.17, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "501", "question": "factors that cause oily skin", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the factors that cause oily skin are primarily genetic/hereditary factors and hormonal shifts in the body. Diet and poor hygiene/topical applications have a limited effect on the oiliness of the skin. Combination skin, which is a mix of oily and dry/normal areas, can also contribute to oily skin in certain areas such as the T-zone.", "pred": "Oily skin is primarily caused by genetic factors, as it is hereditary—if a parent has oily skin, there is a high likelihood the child will too. Hormonal shifts in the body can also contribute, especially when oily skin occurs intermittently. While diet may aggravate acne, it does not cause oily skin, and common myths linking oily foods, chocolate, peanut butter, or alcohol to increased oil production are not supported by the passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer:Oily skin is primarily caused by genetic factors, as it is hereditary—if a parent has oily skin, there is a high likelihood the child will too. Hormonal shifts in the body can also contribute, especially when oily skin occurs intermittently. While diet may aggravate acne, it does not cause oily skin, and common myths linking oily foods, chocolate, peanut butter, or alcohol to increased oil production are not supported by the passages.", "cot": "[\"Answer:Oily skin is primarily caused by genetic factors, as it is hereditary—if a parent has oily skin, there is a high likelihood the child will too. Hormonal shifts in the body can also contribute, especially when oily skin occurs intermittently. While diet may aggravate acne, it does not cause oily skin, and common myths linking oily foods, chocolate, peanut butter, or alcohol to increased oil production are not supported by the passages.\", \"Answer: The factors that cause oily skin are hereditary (genes) and hormonal shifts in the body, as stated in the passages. Diet does not cause oily skin but can aggravate acne, and common myths about oily foods, chocolates, peanut butter, and alcohol increasing oil production are contradicted by the passages.\", \"Answer: Factors that cause oily skin include genetics (heredity), hormonal shifts in the body, and potentially poor hygiene or topical applications, while diet has a limited effect on oiliness.\", \"Answer: The factors that cause oily skin are hereditary (genes) and hormonal shifts in the body, as stated in passage 1. Diet can aggravate acne but does not cause oily skin, and the belief that oily foods or alcohol increase oil production is a myth, according to passage 2.\", \"Answer: Factors that cause oily skin include genetics (heredity) and hormonal shifts in the body, as stated in passage 1. While diet can aggravate acne, it does not cause oily skin, contrary to popular belief found in passage 2.\"]", "time_sec": 26.698, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 40.91, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "503", "question": "factors that cause oily skin", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the main factors that contribute to oily skin are:\n\n1. Genetics: According to passage 1, oily skin is hereditary, meaning that if one's parents have oily skin, they are more likely to experience it themselves.\n2. Hormonal shifts: Passage 1 suggests that hormonal changes in the body can cause oily skin to come and go.\n3. Diet: While diet can aggravate acne, it does not directly cause oily skin, according to passage 1.\n4. Poor hygiene: Passage 2 mentions that poor hygiene and topical applications can contribute to oily skin.\n5. Combination skin: Passage 3 defines different skin types, including combination skin, which can be prone to oily skin in certain areas, such as the T-zone.\n\nThere is no additional information provided in the passages to suggest other factors that might contribute to oily skin. Therefore, the answer to the question is based on the information provided in the passages.", "pred": "The factors that cause oily skin are primarily genetic inheritance (e.g., if a parent has oily skin, the child is likely to have it too) and hormonal shifts in the body, particularly when the oily skin occurs intermittently. Diet does not cause oily skin, though it may aggravate acne; the passages clarify that common beliefs about greasy pizza, chocolates, peanut butter, or alcohol increasing oil production are myths.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The factors that cause oily skin are primarily genetic inheritance (e.g., if a parent has oily skin, the child is likely to have it too) and hormonal shifts in the body, particularly when the oily skin occurs intermittently. Diet does not cause oily skin, though it may aggravate acne; the passages clarify that common beliefs about greasy pizza, chocolates, peanut butter, or alcohol increasing oil production are myths.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The factors that cause oily skin are primarily genetic inheritance (e.g., if a parent has oily skin, the child is likely to have it too) and hormonal shifts in the body, particularly when the oily skin occurs intermittently. Diet does not cause oily skin, though it may aggravate acne; the passages clarify that common beliefs about greasy pizza, chocolates, peanut butter, or alcohol increasing oil production are myths.\", \"Answer:Based on the provided passages, the factors that cause oily skin are:  \\n1. Heredity (genes) — if a parent has oily skin, the individual is likely to have it too.  \\n2. Hormonal shifts — when oily skin comes and goes, it may be due to hormonal changes rather than genetics.  \\n3. Diet can aggravate oily skin but does not cause it (e.g., greasy pizza or chocolate/peanut butter do not directly cause oily skin).  \\n\\nThe passages explicitly state that diet does not cause oily skin (e.g., \\\"Diet can aggravate acne, but it doesn't cause oily skin\\\" and \\\"your diet has a limited effect on the oiliness of the skin\\\"). The only direct causes mentioned are genes and hormonal shifts.\", \"Answer: The factors that cause oily skin, based on the provided passages, are genetics and hormonal shifts in the body. The passages state that oily skin is hereditary, meaning it can be passed down from parents to children (e.g., if a parent has oily skin, the child is likely to have it too). Additionally, oily skin that comes and goes may be related to hormonal shifts in the body, rather than genetics. The passages also clarify that diet does not cause oily skin, as it is a common misconception that foods like pizza, chocolate, or peanut butter increase oil production. The text explicitly states that diet \\\"can aggravate acne, but it doesn't cause oily skin\\\" and that \\\"your diet has a limited effect on the oiliness of the skin.\\\"\", \"Answer: Oily skin is primarily caused by genetic factors, as it is hereditary and likely to occur if a parent has oily skin. Hormonal shifts in the body can also contribute to oily skin, particularly when the condition comes and goes. While diet can aggravate acne, it does not directly cause oily skin, and the common belief that oily foods or chocolate increases oil production is a myth. Additionally, poor hygiene and topical applications may contribute to the appearance of oily skin, but these factors are not the primary cause of natural oiliness.\", \"Answer: The factors that cause oily skin are hereditary (genes) and hormonal shifts in the body. Diet does not cause oily skin, though it can aggravate acne.\"]", "time_sec": 44.931, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 27.67, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "504", "question": "what are the characteristics of tropical rainforest floor", "gt": "The floor of a tropical rainforest biome is characterized by a fast rate of decay due to the heat and moisture. This decay provides rich nutrients for other plants to grow. Various types of fruits, nuts, herbs, and mushrooms grow on the rainforest floor. Additionally, vines and ferns grow all over the rainforest, including the floor, helping to connect the various layers of the biome.", "pred": "The tropical rainforest floor features herbs and mushrooms that grow there, and various types of fruits and nuts grow on the plants and in the trees; bananas also grow on vines in this biome.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The tropical rainforest floor features herbs and mushrooms that grow there, and various types of fruits and nuts grow on the plants and in the trees; bananas also grow on vines in this biome.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The tropical rainforest floor features herbs and mushrooms that grow there, and various types of fruits and nuts grow on the plants and in the trees; bananas also grow on vines in this biome.\", \"Answer: The tropical rainforest floor is characterized by the presence of herbs and mushrooms that grow on the ground, as well as various types of fruits and nuts that grow on plants and in trees. Bananas also grow on vines in this biome.\", \"Answer: The tropical rainforest floor features herbs and mushrooms that grow on the ground, and various types of fruits and nuts grow on the plants and in the trees. Bananas also grow on the vines in this biome.\", \"Answer: The tropical rainforest floor features herbs and mushrooms that grow on the ground, and various types of fruits and nuts grow on plants and trees. Bananas also grow on vines in this biome.\", \"Answer: The tropical rainforest floor features herbs and mushrooms that grow on the ground, with various fruits and nuts growing on plants and trees. Bananas also grow on vines in this biome. Additionally, vines and ferns are widespread, helping to connect the different layers of the rainforest.\"]", "time_sec": 20.754, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 50.0, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "507", "question": "what are the characteristics of tropical rainforest floor", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the characteristics of the tropical rainforest floor are:\n\n1. High levels of rainfall, with some areas receiving up to 400 inches of rain annually.\n2. High humidity due to the large amount of rainfall.\n3. Abundant vegetation, including tall trees, vines, ferns, herbs, mushrooms, fruits, and nuts.\n4. Quick decay of plant and animal matter due to the heat and moisture, which provides rich nutrients for other plants to grow.\n5. Presence of bananas growing on vines.\n\nTherefore, the tropical rainforest floor is characterized by high rainfall, humidity, and lush vegetation, with a diverse array of plant species and a rapid rate of decay and nutrient cycling.", "pred": "The tropical rainforest floor is characterized by the presence of herbs and mushrooms that grow there, as well as various types of fruits and nuts that grow on the plants and in the trees. Bananas also grow on the vines in this biome.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The tropical rainforest floor is characterized by the presence of herbs and mushrooms that grow there, as well as various types of fruits and nuts that grow on the plants and in the trees. Bananas also grow on the vines in this biome.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The tropical rainforest floor is characterized by the presence of herbs and mushrooms that grow there, as well as various types of fruits and nuts that grow on the plants and in the trees. Bananas also grow on the vines in this biome.\", \"Answer: The tropical rainforest floor is characterized by the presence of herbs and mushrooms that grow on the floor, as well as various types of fruits and nuts that grow on the plants and in the trees. Additionally, bananas grow on the vines in this particular biome.\", \"Answer: The tropical rainforest floor features herbs and mushrooms that grow on the floor, and various types of fruits and nuts grow on the plants and in the trees. Bananas also grow on the vines in this biome.\", \"Answer: The tropical rainforest floor features herbs and mushrooms that grow on the ground, and various types of fruits and nuts grow on the plants and in the trees, with bananas also growing on vines.\", \"Answer: The tropical rainforest floor is characterized by the presence of herbs and mushrooms that grow there. Additionally, various types of fruits and nuts grow on the plants and in the trees, and bananas grow on the vines in this particular biome.\"]", "time_sec": 21.108, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 28.57, "GAVIE-Rel": 5.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "508", "question": "what is the difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak", "gt": "Porterhouse steaks are cut from further back on the sirloin and contain more of the tenderloin or fillet than sirloin steaks. They are essentially a larger version of the T-Bone steak, carved from a larger portion of the tenderloin. On the other hand, the sirloin steak is a steak cut from the back of the animal, continuing off the short loin from which T-bone, porterhouse, and club steaks are cut. The sirloin is divided into several different types of steak.", "pred": "Sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion continuing off the short loin, while Porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains more of the tenderloin or fillet. Porterhouse steaks are larger and more expensive than sirloin steaks.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion continuing off the short loin, while Porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains more of the tenderloin or fillet. Porterhouse steaks are larger and more expensive than sirloin steaks.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion continuing off the short loin, while Porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains more of the tenderloin or fillet. Porterhouse steaks are larger and more expensive than sirloin steaks.\", \"Answer: Sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion that continues off the short loin, while Porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains more of the tenderloin or fillet; additionally, Porterhouse steaks are larger and more expensive due to their larger portion of tenderloin.\", \"Answer: The sirloin steak is cut from the rear back portion of the animal, continuing off the short loin, while the porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains a larger portion of the tenderloin or fillet.\", \"Answer: The difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak is that sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion, while porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains a larger portion of the tenderloin or fillet compared to T-bone steaks. Additionally, porterhouse steaks are more expensive due to the larger portion of tenderloin.\", \"Answer: Sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion, while Porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains a larger portion of the tenderloin or fillet compared to T-Bone steaks. The Porterhouse is a larger version of the T-Bone steak, as it is carved from a larger portion of the tenderloin.\"]", "time_sec": 30.801, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 63.41, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "509", "question": "what is the difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak is that porterhouse steak is a larger version of T-Bone steak and it contains more of the tenderloin or fillet than T-Bone steak.", "pred": "Sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion continuing off the short loin, while Porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains more of the tenderloin or fillet than T-Bone steaks, with a price difference due to this composition.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion continuing off the short loin, while Porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains more of the tenderloin or fillet than T-Bone steaks, with a price difference due to this composition.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion continuing off the short loin, while Porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains more of the tenderloin or fillet than T-Bone steaks, with a price difference due to this composition.\", \"Answer: Sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion, while Porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains more of the tenderloin or fillet than T-Bone steaks. Porterhouse steaks are larger and considered the King of the T-Bones, as they are carved from the larger portion of the tenderloin.\", \"Answer: Sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion that continues off the short loin, while Porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains more of the tenderloin or fillet than T-Bone steaks, with a corresponding price difference.\", \"Answer: Sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion, while Porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains more of the tenderloin or fillet. Porterhouse steaks are larger and more expensive than sirloin steaks.\", \"Answer: Sirloin steak is cut from the rear back portion of the animal, while porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains more of the tenderloin or fillet than T-Bone steaks. Additionally, porterhouse steaks are larger and considered the \\\"King of the T-Bones\\\" because they are carved from the larger portion of the tenderloin.\"]", "time_sec": 30.084, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 38.46, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "510", "question": "what is the difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it appears that the main difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak is their location within the sirloin muscle. Sirloin steaks are cut from the back of the animal and continue off the short loin from which other cuts such as T-bone and porterhouse steaks are made. On the other hand, porterhouse steaks are specifically carved from the larger portion of the tenderloin and therefore have more of the tenderloin or fillet compared to T-bone steaks. However, the passages suggest that both sirloin and porterhouse steaks may be referred to as the same thing or as a large version of the T-bone due to their similarity in appearance and taste.", "pred": "Sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion continuing off the short loin, while Porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains a larger portion of the tenderloin or fillet compared to T-Bone steaks.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion continuing off the short loin, while Porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains a larger portion of the tenderloin or fillet compared to T-Bone steaks.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion continuing off the short loin, while Porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains a larger portion of the tenderloin or fillet compared to T-Bone steaks.\", \"Answer: Sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion, while Porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains a larger portion of the tenderloin or fillet compared to T-Bone steaks. The Porterhouse is a larger version of the T-Bone steak, carved from the larger portion of the tenderloin, and both are cut from the short loin area of the beef.\", \"Answer: Sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion continuing off the short loin, while Porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains a larger portion of the tenderloin or fillet compared to T-Bone steaks.\", \"Answer: The difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak is that sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion continuing off the short loin, while porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains a larger portion of the tenderloin or fillet. Porterhouse steaks are larger and more expensive than sirloin steaks due to their larger portion of tenderloin.\", \"Answer: The difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak is that sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion continuing off the short loin, while porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains a larger portion of the tenderloin or fillet.\"]", "time_sec": 31.479, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 42.42, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "516", "question": "history of the word gemini", "gt": "Sure! Here's a brief history of the word Gemini based on the given passages:\n\nThe word \"Gemini\" comes from Latin, meaning \"twins.\" The constellation Gemini was first mentioned in Aristotle's Meteorologica over 300 years BC. In Greek mythology, the twins represented in Gemini are Castor and Pollux, who share the same mother, Leda, but have different fathers. The constellation Gemini is not in the same position as the constellation of Gemini and is a mutable sign that announces change and rules the time of year when Taurus spring has ended.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you need further clarification or if there's anything else I can help you with.", "pred": "The word “Gemini” is a Latin word meaning twins, and it refers to one of the constellations that visually resembles its name. The earliest known reference to the Gemini constellation appears in Aristotle’s *Meteorologica*, written over 300 years BC, where he notes observing an occulting star in Gemini and Jupiter in conjunction with it.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The word “Gemini” is a Latin word meaning twins, and it refers to one of the constellations that visually resembles its name. The earliest known reference to the Gemini constellation appears in Aristotle’s *Meteorologica*, written over 300 years BC, where he notes observing an occulting star in Gemini and Jupiter in conjunction with it.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The word “Gemini” is a Latin word meaning twins, and it refers to one of the constellations that visually resembles its name. The earliest known reference to the Gemini constellation appears in Aristotle’s *Meteorologica*, written over 300 years BC, where he notes observing an occulting star in Gemini and Jupiter in conjunction with it.\", \"Answer: The word “Gemini” is a Latin word meaning twins, and it refers to one of the constellations that visually resembles its name. The earliest known reference to the Gemini constellation appears in Aristotle’s *Meteorologica*, written over 300 years BC, where he notes observing an occulting star in Gemini and Jupiter in conjunction with it.\", \"Answer: The word \\\"Gemini\\\" is a Latin word meaning twins, and it refers to one of the constellations that visually represents its name. The earliest known reference to the Gemini constellation dates back to Aristotle's *Meteorologica*, written over 300 years BC, where he described observing an occulting star in Gemini and Jupiter in conjunction with it.\", \"Answer: The word “Gemini” is a Latin word meaning twins, and the first known reference to the Gemini constellation appears in Aristotle’s *Meteorologica*, written over 300 years BC, where he notes observing an occulting star in Gemini and Jupiter in conjunction with it.\", \"Answer: The word “Gemini” is a Latin word meaning twins, and the first known reference to the Gemini constellation appears in Aristotle’s *Meteorologica* (written over 300 years BC), where he describes observing an occulting star and Jupiter in Gemini.\"]", "time_sec": 28.448, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 23.21, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "518", "question": "how do raffles work", "gt": "Raffles work by selling tickets to participants, who have the opportunity to win prizes. The specific details and mechanics of a raffle can vary. In a reverse raffle, participants purchase tickets with numbers on them, and the last ticket drawn wins the main prize. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold during an event, and 50% of the proceeds go to the winner of a random draw, while the other 50% is allocated for a charitable purpose. In some cases, raffles may also offer additional opportunities to win prizes, such as cash tip board raffles.", "pred": "Raffles work by selling tickets, typically with unique numbers, to participants. A portion of the ticket sales, often called the gross proceeds, is allocated to prizes and sometimes a charitable cause. In a reverse raffle, tickets are numbered from 1 to around 55, and a complete ticket consists of 20 individuals, costing $1000 total with two $500 prizes. In a 50-50 draw, 50% of the ticket sales go into a prize pool, with half awarded to the winner of a random draw, and the remaining 50% is set aside for charity. Tickets may include additional benefits like drinks and dinner, and participants can also enter separate raffles for cash prizes, such as $10,000, which are not included in the ticket price.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Raffles work by selling tickets, typically with unique numbers, to participants. A portion of the ticket sales, often called the gross proceeds, is allocated to prizes and sometimes a charitable cause. In a reverse raffle, tickets are numbered from 1 to around 55, and a complete ticket consists of 20 individuals, costing $1000 total with two $500 prizes. In a 50-50 draw, 50% of the ticket sales go into a prize pool, with half awarded to the winner of a random draw, and the remaining 50% is set aside for charity. Tickets may include additional benefits like drinks and dinner, and participants can also enter separate raffles for cash prizes, such as $10,000, which are not included in the ticket price.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Raffles work by selling tickets, typically with unique numbers, to participants. A portion of the ticket sales, often called the gross proceeds, is allocated to prizes and sometimes a charitable cause. In a reverse raffle, tickets are numbered from 1 to around 55, and a complete ticket consists of 20 individuals, costing $1000 total with two $500 prizes. In a 50-50 draw, 50% of the ticket sales go into a prize pool, with half awarded to the winner of a random draw, and the remaining 50% is set aside for charity. Tickets may include additional benefits like drinks and dinner, and participants can also enter separate raffles for cash prizes, such as $10,000, which are not included in the ticket price.\", \"Answer: Raffles work by selling tickets with unique numbers to participants. In a standard raffle, participants purchase tickets, each with a distinct number, for a set price. The organizer then draws a winning number from all the tickets sold, and the holder of the winning ticket receives a prize. For example, in a reverse raffle, tickets are numbered from 1 to approximately 55, with a table of 20 individuals constituting one complete ticket costing $1000, and there are two $500 prizes (one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled). In a 50-50 draw, half of the proceeds from ticket sales go into a prize pool, and 50% of this pool is awarded to the winner of a random draw, while the remaining 50% is set aside for charity. Tickets are sold throughout the event, often by roaming staff with mobile devices, and the draw occurs towards the end of the event. Additional raffle opportunities, such as cash tip board raffles, may be available for extra chances to win, with prizes like $10,000, which are not included in the ticket price.\", \"Answer: Raffles work by selling tickets with unique numbers, where each ticket represents a chance to win a prize. The reverse raffle, as described in passage 1, involves tickets numbered from 1 to approximately 55, with each ticket costing $50 and a table of 20 individuals constituting one complete ticket for $1000, along with two $500 prizes (one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled). In a 50-50 draw, as explained in passage 2, tickets are sold throughout an event, and the gross proceeds from ticket sales form a prize pool; 50% of this pool goes to the winner of a random draw, while the remaining 50% is allocated for charitable purposes chosen by the sporting club. Additionally, as noted in passage 3, participants may have the opportunity to win cash prizes, such as $10,000, through tip board raffles that are separate from the ticket price.\", \"Answer: Raffles work by selling tickets, often at a set price (e.g., $50 each), where each ticket has a unique number. A drawing is then held to randomly select winning tickets, with prizes awarded to the winners. For example, in a reverse raffle, tickets are numbered 1 to 55, and a table of 20 individuals forms one complete ticket costing $1000, with two $500 prizes awarded (one for an early bird draw and one for the first ticket pulled). In a 50-50 draw, 50% of ticket sales go into a prize pool, and 50% is set aside for charity, with the winner receiving half of the total prize pool.\", \"Answer: Raffles work by selling tickets, where each ticket has a number (e.g., from 1 to approximately 55 in a reverse raffle), and a drawing selects a winning number to determine the winner. For example, in a reverse raffle, a table of 20 individuals constitutes one complete ticket costing $1000 (with two $500 prizes), and tickets are priced at $50 each. In a 50-50 draw, 50% of ticket sales go into a prize pool, with 50% of that pool awarded to the winner of a random draw at the event’s end, while the remaining 50% is allocated for charity. Additional raffles, such as cash tip board raffles, may offer prizes like $10,000, separate from the ticket price.\"]", "time_sec": 72.354, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 46.38, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "519", "question": "how do raffles work", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how raffles work:\n\n1. Reverse Raffle: In this type of raffle, the numbers on the entry tickets are drawn in reverse order, starting with the last number and ending with the first number. For example, if there are 55 entries, the last number drawn wins the grand prize, followed by the next-to-last number, and so on until the first number. Each entry ticket costs $50, and a table of 20 individuals constitutes one complete ticket worth $1,000 and two $500 prizes (for the early bird drawing and the first ticket pulled).\n2. 50-50 Draw: This is a raffle held during a single day at a specific event. Tickets are sold to patrons throughout the event by roving staff with handheld electronic devices. The gross proceeds of 50-50 tickets sold at the event go into a prize pool, and 50% of the prize pool is awarded to the winner of a random draw that takes place at the end of the event. The remaining 50% goes to a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.\n3. Additional Raffles: In addition to the main raffle, there may be additional raffles for drinks and dinner. These raffles are not included in the original ticket price and require separate entry fees. One of these raffles offers the chance to win $10,000!\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "pred": "Raffles work by selling tickets with unique numbers to participants, where a portion of the ticket sales goes into a prize pool. In a reverse raffle, tickets are sold for $50 each, with a table of 20 individuals constituting one complete ticket at $1000, and two $500 prizes awarded—one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold throughout an event by roaming staff, and the prize pool consists of 50% of the ticket sales going to the winner of a random draw at the event’s end, while the remaining 50% is set aside for a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Raffles work by selling tickets with unique numbers to participants, where a portion of the ticket sales goes into a prize pool. In a reverse raffle, tickets are sold for $50 each, with a table of 20 individuals constituting one complete ticket at $1000, and two $500 prizes awarded—one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold throughout an event by roaming staff, and the prize pool consists of 50% of the ticket sales going to the winner of a random draw at the event’s end, while the remaining 50% is set aside for a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Raffles work by selling tickets with unique numbers to participants, where a portion of the ticket sales goes into a prize pool. In a reverse raffle, tickets are sold for $50 each, with a table of 20 individuals constituting one complete ticket at $1000, and two $500 prizes awarded—one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold throughout an event by roaming staff, and the prize pool consists of 50% of the ticket sales going to the winner of a random draw at the event’s end, while the remaining 50% is set aside for a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.\", \"Answer: Raffles work by selling tickets with unique numbers to participants, where each ticket represents an entry into a draw. The winning ticket(s) are then selected randomly, and the holder(s) of those ticket(s) win the prize(s). For example, in a reverse raffle, a table of 20 individuals constitutes one complete ticket costing $1000, with two $500 prizes awarded (one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled). In a 50-50 draw, 50% of the ticket sales go into a prize pool, and the winner receives 50% of that pool, while the remaining 50% is set aside for charity. Tickets are typically priced individually (e.g., $50 each), and participation may include additional opportunities, such as cash tip board raffles with prizes like $10,000, which are not included in the ticket price.\", \"Answer: Raffles work by selling tickets, often numbered from 1 to a specific range (e.g., 1 to 55), where each ticket costs a set price (e.g., $50). A complete ticket may consist of multiple individuals (e.g., 20 people totaling $1000). The raffle draws winning numbers or tickets for prizes, such as $500 for early bird and first-drawn tickets, or larger cash prizes like $10,000 in additional raffles. Additionally, in 50-50 draws, half of the ticket sales go to the winner of a random draw, while the other half is allocated for charity.\", \"Answer: Raffles work by selling numbered tickets (e.g., $50 each) to participants, with each ticket assigned a unique number (e.g., 1 to 55). A table of 20 individuals constitutes one complete ticket at $1000, and prizes (e.g., two $500 prizes) are awarded through drawings (e.g., an early bird draw and a first ticket pull). In a 50-50 draw, 50% of ticket sales go to a winner of a random draw at the event’s end, while the other 50% is set aside for charity. Additional raffles (e.g., cash tip board raffles) may offer prizes like $10,000, with participation requiring extra payment beyond the ticket price.\", \"Answer: Raffles work by having participants purchase tickets, each with a unique number, for a set price. A portion of the ticket sales goes into a prize pool, and winners are selected through a random draw. For example, in a reverse raffle, tickets are numbered from 1 to approximately 55, with a table of 20 individuals constituting one complete ticket at $1000, and two $500 prizes are awarded (one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled). In a 50-50 draw, 50% of the ticket sales go into a prize pool, with 50% of the pool awarded to the winner of the random draw, and the remaining 50% set aside for a charitable purpose. Additionally, some raffles offer extra opportunities to win cash prizes, such as tip board raffles with a chance to win $10,000.\"]", "time_sec": 64.614, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 50.94, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "520", "question": "how do raffles work", "gt": "Sure! Based on the given passages, here's how raffles work:\n\nA reverse raffle works by having participants purchase tickets with numbers on them, usually between 1 and 55. Each ticket costs $50, and a table consists of 20 individuals who pool their money together to form a complete ticket worth $1000. There are also two $500 prizes, one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled.\n\nThe 50-50 draw is a raffle that takes place during a single event, where tickets are sold to patrons throughout the event by roaming staff. 50% of the gross proceeds go into a prize pool, and the winner of the random draw at the end of the event receives 50% of the prize pool. The remaining 50% goes to a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.\n\nFinally, some events may include cash tip board raffles, where participants can pay to play and have the chance to win $10,000. These raffles are not included in the ticket price.", "pred": "Raffles work by selling tickets, often with a number on them, to participants. A portion of the ticket sales, known as the gross proceeds, is allocated to a prize pool. In a standard raffle, half of the prize pool goes to the winner of the random draw, while the remaining half is set aside for a charitable cause. For example, in a 50-50 draw, if $1000 worth of tickets are sold, the winner would receive $500, and $500 would be donated to charity. Additionally, participants may have opportunities to win additional prizes through cash tip board raffles, which are not included in the ticket price.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Raffles work by selling tickets, often with a number on them, to participants. A portion of the ticket sales, known as the gross proceeds, is allocated to a prize pool. In a standard raffle, half of the prize pool goes to the winner of the random draw, while the remaining half is set aside for a charitable cause. For example, in a 50-50 draw, if $1000 worth of tickets are sold, the winner would receive $500, and $500 would be donated to charity. Additionally, participants may have opportunities to win additional prizes through cash tip board raffles, which are not included in the ticket price.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Raffles work by selling tickets, often with a number on them, to participants. A portion of the ticket sales, known as the gross proceeds, is allocated to a prize pool. In a standard raffle, half of the prize pool goes to the winner of the random draw, while the remaining half is set aside for a charitable cause. For example, in a 50-50 draw, if $1000 worth of tickets are sold, the winner would receive $500, and $500 would be donated to charity. Additionally, participants may have opportunities to win additional prizes through cash tip board raffles, which are not included in the ticket price.\", \"Answer: Raffles work by selling tickets for a chance to win prizes. In a standard raffle, participants purchase tickets, each with a unique number, and a drawing determines the winning ticket(s). For example, in a reverse raffle described in passage 1, tickets are numbered 1 to 55, sold at $50 each, with a table of 20 individuals forming one complete ticket for $1000, and two $500 prizes awarded—one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled. In a 50-50 draw (passage 2), 50% of ticket sales go into a prize pool, with 50% of that pool awarded to a randomly drawn winner, while the remaining 50% is set aside for charity. Additional raffles, like cash tip board raffles (passage 3), offer prizes such as $10,000, with participation requiring extra payment beyond the ticket price.\", \"Answer: Raffles work by selling tickets, where each ticket has a chance to win a prize. In a reverse raffle, as described in passage 1, your entry ticket has a number from 1 to approximately 55, and tickets are $50 each. A table of 20 individuals constitutes one complete ticket at $1000, with two $500 prizes awarded—one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled. In a 50-50 draw, as detailed in passage 2, tickets are sold throughout an event, and the gross proceeds form a prize pool; 50% of this pool goes to the winner of a random draw at the event's end, while the remaining 50% is allocated to a charitable cause chosen by the sporting club. Additionally, as noted in passage 3, participants can engage in cash tip board raffles (not included in the ticket price) for a chance to win $10,000.\", \"Answer: Raffles work by selling tickets, often with a number on them, to participants. The proceeds from ticket sales are used to fund prizes, with a portion sometimes allocated for charity, as seen in the 50-50 draw where 50% of the proceeds go to the winner and the other 50% to a charitable cause chosen by the organization. Tickets can have different price points and may be sold individually or in groups, such as a table of 20 individuals constituting one complete ticket. Raffles can include various components like cash tip board raffles for additional prizes, and sometimes include additional perks like drinks and dinner with the ticket price.\", \"Answer: Raffles work by selling tickets with unique numbers to participants, where each ticket represents a chance to win a prize. In a reverse raffle, tickets are numbered from 1 to approximately 55, with each ticket costing $50; a table of 20 individuals constitutes one complete ticket priced at $1000, and there are two $500 prizes awarded—one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold throughout an event by roaming staff using handheld devices, and the gross proceeds from ticket sales form a prize pool; 50% of this pool goes to the winner of a random draw held toward the end of the event, while the remaining 50% is set aside for a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club. Additionally, participants may engage in cash tip board raffles outside the ticket price, with chances to win $10,000.\"]", "time_sec": 67.544, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 44.07, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "521", "question": "how do raffles work", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how raffles work:\n\nA raffle is a game of chance where participants buy tickets with a unique number, and a prize is awarded to the holder of the winning ticket drawn at random. In a reverse raffle, the process is similar, but the tickets are numbered from 1 to approximately 55, and tables consist of 20 individuals, constituting one complete ticket at $1000, with two $500 prizes available. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold during an event, and the gross proceeds go into a prize pool, with 50% going to the winner of the random draw and the remaining 50% going to a charitable cause.\n\nTherefore, raffles typically involve buying tickets with unique numbers, and a random draw determines the winner, with a percentage of the proceeds often going to a charitable cause.", "pred": "Based on the provided passages, raffles work as follows:", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Raffles work by selling numbered tickets, where each ticket represents a chance to win a prize. The passage 1 describes a reverse raffle where tickets are sold for $50 each, with a table of 20 individuals constituting one complete ticket at $1000, and two $500 prizes are awarded—one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled. Passage 2 explains that a 50-50 draw is a raffle held during a single day at an event, where tickets are sold throughout the stadium, and the gross proceeds go into a prize pool; 50% of this pool goes to the winner of a random draw, while the remaining 50% is set aside for a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club. Passage 3 mentions cash tip board raffles where participants have a chance to win $10,000, with drinks and dinner included in the ticket price.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Raffles work by selling numbered tickets, where each ticket represents a chance to win a prize. The passage 1 describes a reverse raffle where tickets are sold for $50 each, with a table of 20 individuals constituting one complete ticket at $1000, and two $500 prizes are awarded—one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled. Passage 2 explains that a 50-50 draw is a raffle held during a single day at an event, where tickets are sold throughout the stadium, and the gross proceeds go into a prize pool; 50% of this pool goes to the winner of a random draw, while the remaining 50% is set aside for a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club. Passage 3 mentions cash tip board raffles where participants have a chance to win $10,000, with drinks and dinner included in the ticket price.\", \"Answer: Based on the provided passages, raffles work as follows:  \\n- A **reverse raffle** involves participants holding numbered tickets (e.g., 1–55), where a table of 20 individuals constitutes one complete ticket costing $1000, with $50 per individual and two $500 prizes (one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled).  \\n- A **50-50 draw** is a raffle held during a single event day, where ticket sales (e.g., by roaming staff with mobile devices) contribute to a prize pool; 50% of the proceeds go to the winner of a random draw at the event’s end, while the remaining 50% is set aside for charity.  \\n- Additional raffles (e.g., cash tip board raffles) offer prizes like $10,000, separate from the ticket price (which includes drinks and dinner).  \\n\\nAnswer: Raffles work by selling numbered tickets (e.g., $50 each) where participants win prizes based on random draws, with proceeds sometimes allocated to charity (50-50 draw) or specific prizes (reverse raffle, cash tip board raffles).\", \"Answer: Based on the provided passages, raffles work as follows:  \\n\\n- A **reverse raffle** involves tickets numbered 1 to approximately 55, sold at $50 each. A complete ticket (for a table of 20 individuals) costs $1000, with two $500 prizes (one for an early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled).  \\n- A **50-50 draw** is a raffle where 50% of ticket sales go into a prize pool, and 50% is set aside for charity. The winner receives 50% of the total prize pool, with the draw occurring toward the end of the event.  \\n- Additional raffle opportunities (e.g., cash tip board raffles) allow participants to win $10,000, though these are not included in the ticket price.  \\n\\nThese details are derived solely from the given passages.  \\n\\nAnswer: Raffles work by selling numbered tickets (e.g., $50 each), with proceeds forming a prize pool. In a reverse raffle, a complete ticket (20 individuals) costs $1000 for two $500 prizes; in a 50-50 draw, 50% of sales fund the prize pool, with 50% going to the winner and 50% to charity. Additional raffles (e.g., cash tip boards) offer $10,000 prizes outside the ticket price.\", \"Answer: Raffles work by selling numbered tickets to participants, with each ticket having a unique number (e.g., 1 to 55 in a reverse raffle). A portion of the ticket sales (e.g., $50 per ticket) contributes to prizes, such as a $1000 prize for a table of 20 people or $500 prizes in a reverse raffle. In a 50-50 draw, 50% of ticket sales go into a prize pool, with 50% awarded to the winner of a random draw and the other 50% allocated to charity. Additional opportunities, like cash tip board raffles, may offer separate prizes (e.g., $10,000) not included in the ticket price.\", \"Answer: Raffles work by having participants purchase tickets, each with a unique number, for a set price. A portion of the ticket sales goes into a prize pool, with a percentage (e.g., 50%) awarded to the winner of a random draw, and the remaining amount allocated for charitable purposes or other designated uses. For example, in a 50-50 draw, half the proceeds from ticket sales form the prize pool, and the winner receives 50% of that pool, while the other 50% supports a charity chosen by the event organizer.\"]", "time_sec": 78.827, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 8.79, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
