een the basket and the offensive player, according to passage 3.\", \"Answer: To guard a tall person in basketball, you should stay as close as possible to them when they are near the three-point line and actively slide your feet from side to side to minimize their shot opportunities, as mentioned in passage 1. Additionally, you should keep a wedge between the basket and the tall offensive player, according to the tips provided in passage 3.\"]", "time_sec": 23.059, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 68.57, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "629", "question": "how to guard a tall person in basketball", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are some strategies for guarding a tall person in basketball:\n\n1. Stay as close as possible to a tall, skilled shooter when you guard them near the three-point line and actively slide your feet from side to side to stay near your opponent and minimize shot opportunities (Passage 1).\n2. Keep a wedge between the basket and the offensive player to defend against a tall player (Passage 3).\n\nTherefore, to guard a tall person in basketball, it is important to stay close to them and create a barrier between them and the basket. These strategies can help limit their shooting opportunities and make it more difficult for them to score.", "pred": "To guard a tall person in basketball, stay close to them near the three-point line, actively slide your feet side to side to stay near your opponent, minimize their shot opportunities, and keep a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer:To guard a tall person in basketball, stay close to them near the three-point line, actively slide your feet side to side to stay near your opponent, minimize their shot opportunities, and keep a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.", "cot": "[\"Answer:To guard a tall person in basketball, stay close to them near the three-point line, actively slide your feet side to side to stay near your opponent, minimize their shot opportunities, and keep a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.\", \"Answer: To guard a tall person in basketball, stay close to them near the three-point line, actively slide your feet side to side to limit their shot opportunities, and maintain a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.\", \"Answer: To guard a tall person in basketball, stay close to them near the three-point line, slide your feet side to side to limit their shot opportunities, and maintain a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.\", \"Answer: To guard a tall person in basketball, stay close to them near the three-point line and actively slide your feet side to side to minimize their shot opportunities. Additionally, maintain a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.\", \"Answer: To guard a tall person in basketball, stay close to them near the three-point line, slide your feet side to side to stay near the opponent, and keep a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.\"]", "time_sec": 20.162, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 55.05, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "630", "question": "how to guard a tall person in basketball", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how to guard a tall person in basketball:\n\n1. Stay as close as possible to the tall, skilled shooter when guarding them near the three-point line.\n2. Actively slide your feet from side to side to stay near your opponent and minimize shot opportunities.\n3. Keep a wedge between the basket and the offensive player to defend against a tall player.\n\nTherefore, to guard a tall person in basketball, it is essential to stay close to them, move quickly laterally to maintain position, and use a defensive strategy such as keeping a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.", "pred": "To guard a tall person in basketball, you should stay as close as possible to them when they are near the three-point line and actively slide your feet from side to side to stay near your opponent and minimize shot opportunities. Additionally, keep a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To guard a tall person in basketball, you should stay as close as possible to them when they are near the three-point line and actively slide your feet from side to side to stay near your opponent and minimize shot opportunities. Additionally, keep a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To guard a tall person in basketball, you should stay as close as possible to them when they are near the three-point line and actively slide your feet from side to side to stay near your opponent and minimize shot opportunities. Additionally, keep a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.\", \"Answer: Stay close to a tall player near the three-point line, slide your feet side to side to stay near them, keep a wedge between the basket and the offensive player, and follow tips from experienced players.\", \"Answer: To guard a tall person in basketball, stay close to them near the three-point line and actively slide your feet side to side to limit their shot opportunities. Additionally, maintain a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.\", \"Answer:To guard a tall person in basketball, stay close to them near the three-point line, slide your feet side to side to stay in position, keep a wedge between the basket and the offensive player, and actively contest shots.\", \"Answer: To guard a tall person in basketball, stay close to them near the three-point line, actively slide your feet side to side to limit their shot opportunities, and maintain a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.\"]", "time_sec": 20.941, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 59.81, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "631", "question": "how to guard a tall person in basketball", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's a brief answer to the question \"How to guard a tall person in basketball\":\n\nTo guard a tall person in basketball, it's important to stay as close as possible to them when they're near the three-point line. Slide your feet from side to side to stay near your opponent and minimize shot opportunities. Additionally, keep a wedge between the basket and the offensive player to defend against them effectively.", "pred": "To guard a tall person in basketball, stay close to them near the three-point line, slide your feet side to side to limit shot opportunities, and maintain a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To guard a tall person in basketball, stay close to them near the three-point line, slide your feet side to side to limit shot opportunities, and maintain a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To guard a tall person in basketball, stay close to them near the three-point line, slide your feet side to side to limit shot opportunities, and maintain a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.\", \"Answer: To guard a tall person in basketball, you should stay as close as possible to them near the three-point line, actively slide your feet from side to side to minimize their shot opportunities, and keep a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.\", \"Answer: Stay close to a tall player near the three-point line, actively slide your feet side to side to stay near them, and keep a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.\", \"Answer: To guard a tall person in basketball, stay as close as possible to them near the three-point line, actively slide your feet side to side to stay near the opponent and minimize shot opportunities, and keep a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.\", \"Answer: To guard a tall person in basketball, stay as close as possible to them near the three-point line and actively slide your feet from side to side to minimize their shot opportunities. Additionally, keep a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.\"]", "time_sec": 20.501, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 58.67, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "632", "question": "similarity and difference between nervous system and endocrine system", "gt": "The nervous system and endocrine system are both critical to maintaining homeostasis in the body by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems. They both use chemical messengers, although their method of transmission and release differs. The nervous system is made of interconnected neuron cells and uses electrochemical pulses for signal transmission. In contrast, the endocrine system is made of discrete glands that use hormones for signal transmission and utilizes the circulatory system to carry these signals.", "pred": "Difference: The nervous system is made of neuron cells while the endocrine system is made of glands. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, while in the endocrine system, chemicals known as hormones are responsible for signal transmission. The nervous system is continuous with interconnected cells, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are discrete and not physically connected. The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Similarity: Both the nervous system and endocrine system are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. Chemical messengers are important in both systems.\n\nDifference: The nervous system is made of neuron cells while the endocrine system is made of glands. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, while in the endocrine system, chemicals known as hormones are responsible for signal transmission. The nervous system is continuous with interconnected cells, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are discrete and not physically connected. The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.", "cot": "[\"Similarity: Both the nervous system and endocrine system are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. Chemical messengers are important in both systems.\\n\\nDifference: The nervous system is made of neuron cells while the endocrine system is made of glands. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, while in the endocrine system, chemicals known as hormones are responsible for signal transmission. The nervous system is continuous with interconnected cells, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are discrete and not physically connected. The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.\", \"Answer: The nervous system and endocrine system share key similarities and exhibit distinct differences.  \\n\\n**Similarities**:  \\n- Both systems maintain homeostasis by coordinating and regulating cellular, tissue, organ, and systemic activities.  \\n- Both are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.  \\n- Chemical messengers are essential in both systems, though their transmission and release methods differ.  \\n\\n**Differences**:  \\n- The nervous system consists of neuron cells and transmits signals via electrochemical pulses. In contrast, the endocrine system is composed of glands and uses hormones as chemical messengers for signal transmission.  \\n- The nervous system is continuous, with interconnected neurons enabling rapid signal transmission. The endocrine system’s organs are discrete and not physically connected, relying on the circulatory system to distribute hormones.  \\n- The nervous system uses direct, fast transmission (e.g., via neurons), while the endocrine system employs slower, widespread hormone distribution through the bloodstream.\", \"Answer: The nervous system and endocrine system share similarities in their roles in maintaining homeostasis and using chemical messengers, but differ in cellular structure, signal transmission, and physical connectivity. Both systems regulate cell activities through chemical messengers and negative feedback mechanisms, as stated in passage 3. However, the nervous system is composed of interconnected neuron cells that transmit electrochemical pulses, while the endocrine system consists of discrete glands that release hormones into the circulatory system for signal transmission, as detailed in passages 1 and 2. Additionally, the nervous system is continuous with physically connected cells, whereas the endocrine system's organs are not physically connected, being discrete in nature.\", \"Similarity: Both the nervous system and endocrine system maintain homeostasis by coordinating and regulating other cells, tissues, organs, and systems, and both are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. They also use chemical messengers, though their transmission methods differ.\\n\\nDifference: The nervous system is made of neuron cells and uses electrochemical pulses for signal transmission, with cells that are interconnected. In contrast, the endocrine system is made of glands, uses hormones as chemical messengers, and its organs are discrete and not physically connected, relying on the circulatory system to transmit signals.\", \"Answer: The nervous system and endocrine system are similar in that both are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems, and both are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. They also both use chemical messengers, though their methods of transmission and release differ. The key differences are that the nervous system is made of neuron cells and transmits signals via electrochemical pulses, while the endocrine system is made of glands and uses hormones for signal transmission. Additionally, the nervous system is continuous with interconnected cells, whereas the endocrine system’s organs are discrete and not physically connected, relying on the circulatory system to transmit signals.\"]", "time_sec": 53.569, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 54.9, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "633", "question": "similarity and difference between nervous system and endocrine system", "gt": "The nervous system and endocrine system have both similarities and differences. \n\nSimilarities:\n1. Both systems are dedicated to maintaining homeostasis and coordinating the activities of cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\n2. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\n3. Chemical messengers play a crucial role in both systems.\n\nDifferences:\n1. The nervous system is composed of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made up of glands.\n2. Transmission in the nervous system occurs through electrochemical pulses, while chemical hormones are responsible for signal transmission in the endocrine system.\n3. The nervous system utilizes interconnected cells to transmit signals, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are discrete and not physically connected.\n4. The nervous system transmits signals through neurons, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.", "pred": "Difference: The nervous system is made of neuron cells while the endocrine system is made of glands. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, while in the endocrine system, chemicals known as hormones are responsible for signal transmission. The nervous system is continuous with interconnected cells, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are discrete and not physically connected. The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system for signal transmission.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Similarity: Both the nervous system and endocrine system are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. Chemical messengers are important in both systems.\n\nDifference: The nervous system is made of neuron cells while the endocrine system is made of glands. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, while in the endocrine system, chemicals known as hormones are responsible for signal transmission. The nervous system is continuous with interconnected cells, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are discrete and not physically connected. The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system for signal transmission.", "cot": "[\"Similarity: Both the nervous system and endocrine system are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. Chemical messengers are important in both systems.\\n\\nDifference: The nervous system is made of neuron cells while the endocrine system is made of glands. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, while in the endocrine system, chemicals known as hormones are responsible for signal transmission. The nervous system is continuous with interconnected cells, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are discrete and not physically connected. The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system for signal transmission.\", \"Answer: The nervous system and endocrine system are both devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems, and both are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. Both systems use chemical messengers, though they differ in transmission and release methods. The nervous system is made of neuron cells and transmits signals via electrochemical pulses, with cells that are continuously interconnected. In contrast, the endocrine system is made of glands, transmits signals via hormones, and its organs are discrete and not physically connected, using the circulatory system for signal transmission.\", \"Answer: Both the nervous system and endocrine system are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems, and both are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. Chemical messengers are important in both systems, although their method of transmission and release differs. The nervous system is made of neuron cells and uses electrochemical pulses for transmission, while the endocrine system is made of glands and uses chemicals known as hormones for signal transmission. The nervous system is continuous with interconnected cells, whereas the endocrine system's organs are discrete and not physically connected, using the circulatory system to transmit signals.\", \"Answer: Similarity: Both systems maintain homeostasis by regulating cells, tissues, organs, and systems through chemical messengers and negative feedback.  \\nDifference: The nervous system uses electrochemical pulses in interconnected neurons for rapid, continuous signal transmission, while the endocrine system uses hormones from discrete glands transmitted via the circulatory system, which is slower and non-continuous.\", \"Answer: Similarity: Both the nervous system and endocrine system are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. Chemical messengers are important in both systems, although their method of transmission and release differs in the two systems. Difference: The nervous system is made of neuron cells while the endocrine system is made of glands. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, while chemicals known as hormones are responsible for signal transmission in the endocrine system. The nervous system is continuous with interconnected cells, while the organs of the endocrine system are discrete and not physically connected. The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.\"]", "time_sec": 46.592, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 61.31, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "634", "question": "similarity and difference between nervous system and endocrine system", "gt": "Based on the given passages, we can see that the nervous system and endocrine system have some similarities and differences. \n\nSimilarities:\n- Both systems are responsible for maintaining homeostasis in the body.\n- Both systems use chemical messengers to coordinate and regulate the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\n- Negative feedback mechanisms are used to regulate both systems.\n\nDifferences:\n- The nervous system is made up of neuron cells while the endocrine system is made up of glands.\n- Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses while transmission in the endocrine system is done through chemicals called hormones.\n- The nervous system is a continuous network of interconnected cells while the endocrine system consists of discrete organs that are not physically connected.\n- The nervous system uses neurons to transmit signals while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.", "pred": "Difference: The nervous system consists of neuron cells that transmit electrochemical pulses, while the endocrine system is made of glands that release hormones. The nervous system’s cells are physically interconnected, whereas endocrine organs are discrete and not physically connected. The nervous system transmits signals directly via neurons, whereas the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to carry hormonal signals.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Similarity: Both systems maintain homeostasis by regulating other cells, tissues, organs, and systems, and both use chemical messengers and negative feedback mechanisms.\n\nDifference: The nervous system consists of neuron cells that transmit electrochemical pulses, while the endocrine system is made of glands that release hormones. The nervous system’s cells are physically interconnected, whereas endocrine organs are discrete and not physically connected. The nervous system transmits signals directly via neurons, whereas the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to carry hormonal signals.", "cot": "[\"Similarity: Both systems maintain homeostasis by regulating other cells, tissues, organs, and systems, and both use chemical messengers and negative feedback mechanisms.\\n\\nDifference: The nervous system consists of neuron cells that transmit electrochemical pulses, while the endocrine system is made of glands that release hormones. The nervous system’s cells are physically interconnected, whereas endocrine organs are discrete and not physically connected. The nervous system transmits signals directly via neurons, whereas the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to carry hormonal signals.\", \"Answer: The nervous system and endocrine system share similarities in their roles in maintaining homeostasis through regulation of cells, tissues, organs, and systems, and both utilize negative feedback mechanisms. Additionally, chemical messengers are important in both systems. However, they differ in their cellular composition (nervous system uses neuron cells while endocrine system uses glands), method of signal transmission (nervous system uses electrochemical pulses while endocrine system uses hormones), and physical structure (nervous system is continuous with interconnected cells, while endocrine system organs are discrete and not physically connected).\", \"Answer: The nervous system and endocrine system share key similarities and exhibit distinct differences.  \\n\\n**Similarities**:  \\n- Both systems maintain homeostasis by coordinating and regulating cellular, tissue, organ, and systemic activities.  \\n- Both utilize chemical messengers (neurotransmitters in the nervous system, hormones in the endocrine system) for signal transmission.  \\n- Both are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.  \\n\\n**Differences**:  \\n- **Cell Structure and Transmission**: The nervous system consists of interconnected neuron cells that transmit signals via electrochemical pulses. The endocrine system is composed of discrete glands that release hormones (chemical messengers) into the bloodstream.  \\n- **Physical Connectivity**: The nervous system is continuous with physically connected cells, while the endocrine system’s organs are not physically connected and function as separate units.  \\n- **Signal Transmission Pathway**: The nervous system uses direct neural pathways for rapid signal transmission, whereas the endocrine system relies on the circulatory system to distribute hormones, resulting in slower, longer-term signaling.  \\n\\nUnable to answer based on given passages.\", \"Answer: Similarity: Both the nervous system and endocrine system are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. Chemical messengers are important in both systems. Difference: The nervous system is made of neuron cells while the Endocrine System is made of glands. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, while chemicals known as hormones are responsible for signal transmission in the Endocrine System. The nervous system is continuous with interconnected cells, whereas the Endocrine System's organs are discrete and not physically connected. The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, while the Endocrine System uses the circulatory system for signal transmission.\", \"Answer: Similarity: Both the nervous system and endocrine system are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems, and both are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. Chemical messengers are important in both systems. Difference: The nervous system is made of neuron cells and uses electrochemical pulses for transmission, while the endocrine system is made of glands and uses hormones (chemical messengers) for signal transmission. The nervous system is continuous with physically interconnected cells, whereas the endocrine system's organs are discrete and not physically connected, using the circulatory system to transmit signals.\"]", "time_sec": 53.243, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 48.12, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "635", "question": "similarity and difference between nervous system and endocrine system", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are the key similarities and differences between the nervous system and the endocrine system:\n\nSimilarities:\n\n* Both systems are involved in maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\n* Both systems use chemical messengers (neurotransmitters and hormones) to transmit signals.\n* Both systems have negative feedback mechanisms to regulate their function.\n\nDifferences:\n\n* The nervous system is made up of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is composed of glands.\n* Transmission of signals occurs through electrical impulses in the nervous system, while hormones are responsible for signal transmission in the endocrine system.\n* The nervous system has a continuous structure, with cells connected to each other, while the endocrine system consists of discrete organs not physically connected to each other.\n* The nervous system uses neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system relies on the circulatory system to transmit signals.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "pred": "Both the nervous system and endocrine system are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems, and both are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. They both use chemical messengers, though their transmission and release methods differ. The key differences are: the nervous system is made of neuron cells and transmits signals via electrochemical pulses, while the endocrine system is made of glands and uses hormones for signal transmission. The nervous system is continuous with interconnected cells, whereas the endocrine system’s organs are discrete and not physically connected, using the circulatory system to transmit signals.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Both the nervous system and endocrine system are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems, and both are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. They both use chemical messengers, though their transmission and release methods differ. The key differences are: the nervous system is made of neuron cells and transmits signals via electrochemical pulses, while the endocrine system is made of glands and uses hormones for signal transmission. The nervous system is continuous with interconnected cells, whereas the endocrine system’s organs are discrete and not physically connected, using the circulatory system to transmit signals.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Both the nervous system and endocrine system are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems, and both are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. They both use chemical messengers, though their transmission and release methods differ. The key differences are: the nervous system is made of neuron cells and transmits signals via electrochemical pulses, while the endocrine system is made of glands and uses hormones for signal transmission. The nervous system is continuous with interconnected cells, whereas the endocrine system’s organs are discrete and not physically connected, using the circulatory system to transmit signals.\", \"Answer: The nervous system and endocrine system share key similarities and exhibit distinct differences in structure and function. Both systems are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of cells, tissues, organs, and systems, and both are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. Additionally, chemical messengers play a role in both systems, though their methods of transmission and release differ.\\n\\nIn terms of differences, the nervous system is composed of neuron cells and transmits signals via electrochemical pulses. It is continuous, with interconnected cells. In contrast, the endocrine system is made up of discrete glands that release hormones, which travel through the circulatory system to transmit signals. The nervous system’s cells are physically connected, while the endocrine system’s organs are not physically connected.\", \"Answer: Similarity: Both systems maintain homeostasis by regulating cells, tissues, organs, and systems through chemical messengers, and both utilize negative feedback mechanisms. Difference: The nervous system is composed of interconnected neurons that transmit electrochemical pulses for rapid, direct communication, while the endocrine system consists of discrete glands that release hormones into the circulatory system for slower, widespread signal transmission.\", \"Answer: Both the nervous system and endocrine system are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems, and both are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. Chemical messengers are important in both systems, although their method of transmission and release differs: the nervous system uses electrochemical pulses through interconnected neurons, while the endocrine system uses hormones released by discrete glands that travel via the circulatory system.\", \"Answer: Similarities between the nervous system and endocrine system include both systems' roles in maintaining homeostasis by regulating cells, tissues, organs, and systems; both are controlled by negative feedback mechanisms; and chemical messengers are vital in both, despite differences in transmission and release. Differences include the nervous system being composed of interconnected neurons that transmit electrochemical pulses, while the endocrine system consists of discrete glands that release hormones into the circulatory system for signal transmission.\"]", "time_sec": 40.573, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 60.36, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "636", "question": "similarity and difference between nervous system and endocrine system", "gt": "Sure! Based on the given passages, here are the key differences and similarities between the nervous system and the endocrine system:\n\nDifferences:\n\n* The nervous system is made of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made of glands.\n* Transmission in the nervous system occurs through electrochemical pulses, while in the endocrine system, it is done through chemicals called hormones.\n* The nervous system is continuous and interconnected, while the endocrine system consists of discrete organs.\n* The nervous system uses neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system.\n\nSimilarities:\n\n* Both systems are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\n* Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\n* Chemical messengers are important in both systems, although their method of transmission and release differs.\n\nTherefore, based on the given passages, the nervous system and endocrine system have different structures and methods of transmission, but they share similar functions and regulatory mechanisms.", "pred": "The nervous system and endocrine system share similarities in their roles in maintaining homeostasis and using chemical messengers, but differ in cellular composition, signal transmission methods, and physical structure. Both systems regulate cell activities through chemical messengers and employ negative feedback mechanisms. However, the nervous system consists of interconnected neuron cells that transmit electrochemical pulses, while the endocrine system comprises discrete glands that release hormones. The nervous system transmits signals directly via neurons, whereas the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to carry hormonal signals. Additionally, the nervous system is continuous with physically connected cells, contrasting with the endocrine system's non-physically connected, discrete organs.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The nervous system and endocrine system share similarities in their roles in maintaining homeostasis and using chemical messengers, but differ in cellular composition, signal transmission methods, and physical structure. Both systems regulate cell activities through chemical messengers and employ negative feedback mechanisms. However, the nervous system consists of interconnected neuron cells that transmit electrochemical pulses, while the endocrine system comprises discrete glands that release hormones. The nervous system transmits signals directly via neurons, whereas the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to carry hormonal signals. Additionally, the nervous system is continuous with physically connected cells, contrasting with the endocrine system's non-physically connected, discrete organs.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The nervous system and endocrine system share similarities in their roles in maintaining homeostasis and using chemical messengers, but differ in cellular composition, signal transmission methods, and physical structure. Both systems regulate cell activities through chemical messengers and employ negative feedback mechanisms. However, the nervous system consists of interconnected neuron cells that transmit electrochemical pulses, while the endocrine system comprises discrete glands that release hormones. The nervous system transmits signals directly via neurons, whereas the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to carry hormonal signals. Additionally, the nervous system is continuous with physically connected cells, contrasting with the endocrine system's non-physically connected, discrete organs.\", \"Answer: Both the nervous system and endocrine system are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems, and both are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. They use chemical messengers, though the nervous system uses electrochemical pulses in neurons, while the endocrine system uses hormones released by glands into the circulatory system. The nervous system is continuous with interconnected cells, whereas the endocrine system consists of discrete, unconnected glands.\", \"Similarity:\\n- Both systems are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\\n- Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\\n- Chemical messengers are important in both systems.\\n\\nDifference:\\n- The nervous system is made of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made of glands.\\n- Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, while in the endocrine system, chemicals known as hormones are responsible for signal transmission.\\n- The nervous system is continuous with interconnected cells, while the endocrine system's organs are discrete and not physically connected.\\n- The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.\", \"Answer: Similarities: Both systems maintain homeostasis by regulating other cells, tissues, organs, and systems; both use chemical messengers and negative feedback mechanisms. Differences: The nervous system consists of neuron cells transmitting electrochemical pulses, while the endocrine system uses discrete glands releasing hormones via the circulatory system. The nervous system is continuous with interconnected cells, whereas the endocrine system's organs are physically separate and not directly connected.\", \"Answer: The nervous system and endocrine system share key similarities and differences. **Similarities**: Both systems maintain homeostasis by coordinating and regulating cells, tissues, organs, and systems, and both are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. They also use chemical messengers (neurotransmitters in the nervous system, hormones in the endocrine system) to transmit signals, though their transmission methods differ. **Differences**: The nervous system is composed of interconnected neuron cells and transmits signals via electrochemical pulses, while the endocrine system consists of discrete glands that release hormones into the circulatory system for signal transmission. The nervous system is continuous and physically interconnected, whereas the endocrine system’s organs are not physically connected.\"]", "time_sec": 45.098, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 54.44, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "637", "question": "health benefits to jalapenos", "gt": "Jalapenos have numerous health benefits due to their capsaicin content. They have pain-killing properties and can serve as a natural nasal decongestant. They may also aid in weight loss and prevent heart disease, heart attacks, and possibly even cancer. Additionally, jalapenos are full of vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants. They can also help increase cardiovascular health and defend heart health.", "pred": "The health benefits of jalapeños include pain relief due to their capsaicin content, acting as a spicy weight loss alternative, serving as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, being rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants, and supporting cardiovascular health by defending against heart attacks, strokes, and high blood pressure.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The health benefits of jalapeños include pain relief due to their capsaicin content, acting as a spicy weight loss alternative, serving as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, being rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants, and supporting cardiovascular health by defending against heart attacks, strokes, and high blood pressure.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The health benefits of jalapeños include pain relief due to their capsaicin content, acting as a spicy weight loss alternative, serving as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, being rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants, and supporting cardiovascular health by defending against heart attacks, strokes, and high blood pressure.\", \"Answer: The health benefits of jalapeños include pain relief due to their capsaicin content, serving as a spicy weight loss alternative, acting as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, being rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants, and contributing to cardiovascular health by defending heart health and increasing cardiovascular benefits.\", \"Answer: The health benefits of jalapeños include pain relief due to their capsaicin content, serving as a spicy weight loss alternative, acting as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially helping to prevent heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, being rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants, and supporting cardiovascular health by defending heart health and increasing cardiovascular benefits.\", \"Answer: Jalapeños offer several health benefits, including pain relief due to their capsaicin content, serving as a spicy weight loss alternative, acting as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, and being rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants that support cardiovascular health.\", \"Answer: The health benefits of jalapeños mentioned in the passages include pain relief due to their capsaicin content, acting as a spicy weight loss alternative, serving as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attacks, and cancer, and being rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants. Specifically, passage 2 lists these benefits, and passage 3 highlights cardiovascular health benefits, stating that jalapeños can help defend heart health and increase cardiovascular benefits, which may help with conditions like heart attack, strokes, or high blood pressure.\"]", "time_sec": 33.126, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 48.89, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "638", "question": "health benefits to jalapenos", "gt": "Jalapenos have health benefits such as pain relief, weight loss, nasal decongestant properties, and potential prevention of heart disease, heart attack, and cancer. They are also rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants. Additionally, jalapenos can help defend heart health and provide cardiovascular benefits.", "pred": "The health benefits of jalapeños include pain relief due to their capsaicin content, acting as a spicy weight loss alternative, serving as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, being rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants, and supporting cardiovascular health by helping to defend against heart attacks, strokes, and high blood pressure.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The health benefits of jalapeños include pain relief due to their capsaicin content, acting as a spicy weight loss alternative, serving as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, being rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants, and supporting cardiovascular health by helping to defend against heart attacks, strokes, and high blood pressure.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The health benefits of jalapeños include pain relief due to their capsaicin content, acting as a spicy weight loss alternative, serving as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, being rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants, and supporting cardiovascular health by helping to defend against heart attacks, strokes, and high blood pressure.\", \"Answer: The health benefits of jalapeños include pain relief due to their capsaicin content, serving as a spicy weight loss alternative, acting as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attacks, and cancer, being rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants, and supporting cardiovascular health by helping to defend against heart attacks, strokes, and high blood pressure.\", \"Answer: The health benefits of jalapeños include pain relief due to their capsaicin content, serving as a spicy weight loss alternative, acting as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, and providing essential vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants. Specifically, jalapeños support cardiovascular health by helping to defend against heart-related conditions like heart attacks and high blood pressure.\", \"Answer: Jalapeños offer several health benefits, including pain relief due to their pain-killing properties, support for weight loss as a spicy alternative, acting as a natural nasal decongestant, and potentially helping to prevent heart disease, heart attack, and cancer. They are also rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants, contributing to overall health. Specifically, they support cardiovascular health by helping to defend against heart-related conditions and improve cardiovascular health.\", \"Answer: The health benefits of jalapeños include pain relief due to their capsaicin content, acting as a spicy weight loss alternative, serving as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attacks, and cancer, and being rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants. Specifically, jalapeños support cardiovascular health by helping to defend against heart attacks, strokes, and high blood pressure.\"]", "time_sec": 33.947, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 54.55, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "639", "question": "health benefits to jalapenos", "gt": "Based on the given passages, jalapeños have various health benefits such as pain relief, weight loss, nasal decongestion, prevention of heart disease and even cancer, being full of vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants. Additionally, they have cardiovascular health benefits.", "pred": "Jalapeños offer several health benefits, including pain relief due to their capsaicin content, acting as a spicy weight loss alternative, serving as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, and providing essential vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants for overall health.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Jalapeños offer several health benefits, including pain relief due to their capsaicin content, acting as a spicy weight loss alternative, serving as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, and providing essential vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants for overall health.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Jalapeños offer several health benefits, including pain relief due to their capsaicin content, acting as a spicy weight loss alternative, serving as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, and providing essential vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants for overall health.\", \"Answer:Jalapeños offer several health benefits, including pain relief due to their capsaicin content, serving as a spicy weight loss alternative, acting as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, and providing essential vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants. Specifically, they support cardiovascular health by helping defend against heart-related conditions and improving overall heart health.\", \"Answer: Jalapeños offer several health benefits, including pain relief due to their pain-killing properties, support for weight loss as a spicy alternative, acting as a natural nasal decongestant, and potentially helping to prevent heart disease, heart attacks, and cancer. They are also rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants, contributing to overall health and specifically supporting cardiovascular health by defending the heart and enhancing cardiovascular benefits.\", \"Answer: Jalapeños offer several health benefits, including pain relief due to their pain-killing properties, support for weight loss as a spicy alternative, acting as a natural nasal decongestant, and potentially helping to prevent heart disease, heart attacks, and cancer. They are also rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants, contributing to overall health. Specifically, they support cardiovascular health by defending against heart attacks, strokes, and high blood pressure.\", \"Answer: The health benefits of jalapeños include pain relief due to their pain-killing properties, support for weight loss as a spicy alternative, acting as a natural nasal decongestant, potential prevention of heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, and providing essential vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants. Specifically, passage 2 lists these benefits, and passage 3 highlights cardiovascular health benefits, noting that jalapeños can help defend heart health and improve cardiovascular health, addressing concerns like heart attack, strokes, and high blood pressure.\"]", "time_sec": 34.661, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 46.15, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "640", "question": "health benefits to jalapenos", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are some health benefits of jalapenos:\n\n* Pain relief\n* Weight loss\n* Natural nasal decongestant\n* May help prevent heart disease, heart attack, and cancer\n* Good source of vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants\n* Cardiovascular health benefits, including defending against heart health concerns and increasing cardiovascular health benefits.\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question \"What are the health benefits of jalapenos?\" can be summarized as:\n\n* Pain relief\n* Weight loss\n* Nasal decongestant\n* Anti-cancer properties\n* Cardiovascular health benefits\n* Rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants.", "pred": "Jalapeño health benefits include pain relief due to capsaicin, acting as a spicy weight loss alternative, serving as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, and being rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer:Jalapeño health benefits include pain relief due to capsaicin, acting as a spicy weight loss alternative, serving as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, and being rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants.", "cot": "[\"Answer:Jalapeño health benefits include pain relief due to capsaicin, acting as a spicy weight loss alternative, serving as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, and being rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants.\", \"Answer: The health benefits of jalapeños include pain relief due to their capsaicin content, acting as a spicy weight loss alternative, serving as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attacks, and cancer, and providing essential vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants. Specifically, jalapeños support cardiovascular health by helping to defend against heart-related conditions like heart attacks and high blood pressure.\", \"Answer: Jalapeños offer several health benefits, including pain relief due to their pain-killing properties, support for weight loss as a spicy alternative, acting as a natural nasal decongestant, and potentially helping to prevent heart disease, heart attacks, and cancer. They are also rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants, contributing to overall health. Specifically, they support cardiovascular health by defending against heart-related conditions and enhancing cardiovascular benefits.\", \"Answer: The health benefits of jalapeños include pain relief due to their capsaicin content, acting as a spicy weight loss alternative, serving as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attacks, and cancer, and being rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants. Specifically, jalapeños may help defend against cardiovascular health concerns such as heart attack, strokes, and high blood pressure.\", \"Answer: The health benefits of jalapeños include pain relief due to their capsaicin content, acting as a spicy weight loss alternative, serving as a natural nasal decongestant, potentially preventing heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, and being rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants that support cardiovascular health.\"]", "time_sec": 31.344, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 38.3, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "641", "question": "differences and similarities between red and white blood cells", "gt": "Both red blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) are found in the blood and are part of the circulatory system. They are both formed from pluripotent stem cells in the red bone marrow and contain cell membranes and cytoplasm. The main difference is their functions. RBCs are responsible for delivering oxygen from the lungs to the tissue in the body, while WBCs are part of the immune system and help the body combat diseases, viruses, etc.", "pred": "Similarities: Both red blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) are formed elements of blood and are found in the circulatory system. They are both formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow. Differences: Red blood cells are responsible for oxygen delivery from the lungs to tissues throughout the body, while white blood cells are part of the immune system and help the body combat disease, viruses, and other pathogens.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Similarities: Both red blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) are formed elements of blood and are found in the circulatory system. They are both formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow. Differences: Red blood cells are responsible for oxygen delivery from the lungs to tissues throughout the body, while white blood cells are part of the immune system and help the body combat disease, viruses, and other pathogens.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Similarities: Both red blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) are formed elements of blood and are found in the circulatory system. They are both formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow. Differences: Red blood cells are responsible for oxygen delivery from the lungs to tissues throughout the body, while white blood cells are part of the immune system and help the body combat disease, viruses, and other pathogens.\", \"Answer: Similarities: Both red blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) are formed elements of blood and are found in the circulatory system. They are both formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow. Differences: RBCs are responsible for oxygen delivery from the lungs to tissues, while WBCs are part of the immune system and help combat disease, viruses, etc. RBCs carry oxygen, while WBCs do not have this function.\", \"Answer: Similarities: Both red blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) are formed elements of blood and are found in the circulatory system. They are both formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow and have a cell membrane and cytoplasm. Differences: RBCs are responsible for oxygen delivery from the lungs to the body's tissues, while WBCs are part of the immune system and help combat disease, viruses, and other pathogens. RBCs do not have a nucleus, whereas WBCs do. The passages indicate that RBCs carry oxygen and are part of the circulatory system, while WBCs are part of the immune system and help the body fight disease.\", \"Answer: Both red blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) are formed elements of blood and are found in the circulatory system. They are both formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow. However, their functions differ: RBCs are responsible for oxygen delivery from the lungs to the body's tissues, while WBCs are part of the immune system that combats disease and viruses. Additionally, both cell types have a cell membrane and cytoplasm.\", \"Answer: Similarities: Both red blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) are formed elements of blood and are found in the circulatory system. They are both formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow. Differences: Red blood cells are responsible for oxygen delivery from the lungs to tissues, while white blood cells are part of the immune system and help combat disease, viruses, etc.\"]", "time_sec": 40.47, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 68.04, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "643", "question": "differences and similarities between red and white blood cells", "gt": "Based on the given passages, we can see that the primary difference between red and white blood cells is their respective functions within the body. Red blood cells are responsible for oxygen delivery to tissues, while white blood cells are part of the immune system and help combat diseases and viruses. However, both types of cells share some common features, such as being formed elements of blood, being formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow, having a cell membrane and cytoplasm, and circulating in the fluids in blood vessels, which is pumped by the heart.", "pred": "Similarities: Both red blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) are formed elements of blood and are found in the circulatory system. They are formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow. Differences: RBCs are responsible for oxygen delivery from the lungs to tissues, while WBCs are part of the immune system and help combat disease, viruses, and other pathogens. RBCs lack a cell membrane and cytoplasm, whereas WBCs have both a cell membrane and cytoplasm.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Similarities: Both red blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) are formed elements of blood and are found in the circulatory system. They are formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow. Differences: RBCs are responsible for oxygen delivery from the lungs to tissues, while WBCs are part of the immune system and help combat disease, viruses, and other pathogens. RBCs lack a cell membrane and cytoplasm, whereas WBCs have both a cell membrane and cytoplasm.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Similarities: Both red blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) are formed elements of blood and are found in the circulatory system. They are formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow. Differences: RBCs are responsible for oxygen delivery from the lungs to tissues, while WBCs are part of the immune system and help combat disease, viruses, and other pathogens. RBCs lack a cell membrane and cytoplasm, whereas WBCs have both a cell membrane and cytoplasm.\", \"Answer: Similarities between red and white blood cells include that both are formed elements of blood and are formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow. Differences include that red blood cells are responsible for oxygen delivery from the lungs to the body's tissues, while white blood cells are part of the immune system that combat disease and viruses. Red blood cells are part of the circulatory system and carry oxygen throughout the body, while white blood cells are found in the circulatory system but primarily function in immune defense rather than oxygen transport.\", \"Answer: Similarities: Both red blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) are formed elements of blood and are found in the circulatory system. They are formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow. Differences: Red blood cells are responsible for oxygen delivery from the lungs to tissues, while white blood cells are part of the immune system and help combat disease, viruses, and other pathogens. Red blood cells do not have a cell membrane or cytoplasm, whereas white blood cells have both a cell membrane and cytoplasm. Red blood cells are involved in the circulatory system, while white blood cells are part of the immune system.\", \"Answer: Similarities: Both red blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) are formed elements of blood and are found in the circulatory system. They are both formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow. Differences: Red blood cells are responsible for oxygen delivery from the lungs to the body's tissues, while white blood cells are part of the immune system and help combat disease, viruses, and other pathogens. Red blood cells lack a cell membrane and cytoplasm, whereas white blood cells have both a cell membrane and cytoplasm.\", \"Answer: Red blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) share several similarities: both are formed elements of blood, both are formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow, both are found in blood and the circulatory system, and both have cell membranes and cytoplasm. However, they differ significantly in function: RBCs deliver oxygen from the lungs to body tissues, while WBCs are part of the immune system that combat disease, viruses, and other pathogens. The passages indicate RBCs carry oxygen and are part of the circulatory system, whereas WBCs are part of the immune system and help the body combat disease, viruses, etc.\"]", "time_sec": 45.945, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 50.85, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 5.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "644", "question": "how to plant a potato that has sprouted", "gt": "To plant a potato that has sprouted, first cut large potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each, being careful not to break the long sprouts. Place these chunks on a tray and let them dry out in a cool, dark place overnight. Small, sprouted potatoes can be planted whole. Next, prepare your planting area by turning the soil over with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches, removing any large rocks or debris. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep. Cut your potatoes into cubes of about 1 to 2 inches, making sure to leave a sprout on each cube. Place these cubes in the trench, sprouted side facing up, spaced about 6 to 8 inches apart. Cover the potatoes with compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings. Water the potatoes thoroughly and regularly when the soil is dry to the touch. Finally, add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, keeping it at least 4 inches away from the sprouting potato vines. This mulch will help keep the soil cool and maintain a consistent moisture level.", "pred": "To plant a sprouted potato, first cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each, using a sharp knife and avoiding breaking the long sprouts; small potatoes can be planted whole. Place the chunks on a tray in a cool, dark location overnight to dry the cut ends. Then, turn the soil over to a depth of 12 inches, remove rocks and debris, and dig a 4‑inch‑wide trench 6 to 8 inches deep. Separate each cube about 6 to 8 inches apart in the trench, positioning the sprouted or eye side facing up, and cover with a layer of compost before backfilling with soil. Water the potatoes thoroughly and continue regular watering when the soil is dry, adding a 4‑inch mulch layer around the plants while keeping it 4 inches away from the vines.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, first cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each, using a sharp knife and avoiding breaking the long sprouts; small potatoes can be planted whole. Place the chunks on a tray in a cool, dark location overnight to dry the cut ends. Then, turn the soil over to a depth of 12 inches, remove rocks and debris, and dig a 4‑inch‑wide trench 6 to 8 inches deep. Separate each cube about 6 to 8 inches apart in the trench, positioning the sprouted or eye side facing up, and cover with a layer of compost before backfilling with soil. Water the potatoes thoroughly and continue regular watering when the soil is dry, adding a 4‑inch mulch layer around the plants while keeping it 4 inches away from the vines.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, first cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each, using a sharp knife and avoiding breaking the long sprouts; small potatoes can be planted whole. Place the chunks on a tray in a cool, dark location overnight to dry the cut ends. Then, turn the soil over to a depth of 12 inches, remove rocks and debris, and dig a 4‑inch‑wide trench 6 to 8 inches deep. Separate each cube about 6 to 8 inches apart in the trench, positioning the sprouted or eye side facing up, and cover with a layer of compost before backfilling with soil. Water the potatoes thoroughly and continue regular watering when the soil is dry, adding a 4‑inch mulch layer around the plants while keeping it 4 inches away from the vines.\", \"Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, cut it into cubes with two or three eyes each, ensuring a sprout remains on each cube. Dig a trench 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, place the cubes with the sprouted side up, spaced 6 to 8 inches apart, cover with compost, then replace the soil.\", \"Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each (as described in passage 1), then place the chunks in a trench 6 to 8 inches deep and 4 inches wide (from passage 3), ensuring the sprouted or eye side faces up. Space the chunks 6 to 8 inches apart in the trench, cover them with compost, and then replace the dirt. After planting, water the potatoes thoroughly and continue regular watering when the soil is dry, while adding a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, keeping it 4 inches away from the vines (passage 1).\", \"Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, first cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each, ensuring the long sprouts remain intact, and let the cut ends dry overnight in a cool, dark location. Alternatively, small potatoes can be planted whole. Then, turn the soil over to a depth of 12 inches, breaking it up finely, and remove large rocks and debris. Dig a trench 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, spacing multiple rows 24 to 36 inches apart. Place the potato chunks or whole potatoes into the trench with the sprouted or eye side facing up, spacing them 6 to 8 inches apart. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top. Water the planted potatoes thoroughly and continue regular watering when the soil is dry to the touch. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, keeping it at least 4 inches away from the sprouting vines to maintain soil coolness and moisture.\", \"Answer: Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each, using a sharp knife without breaking the long sprouts. Place the chunks on a tray and set them in a cool, dark location overnight to dry out. Plant the chunks in a trench 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, with the sprouted or eye side facing up, spaced about 6 to 8 inches apart. Cover the chunks with compost and then replace the dirt on top. Water the potatoes thoroughly after planting and continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting vines.\"]", "time_sec": 56.475, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 62.78, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.2, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "645", "question": "how to plant a potato that has sprouted", "gt": "To plant a sprouted potato, cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each. Place the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet and let them dry out overnight. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep in a prepared planting area. Place the sprouted or eye side of the potato chunks facing up, separating each chunk by about 6 to 8 inches. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top. Water the potatoes thoroughly and continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants to keep the soil cool and maintain moisture.", "pred": "To plant a sprouted potato, cut it into cubes about 1 to 2 inches on each side, ensuring each cube has a sprout or eye facing up, and place the cubes into a trench 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, spacing them 6 to 8 inches apart, then cover with compost and soil, as detailed in the instructions passage.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, cut it into cubes about 1 to 2 inches on each side, ensuring each cube has a sprout or eye facing up, and place the cubes into a trench 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, spacing them 6 to 8 inches apart, then cover with compost and soil, as detailed in the instructions passage.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, cut it into cubes about 1 to 2 inches on each side, ensuring each cube has a sprout or eye facing up, and place the cubes into a trench 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, spacing them 6 to 8 inches apart, then cover with compost and soil, as detailed in the instructions passage.\", \"Answer: Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each (small potatoes can be planted whole), place them in a cool, dark location overnight to dry the cut ends, then plant the sprouted or eye side of the chunks 6 to 8 inches apart in a 4-inch wide and 6 to 8 inches deep trench, covering them with compost and soil after ensuring the soil is turned over to 12 inches depth and free of rocks and debris.\", \"Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, first cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each, ensuring the long sprouts remain intact. Place the chunks on a tray and let them dry in a cool, dark location overnight. Then, turn the soil over to a depth of 12 inches, removing rocks and debris. Dig a trench 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, spacing rows 24 to 36 inches apart if planting multiple rows. Place the potato chunks in the trench with the sprouted or eye side facing up, spacing them 6 to 8 inches apart. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost, then replace the dirt on top. Water the potatoes thoroughly and continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting vines to keep the soil cool and maintain consistent moisture.\", \"Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, first cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each, ensuring not to break the long sprouts. Place the chunks on a tray in a cool, dark location overnight to dry. Alternatively, if planting whole small potatoes, ensure they are sprouted and then water them thoroughly, continuing regular watering when the soil is dry. For larger sprouted potatoes, cut them into 1 to 2 inch cubes, leaving a sprout on each, and place them in a trench 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, with the sprouted side facing up. Space the cubes 6 to 8 inches apart, cover with compost, and then replace the dirt. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, keeping it 4 inches away from the sprouting vines to maintain soil coolness and moisture.\", \"Answer: Cut large, sprouted potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each, being careful to not break the long sprouts. Place the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet, and set them in a cool, dark location overnight so the cut ends can dry out. Then, turn the soil over in your planned planting area with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and break the dirt up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris while hoeing. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep. Place the sprouted or eye side of the potato cubes facing up in the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings. Water the potatoes thoroughly and continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines.\"]", "time_sec": 58.988, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 46.15, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "646", "question": "how to plant a potato that has sprouted", "gt": "To plant a potato that has sprouted, follow these steps:\n\n1. Cut large, sprouted potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each using a sharp knife.\n2. Set the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet and allow them to dry out for a cool, dark location overnight.\n3. Once the cut ends are dry, thoroughly wash the potatoes.\n4. Plant the potatoes in the ground, either whole or cut into cubes with a sprout on each cube.\n5. Space the potatoes about 6 to 8 inches apart and cover them with a layer of compost.\n6. Replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\n7. Water the potatoes regularly when the soil is dry to the touch.\n8. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines.", "pred": "To plant a sprouted potato, first cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each, ensuring not to break the long sprouts, and place them in a cool, dark location overnight to dry the cut ends; small potatoes can be planted whole after thorough watering, and the planting process involves turning the soil to a depth of 12 inches, breaking it up finely, removing rocks and debris, digging a 4-inch wide and 6 to 8 inches deep trench, spacing rows 24 to 36 inches apart if planting multiple rows, cutting potatoes into 1 to 2-inch cubes with a sprout on each, placing the cubes in the trench with the sprouted or eye side facing up, separating each cube by about 6 to 8 inches, covering with compost and then replacing the dirt on top, followed by thorough watering and regular watering when the soil is dry to the touch, and adding a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants while pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, first cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each, ensuring not to break the long sprouts, and place them in a cool, dark location overnight to dry the cut ends; small potatoes can be planted whole after thorough watering, and the planting process involves turning the soil to a depth of 12 inches, breaking it up finely, removing rocks and debris, digging a 4-inch wide and 6 to 8 inches deep trench, spacing rows 24 to 36 inches apart if planting multiple rows, cutting potatoes into 1 to 2-inch cubes with a sprout on each, placing the cubes in the trench with the sprouted or eye side facing up, separating each cube by about 6 to 8 inches, covering with compost and then replacing the dirt on top, followed by thorough watering and regular watering when the soil is dry to the touch, and adding a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants while pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines.", "cot": "[\"Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, first cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each, ensuring not to break the long sprouts, and place them in a cool, dark location overnight to dry the cut ends; small potatoes can be planted whole after thorough watering, and the planting process involves turning the soil to a depth of 12 inches, breaking it up finely, removing rocks and debris, digging a 4-inch wide and 6 to 8 inches deep trench, spacing rows 24 to 36 inches apart if planting multiple rows, cutting potatoes into 1 to 2-inch cubes with a sprout on each, placing the cubes in the trench with the sprouted or eye side facing up, separating each cube by about 6 to 8 inches, covering with compost and then replacing the dirt on top, followed by thorough watering and regular watering when the soil is dry to the touch, and adding a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants while pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines.\", \"Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, first cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each, ensuring the long sprouts remain intact. Place the chunks on a tray in a cool, dark location overnight to dry the cut ends. Then, turn the soil over to a depth of 12 inches, remove rocks and debris, and dig a trench 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep. Place the sprouted side of the potato chunks facing up in the trench, spacing them 6 to 8 inches apart. Cover the potatoes with compost and then replace the dirt on top. Water the planted potatoes thoroughly and continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, keeping it 4 inches away from the sprouting vines to maintain soil coolness and moisture.\", \"Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, first cut it into cubes with two or three eyes each, ensuring each cube has a sprout. Place the cubes in a trench 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, with the sprouted or eye side facing up, spaced 6 to 8 inches apart. Cover with compost and then replace the dirt. Alternatively, small potatoes can be planted whole after being thoroughly watered and kept in a cool, dark location overnight to dry cut ends.\", \"Answer: Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each (or plant small potatoes whole), place the chunks on a tray and let them dry overnight in a cool, dark location, then plant them in a trench 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep with the sprouted/eye side facing up, spacing each chunk about 6 to 8 inches apart, covering with compost and soil, watering thoroughly and regularly, and adding a 4‑inch layer of mulch around the plants while keeping it 4 inches away from the vines.\", \"Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each (or use small potatoes whole), let the cut ends dry overnight, then place the sprouted side up in a trench 4 inches wide and 6–8 inches deep, spacing chunks 6–8 inches apart, and cover with soil or compost.\"]", "time_sec": 52.755, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thin