{"id": "647", "question": "how to plant a potato that has sprouted", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are the steps to plant a potato that has sprouted:\n\n1. Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each, making sure to not break the long sprouts.\n2. Allow the cut ends to dry out overnight in a cool, dark location.\n3. Plant the potato chunks or whole potatoes in well-draining soil, spacing them 24 to 36 inches apart.\n4. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, and place the potato pieces in the bottom of the trench with the sprouted side facing up.\n5. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\n\nNote that the passages do not provide specific information on how to care for the potatoes after planting, such as watering and fertilization.", "pred": "Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each, place them in a cool, dark location overnight to dry the cut ends, then plant the chunks in a trench 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep with the sprouted or eye side facing up, spacing each chunk 6 to 8 inches apart, and cover with compost and soil.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each, place them in a cool, dark location overnight to dry the cut ends, then plant the chunks in a trench 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep with the sprouted or eye side facing up, spacing each chunk 6 to 8 inches apart, and cover with compost and soil.", "cot": "[\"Answer: Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each, place them in a cool, dark location overnight to dry the cut ends, then plant the chunks in a trench 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep with the sprouted or eye side facing up, spacing each chunk 6 to 8 inches apart, and cover with compost and soil.\", \"Answer: Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each, using a sharp knife without breaking the long sprouts. Place the chunks on a tray and set them in a cool, dark location overnight to dry. Plant the chunks in a trench 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, with the sprouted or eye side facing up. Space each chunk 6 to 8 inches apart, cover with compost, and then replace the dirt. Water the planted potatoes thoroughly and continue regular watering when the soil is dry to the touch, adding a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants while keeping it 4 inches away from the vines.\", \"Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, first cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each, ensuring the long sprouts remain intact. Place the chunks on a tray in a cool, dark location overnight to dry the cut ends. Alternatively, small potatoes can be planted whole after being thoroughly watered. For planting, dig a trench 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, ensuring the soil is turned over to 12 inches depth and free of rocks. Position the sprouted or eye-side of the potato chunks 6 to 8 inches apart in the trench, then cover with compost and soil. Maintain regular watering when the soil is dry and apply a 4-inch mulch layer, keeping it 4 inches away from the vines to retain moisture and cool the soil.\", \"Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each (as in passage 1) or into 1 to 2 inch cubes, leaving a sprout on each cube (as in passage 3). Place the cubes in a trench 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, with the sprouted or eye side facing up, separating each cube by 6 to 8 inches (passage 3). Cover with compost and then replace the dirt (passage 3). This method ensures proper planting of sprouted potatoes.\", \"Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each, place them in a cool, dark location overnight to dry, then plant the sprouted side up in a trench 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, spacing each chunk 6 to 8 inches apart, and cover with compost and soil.\"]", "time_sec": 44.719, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 56.72, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "648", "question": "color of urin meaning", "gt": "The color of urine can indicate various health conditions. Normal urine is typically pale yellow, due to the pigment urochrome. If kidney diseases are present, the filtration and re-absorption stages of urine production may be malfunctioning, altering this pale color. Red urine is typically a sign of blood in the urine, which can indicate serious conditions like infections or cancer. Deep purple urine is an identifying characteristic of porphyria, a rare, inherited disorder of red blood cells. Foods and medications can also affect urine color, with beets, berries, and fava beans being among the most likely to cause changes.", "pred": "The color of urine can indicate various health conditions. Seeing red urine typically means there is blood in the urine, which may signify serious issues like infection or cancer, requiring medical attention. Deep purple urine is a sign of porphyria, a rare inherited disorder of red blood cells. Normal urine color is pale yellow, which is altered in cases of kidney diseases affecting filtration and reabsorption.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The color of urine can indicate various health conditions. Seeing red urine typically means there is blood in the urine, which may signify serious issues like infection or cancer, requiring medical attention. Deep purple urine is a sign of porphyria, a rare inherited disorder of red blood cells. Normal urine color is pale yellow, which is altered in cases of kidney diseases affecting filtration and reabsorption.", "cot": "[\"Answer: The color of urine can indicate various health conditions. Seeing red urine typically means there is blood in the urine, which may signify serious issues like infection or cancer, requiring medical attention. Deep purple urine is a sign of porphyria, a rare inherited disorder of red blood cells. Normal urine color is pale yellow, which is altered in cases of kidney diseases affecting filtration and reabsorption.\", \"Answer: The color of urine can indicate various health conditions. Seeing red urine typically suggests the presence of blood, which may signal serious issues like infection or cancer. Deep purple urine is a sign of porphyria, a rare inherited disorder affecting red blood cells. Normal urine color is pale yellow, caused by the pigment urochrome, but this can change due to kidney diseases affecting filtration and re-absorption. Foods like beets, berries, and fava beans can also alter urine color.\", \"Answer: The color of urine can indicate various health conditions. Pale yellow is considered normal, due to the pigment urochrome, but red urine may signify blood, which could indicate serious issues like infection or cancer, and deep purple urine can be a sign of porphyria, a rare inherited disorder. Additionally, certain foods and medications can alter urine color.\", \"Answer: The color of urine can indicate various health conditions. Seeing red urine typically means there is blood in the urine, which could be a sign of a serious condition like an infection or cancer. Deep purple urine is a characteristic of porphyria, a rare inherited disorder of red blood cells. The normal color of urine is pale yellow, which is due to the pigment urochrome, but this color can be altered in cases of kidney diseases.\", \"Answer: The color of urine can indicate various health conditions. For example, red urine typically signifies the presence of blood, which may indicate serious issues like infection or cancer. Deep purple urine is associated with porphyria, a rare inherited disorder. Normal urine is pale yellow due to the pigment urochrome, but this can change with kidney diseases affecting filtration and re-absorption.\"]", "time_sec": 33.361, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-trust-remote-code", "method": "sc", "num_rollout": 5, "temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "enable_th